'8011584 | https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/7098601/beck-hill-trail',1,'2023-09-12 10:08:27','2023-09-19 06:00:29'),(159,'High Grade','8011584','hikingproject','7098658',6,NULL,'Ridge top hiking connects a main parking area near I-15 to the Prospector Trail.',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,37.2067258,-113.3972219,'',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'Easy/Intermediate',3125,3042,NULL,0.4,'Point to Point',NULL,85,15,0.04,0.13,NULL,NULL,'https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/gpx/7098658','8011584 | https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/7098658/high-grade',1,'2023-09-12 10:08:29','2023-09-19 06:00:29'),(160,'Prospector Trail','8011584','hikingproject','7034721',6,NULL,'A 7.1 mile long twisty and rolling trail.',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,37.1571345,-113.4796244,'',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'Intermediate',3340,3100,NULL,7.1,'Point to Point',NULL,320,474,0.02,0.15,NULL,NULL,'https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/gpx/7034721','8011584 | https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/7034721/prospector-trail',1,'2023-09-12 10:08:30','2023-09-19 06:00:29'),(161,'Broken Mesa Rim Trail','8011584','hikingproject','7037976',6,'Located 5 miles north of St. George, the Broken Mesa Rim Trail is quite rocky, traversing lava flows. The trail can be used year-round. It can be done as an 8-mile point-to-point with shuttle after adding Icehouse Trail, or as a 14.5-mile loop. If you do the loop option, you\'ll climb a total 1300ft.\r\n\r\nThere are some nice views along this trail.','This trail is quite rocky but has nice views.',NULL,NULL,NULL,'Leashed',37.2232943,-113.5739834,'Views',NULL,'BLM Utah - St. George Field Office','http://www.blm.gov/ut/st/en/fo/st__george.html',NULL,'Difficult',5106,4311,NULL,4.6,'Point to Point',NULL,451,808,0.05,0.14,NULL,NULL,'https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/gpx/7037976','8011584 | https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/7037976/broken-mesa-rim-trail',1,'2023-09-12 10:08:31','2023-09-19 06:00:29'),(162,'Icehouse Trail','8011584','hikingproject','7039994',6,NULL,'This is a six-mile downhill romp from the base of the Pine Valley Mountains down to Washington.',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,37.2288735,-113.5326160,'',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'Difficult',4980,3025,NULL,6.8,'Point to Point',NULL,26,1981,0.06,0.17,NULL,NULL,'https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/gpx/7039994','8011584 | https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/7039994/icehouse-trail',1,'2023-09-12 10:08:33','2023-09-19 06:00:29'),(163,'Dino Cliffs Trail','8011584','hikingproject','7039995',6,NULL,'Good short slickrock section with nice vistas and dinosaur tracks.',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,37.1608000,-113.4774774,'',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'Intermediate/Difficult',3295,3127,NULL,2.0,'Point to Point',NULL,83,175,0.02,0.23,NULL,NULL,'https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/gpx/7039995','8011584 | https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/7039995/dino-cliffs-trail',1,'2023-09-12 10:08:36','2023-09-19 06:00:29'),(164,'Grapevine Trail','8011584','hikingproject','7039996',6,NULL,'A short doubletrack connector to Church Rocks.',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,37.1504616,-113.4894609,'',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'Easy',3275,3083,NULL,1.0,'Point to Point',NULL,193,0,0.04,0.07,NULL,NULL,'https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/gpx/7039996','8011584 | https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/7039996/grapevine-trail',1,'2023-09-12 10:08:38','2023-09-19 06:00:29'),(165,'Owenhtml5-dom-document-internal-entity2-039-ends Loop','8011584','hikingproject','7042373',6,NULL,'A super fun hike over winding red rock overlooking the city.',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,37.1188649,-113.5829037,'',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'Intermediate',3252,3076,NULL,1.4,'Point to Point',NULL,342,236,0.08,0.21,NULL,NULL,'https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/gpx/7042373','8011584 | https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/7042373/owens-loop',1,'2023-09-12 10:08:42','2023-09-19 06:00:29'),(166,'City Creek Trail','8011584','hikingproject','7042378',6,NULL,'This trail heads over sand and red rock with great views of town and the surrounding hills/rock formations.',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,37.1347723,-113.5975103,'',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'Intermediate',3317,3058,NULL,2.9,'Point to Point',NULL,416,365,0.05,0.30,NULL,NULL,'https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/gpx/7042378','8011584 | https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/7042378/city-creek-trail',1,'2023-09-12 10:08:45','2023-09-19 06:00:29'),(167,'Brooks Nature Trail','8011584','hikingproject','7066018',6,'Family Friendly\n            \n                Short, with a variety of things to see along the way.','The best route to get to Owen\'s Loop, through a surprisingly pretty riparian area.',NULL,NULL,NULL,'Unknown',37.1159495,-113.5830205,'Lake / Wildlife',NULL,'Red Cliffs Desert Reserve','http://www.redcliffsdesertreserve.com/',NULL,'Easy/Intermediate',3117,2936,NULL,0.3,'Point to Point',NULL,181,0,0.11,0.20,NULL,NULL,'https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/gpx/7066018','8011584 | https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/7066018/brooks-nature-trail',1,'2023-09-12 10:08:49','2023-09-19 06:00:29'),(168,'Mustang Pass','8011584','hikingproject','7094899',6,NULL,'A rocky singletrack through charred post-fire desert with good views of the surrounding rocks.',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,37.1664626,-113.5263817,'',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'Intermediate/Difficult',3523,3198,NULL,2.8,'Point to Point',NULL,348,305,0.04,0.09,NULL,NULL,'https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/gpx/7094899','8011584 | https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/7094899/mustang-pass',1,'2023-09-12 10:08:50','2023-09-19 06:00:29'),(169,'Church Rocks','8011584','hikingproject','7095762',6,NULL,'A short, fun, mostly smooth-rock hike.',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,37.1614014,-113.4714497,'',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'Intermediate/Difficult',3364,3212,NULL,2.3,'Point to Point',NULL,211,227,0.04,0.11,NULL,NULL,'https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/gpx/7095762','8011584 | https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/7095762/church-rocks',1,'2023-09-12 10:08:53','2023-09-19 06:00:29'),(170,'Bracken Connector','8011584','hikingproject','7095851',6,NULL,'Needs Summary',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,37.1611222,-113.4764803,'',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'Difficult',3309,3215,NULL,0.4,'Point to Point',NULL,23,94,0.06,0.12,NULL,NULL,'https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/gpx/7095851','8011584 | https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/7095851/bracken-connector',1,'2023-09-12 10:08:55','2023-09-19 06:00:29'),(171,'Spanish Wash','8011584','hikingproject','7095852',6,NULL,'Unrunnable slog through deep sand.',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,37.1786025,-113.4526390,'',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'Easy/Intermediate',3279,3216,NULL,0.6,'Point to Point',NULL,14,76,0.03,0.11,NULL,NULL,'https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/gpx/7095852','8011584 | https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/7095852/spanish-wash',1,'2023-09-12 10:08:56','2023-09-19 06:00:29'),(172,'Historic Babylon Trail','8011584','hikingproject','7051583',6,'The trailhead has space for about three or four cars. Watch for an obvious gap in the cliffs very close to the trailhead, this is where you should park. Just before exiting the gap, look to the left for some interesting petroglyphs. \r\n\r\nThe trail follows a dry wash through some heavy vegetation for a short distance. When you emerge from the vegetation, the Little Purgatory Trail will be on your left. Continue straight as the Babylon Trail works its way uphill across varied terrain. At some points the trail is difficult to follow. Watch for cairns, some of which are small.\r\n\r\nAt about 2.5 miles, the trail reaches a barbed wire fence overlooking the town of Leeds. Make a sharp left and the trail will steadily climb, at times passing through the fence in several locations. After one long switchback, the trail ends and the Little Purgatory Trail begins.','Open desert trail with no shade and some interesting petroglyphs at the beginning.',NULL,NULL,NULL,'Unknown',37.2019820,-113.3488656,'Views',NULL,'Red Cliffs Desert Reserve','http://www.redcliffsdesertreserve.com/',NULL,'Difficult',3705,2907,NULL,3.0,'Point to Point',NULL,855,57,0.06,0.18,NULL,NULL,'https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/gpx/7051583','8011584 | https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/7051583/historic-babylon-trail',1,'2023-09-12 10:08:57','2023-09-19 06:00:29'),(173,'Little Purgatory Trail','8011584','hikingproject','7051585',6,'Full exposure to the sun. Hike this trail early in the morning.','Open desert trail with a very long and steep section. Offers great views of Sandstone Mountain and the Red Cliffs.',NULL,NULL,NULL,'Unknown',37.2055452,-113.3492159,'Views',NULL,'Red Cliffs Desert Reserve','http://www.redcliffsdesertreserve.com/',NULL,'Difficult',3819,2963,NULL,2.8,'Point to Point',NULL,867,104,0.07,0.16,NULL,NULL,'https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/gpx/7051585','8011584 | https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/7051585/little-purgatory-trail',1,'2023-09-12 10:09:02','2023-09-19 06:00:29'),(174,'The Vortex','8022977','hikingproject','7077313',6,'The gravel road becomes very muddy in spots after it has rained.','Red rock country with beautiful views of Dammeron Valley and Pine Mountain.',NULL,NULL,NULL,'Leashed',37.2800402,-113.7391656,'Views',NULL,'BLM Utah - St. George Field Office','http://www.blm.gov/ut/st/en/fo/st__george.html',NULL,'Difficult',4405,4018,NULL,1.1,'Point to Point',NULL,399,101,0.09,0.24,NULL,NULL,'https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/gpx/7077313','8022977 | https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/7077313/the-vortex',1,'2023-09-12 10:09:04','2023-09-19 06:00:29'),(175,'Chinle Trail','8015770','hikingproject','7001597',7,'The Chinle Trail starts from the Anasazi Way sub-division and crosses the open desert as it winds toward the base of Mount Kinesava. The trail travels through the open desert and offers up some amazing and sweeping views of the cliffs behind great formations like the West Temple and Mount Kinesava. \r\n\r\nChinle Trail crosses areas of petrified wood and has highly developed areas of the cryptobiotic soil. This trail is very hot in the summer but pleasant in the spring and fall. Coalpits Wash has no developed trail, but there is a well beaten path that is easy to follow to the junction with Scoggins Wash. At the junction of Scoggins Wash, bear left and head up to the narrows and pools nestled between enormous boulders and small waterfalls. This trail is best from the fall to the spring due to its low elevation.','Wind your way to the base of Mount Kinesava on this open desert trail.',NULL,NULL,NULL,'No Dogs',37.1613155,-113.0194085,'Birding / Fall Colors / River/Creek / Views / Wildflowers / Wildlife',NULL,'National Park Service - Zion National Park','http://nps.gov/zion',NULL,'Intermediate/Difficult',4401,3810,NULL,8.1,'Point to Point',NULL,875,576,0.03,0.13,NULL,NULL,'https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/gpx/7001597','8015770 | https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/7001597/chinle-trail',1,'2023-09-12 10:09:06','2023-09-19 06:00:29'),(176,'Eagle Crags Trail','8015770','hikingproject','7100664',7,'The road to the top is rough. Though I saw one non-AWD/4WD get to the top, I would have liked to have beheld that, as well as him getting back down. Ignore all the signs from the homeowners who don\'t want people to use that access road. It is BLM at the top (with amazing distributed camp sites) and the access road is 100% ok for anyone to use.','Short trail with amazing views of Zion and the surrounding canyons.',NULL,NULL,NULL,'Leashed',37.1474257,-113.0311405,'Views',NULL,'BLM Utah - St. George Field Office','http://www.blm.gov/ut/st/en/fo/st__george.html',NULL,'Intermediate/Difficult',5300,4259,NULL,2.9,'Point to Point',NULL,1046,164,0.08,0.28,NULL,NULL,'https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/gpx/7100664','8015770 | https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/7100664/eagle-crags-trail',1,'2023-09-12 10:09:07','2023-09-19 06:00:29'),(177,'Barrel Roll','8010543','hikingproject','7003446',6,'Barrel Roll is an excellent, well-marked trail that\'s easy to keep a steady pace on. The trail has some decent elevation gain but never feels too steep. Earn some great views of Santa Clara and St. George from multiple vantages along this trail.\r\n\r\nBarrel Roll is a loop that can be used in either a clockwise or counter-clockwise direction. What makes this trail special is its close proximity to the communities of Santa Clara and Ivins, yet when on the trail suburban life does not intrude. Views along the trail vary from the spectacular Red Mountain and Pine Valley Mountains to the Santa Clara River corridor, and the desert mesas of the Santa Clara River Reserve.','A rolling trail - not too difficult - with excellent views of Santa Clara and St. George.',NULL,NULL,NULL,'Unknown',37.1210281,-113.6757715,'Views / Wildflowers',NULL,'BLM Utah - St. George Field Office','http://www.blm.gov/ut/st/en/fo/st__george.html',NULL,'Intermediate',3454,3156,NULL,5.8,'Point to Point',NULL,523,506,0.03,0.14,NULL,NULL,'https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/gpx/7003446','8010543 | https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/7003446/barrel-roll',1,'2023-09-12 10:09:08','2023-09-19 06:00:29'),(178,'Sidewinder','8010543','hikingproject','7003768',6,NULL,'Wind along a narrow mesa on switchbacks as this trail takes full advantange of awe-inspiring vistas.',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,37.1233130,-113.6859674,'',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'Intermediate',3643,3291,NULL,1.7,'Point to Point',NULL,372,44,0.05,0.09,NULL,NULL,'https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/gpx/7003768','8010543 | https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/7003768/sidewinder',1,'2023-09-12 10:09:10','2023-09-19 06:00:29'),(179,'Precipice','8010543','hikingproject','7003770',6,'The Precipice Trail starts on the left about 50 feet from the trailhead stepover off the Barrel Roll Trail. It connects to the Sidewinder Trail and can provide an alternative route for the lower section of Barrel Roll. Although it sits just below Barrel Roll, Precipice offers different terrain, plants, and views. \r\n \r\nPrecipice is so named because of the tight squeeze that occurs in the middle section of the trail. Huge rocks slabs tumble down the slope from the upper rim and pinch the trail against a lower cliff edge. While trail construction has moderated the technical aspects of the trail, exposure is still a factor.','Beautiful desert trail making its way through some awesome vantages and areas of exposure.',NULL,NULL,NULL,'Unknown',37.1211069,-113.6750798,'Views',NULL,'BLM Utah - St. George Field Office','http://www.blm.gov/ut/st/en/fo/st__george.html',NULL,'Intermediate',3291,3146,NULL,0.9,'Point to Point',NULL,147,2,0.03,0.14,NULL,NULL,'https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/gpx/7003770','8010543 | https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/7003770/precipice',1,'2023-09-12 10:09:11','2023-09-19 06:00:29'),(180,'Suicidal Tendencies','8010543','hikingproject','7003772',6,'To reach the beginning of the trail, take either Barrel Roll Trail or Precipice Trail to Sidewinder Trail. Note that the round trip from the Cove Wash Trailhead and back is about 13.2 miles. From the top of Sidewinder Trail, Suicidal Tendencies immediately drops down a steep slope with exposed switchbacks. You should expect a steep descent, filled with switchbacks and precipitous exposure.  After you navigate down the edge of Sidewinder Mesa, youll wind across the valley floor before ascending up the next mesa.\n \nThe ascent is similar in technicality to the previous descent, a few technical rock sections with some exposure. Near the top of the climb is Hangover, a large rock that hangs above the trail.  Watch your head. Past the Hangover is a technical rock move nicknamed Thread the Needle, because you must fit between rocks on each side of the trail.  After this section, youve reached the top of the Mesa.\n \nOnce on top of the mesa, the hiking changes dramatically. The technicality drops significantly and you simply follow gentle switchbacks to the high point of the mesa. Here you are rewarded with views of the Beaver Dam Mountains to the west. Continuing on after the point, you descend through Juniper trees and occasional slick rock sections to complete the loop back to the top of Thread the Needle. From this point you\'ll hike the first part of the trail in reverse. Enjoy the journey but keep an eye out for other users and desert wildlife.','\"Thread the Needle\" on this highly exposed trail.',NULL,NULL,NULL,'Unknown',37.1217945,-113.6982474,'Views',NULL,'BLM Utah - St. George Field Office','http://www.blm.gov/ut/st/en/fo/st__george.html',NULL,'Difficult',4070,3433,NULL,4.1,'Point to Point',NULL,645,553,0.06,0.15,NULL,NULL,'https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/gpx/7003772','8010543 | https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/7003772/suicidal-tendencies',1,'2023-09-12 10:09:12','2023-09-19 06:00:29'),(181,'Pushing Tin Loop','8010543','hikingproject','7030329',6,'A fun, easy, clean, multi-use singletrack trail. This is a very new trail, and it\'s very easy to follow and has low traffic. The first quarter-mile is doubletrack that parallels a sandy wash before it crosses through it for a few hundred yards to reach the singletrack portion of the lollipop loop. Ahead, the trail is pretty straightforwards, but there are two potentially unclear points detailed below.\r\n\r\nIf you hike the trail counter-clockwise (as mapped), there are a few small switchback and a potentially confusing turn 0.6 miles in. The eastern edge of the trail has been lined with rocks to keep you from turning to the right (the wrong direction). Continuing counter-clockwise, there is a fork in the trail at 1.25 miles. If you go right, there is a Alternate Line with very small technical section that is more of a mountain bike section with a few drops and rocks that you have to jump over or off of. If instead you go left, it continues as an easy trail. Both directions reconnect to each other after less than 30 or 40 yards.\r\n\r\nAt about two miles into the trail, you\'ll approach Will Gilbert Point. There\'s a sign with a bench which folds down into a table. It faces southwest and is a great place to sit and relax, eat a snack, etc. At 2.5 miles, you\'ll complete the circle and can head right to return to the the trailhead. Heading left (more like going straight) will take you around the loop again.\r\n\r\nThis trail can be accessed via the parking lot at the trailhead or via the Meditation Rock Connector or the Saddle Trail Connector.','A short, easy, and fun singletrack loop with great morning views and occasional airplanes overhead.',NULL,NULL,NULL,'Off-leash',37.0051381,-113.5275764,'River/Creek / Views',NULL,'Desert Canyons Development','http://www.desertcanyons.net/Trail_System.html',NULL,'Intermediate',2870,2721,NULL,2.7,'Point to Point',NULL,165,165,0.02,0.07,NULL,NULL,'https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/gpx/7030329','8010543 | https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/7030329/pushing-tin-loop',1,'2023-09-12 10:09:13','2023-09-19 06:00:29'),(182,'Kentucky Lucky Chicken Trail','8010543','hikingproject','7030628',6,NULL,'A short and technical loop.',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,37.0657682,-113.5657369,'',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'Intermediate/Difficult',3097,2790,NULL,3.9,'Loop',NULL,404,411,0.04,0.12,NULL,NULL,'https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/gpx/7030628','8010543 | https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/7030628/kentucky-lucky-chicken-trail',1,'2023-09-12 10:09:14','2023-09-19 06:00:29'),(183,'Rim Rock Trail','8010543','hikingproject','7038992',6,NULL,'A fun lollypop loop out of the Sand Cove Trailhead.',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,37.1206771,-113.6746486,'',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'Difficult',3430,3044,NULL,3.2,'Point to Point',NULL,425,217,0.04,0.09,NULL,NULL,'https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/gpx/7038992','8010543 | https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/7038992/rim-rock-trail',1,'2023-09-12 10:09:15','2023-09-19 06:00:29'),(184,'Rim Ramble','8010543','hikingproject','7038994',6,NULL,'A sweet side loop you can add to Rim Reaper for more singletrack.',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,37.1173177,-113.6790504,'',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'Difficult',3311,3245,NULL,0.8,'Point to Point',NULL,82,26,0.03,0.09,NULL,NULL,'https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/gpx/7038994','8010543 | https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/7038994/rim-ramble',1,'2023-09-12 10:09:16','2023-09-19 06:00:29'),(185,'Rim Runner Trail','8010543','hikingproject','7038995',6,'Rim Ramble splits from Rim Rock 1.6 miles into that hike. Combine this loop trail with Rim Rock Trail for more sweet singletrack and great vistas. The trail is firm coarse sandy soil with large rounded rocks.','Sweet little side loop with grand vistas in every direction.',NULL,NULL,NULL,'Unknown',37.1138220,-113.6848265,'Views',NULL,'BLM Utah - St. George Field Office','http://www.blm.gov/ut/st/en/fo/st__george.html',NULL,'Difficult',3447,3394,NULL,0.7,'Loop',NULL,54,39,0.03,0.07,NULL,NULL,'https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/gpx/7038995','8010543 | https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/7038995/rim-runner-trail',1,'2023-09-12 10:09:17','2023-09-19 06:00:29'),(186,'Temple Quarry Trail','8010543','hikingproject','7045299',6,'This trail is located in the heart of town and provides spectacular birds-eye views of St. George as well as holds great historical importance. The 2.5-mile-long Temple Quarry Trail is a family-friendly hike for anyone looking to connect with the heritage of St. George.\r\n\r\nFrom 1871 to 1874 the Temple Quarry Trail was used by Mormon Pioneers to access the quarry on the southwest side of what’s known locally as the “black hill.” The quarry holds a vast deposit of lava rocks needed by the Mormon pioneers to construct the foundation and basement of the St. George Temple of The Church of Jesus Christ Of Latter-day Saints. The lava rocks are naturally resistant to the alkali that is found in the soil in St. George.\r\n\r\nThe beginning of the hike is slightly inclined; however, stairs have been added to assist in the ascent. After the initial incline, the well-marked trail is relatively level with benches and historical markers along the way. The trail wraps around the black hill, providing amazing views of Green Valley, Red Mountain, and Snow Canyon State Park. Near the turn around point, you\'ll find a loop where the wagons used to U-turn to haul the blocks back to town.\r\n\r\nBoth dogs and mountain bikes are allowed on the trail. This trail is fun for all ages, and the historical aspect allows for some fun teaching and observation moments.','A short singletrack with great views and a bit of history.',NULL,NULL,NULL,'Off-leash',37.1028252,-113.5958484,'Views / Wildlife',NULL,'BLM Utah - St. George Field Office','http://www.blm.gov/ut/st/en/fo/st__george.html',NULL,'Intermediate',2977,2934,NULL,1.2,'Point to Point',NULL,60,30,0.01,0.08,NULL,NULL,'https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/gpx/7045299','8010543 | https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/7045299/temple-quarry-trail',1,'2023-09-12 10:09:19','2023-09-19 06:00:29'),(187,'Gates Lane','8010543','hikingproject','7047965',6,NULL,'Needs Summary',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,37.1328817,-113.6599162,'',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'Intermediate',3138,2761,NULL,1.6,'Point to Point',NULL,381,8,0.05,0.15,NULL,NULL,'https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/gpx/7047965','8010543 | https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/7047965/gates-lane',1,'2023-09-12 10:09:20','2023-09-19 06:00:29'),(188,'Desert Canyon Loop','8010543','hikingproject','7051031',6,NULL,'This trail is built for bikes but open to pedestrians, and is mostly dirt singletrack with very few rocks to cross.',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,37.0127707,-113.5164733,'',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'Easy/Intermediate',2946,2722,NULL,2.5,'Point to Point',NULL,233,184,0.03,0.07,NULL,NULL,'https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/gpx/7051031','8010543 | https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/7051031/desert-canyon-loop',1,'2023-09-12 10:09:22','2023-09-19 06:00:29'),(189,'Black Brush','8010543','hikingproject','7047963',6,NULL,'Needs Summary',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,37.1209565,-113.6752325,'',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'Intermediate/Difficult',3306,2907,NULL,3.3,'Point to Point',NULL,428,528,0.06,0.19,NULL,NULL,'https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/gpx/7047963','8010543 | https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/7047963/black-brush',1,'2023-09-12 10:09:23','2023-09-19 06:00:29'),(190,'Maverick','8010543','hikingproject','7047967',6,NULL,'Needs Summary',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,37.1290572,-113.6632849,'',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'Intermediate',3121,2807,NULL,1.0,'Point to Point',NULL,319,5,0.06,0.17,NULL,NULL,'https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/gpx/7047967','8010543 | https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/7047967/maverick',1,'2023-09-12 10:09:24','2023-09-19 06:00:29'),(191,'Stucki Springs','8010543','hikingproject','7035223',6,NULL,'Another loop on the Bearclaw Poppy trail network.',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,37.0995294,-113.6758973,'',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'Intermediate',3336,2846,NULL,5.7,'Point to Point',NULL,320,490,0.03,0.06,NULL,NULL,'https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/gpx/7035223','8010543 | https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/7035223/stucki-springs',1,'2023-09-12 10:09:25','2023-09-19 06:00:29'),(192,'Bearclaw Poppy Trail','8010543','hikingproject','7035776',6,NULL,'A pleasant trail that connects to many others.',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,37.0888244,-113.6414289,'',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'Intermediate',2984,2575,NULL,3.9,'Point to Point',NULL,111,460,0.03,0.05,NULL,NULL,'https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/gpx/7035776','8010543 | https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/7035776/bearclaw-poppy-trail',1,'2023-09-12 10:09:27','2023-09-19 06:00:29'),(193,'Kolob Arch Trail','8010544','hikingproject','7001584',7,'Located deep in the backcountry of Zion National Parks Kolob Canyons District, and hidden in a small side canyon, sits Kolob Arch, perched high on the canyon wall with a majestic curve like a giant condors wing. Because of its remote location and virtual inaccessibility, Kolob Arch for years has challenged cowboys, rangers, hikers, climbers, and photographers alike. For most of the twentieth century many believed that Kolob was in fact the worlds largest freestanding arch, leading to years of debate and the motivation for various parties of adventurous thrill seekers to climb on and around the massive span in hopes of securing a defensible measurement.\n\nDespite its isolated location, Kolob Arch has become a favorite backcountry destination for thousands of visitors to Zion. They discover what most arch seekers will tell you: while beauty awaits every seeker at the end of the path, the reward begins unfolding at the trailhead. Along the trail to Kolob Arch lies some of the most beautiful scenery in Zion. Geology becomes art as the La Verkin Creek, with its soothing sounds of life-giving water, sculpts some of the most colorful canyon walls in Southwestern Utah. Awe inspiring views of the Kolob Terrace along the trail give a tired hiker many places to stop and recharge while gazing upon the high plateau country of Zion. \n\nWildlife abounds in these protected canyons and hikers will encounter reptiles, birds, and mammals. Seven miles along the trail, one is rewarded with a sight not seen by most of Zions two and a half million annual visitors, the impressive expanse of Kolob Arch. With a span of 287 feet and a thickness of 75 feet, most experts now agree that Kolob Arch is not the worlds largest span in terms of measurement, but visitors concur with the claim that it is certainly one of the most beautiful and massive arches surrounded and protected by the majestic scenery of Zion.','An easy trail boasting views of one of the world\'s largest arches.',NULL,NULL,NULL,'No Dogs',37.4158677,-113.1511823,'Birding / Cave / Fall Colors / River/Creek / Views / Wildflowers / Wildlife',NULL,'National Park Service - Zion National Park','http://nps.gov/zion',NULL,'Intermediate',5346,5225,NULL,0.5,'Point to Point',NULL,122,0,0.04,0.06,NULL,NULL,'https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/gpx/7001584','8010544 | https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/7001584/kolob-arch-trail',1,'2023-09-12 10:09:28','2023-09-19 06:00:29'),(194,'Deertrap Mountain Trail','8010544','hikingproject','7001589',7,'Follow Deertrap Mountain Trail west, away from Cable Mountain Trail as it undulates in a serpentine manner to the rim of Zion Canyon.\r\n\r\nFrom the rim, you\'ll be able to view multiple points of interest:\r\nMountain of the SunEmerald PoolThe Twin BrothersAngels LandingThe Court of the Patriarchs...and many more!','A relatively short journey to a rarely visited viewpoint.',NULL,NULL,NULL,'No Dogs',37.2527094,-112.9169376,'Birding / Fall Colors / River/Creek / Views / Wildflowers / Wildlife',NULL,'National Park Service - Zion National Park','http://nps.gov/zion',NULL,'Intermediate',6900,6685,NULL,2.7,'Point to Point',NULL,334,467,0.06,0.26,NULL,NULL,'https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/gpx/7001589','8010544 | https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/7001589/deertrap-mountain-trail',1,'2023-09-12 10:09:29','2023-09-19 06:00:29'),(195,'Hidden Canyon Trail','8010544','hikingproject','7001590',7,'The Hidden Canyon Trail follows along a cliff face to the mouth of a narrow canyon. It switchbacks its way up the face from a junction with East Rim Trail near the East Rim Trailhead. \r\n\r\nThis section of trail is moderately strenuous due to the steep grades. As you ascend, the technicality of the trail decreases (grades flatten) but the difficulty remains high due to the extreme exposure. Utilize the chains that have been bolted to the cliff in order to stabilize your body and, inevitably, your mind. \r\n\r\nKeep following the trail to the mouth of the canyon and its terminus. From here, one can hike and scramble further into the hanging canyon but the official trail ends.','Long, steep drop-offs make this a trail only for those with a controllable fear of heights.',NULL,NULL,NULL,'No Dogs',37.2695323,-112.9361796,'Birding / Cave / Views / Wildlife',NULL,'National Park Service - Zion National Park','http://nps.gov/zion',NULL,'Difficult',5265,4746,NULL,1.1,'Point to Point',NULL,520,0,0.09,0.60,NULL,NULL,'https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/gpx/7001590','8010544 | https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/7001590/hidden-canyon-trail',1,'2023-09-12 10:09:30','2023-09-19 06:00:29'),(196,'Observation Point Trail','8010544','hikingproject','7001591',7,'Observation Point Trail works its way out to the edge of the canyon rim and offers absolutely astounding panoramic views of the canyon. You\'ll have clear views of Angel\'s Landing to the southwest, while the Great White Throne will be in sight due south.','A simple trail leading to simply incredible views.',NULL,NULL,NULL,'No Dogs',37.2810838,-112.9365928,'Birding / Views / Wildlife',NULL,'National Park Service - Zion National Park','http://nps.gov/zion',NULL,'Intermediate/Difficult',6523,6495,NULL,0.4,'Point to Point',NULL,28,5,0.02,0.04,NULL,NULL,'https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/gpx/7001591','8010544 | https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/7001591/observation-point-trail',1,'2023-09-12 10:09:31','2023-09-19 06:00:29'),(197,'Weeping Rock Trail','8010544','hikingproject','7001596',7,'Family Friendly\n            \n                Though somewhat steep, anyone can manage this paved path. Especially when it ends at such a stunning geologic oddity!','A short, but steep trail leading to a beautiful rock alcove.',NULL,NULL,NULL,'No Dogs',37.2708619,-112.9385152,'Views',NULL,'National Park Service - Zion National Park','http://nps.gov/zion',NULL,'Easy/Intermediate',4435,4372,NULL,0.2,'Point to Point',NULL,64,0,0.06,0.14,NULL,NULL,'https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/gpx/7001596','8010544 | https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/7001596/weeping-rock-trail',1,'2023-09-12 10:09:33','2023-09-19 06:00:29'),(198,'Watchman Trail','8010544','hikingproject','7001599',7,'Starting from the Visitor Center, this trail winds its way east, easily at first, then quite steeply. Eventually you\'ll gain the Watchman Trail mesa which affords views of the Towers of the Virgin, lower Zion Canyon, and Springdale.','A relatively short jaunt to a mesa overlooking the Visitor Center.',NULL,NULL,NULL,'No Dogs',37.2013381,-112.9864942,'Birding / Fall Colors / Views / Wildflowers / Wildlife',NULL,'National Park Service - Zion National Park','http://nps.gov/zion',NULL,'Intermediate',4422,3931,NULL,1.8,'Point to Point',NULL,559,78,0.07,0.32,NULL,NULL,'https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/gpx/7001599','8010544 | https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/7001599/watchman-trail',1,'2023-09-12 10:09:34','2023-09-19 06:00:29'),(199,'La Verkin Creek Trail','8010544','hikingproject','7001602',7,'The trail begins at Lee Pass and passes by the open canyons of the nearby cliffs as it travels along Timber Creek. After coming around the corner and within view of La Verkin Creek, the trail descends the slope down to the creek bottom on a hard packed trail. \r\n\r\nOnce on the creek bed, the trail heads upstream offering amazing views of the surrounding cliffs. The trail is 7 miles from Lee Pass to Kolob Arch, one of the main destinations of the route and possibly the world\'s largest free-standing arch. From there you can continue up canyon to Beartrap Canyon and Willis Canyon to extend your trip or offer day hiking options. This is a great area to spend a few days.','Head up La Verkin Creek to experience amazing views of the surrounding cliffs and side trips.',NULL,NULL,NULL,'No Dogs',37.4522180,-113.1913819,'Birding / Fall Colors / River/Creek / Views / Wildflowers / Wildlife',NULL,'National Park Service - Zion National Park','http://nps.gov/zion',NULL,'Intermediate/Difficult',6411,5052,NULL,10.9,'Point to Point',NULL,1742,1718,0.06,0.34,NULL,NULL,'https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/gpx/7001602','8010544 | https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/7001602/la-verkin-creek-trail',1,'2023-09-12 10:09:36','2023-09-19 06:00:30'),(200,'Connector Trail','8010544','hikingproject','7001603',7,'Though not a destination in and of itself, Connector Trail provides useful connection between Hop Valley Trail and Wildcat Canyon Trail. Of course, like most of Zion, this trail is still very beautiful.','Connect the Hop Valley Trail with Wildcat Canyon Trail on this low traffic trail.',NULL,NULL,NULL,'No Dogs',37.3395646,-113.0618628,'Birding / Fall Colors / River/Creek / Views / Wildflowers / Wildlife',NULL,'National Park Service - Zion National Park','http://nps.gov/zion',NULL,'Intermediate',6917,6388,NULL,4.0,'Point to Point',NULL,144,672,0.04,0.10,NULL,NULL,'https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/gpx/7001603','8010544 | https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/7001603/connector-trail',1,'2023-09-12 10:09:39','2023-09-19 06:00:30'),(201,'Wildcat Canyon Trail','8010544','hikingproject','7001604',7,'This trail starts from the Wildcat Trailhead and goes past the Northgate Peaks Trail junction, offering views of the Northgate Peaks as it winds through the ponderosa pine forest. It opens into the meadows before emerging onto the edge of Wildcat Canyon offering, sweeping views down into the deep canyon. \r\n\r\nAfter crossing the canyon, the trail climbs slightly until connecting up with the West Rim Trail. This area has no established campsites but does require a permit.','A rolling backcountry trail that meanders up to the West Rim Trail from Kolob Terrace Drive.',NULL,NULL,NULL,'Leashed',37.3398432,-113.0756340,'Birding / Fall Colors / Views / Wildflowers / Wildlife',NULL,'National Park Service - Zion National Park','http://nps.gov/zion',NULL,'Intermediate',7444,6915,NULL,5.9,'Point to Point',NULL,796,327,0.04,0.11,NULL,NULL,'https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/gpx/7001604','8010544 | https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/7001604/wildcat-canyon-trail',1,'2023-09-12 10:09:42','2023-09-19 06:00:30'),(202,'Northgate Peaks Trail','8010544','hikingproject','7001605',7,'This mostly flat trail originates at a junction with Wildcat Canyon Trail, about a mile from the trailhead. This is one of the park\'s lesser used trails, but given its tame nature, it makes for a great family hike. \r\n\r\nThe trail heads due south for just over a mile from its junction with Wildcat Canyon Trail and passes through open grasslands and pinyon-juniper. The Northgate Peaks Trail ends between the east and west Northgate Peaks. From here, you\'ll have great views of the North Guardian Angel, a large sandstone formation that juts up from the surrounding landscape. \r\n\r\nThere are multiple trails heading up either of the Northgate Peaks if you\'re looking for a scramble to a better view. It should be noted that these approaches are not sanctioned park trails - please be considerate of your surroundings and stick to defined paths to avoid trampling delicate desert fauna.','A mostly level trail linking Wildcat Canyon Trail to the base of the Northgate Peaks.',NULL,NULL,NULL,'Leashed',37.3400503,-113.0595362,'Fall Colors / Views / Wildflowers / Wildlife',NULL,'National Park Service - Zion National Park','http://nps.gov/zion',NULL,'Intermediate',6943,6903,NULL,1.1,'Point to Point',NULL,22,46,0.01,0.07,NULL,NULL,'https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/gpx/7001605','8010544 | https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/7001605/northgate-peaks-trail',1,'2023-09-12 10:09:43','2023-09-19 06:00:30'),(203,'East Mesa Trail','8010544','hikingproject','7001614',7,'This somewhat unremarkable trail takes you from Zion\'s east mesa to East Rim Trail, snaking its way through dense forest as it goes. Though not a sought after hike compared to the other trails around it, East Mesa does provide great views of the Pink Cliffs and Brian Head Peak (11,306\'). Additionally, you\'ll get great views of Bulloch Gulch to the north, its surrounding canyon, and have access to one of the best views in the park from Observation Point Trail.\r\n\r\nStarting from East Rim Trail, East Mesa climbs very steeply, switchbacking multiple times until it eventually contours south of a small mesa. Once the trail levels off, all of the hard stuff is done. Hike a short ways north and you\'ll pass Observation Point Trail on your left. Be sure not to miss this must-see trail as it\'s a short connector leading to some of the best views in Zion.\r\n\r\nOnce past Observation Point Trail, follow East Mesa east as it winds its way down the park\'s eastern mesa at an easy grade. Eventually you\'ll connect with Fir Rd., a dirt road just to the east of the park.','A pleasant hike through a pinion and juniper forest, until reaching Observation Point Trail.',NULL,NULL,NULL,'No Dogs',37.2742146,-112.9286606,'Birding / Fall Colors / Views / Wildflowers / Wildlife',NULL,'National Park Service - Zion National Park','http://nps.gov/zion',NULL,'Intermediate',6797,5627,NULL,4.4,'Point to Point',NULL,1177,278,0.06,0.32,NULL,NULL,'https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/gpx/7001614','8010544 | https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/7001614/east-mesa-trail',1,'2023-09-12 10:09:44','2023-09-19 06:00:30'),(204,'Hop Valley Trail','8010544','hikingproject','7001617',7,'The trail begins off the Kolob Terrace Road and wanders through the open fields with wide open views of the surrounding rock formations. As you near Hop Valley, the trail starts its descent. The valley floor of Hop Valley is breathtaking with its flat sandy bottom and vertical walls rising on both sides. The trail is sandy but well worn. The campsites are at the far end of the valley before the descent into La Verkin Creek. The descent into La Verkin Creek is steep and offers access to the Kolob Arch. Camping is permitted in designated sites only.','Wander above, then through one of Zion\'s beautiful valleys.',NULL,NULL,NULL,'No Dogs',37.3402574,-113.1131566,'Birding / Fall Colors / River/Creek / Views / Wildflowers / Wildlife',NULL,'National Park Service - Zion National Park','http://nps.gov/zion',NULL,'Intermediate/Difficult',6380,5342,NULL,6.6,'Point to Point',NULL,282,1319,0.05,0.24,NULL,NULL,'https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/gpx/7001617','8010544 | https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/7001617/hop-valley-trail',1,'2023-09-12 10:09:45','2023-09-19 06:00:30'),(205,'East Rim Trail','8010544','hikingproject','7001615',7,'As you take off from the East Rim Trailhead, the trail points directly toward the beautiful sandstone cliffs this area is famous for. The trail climbs up onto the Plateau for the first three miles. At 2.8 miles from the East Rim Trailhead, you\'ll reach Jolly Gulch. This unmistakable canyon seems to drop out beneath your feet as you see it from the trail. The gulch marks a great turnaround point for a shorter hike from the East Entrance and back.\r\n\r\nThe middle section of the trail wanders across the plateau where the altitude is higher and the weather is a little cooler than the main canyon. The trail passes through a ponderosa pine forest and, after crossing the flat plateau where the ponderosa trees grow, it opens up with amazing views of the Echo Canyon Basin.\r\n\r\nStave Springs is located about five miles from the East Entrance Trailhead or Weeping Rock. It is a pipe in the ground that water comes out of however, it can become dry some years. Be sure and check spring conditions for an update. Near the springs, the Cable Mountain Trail branches off to the left (west). Shortly after, north of the springs, Stave Spring Trail branches to the right and leads to a dirt road which is part of the Zion Ponderosa Ranch Resort.\r\n\r\nApproximate Location of the Spring:\r\nN 3715\'40.2\"\r\nW 11254\'17.1\"\r\nElevation 6493 ft\r\n\r\nAfter hiking over the rim of the canyon, you sharply descend 2,300 feet down to the floor of Zion Canyon ending at Weeping Rock, passing through Echo Canyon and down the Observation Point Trail. \r\n\r\nThe East Rim Trail trail is best in the late spring and fall. The trail typically has deep snow in the winter that can remain until late spring. During the summer months the trail can get very dry and hot. This area has no established campsites but does require a permit and is available on a walk-up basis.','An extensive trail that connects the Park\'s East Entrance to Zion Canyon.',NULL,NULL,NULL,'No Dogs',37.2343093,-112.8773489,'Birding / Fall Colors / River/Creek / Views / Wildflowers / Wildlife',NULL,'National Park Service - Zion National Park','http://nps.gov/zion',NULL,'Intermediate/Difficult',6721,4389,NULL,10.3,'Point to Point',NULL,1162,2509,0.07,0.56,NULL,NULL,'https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/gpx/7001615','8010544 | https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/7001615/east-rim-trail',1,'2023-09-12 10:09:46','2023-09-19 06:00:30'),(206,'Cable Mountain Trail','8010544','hikingproject','7001616',7,'From East Rim Trail and Stave Spring, head south as Cable Mountain Trail contours, then climbs one of Zion\'s eastern mesas at a medium grade. Bending westward, Cable Mountain Trail skirts juniper and pinyon pine forest, eventually passing Deertrap Mountain Trail on the left.\r\n\r\nFrom Deertrap Mountain Trail, the trail rises slightly, then begins a slow descent to the edge of the canyon as it heads northwest. Almost a mirror image of the ascent you took to get to this point, the descent to the canyon\'s edge is a mellow hike ending in a populous pinyon-juniper forest.\r\n\r\nThe end of this trail plays host to quite a few wonders:\r\nViews of Angel\'s LandingViews of The Great White Throne...and the historic Cable Mountain Draw Works\r\nThe Cable Mountain Draw Works is an early 20th century aerial tramway that was used to move lumber and timber from the mesas of Cable Mountain to the floor of Zion Canyon. The structure was listed in the National Register of Historic Places in 1978.\r\n\r\nThe Draw Works was originally conceived and constructed by Springdale pioneer David Flanigan, who regarded the Draw Works as a fulfillment of an 1863 prophecy by Brigham Young that lumber would one day be transported from the canyon rim as the hawk flies. During operation of the Draw Works, from 1901 to 1927, the structure was used to move hundreds of thousands of board feet of lumber, goods, people occasionally, and a dog once, to the canyon floor 2,000 feet below.','A straightforward, rolling mesa trail culminating at a historic viewpoint.',NULL,NULL,NULL,'No Dogs',37.2622259,-112.9063735,'Birding / Fall Colors / Views / Wildflowers / Wildlife',NULL,'National Park Service - Zion National Park','http://nps.gov/zion',NULL,'Intermediate',6897,6453,NULL,3.0,'Point to Point',NULL,444,442,0.06,0.17,NULL,NULL,'https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/gpx/7001616','8010544 | https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/7001616/cable-mountain-trail',1,'2023-09-12 10:09:49','2023-09-19 06:00:30'),(207,'Wrangler Trail','8010544','hikingproject','7001586',7,'The Wrangler Trail runs from Zion Lodge south along the Virgin River to the Court of the Patriarchs.','A short, flat connector trail in Zion Canyon.',NULL,NULL,NULL,'No Dogs',37.2498421,-112.9580356,'Birding / Fall Colors / River/Creek / Views / Wildflowers / Wildlife',NULL,'National Park Service - Zion National Park','http://nps.gov/zion',NULL,'Easy',4263,4242,NULL,1.0,'Point to Point',NULL,3,21,0.00,0.01,NULL,NULL,'https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/gpx/7001586','8010544 | https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/7001586/wrangler-trail',1,'2023-09-12 10:09:50','2023-09-19 06:00:30'),(208,'Kayenta Trail','8010544','hikingproject','7001587',7,'This short connector trail links the Grotto Bus Stop to the Emerald Pools. Hike along the mostly flat, well-maintained trail as it parallels the Virgin River to the south.','A short trail linking the Grotto Bus Stop to Emerald Pools.',NULL,NULL,NULL,'No Dogs',37.2602169,-112.9515946,'Birding / Fall Colors / River/Creek / Views / Wildflowers / Wildlife',NULL,'National Park Service - Zion National Park','http://nps.gov/zion',NULL,'Easy/Intermediate',4447,4356,NULL,0.6,'Point to Point',NULL,91,9,0.03,0.06,NULL,NULL,'https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/gpx/7001587','8010544 | https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/7001587/kayenta-trail',1,'2023-09-12 10:09:53','2023-09-19 06:00:30'),(209,'Parus Trail','8010544','hikingproject','7001598',7,'Family Friendly\n            \n                One of the few ADA accessible trails in Zion, the whole family will enjoy the constant natural symphony provided by the Virgin River on this canyon bottom path.','A wide, paved path that heads northward from the visitor center.',NULL,NULL,NULL,'Leashed',37.2013023,-112.9885334,'Birding / Fall Colors / River/Creek / Views / Wildflowers / Wildlife',NULL,'National Park Service - Zion National Park','http://nps.gov/zion',NULL,'Easy',4034,3934,NULL,1.7,'Point to Point',NULL,101,13,0.01,0.03,NULL,NULL,'https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/gpx/7001598','8010544 | https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/7001598/parus-trail',1,'2023-09-12 10:09:56','2023-09-19 06:00:30'),(210,'West Rim Trail','8010544','hikingproject','7001606',7,'The trail starts at the West Rim Trailhead near Lava Point and traverses across the high alpine elevation with sweeping views out into the Wildcat Canyon area for the first 6.5 miles as you drop into Potato Hollow. Next, the trail climbs out of Potato Hollow before it splits into the Telephone Canyon Trail and the West Rim Trail.\r\n\r\nThe West Rim Trail follows along the rim with views of Phantom Valley and the canyons to the south. Although it is a little scary to hike so close to precipitous drop-off, this trail is well worth the journey. The views across Right Fork Canyon are astounding.\r\n\r\nThese include:\r\nChurch MesaInclined TempleIvins Mountainand Greatheart Mesa\r\nNear its southern terminus, this trail passes the famed Angel\'s Landing hike. This is not to be missed!','A major trail leading from Zion NP\'s frontcountry deep into the backcountry.',NULL,NULL,NULL,'No Dogs',37.3815627,-113.0230107,'Birding / Cave / Fall Colors / Lake / River/Creek / Views / Waterfall / Wildflowers / Wildlife',NULL,'National Park Service - Zion National Park','http://nps.gov/zion',NULL,'Difficult',7453,4338,NULL,14.7,'Point to Point',NULL,867,3981,0.06,0.78,NULL,NULL,'https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/gpx/7001606','8010544 | https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/7001606/west-rim-trail',1,'2023-09-12 10:09:58','2023-09-19 06:00:30'),(211,'Telephone Canyon Trail','8010544','hikingproject','7001607',7,'The Telephone Canyon Trail provides a shortcut between Potato Hollow and Cabin Springs. It traverses a burn area and does not really go through a canyon (it is ambitious to call it such anywhere and downright insulting to use that term given the real canyons in the area). \r\n\r\nIt may be a good alternative option when there is snow on the rim as this stretch is quite exposed due to the burn and likely to melt earlier. \r\n\r\nCamping is in designated sites only.','Connect Potato Hollow and Cabin Springs on this open-meadow trail.',NULL,NULL,NULL,'No Dogs',37.3047603,-112.9858833,'Birding / Fall Colors / River/Creek / Views / Wildflowers / Wildlife',NULL,'National Park Service - Zion National Park','http://nps.gov/zion',NULL,'Intermediate/Difficult',7263,6723,NULL,1.9,'Point to Point',NULL,6,544,0.06,0.17,NULL,NULL,'https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/gpx/7001607','8010544 | https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/7001607/telephone-canyon-trail',1,'2023-09-12 10:09:59','2023-09-19 06:00:30'),(212,'Angelhtml5-dom-document-internal-entity2-039-ends Landing Trail','8010544','hikingproject','7001608',7,'Access this trail via the West Rim Trail. Past Scout Lookout, you\'ll encounter both exposure and lots of people. Be wary of dangerous ice during non-summer months and do not proceed to the summit if storms are in the area or if the rock is wet. Be ready for wild temperature swings - from scorching sun on Walter\'s Wiggle to chilly shade in Refrigerator Canyon. Additionally, the stimulating summit pitch can be windy.\r\n\r\nThere are chains anchored by posts as optional assistance when the route is exposed. Grab them if you want. Regardless, you\'ll feel the rush. Exposure eases the last few hundred meters to the summit. Take a seat, stretch, and enjoy the divine view.','A one of a kind trail with unrivaled views.',NULL,NULL,NULL,'No Dogs',37.2749223,-112.9508131,'Views',NULL,'National Park Service - Zion National Park','http://nps.gov/zion',NULL,'Difficult',5728,5338,NULL,0.5,'Point to Point',NULL,390,0,0.16,0.44,NULL,NULL,'https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/gpx/7001608','8010544 | https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/7001608/angels-landing-trail',1,'2023-09-12 10:10:01','2023-09-19 06:00:30'),(213,'Grotto Trail','8010544','hikingproject','7001609',7,'Family Friendly\n            \n                Take a quick walk after a picnic at The Grotto and enjoy the sights and sounds of the Virgin River.','A useful connector trail.',NULL,NULL,NULL,'No Dogs',37.2602169,-112.9515946,'Birding / Fall Colors / River/Creek / Views / Wildflowers',NULL,'National Park Service - Zion National Park','http://nps.gov/zion',NULL,'Easy',4335,4287,NULL,0.8,'Point to Point',NULL,22,70,0.02,0.08,NULL,NULL,'https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/gpx/7001609','8010544 | https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/7001609/grotto-trail',1,'2023-09-12 10:10:02','2023-09-19 06:00:30'),(214,'Upper Emerald Pools Trail','8010544','hikingproject','7001610',7,'This is a very steep, often sandy and hot trail that connects to the Upper Emerald Pool. The trail leads through rocky outcrops and up to the base of a sheer cliff.','A steep trail connecting the middle and upper Emerald Pools.',NULL,NULL,NULL,'Leashed',37.2571425,-112.9601017,'Birding / Cave / Fall Colors / Lake / River/Creek / Views / Waterfall / Wildflowers / Wildlife',NULL,'National Park Service - Zion National Park','http://nps.gov/zion',NULL,'Intermediate',4755,4419,NULL,0.4,'Point to Point',NULL,356,42,0.18,0.64,NULL,NULL,'https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/gpx/7001610','8010544 | https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/7001610/upper-emerald-pools-trail',1,'2023-09-12 10:10:04','2023-09-19 06:00:30'),(215,'Lower Emerald Pools Trail','8010544','hikingproject','7001611',7,'Family Friendly\n            \n                Perfect for a quick out-and-back outing with the entire family. The more intrepid of the bunch can head up Upper Emerald Pools Trail, if they want.','An easy, well-maintained paved path leading to some of Zion\'s most cherished sights.',NULL,NULL,NULL,'Unknown',37.2506572,-112.9594190,'Birding / Cave / Fall Colors / Lake / River/Creek / Views / Waterfall / Wildflowers / Wildlife',NULL,'National Park Service - Zion National Park','http://nps.gov/zion',NULL,'Easy/Intermediate',4434,4307,NULL,0.8,'Point to Point',NULL,128,4,0.03,0.05,NULL,NULL,'https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/gpx/7001611','8010544 | https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/7001611/lower-emerald-pools-trail',1,'2023-09-12 10:10:05','2023-09-19 06:00:30'),(216,'The Narrows - Wall Street to Big Springs Route','8010544','hikingproject','7045744',7,'Hiking in The Narrows requires careful planning before starting, as it is a serious endeavor that can be both challenging and risky. Your safety is your responsibility and requires sound judgment, proper preparation, and constant observation. Contact the Zion park rangers at the visitor center for current water conditions, flash flood potential, and weather forecast. \r\n\r\nUpon reaching the end of The Narrows - Bottom Up Route, Go LEFT to begin this extension. Heading right leads into Orderville Canyon, which is prohibited by the park service.\r\n\r\nWall Street is the narrowest part of The Narrows and is where it truly becomes a slot canyon—at this point, the 2000 foot walls around you close to within 20 feet in areas. At approximately 0.5 miles from the Orderville fork, you\'ll pass Floating Rock: a giant rock bisecting the river that you can navigate around. \r\n\r\nFurther up the canyon, keep your eyes and ears peeled for Hiccup Springs. While it is not very visible, other than a little crack on the left side of the river, you should be able to hear the gurgling noise when approximately 0.8 miles from the Orderville fork.\r\n\r\nFrom here, the water will increasingly deepen in sections and the canyon walls will open up as you approach the Boulder Pair (approximately 1.5 miles). The Boulder Pair is a set of 2 giant boulders that block the river and mark the beginning of the boulder-obstacle portion of the route. While it\'s not very difficult to get through this obstacle, you\'ll still want to be careful as it\'s a long hike out to the parking area.\r\n\r\nNext up, you\'ll find Sipping Turtle (another gurgling noise maker) roughly 1.8 miles from the Orderville fork. From here, it\'s only another 0.4 miles until you reach Big Springs and the end of your journey—you\'ll need to stop here unless you have a permit. Big Springs marks a great end to your journey, as the giant boulder and obvious waterfall are a great place to stop, grab some water and lunch, and prepare for your journey out.','If you\'re looking to extend the Narrows experience and find more of a challenge then look no further!',NULL,NULL,NULL,'No Dogs',37.3080542,-112.9467617,'River/Creek',NULL,'National Park Service - Zion National Park','http://nps.gov/zion',NULL,'Difficult',4841,4630,NULL,2.1,'Point to Point',1.9,700,620,0.12,0.43,NULL,NULL,'https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/gpx/7045744','8010544 | https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/7045744/the-narrows-wall-street-to-big-springs-route',1,'2023-09-12 10:10:07','2023-09-19 06:00:36'),(217,'Pine Creek Waterfall Trail','8010544','hikingproject','7050813',7,'Family Friendly\n            \n                Swimming and an easy trail along a creek. Waterfall with deep waters during summer.','A short trail to the waterfall that is flat. When you reach the waterfall there is an area to swim.',NULL,NULL,NULL,'Unknown',37.2166415,-112.9654467,'Swimming / Waterfall',NULL,'National Park Service - Zion National Park','http://nps.gov/zion',NULL,'Intermediate',4178,4103,NULL,0.4,'Point to Point',NULL,78,9,0.05,0.11,NULL,NULL,'https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/gpx/7050813','8010544 | https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/7050813/pine-creek-waterfall-trail',1,'2023-09-12 10:10:10','2023-09-19 06:00:30'),(218,'Many Pools Trail','8010544','hikingproject','7107272',7,NULL,'This is a moderate hike up mostly sandstone rock and some sandy wash, passing dozens of small pools of water.',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,37.2218994,-112.9149883,'',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'Intermediate',5883,5320,NULL,1.1,'Point to Point',NULL,563,17,0.10,0.23,NULL,NULL,'https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/gpx/7107272','8010544 | https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/7107272/many-pools-trail',1,'2023-09-12 10:10:12','2023-09-19 06:00:30'),(219,'Sand Bench Trail','8010544','hikingproject','7001612',7,'Hike atop a massive landslide under The Sentinel. The trail is characterized by deep sand and little shade, so be sure to bring plenty of water and a can-do mentality. Due to the loose nature of this trail, be prepared for forward progress to be a little slower than normal.','A lollipop trail that traverses the remains of a massive ancient landslide.',NULL,NULL,NULL,'No Dogs',37.2364263,-112.9665875,'Birding / Fall Colors / River/Creek / Views / Wildflowers / Wildlife',NULL,'National Park Service - Zion National Park','http://nps.gov/zion',NULL,'Intermediate',4735,4272,NULL,4.1,'Point to Point',NULL,608,677,0.06,0.21,NULL,NULL,'https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/gpx/7001612','8010544 | https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/7001612/sand-bench-trail',1,'2023-09-12 10:10:14','2023-09-19 06:00:30'),(220,'Left Fork (The Subway)','8010544','hikingproject','7029082',7,'Permits are required to access this area from either direction. Please visit  nps.gov/zion/planyourvisit/html5-dom-document-internal-entity1-hellip-end for more information.','A bottom-up hike of the classic, beautiful, and super FUN route, perfect for a curious and adventurous soul.',NULL,NULL,NULL,'No Dogs',37.2848219,-113.0957114,'Birding / River/Creek / Swimming / Views / Waterfall / Wildflowers / Wildlife',NULL,'National Park Service - Zion National Park','http://nps.gov/zion',NULL,'Difficult',5755,4665,NULL,3.9,'Point to Point',NULL,1218,919,0.10,1.66,NULL,NULL,'https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/gpx/7029082','8010544 | https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/7029082/left-fork-the-subway',1,'2023-09-12 10:10:18','2023-09-19 06:00:30'),(221,'Ariel Point','8013242','hikingproject','7003210',2,'This former logging road leads to an unobstructed viewpoint on a rocky outcrop.  The out-and-back trail to Ariel Point is said to lead to the best of the nearby viewpoints.  Some route finding on the plateau may be necessary on this fading trail (following the GPS track on your phone using this website\'s Hiking Project mobile app is highly recommended).\r\n\r\nStarting from its junction with the Komo Point Trail, take the Ariel Point Trail south, being sure to stay on the old roadbed.  When in doubt, keep to the highest point on the ridgeline as it descends towards the canyon rim.  You may see old trail markers on the trees, but these disappear in the burned area.  Pass through sparse trees and occasional meadows before coming to a junction with the Walhalla Connector.  \r\n\r\nContinue straight south to stay on the Ariel Point Trail.  The path\'s descent becomes a bit steeper as it gets closer to the rim.  The trail ends near Ariel Point which lies on a rugged projection from the rim.','This is an infrequently used forested trail to a viewpoint on the North Rim.',NULL,NULL,NULL,'No Dogs',36.1936925,-111.9941792,'Views',NULL,'National Park Service - Grand Canyon National Park','http://www.nps.gov/grca/index.htm',NULL,'Easy/Intermediate',8244,7941,NULL,3.2,'Point to Point',NULL,33,319,0.02,0.09,NULL,NULL,'https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/gpx/7003210','8013242 | https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/7003210/ariel-point',1,'2023-09-12 10:11:13','2023-09-19 06:00:30'),(222,'Beamer','8013242','hikingproject','7003212',2,'Walk the Tanner Trail to the river and start up-canyon. Small outcroppings of Dox Sandstone present minor obstructions (with obvious solutions) at a couple of spots along the way, but in general, the route between Tanner Canyon and Palisades Creek is straightforward. Riparian vegetation is dense near the shoreline so the trail tends toward a line a short distance above the water where the brush starts to give way to rocky slopes.\n\nThe character of the Beamer Trail changes dramatically at Palisades Canyon. A relatively easy, straight-line stroll across sandy slopes becomes a tedious, demanding trek along narrow, exposed ledges at the very brink of high cliffs.\n\nTapeats Sandstone outcrops emerging from deep water make it impossible to stay near the river above the mouth of Palisades Creek. Climb about 300 vertical feet up the talus immediately north of the mouth of Palisades to the top of the Tapeats. This slope offers the only break in the sandstone cliff in the general vicinity so the place to start up should be obvious. The top of the Tapeats is the route all the way to the Little Colorado. The trail is badly eroded, narrow, and, in places, remarkably exposed at the edge of an impressive precipice, so hikers should walk carefully. \n\nHikers with a known fear of heights may find this trail segment difficult. Its almost like a junior version of the Tonto Trail, contouring around each of the many small, steep gullies that drain Palisades of the Desert. The trail is reasonably well-defined, but if there are to be route finding problems they will probably occur at the point the trail crosses the drainages. It is possible to scramble down to walk the shoreline  mile below the confluence, but the main trail stays on the Tapeats rim all the way to the Little Colorado River. \n\nNOTE: If you encounter remnants of mining or other historic activities, please leave artifacts in place for other visitors to enjoy and historians to interpret. The stories of these places and people can be lost when objects are moved.','A relatively easy hike on sandy slopes becomes a tedious, demanding trek along narrow clifftops.',NULL,NULL,NULL,'No Dogs',36.1031213,-111.8332909,'River/Creek / Views / Wildlife',NULL,'National Park Service - Grand Canyon National Park','http://www.nps.gov/grca/index.htm',NULL,'Difficult',3203,2685,NULL,9.9,'Point to Point',NULL,1692,1650,0.06,0.43,NULL,NULL,'https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/gpx/7003212','8013242 | https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/7003212/beamer',1,'2023-09-12 10:11:17','2023-09-19 06:00:30'),(223,'Bill Hall','8013242','hikingproject','7003213',2,'The historical point of access to the esplanade was via the gentler but longer Thunder River Trail. Nowadays, many hikers opt for the more challenging Bill Hall Trail, which shaves 2.5 miles off the hike. For the first  mile, the Bill Hall Trail contours on the rim, actually ascending a bit before entering the canyon on the east side of Monument Point. \n\nThe trail drops steeply through the Kaibab and Toroweap Formations north of Bridgers Knoll, then contours northwest to the Coconino descent west of Monument Point. Many steep switchbacks provide passage through the Coconino Sandstone and on to a small drainage leading down to the Esplanade and the junction with the trail from Indian Hollow. A short rope may be useful to lower the packs at a short (15 feet) scramble. The descent from Indian Hollow is less demanding. It was originally designed as a stock trail and features a more reasonable gradient but requires extra walking.\n\nBill Hall was a seasonal park ranger on the North Rim who was killed in the line of duty (automobile accident) in 1979.','An alternative start to the Thunder River Trail, more difficult but shaves ~2.5miles off the start.',NULL,NULL,NULL,'No Dogs',36.4346967,-112.4299430,'Birding / Views / Wildflowers / Wildlife',NULL,'National Park Service - Grand Canyon National Park','http://www.nps.gov/grca/index.htm',NULL,'Difficult',7198,5433,NULL,2.5,'Point to Point',NULL,174,1785,0.15,0.56,NULL,NULL,'https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/gpx/7003213','8013242 | https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/7003213/bill-hall',1,'2023-09-12 10:11:19','2023-09-19 06:00:30'),(224,'Clear Creek','8013242','hikingproject','7003223',2,'The trailhead is approximately  mile north of Phantom Ranch on the North Kaibab Trail. The junction is marked by a wooden sign.\n\nFrom the junction with the North Kaibab Trail, the Clear Creek Trail climbs through a series of switchbacks to the southeast towards Phantom Overlook (a sharp switchback with a landing where there are a few stone benches; from this point there is a good view looking straight down at Phantom Ranch). After passing Phantom Overlook, the trail continues up to the base of the Tapeats and then traverses to the east for another mile to the Tonto Platform. While walking along the base of the Tapeats, hikers are exposed to the Great Unconformity, a gap in the geologic record spanning more than 1 billion years. After a final ascent to the top of the Tapeats, the trail contours along the Tonto Platform, crossing Sumner Wash and two minor drainages. The trail turns to the north when it intersects with Clear Creek drainage below Demaray Point. When Clear Creek Canyon appears on the right, hikers are still only half way to their destination. From here, the trail crosses shallow Zoroaster Canyon and then continues over an unnamed drainage to the north. Finally, at the end of the Tonto traverse, the trail drops into a drainage by traversing a long slope of brilliantly orange-colored Hakatai Shale. The trail ends at a dry tributary creek bed, looking up this drainage one can see Brahma Temple. From here there is no trail, so it is necessary to hike down the drainage for approximately 150 meters to its confluence with Clear Creek. Most backpackers camp at the many impacted sites just downstream from the confluence.\n\nA faint route continues downstream to the confluence with the east fork of Clear Creek. Hiking to the Colorado River via the creek requires scrambling and numerous creek crossings. It is approximately six miles one way. A quarter mile from the river there is a pour off that requires a fifteen foot down climb. This climb can be wet and icy, so use extreme caution, and don\'t attempt if you don\'t feel comfortable with free climbing. Allow a full day to complete this hike. \n\nMany hikers also day hike up Clear Creek toward Cheyava Falls (800 feet tall when flowing). This is a five mile hike one-way and follows the creek bed. Allow a full day to complete this hike. Cheyava Falls only flows in the spring after winters with high snowfall.','The only trail across the Tonto Platform that leads to the tallest seasonal waterfall in the park.',NULL,NULL,NULL,'Unknown',36.1106693,-112.0916195,'River/Creek / Views / Waterfall / Wildlife',NULL,'National Park Service - Grand Canyon National Park','http://www.nps.gov/grca/index.htm',NULL,'Intermediate',4246,2651,NULL,8.8,'Point to Point',NULL,2716,1727,0.10,0.87,NULL,NULL,'https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/gpx/7003223','8013242 | https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/7003223/clear-creek',1,'2023-09-12 10:11:22','2023-09-19 06:00:30'),(225,'Cliff Springs','8013242','hikingproject','7003224',2,'This trail begins directly across the road from a small pullout on a curve 0.3 miles / 0.5 km down the road from Cape Royal. It meanders down a forested ravine and ends where a chest-high boulder rests under a large overhang. The spring is on the cliff side of the boulder.','A forested trail leading to a seeping cliffside spring.',NULL,NULL,NULL,'No Dogs',36.1245876,-111.9478621,'River/Creek / Views / Wildflowers',NULL,'National Park Service - Grand Canyon National Park','http://www.nps.gov/grca/index.htm',NULL,'Intermediate',7759,7583,NULL,0.4,'Point to Point',NULL,44,179,0.10,0.21,NULL,NULL,'https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/gpx/7003224','8013242 | https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/7003224/cliff-springs',1,'2023-09-12 10:11:23','2023-09-19 06:00:30'),(226,'Deer Creek','8013242','hikingproject','7003227',2,'Family Friendly\n            \n                Easy to get to if you\'re coming from a raft, this is spectacularly fun for older kids who could, with a lot of care, explore parts of the slot canyon.  To say nothing of the waterfall!','The huge outpourings of water in this area have attracted people since prehistoric times.',NULL,NULL,NULL,'No Dogs',36.4010682,-112.4760265,'Birding / River/Creek / Views / Waterfall / Wildflowers / Wildlife',NULL,'National Park Service - Grand Canyon National Park','http://www.nps.gov/grca/index.htm',NULL,'Intermediate/Difficult',3766,1964,NULL,3.2,'Point to Point',NULL,81,1882,0.12,0.44,NULL,NULL,'https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/gpx/7003227','8013242 | https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/7003227/deer-creek',1,'2023-09-12 10:11:26','2023-09-19 06:00:30'),(227,'Escalante','8013242','hikingproject','7003232',2,'Hike this route in either direction, but traveling with the flow of the land is appealing so this passage will be described from the perspective of a down-canyon hiker. \r\n\r\nShallow gullies must be crossed at intervals, but generally speaking, the route from Tanner to Cardenas is straightforward. Cardenas Creek is almost always dry, but there is easy access to the Colorado River via the bed of the drainage. This is the last reliable water source until one reaches the river at the mouth of Escalante Creek. The trail crosses Cardenas Creek about two hundred yards above the shoreline and climbs the Dox Hill immediately west.\r\n\r\nThe route crosses the unnamed drainage and traverses west toward the crest of the ridge north of Escalante Creek. Caution is indicated throughout this area, as there are many places where you\'ll want to avoid a misstep at all cost. The trail seems to traverse west forever, finally going to the top of the ridge just short of the west end. A fine view in all directions is the reward for all the side hill walking.\r\n\r\nThe trail climbs away from the river below the mouth of Escalante Creek and follows a rising ramp of Shinumo Quartzite down the canyon. Hike the top of this formation around into Seventyfive Mile Creek. Seventyfive Mile Creek is normally dry in its lower reaches, but there is access to the river at various points throughout the stretch from Escalante to Papago Creeks. Trails along the beach form the route downriver towards Papago Creek. Eventually, sand gives way to rock and a series of ledges require a bit of attention to avoid being rimmed up. In general, stay as close to the river as is conveniently possible. The trail goes up and over a small outcrop of sandstone before dropping back to river level at the mouth of Papago Creek.\r\n\r\nExit Papago on the west and work up a series of tall ledges. Start up immediately west of the mouth of the drainage. The holds are big and secure and leads up from ledge to ledge. The climbing is easy, but no mistakes can be made. About 35 feet up the angle relents and one can climb the slopes to a horizontal trail about 300 vertical feet above the river.\r\n\r\nTraverse less than 100 yards down the canyon before coming to the top of a talus filled runnel that allows passage back to the shoreline. This gully seems dangerous - steep, with lots of big boulders in precarious balance.\r\n\r\nOnce at Hance Rapids you can continue west via the East Tonto Trail or ascend to the rim on the New Hance Trail.','The Escalante Route has a reputation for requiring a bit extra from Grand Canyon visitors.',NULL,NULL,NULL,'No Dogs',36.1031213,-111.8332909,'Birding / River/Creek / Views / Wildflowers / Wildlife',NULL,'National Park Service - Grand Canyon National Park','http://www.nps.gov/grca/index.htm',NULL,'Very Difficult',3885,2583,NULL,11.3,'Point to Point',NULL,2156,2250,0.07,0.62,NULL,NULL,'https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/gpx/7003232','8013242 | https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/7003232/escalante',1,'2023-09-12 10:11:30','2023-09-19 06:00:30'),(228,'Francois Matthes','8013242','hikingproject','7003233',2,'This is an isolated former logging road that is being slowly reclaimed by nature.  Starting from its junction with the Walhalla Glades Trail, the Francois Matthes Trail heads southwest over the plateau.  It descends steadily on a broad ridge through scattered pine trees and a wildfire area.  \n\nContinue straight through the intersection with the Walhalla Connector and cross a few open meadows.  After a couple of slightly steeper downhill sections, you\'ll arrive at the rim for a unique perspective view of Zoroaster Temple and Wotans Throne that is partly obscured by brush.  You can also see Cheyava Falls and the Clear Creek drainage.  Retrace your steps back to the parking area.  \n\nHistorical factoid:  This trail is named after Francois E. Matthes a geologist and expert topographical mapper responsible for creating maps of many remote areas of the west.  He helped advance theories about glacial geology and his maps helped a great deal of US lands become protected as National Parks.','This infrequently used out-and-back trail to Francois Matthes Point almost guarantees solitude.',NULL,NULL,NULL,'No Dogs',36.2143078,-111.9603486,'Views',NULL,'National Park Service - Grand Canyon National Park','http://www.nps.gov/grca/index.htm',NULL,'Easy',8430,8013,NULL,4.8,'Point to Point',NULL,39,442,0.02,0.07,NULL,NULL,'https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/gpx/7003233','8013242 | https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/7003233/francois-matthes',1,'2023-09-12 10:11:32','2023-09-19 06:00:30'),(229,'Ken Patrick','8013242','hikingproject','7003243',2,'The rocky and remote Ken Patrick Trail would be a very long out-and-back, so most folks wanting to go the whole length set up a car shuttle and complete it in only one direction.  Begin the Ken Patrick Trail from the mule stable area at the far end of the North Kaibab trailhead parking lot.  Travel through scattered woods and meadows.  This western end from the trailhead to the junction with Uncle Jim Trail is the most heavily used part and is easy to follow.  This section of the Ken Patrick Trail also allows mules, so be courteous and also watch out for their droppings.  \r\n\r\nContinue past the Uncle Jim Trail junction generally uphill with steep dips into and then up out of several eroded valleys.  This section of trail to the signed junction with the Old Bright Angel Trail is still easy to follow and crosses a huge burned area that still appears ghostly after the 2000 Outlet fire (a prescribed burn got out of control).  There may be some downed trees to navigate, but regrowth is well established here.  Next, plunge deep into a canyon formed by Bright Angel Creek and then arduously climb back out.  This final section of trail is less defined and some route finding care is needed.  Head northeast and do not wander too far from the rim.  Some users also report having to bushwhack through dense undergrowth.  Traverse the forested upper Walhalla plateau and then cross Cape Royal Road.  \r\n\r\nFor the remainder of the hike, the route finding becomes much easier and you are treated to numerous wonderful views down into the canyon over the rim.  You\'ll be able to see upper Bright Angel Canyon, Mount Hayden, Marble Canyon and Nankoweap Creek basin.  Navigate one last long section of ups and downs into and out of wooded ravines before the trail ends at the southern end of the Point Imperial parking area.  \r\n\r\nHistorical Factoid: This trail was named after Grand Canyon ranger Kenneth C. Patrick who worked at the national park in the 1960s, but was later killed in 1973 while on duty at Point Reyes National Seashore in California.','Wind through the undulating forest & along the rim from Point Imperial to the North Kaibab Trailhead',NULL,NULL,NULL,'No Dogs',36.2176199,-112.0557857,'Fall Colors / Views / Wildflowers',NULL,'National Park Service - Grand Canyon National Park','http://www.nps.gov/grca/index.htm',NULL,'Intermediate',8815,8110,NULL,9.9,'Point to Point',NULL,1872,1327,0.06,0.48,NULL,NULL,'https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/gpx/7003243','8013242 | https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/7003243/ken-patrick',1,'2023-09-12 10:11:34','2023-09-19 06:00:30'),(230,'Komo Point','8013242','hikingproject','7003244',2,'The out-and-back Komo Point Trail starts from the Cape Royal Road on the North Rim.  This is a seldom traveled former forest road.  The route goes gently and steadily downhill southwards to the rim of the canyon.  It passes through numerous skeletons of burned out trees from a huge wildfire.  There are several downed trees making the going slow.  The trail starts out quite easy to follow but grows fainter making it a challenge to stay on track. Be sure to use the Hiking Project mobile app if you\'re worried about route finding.\r\n\r\nAbout 2/3 of the way along the trail it forks. The left (southern) fork leads to Ariel Point and the right (western) fork is the continuation of the Komo Point Trail.  Upon reaching the rocky and rugged rim, look for the large faded yellow ball- it was used as a reference point when the canyon was being mapped by airplane.  Retrace your steps back to the parking area.','This former road is very faint in places, leading through a burn area to an impressive overlook',NULL,NULL,NULL,'No Dogs',36.2305048,-111.9826358,'Views',NULL,'National Park Service - Grand Canyon National Park','http://www.nps.gov/grca/index.htm',NULL,'Easy/Intermediate',8499,8058,NULL,5.2,'Point to Point',NULL,20,461,0.02,0.05,NULL,NULL,'https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/gpx/7003244','8013242 | https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/7003244/komo-point',1,'2023-09-12 10:11:35','2023-09-19 06:00:30'),(231,'North Bass','8013242','hikingproject','7003259',2,'The remote North Bass trailhead is located at Swamp Point. Swamp Point is at the end of Swamp Ridge Road. Though it is possible to access the trailhead from the North Rim Village by taking the Point Sublime and Kanabownits Roads, it should be noted that passage is much easier from the west via Forest Service Roads. A North Kaibab National Forest map is essential. \r\n\r\nFrom Swamp Point, the trail drops nearly a mile via switchbacks to an intersection at Muav Saddle. To your right stands an old patrol cabin. The Powell Plateau Trail continues south and ascends to Powell Plateau. The North Bass Trail is to the left and contours the base of the Coconino Sandstone for approximately  mile to the somewhat reliable Queen Anne Spring. \r\n\r\nThe one-mile descent from the spring to a benchmark in White Creek was restored to the historic trail alignment and completely rebuilt in 2005. Continue down White Creek until you reach a waterfall. The bypass is on creek right about 50 feet before reaching the waterfall. Continue down the bed of White Creek until you come to the large pour-off in the Redwall. \r\n\r\nThe cairned trail to the right crosses four drainages as it traverses along the Redwall rim. After crossing the fourth drainage the trail continues along a cliff to the start of the Redwall descent. The descent through the Redwall to the bed of White Creek is steep and rocky, but well marked. Continue down the drainage to a pour-off in the Bright Angel Shale which can be bypassed on creek right. The bypass is approximately  mile in length and contours along the Tonto before dropping back into White Creek. \r\n\r\nThe trail continues down the drainage until reaching a benchmark at the top of the Tapeats Sandstone. At the mouth of Redwall Canyon, a large pour-off in the Tapeats prevents further travel in the drainage. In 2005, the trail was restored to its historic alignment and today bypasses this fall approximately  mile above the Tapeats pour off, contouring along the Tonto to the west of White Creek. A final quick descent drops the hiker alongside Shinumo Creek about  mile downstream of the White/Shinumo confluence. \r\n\r\nThe trail follows Shinumo Creek downstream for approximately one and half miles before abruptly exiting the drainage on the east side of the creek. A climb of approximately 700 feet is required to reach a saddle before the trail descends to a large beach and terrace above the Colorado River.','This holds a well-deserved spot as one of the most difficult and demanding Grand Canyon trails.',NULL,NULL,NULL,'Unknown',36.3356346,-112.3499210,'Birding / Fall Colors / River/Creek / Swimming / Views / Waterfall / Wildflowers / Wildlife',NULL,'National Park Service - Grand Canyon National Park','http://www.nps.gov/grca/index.htm',NULL,'Very Difficult',7503,2233,NULL,11.9,'Point to Point',NULL,1030,6300,0.12,0.71,NULL,NULL,'https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/gpx/7003259','8013242 | https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/7003259/north-bass',1,'2023-09-12 10:11:38','2023-09-19 06:00:30'),(232,'AZT - Passage 38 - North Kaibab','8013242','hikingproject','7003260',2,'This trail is the second section of the AZT - Passage 38 - Grand Canyon - Inner Gorge, following the South Kaibab trail as the first section. \r\n\r\nThe North Kaibab initially descends steeply down the head of a valley via a series of numerous switchbacks. After the lush verdancy and impressive views below the trailhead, the trail reaches the distinctive Supai Tunnel, where hikers will find potable water (mid-May to mid-October) and pit toilets. Switchbacks continue to the massive cliffs of Redwall Limestone, where for long stretches the trail has been blasted out of the cliff in a half tunnel. The Redwall section is awe-inspiring with fantastic exposure and views.\r\n\r\nWhere the bulk of the trail\'s descent meets the flatter bottom of Bright Angel Canyon, Roaring Springs is a worthwhile side trip. Here, water gushes forth directly out of the cliffs, cascading over moss and fern to form Bright Angel Creek.\r\n\r\nAt mile 5.4 an unexpected house comes into view. The Pumphouse Residence, also known as the Aiken Residence, is where artist and park employee Bruce Aiken lived and painted for decades. In years past the lucky hiker could get a refreshing cup of lemonade from one of the Aiken kids who were raised at the bottom of the Grand Canyon. \r\n\r\nThose fortunate to overnight at Cottonwood Campground will have the time to explore a beautiful oasis located 1.6 miles down-creek from the campground: Ribbon Falls is a true gem of the North Kaibab Trail. The waterfall is located in a grotto on the west side of Bright Angel Creek, so reaching it is a short but extremely rewarding side trip. Hikers caught in the heat of the day should consider taking a siesta here from 10 a.m. to 4 p.m. \r\n\r\nBetween Cottonwood Campground and Bright Angel Campground the trail enters the Inner Gorge, a narrow canyon where the trail is boxed in on either side by 1.7 billion-year-old Vishnu Schist. Though the trail here is not challenging from the perspective of elevation profile, be aware that this is a particularly dangerous stretch of trail during summer months. \r\n\r\nBecause the entire 7.2 mile stretch is at low elevation, it becomes extremely hot from early morning to late afternoon. The gorge of black rock through which the trail passes becomes like an oven and can be compared to walking through a parking lot in Phoenix or Las Vegas in summertime. Always plan on reaching Bright Angel Campground before ten in the morning.','This trail is the least visited & most difficult of the three maintained trails at Grand Canyon NP.',NULL,NULL,NULL,'No Dogs',36.2170981,-112.0566480,'Fall Colors / River/Creek / Views / Waterfall / Wildflowers / Wildlife',NULL,'National Park Service - Grand Canyon National Park','http://www.nps.gov/grca/index.htm',NULL,'Difficult',8222,2454,NULL,14.7,'Point to Point',NULL,616,6384,0.09,0.63,NULL,NULL,'https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/gpx/7003260','8013242 | https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/7003260/azt-passage-38-north-kaibab',1,'2023-09-12 10:11:42','2023-09-19 06:00:30'),(233,'Bridle Trail','8013242','hikingproject','7003262',2,NULL,'The only trail on the N Rim allowing dogs and bikes, but it\'s an enjoyable hike for everyone else too',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,36.1959761,-112.0513390,'',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'Easy/Intermediate',8335,8181,NULL,2.0,'Point to Point',NULL,198,117,0.03,0.13,NULL,NULL,'https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/gpx/7003262','8013242 | https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/7003262/bridle-trail',1,'2023-09-12 10:11:44','2023-09-19 06:00:30'),(234,'Old Bright Angel','8013242','hikingproject','7003263',2,'The Old Bright Angel Trail is also known as the Old Kaibab Trail.  Good route finding skills are required for this trail, especially for the first several miles.  The start of the trail, as it leaves the junction with the Ken Patrick trail is marked, but be sure to stay on the main trail and not be fooled by faint game trails.  \r\n\r\nTraverse the east side of the valley and switchback down into a dry streambed.  After some distance following the streambed, descend again to Bright Angel Creek itself and enjoy the views.  Pass a few shortfalls, keeping to the west bank of the creek, ignoring a couple initial false paths that appear to cross it.  The true crossing is later than you imagine, and keeping to the trail here is difficult due to social trails and dense undergrowth.  There may be cairns visible so keep an eye out.         \r\n\r\nOnce on the eastern side of Bright Angel Creek, in about a mile you reach the confluence with Roaring Springs.  From here you can continue along the eastern side of the drainage until the trail ends and you reach the intersection with the North Kaibab Trail at the Pumphouse seasonal ranger station and seasonal water source.  If using the Old Bright Angel Trail to form part of a loop, some folks recommend doing Old Bright Angel for the descent because it is easier to find the trail in that direction than when ascending.  \r\n\r\nHistorical notes:  this trail was originally created by F.E. Matthes who surveyed much of the Grand Canyon for the US government in 1902.  It apparently crossed Bright Angel Creek 94 times but was later improved with the purpose of allowing tourists to reach the North Rim.  Once the park service finished the North Kaibab trail, fewer and fewer people used the Old Bright Angel Trail and it has become overgrown.','This unmaintained path along the upper part of Bright Angel Canyon is good for making long loops.',NULL,NULL,NULL,'No Dogs',36.2395475,-112.0162778,'Fall Colors / Views / Waterfall / Wildlife',NULL,'National Park Service - Grand Canyon National Park','http://www.nps.gov/grca/index.htm',NULL,'Difficult',8507,4541,NULL,5.1,'Point to Point',NULL,368,4333,0.17,0.78,NULL,NULL,'https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/gpx/7003263','8013242 | https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/7003263/old-bright-angel',1,'2023-09-12 10:11:46','2023-09-19 06:00:30'),(235,'Point Imperial','8013242','hikingproject','7003267',2,'This mostly flat trail starts from the north side of the Point Imperial overlook parking lot.  (Point Imperial is the highest of the North Rim overlooks)  First, head through open meadow and into a big burn area.  These looming pine and aspen tree shards are all that\'s left after the 2000 Outlook wildfire raged here.  Historical factoid: This fire was the result of a prescribed burn that got out of control and it temporarily trapped tourists at Imperial Point!  \r\n\r\nThe post-fire vegetation is coming back and wildflowers thrive here in the early season.  The trail approaches the rim and good views into Marble Canyon can be had from various points.  To the north, you can see Boundary Ridge and its highest point, Saddle Mountain.  Continue across the plateau over increasingly hilly terrain.  Eventually, the trail ends at a dirt turnaround and parking area.  \r\n\r\nNote: some guidebooks list this trail as a northern extension of the Ken Patrick Trail.','Take this easy hike through an area of the Kaibab forest recovering from the 2000 Outlet wildfire.',NULL,NULL,NULL,'No Dogs',36.2790249,-111.9782700,'Birding / Fall Colors / Views / Wildflowers',NULL,'National Park Service - Grand Canyon National Park','http://www.nps.gov/grca/index.htm',NULL,'Intermediate',8892,8758,NULL,2.7,'Point to Point',NULL,278,202,0.03,0.12,NULL,NULL,'https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/gpx/7003267','8013242 | https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/7003267/point-imperial',1,'2023-09-12 10:11:49','2023-09-19 06:00:30'),(236,'Powell Plateau','8013242','hikingproject','7003268',2,'This huge and remote chunk of plateau land is surrounded on all sides by the depths of the Grand Canyon and is reached via a thin neck of land connecting it to the north rim.  Exploring the entire plateau would take days, but this trail will give a sampling of what is on offer.  \r\n\r\nStart from Swamp Point where the Powell Plateau Trail forks from the North Bass Trail.  After the initial North Bass switchbacks, Powell Point Trail begins straight ahead (southwest) and the North Bass Trail turns sharply left (east).  Before heading up to the plateau, it is worth poking around the NPS ranger cabin.  Although it is also called Teddy\'s Cabin, Theodore Roosevelt never stayed here!  \r\n\r\nStart climbing switchbacks up to the plateau, leaving the Muav Saddle behind.  You should still be able to see evidence of the wildfires that burned in this area as recently as 1989.  After a few steep sections, the grade becomes more constant and before long you\'re among pines that signal the plateau.  Once on the plateau, the trail is almost completely flat.\r\n\r\nTurn south and trace the edge of the plateau, hiking through a lovely forest of ponderosa pines and wildflowers.  Depending on your destination, the trail often becomes faint, but careful route finding allows users to reach as far as Dutton Canyon.  There are a few cairns and markers on trees but expect to lose the way eventually.  Most people stop once the trail runs out and retrace their steps back to the trailhead.  An adventurous and worthwhile goal is to persevere to Dutton Point which is the only overlook on the plateau to get views of the Colorado River.  This is also one of the locations used to create the famous \"Chasm of the Colorado\" composite painting which used to hang in the US Senate.  To reach Dutton Point, you\'ll have to bushwhack across a few ravines choked by prickly brush. (Note: be sure your navigation skills and water supplies are up to this added excursion)   \r\n\r\nHistorical notes:  the Anasazi lived on this plateau during the 11th and 12th centuries.  In 1870 the explorer John Wesley Powell visited and the plateau was named for him.  Teddy Roosevelt and others hunted mountain lions for sport here. Dutton Point and Dutton Canyon are named after the geologist Clarence Dutton.','An isolated feeling pervades this wonderfully forested peninsula of land into the Grand Canyon.',NULL,NULL,NULL,'No Dogs',36.3336590,-112.3552301,'Views / Wildflowers / Wildlife',NULL,'National Park Service - Grand Canyon National Park','http://www.nps.gov/grca/index.htm',NULL,'Intermediate',7663,6711,NULL,3.3,'Point to Point',NULL,1000,110,0.06,0.32,NULL,NULL,'https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/gpx/7003268','8013242 | https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/7003268/powell-plateau',1,'2023-09-12 10:11:51','2023-09-19 06:00:30'),(237,'Saddle Horse Loop','8013242','hikingproject','7003279',2,'This 45-minute loop offers spectacular views of the Colorado River. Access the trail along the road between Toroweap Overlook and Tuweep Campground, or from a trailhead near campsite 5 in the Tuweep Campground.  This mostly level trail approaches the canyon edge and has spectacular straight-down views of the river.','A short loop that provides amazing views of the Colorado River.',NULL,NULL,NULL,'No Dogs',36.2239322,-113.0606771,'Views',NULL,'National Park Service - Grand Canyon National Park','http://www.nps.gov/grca/index.htm',NULL,'Easy/Intermediate',4581,4473,NULL,1.1,'Point to Point',NULL,100,109,0.04,0.08,NULL,NULL,'https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/gpx/7003279','8013242 | https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/7003279/saddle-horse-loop',1,'2023-09-12 10:11:52','2023-09-19 06:00:30'),(238,'Saddle Mountain','8013242','hikingproject','7003280',2,'The Saddle Mountain trail does not actually reach the summit of Saddle Mountain, instead it reaches the deep cut in the north rim, just below Saddle Mountain.  The trail is also called Trail #31 to the Nankoweap Saddle Overlook.\r\n\r\nStarting from the sometimes difficult to reach Saddle Trailhead (off FR610), hike to the east, descending immediately off the north rim.  Pass a small knob that has nice views and then up steeply and over a larger hill, all along a ridgeline traverse.  After descending to an open plateau, the trail becomes comparatively level for a time and soon plunges down through a more wooded burn area.  Recover with some travel through flatter, parched ground before the trail ends at an intersection with the Nankoweap (or #57) Trail.','A pleasant alternative to reach Nankoweap Trail, this remote trail has great views too.',NULL,NULL,NULL,'No Dogs',36.3031216,-111.9947182,'Views / Wildflowers',NULL,'National Park Service - Grand Canyon National Park','http://www.nps.gov/grca/index.htm',NULL,'Very Difficult',8836,7558,NULL,2.3,'Point to Point',NULL,357,1630,0.16,1.47,NULL,NULL,'https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/gpx/7003280','8013242 | https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/7003280/saddle-mountain',1,'2023-09-12 10:11:53','2023-09-19 06:00:30'),(239,'Schmutz Spring','8013242','hikingproject','7003281',2,NULL,'Needs Adoption',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,36.3392672,-112.9271155,'',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'Intermediate',5772,4474,NULL,2.1,'Point to Point',NULL,29,1326,0.12,0.36,NULL,NULL,'https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/gpx/7003281','8013242 | https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/7003281/schmutz-spring',1,'2023-09-12 10:11:54','2023-09-19 06:00:30'),(240,'Tapeats Creek','8013242','hikingproject','7003291',2,'The huge outpourings of water at Thunder River, Tapeats Spring, and Deer Spring have attracted people since prehistoric times and today this little corner of Grand Canyon is exceedingly popular among seekers of the remarkable. \n\nTake the Thunder River Trail generally south across the Esplanade. The trail crosses expansive slickrock sections as it works around several small drainages, so hikers need to be alert for cairns that mark the route ahead. Try to locate the next cairn before the last one is lost from view. About 2.5 miles of reasonably flat trail brings hikers to the southern edge of the Esplanade, a wonderful canyon view, and the top of a series of steep switchbacks through the Supai and Redwall Formations to the floor of Surprise Valley. The descent is long and rough and the southern exposure makes the entire area infamously hot. Avoid hiking in Surprise Valley after 10 a.m. during warm weather. A large cairn marks a fork in the trail  east (left) to Thunder River and Tapeats Creek, west (right) to Deer Creek. \n\nThe trail to Thunder River and Tapeats Creek winds east across the floor of Surprise Valley, crossing shallow drainages and low hills for about a mile before dropping down steep switchbacks to Thunder River. After so many hot, dry miles the cascades of Thunder River seem almost too good to be true and the place demands a stop for rest and relaxation. The designated Upper Tapeats campsite (AW7) is about  mile below the confluence of Thunder River and Tapeats Creek on the west side of the creek. Hikers headed for the Colorado River can cross to the east side of the creek either just below the campsite or about  mile down canyon and continue about two miles to a second creek crossing back to the west side. A short rope may come in handy to lower packs at a little downclimb (8 feet) above the lower crossing. Snowmelt or heavy rain may make creek crossings impossible. During periods of high water hikers must use a sketchy, seldom used trail that stays west of the creek all the way to the river. This option is narrow and exposed and should be used only as a last resort, but when Tapeats Creek is in flood it may be the only possibility.','The approach is long, hot and dry, but the journey to this canyon destination is near perfection.',NULL,NULL,NULL,'No Dogs',36.3922249,-112.4516912,'Birding / River/Creek / Views / Waterfall / Wildflowers / Wildlife',NULL,'National Park Service - Grand Canyon National Park','http://www.nps.gov/grca/index.htm',NULL,'Intermediate/Difficult',2481,1989,NULL,2.3,'Point to Point',NULL,239,731,0.08,0.74,NULL,NULL,'https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/gpx/7003291','8013242 | https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/7003291/tapeats-creek',1,'2023-09-12 10:11:57','2023-09-19 06:00:30'),(241,'Thunder River','8013242','hikingproject','7003292',2,'Take the Thunder River Trail generally south across the Esplanade. The trail crosses expansive slickrock sections as it works around several small drainages, so hikers need to be alert for cairns that mark the route ahead. Try to locate the next cairn before the last one is lost from view. About 2.5 miles of reasonably flat walking brings hikers to the southern edge of the Esplanade, a wonderful canyon view, and the top of a series of steep switchbacks through the Supai and Redwall Formations to the floor of Surprise Valley. The descent is long and rough and the southern exposure makes the entire area infamously hot. Avoid hiking in Surprise Valley after 10 a.m. during warm weather. A large cairn marks a fork in the trail  east (left) to Thunder River and Tapeats Creek, west (right) to Deer Creek. \r\n\r\nThe trail to Thunder River and Tapeats Creek winds east across the floor of Surprise Valley, crossing shallow drainages and low hills for about a mile before dropping down steep switchbacks to Thunder River. After so many hot, dry miles the cascades of Thunder River seem almost too good to be true and the place demands a stop for rest and relaxation. The designated Upper Tapeats campsite (AW7) is about  mile below the confluence of Thunder River and Tapeats Creek on the west side of the creek. Hikers headed for the Colorado River can cross to the east side of the creek either just below the campsite or about  mile down canyon and continue about two miles to a second creek crossing back to the west side. \r\n\r\nA short rope may come in handy to lower packs at a little downclimb (8 feet) above the lower crossing. Snowmelt or heavy rain may make creek crossings impossible. Spring warming trends and intermittent high creek levels are more likely after mid-March with the peak flows often in May. During periods of high water, hikers must use a sketchy, seldom-used trail that stays west of the creek all the way to the river. This option is narrow and exposed and should be used only as a last resort, but when Tapeats Creek is in flood it may be the only possibility. Lower Tapeats (AW8) is a designated campsite, please look for the tent symbol sign and camp on the west side of the creek.','Booming streams of water emerge from mysterious caves to transform the harsh desert into an oasis.',NULL,NULL,NULL,'No Dogs',36.4589909,-112.4882436,'Birding / Cave / River/Creek / Views / Waterfall / Wildflowers / Wildlife',NULL,'National Park Service - Grand Canyon National Park','http://www.nps.gov/grca/index.htm',NULL,'Intermediate/Difficult',6235,2490,NULL,12.6,'Point to Point',NULL,883,4615,0.08,0.71,NULL,NULL,'https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/gpx/7003292','8013242 | https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/7003292/thunder-river',1,'2023-09-12 10:12:01','2023-09-19 06:00:30'),(242,'Tiyo Point','8013242','hikingproject','7003293',2,'This former road is becoming increasingly overgrown by the encroaching forest.  This being said, it is generally easy to follow with some blown down trees to climb over or around.  There are some old trail markers nailed to trees, but you may not spot them.  \r\n\r\nAfter an initial short ascent from the start at Point Subline Road, the trail is gently downhill over rolling terrain.  Wildflowers make their home here, but they will likely be your only company on this infrequently used primitive trail.  There is a single picnic area near the end of the trail.','Hike to this remote overlook if you need peaceful isolation and shady forest.',NULL,NULL,NULL,'No Dogs',36.2584919,-112.1030460,'Views / Wildflowers / Wildlife',NULL,'National Park Service - Grand Canyon National Park','http://www.nps.gov/grca/index.htm',NULL,'Easy/Intermediate',8319,7777,NULL,6.6,'Point to Point',NULL,183,570,0.02,0.08,NULL,NULL,'https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/gpx/7003293','8013242 | https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/7003293/tiyo-point',1,'2023-09-12 10:12:02','2023-09-19 06:00:30'),(243,'Tuckup Trail','8013242','hikingproject','7003295',2,'Experience Tuweep\'s unique geology on this 3.1 mile old jeep trail. There are two access points: the first is signed before arriving at Tuweep Campground and the second leaves from campsite 10 in the campground.\r\n\r\nThis trail segment is part of a larger network that spans a total distance of around 60 miles, east to west, and is one of the most challenging and least visited of the Grand Canyon\'s named trails.','A visit to Tuweep provides an opportunity for an uncrowded, rustic, and remote experience.',NULL,NULL,NULL,'No Dogs',36.2302729,-113.0643063,'Views',NULL,'National Park Service - Grand Canyon National Park','http://www.nps.gov/grca/index.htm',NULL,'Intermediate',4655,4357,NULL,3.1,'Point to Point',NULL,89,373,0.03,0.09,NULL,NULL,'https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/gpx/7003295','8013242 | https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/7003295/tuckup-trail',1,'2023-09-12 10:12:03','2023-09-19 06:00:30'),(244,'Tuckup to Tuweep Campground Connector','8013242','hikingproject','7003296',2,NULL,'Needs Adoption',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,36.2260265,-113.0601920,'',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'Easy',4598,4566,NULL,1.2,'Point to Point',NULL,114,120,0.04,0.12,NULL,NULL,'https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/gpx/7003296','8013242 | https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/7003296/tuckup-to-tuweep-campground-connector',1,'2023-09-12 10:12:04','2023-09-19 06:00:30'),(245,'Unkar Delta','8013242','hikingproject','7003300',2,'This trail guide is used by Colorado River boating parties during visits to Unkar Delta, once the home of prehistoric peoples. The trail is a 0.9 mile loop, over alluvial terraces and drainages. Allow approximately forty-five minutes, round-trip. This trail system has been developed to permit visitation while protecting the fragile desert environment and prehistoric remains.\n\nUnkar Delta trail is a 0.8 mile loop, over alluvial terraces and drainages. Allow approximately forty-five minutes, round-trip. This trail system has been developed to permit visitation while protecting the fragile desert environment and prehistoric remains.\n\nHuman use of Unkar Delta began around AD 850. While the excavators suggested four distinct phases of occupation, recent interpretation suggests a gradual process of growth to a peak population around the year AD 1100. For reasons not yet fully understood, the Pueblo people migrated away from the delta by AD 1200. To the modern Hopi, their clans that migrated from Unkar did so in fulfillment of the covenant with the spiritual guardian, Masaw, so that today, the Hopis see Unkar as a footprint of the clans who once inhabited this village. \n\nFarming was the key to life on Unkar Delta. Early living sites were built on the fertile terraces along Unkar Creek. As the population grew, and with it the demand for food, living sites were moved to the talus slopes above the floodplain to make room for more crops.\n\nThe sites along the low terraces were occupied earlier in the history of the delta (AD 850- 1050), those on the talus slopes, later (AD 1100- 1200). It is unlikely that any dwelling was occupied for more than thirty years, and not all were in use at the same time. The length of life for the people living on Unkar was not long, averaging thirty-four years.','The walk you are about to undertake passes the ruins of many ancestral Puebloan dwellings.',NULL,NULL,NULL,'No Dogs',36.0807780,-111.8774791,'Birding / River/Creek / Swimming / Views / Wildlife',NULL,'National Park Service - Grand Canyon National Park','http://www.nps.gov/grca/index.htm',NULL,'Easy',2719,2644,NULL,0.9,'Loop',NULL,102,82,0.04,0.15,NULL,NULL,'https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/gpx/7003300','8013242 | https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/7003300/unkar-delta',1,'2023-09-12 10:12:06','2023-09-19 06:00:30'),(246,'Walhalla Glades','8013242','hikingproject','7003304',2,'This former forest road runs north-south along the length of the Walhalla Glades area.  The lengthy route is surrounded on three sides by the Grand Canyon.  The geography of the peninsula also lends it a unique climate- warm updrafts from the canyon keep the area warmer than most others on the North Rim, allowing more hospitable living and growing conditions for ancient peoples.  \r\n\r\nFrom the pull off along Cape Royal Road, the Walhalla Glades Trail descends gradually over rolling grassy terrain.  It initially parallels the road before bending away through open fields of burned snags.  Soon, the Francois Matthes trail branches off to the right, heading southwest.  The Walhalla Glades Trail continues south through alternating open fields of burned pines and more wooded areas.  The Walhalla Connector junction is next, followed by a long stretch where the path makes its closest approach to the canyon rim.\r\n\r\nAfter another couple of miles, the trail ends near the southernmost point of the peninsula, across from Cape Royal and The Angels Window overlook area.','This out-and-back primitive trail is isolated and may be difficult to follow in places.',NULL,NULL,NULL,'No Dogs',36.2232292,-111.9657924,'Views',NULL,'National Park Service - Grand Canyon National Park','http://www.nps.gov/grca/index.htm',NULL,'Easy',8495,7914,NULL,7.9,'Point to Point',NULL,69,650,0.02,0.07,NULL,NULL,'https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/gpx/7003304','8013242 | https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/7003304/walhalla-glades',1,'2023-09-12 10:12:09','2023-09-19 06:00:30'),(247,'Walhalla Connector','8013242','hikingproject','7003306',2,NULL,'This is an east-west connector joining the Walhalla Glades, Francois Matthes and Ariel Point Trails.',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,36.1770311,-111.9528028,'',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'Intermediate',8246,8037,NULL,2.9,'Point to Point',NULL,342,403,0.05,0.17,NULL,NULL,'https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/gpx/7003306','8013242 | https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/7003306/walhalla-connector',1,'2023-09-12 10:12:11','2023-09-19 06:00:30'),(248,'Widforss Point Trail','8013242','hikingproject','7003309',2,'The Widforss Point Trail follows the canyon rim for approximately 2.5 miles/4 kilometers, then heads into the forest to emerge at Widforss Pointa distance of roughly 5 miles/8 kilometers one way (almost 10 miles/16 kilometers round trip).\n\nThis trail is easy to follow.  It travels along the rim above a dramatic tributary canyon that drains into Bright Angel Creek.  Then heads into a lovely forest of conifers and aspens.   Superb views of the Grand Canyon appear when reaching Widforss Point.','Stunning views of the North Rim after traveling through a lovely forest of fir, spruce, & aspen.',NULL,NULL,NULL,'No Dogs',36.2237437,-112.0650653,'Fall Colors / Views',NULL,'National Park Service - Grand Canyon National Park','http://www.nps.gov/grca/index.htm',NULL,'Intermediate',8267,7965,NULL,4.9,'Point to Point',NULL,495,628,0.04,0.21,NULL,NULL,'https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/gpx/7003309','8013242 | https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/7003309/widforss-point-trail',1,'2023-09-12 10:12:14','2023-09-19 06:00:30'),(249,'Uncle Jim Trail','8013242','hikingproject','7005751',2,'Family Friendly\n            \n                Pleasant and shaded loop with fantastic views.','This pleasant loop trail is shaded and easy, offering views of Bright Angel Canyon.',NULL,NULL,NULL,'No Dogs',36.2269975,-112.0490124,'Fall Colors / Views / Wildflowers / Wildlife',NULL,'National Park Service - Grand Canyon National Park','http://www.nps.gov/grca/index.htm',NULL,'Intermediate',8454,8256,NULL,2.6,'Point to Point',NULL,227,229,0.03,0.18,NULL,NULL,'https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/gpx/7005751','8013242 | https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/7005751/uncle-jim-trail',1,'2023-09-12 10:12:17','2023-09-19 06:00:30'),(250,'Lava Falls','8013242','hikingproject','7005911',2,'The park service does not recommend this extremely steep, unmaintained trail, but some folks brave it.  People report needing ice axes and good route finding skills.  This grueling slog through lots of loose scree and over shifting blocks of lava is treacherous because a false step can start a rock slide or launch you over a drop-off.  \r\n\r\nUpon reaching river level, the trail turns right to follow the bank where you\'ll find a few sandy beaches and a viewpoint.  The trail ends on cliffs above the famous Lava rapid where, if you\'re lucky, you\'ll see rafters caroming through.  There is no shade or water on this serious excursion so plan accordingly.  (River water must be filtered prior to drinking)\r\n\r\nThe Hiking Project mobile app may be useful for wayfinding but make sure your data is current and you device charged!','Intense and challenging or downright dangerous!',NULL,NULL,NULL,'No Dogs',36.2115029,-113.0835751,'River/Creek / Views',NULL,'National Park Service - Grand Canyon National Park','http://www.nps.gov/grca/index.htm',NULL,'Very Difficult',4178,1676,NULL,1.3,'Point to Point',NULL,16,2512,0.36,0.75,NULL,NULL,'https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/gpx/7005911','8013242 | https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/7005911/lava-falls',1,'2023-09-12 10:12:18','2023-09-19 06:00:30'),(251,'AZT - Passage 39 - Grand Canyon - North Rim','8013242','hikingproject','7015616',2,NULL,'Needs Adoption',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,36.2169676,-112.0566840,'',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'Easy',9140,8062,NULL,12.5,'Point to Point',NULL,1515,738,0.03,0.15,NULL,NULL,'https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/gpx/7015616','8013242 | https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/7015616/azt-passage-39-grand-canyon-north-rim',1,'2023-09-12 10:12:19','2023-09-19 06:00:30'),(252,'Rainbow Rim Trail #10','8013242','hikingproject','7017190',2,'The trail is located in a very remote area, the nearest services are many miles away, so be prepared for backcountry conditions. Because of the trail\'s remoteness, many riders will take advantage of the plentiful primitive camping at one of the five overlook points and spend a couple of days exploring the area.  Bring everything you might need including water and food, no services are available.  The forest roads out to the rainbow rim are ok (not great) for two-wheel drive cars.\r\n\r\nMany riders prefer the flow of the trail from north to south, but it rides well either way.  Setting up camp at Locust Point for example, which is the midpoint of the trial, enables you to divide up the trail as two separate out-and-back rides: 1) Locust Point south to Timp Point and back and, 2) Locust Point north to Parissawampitts Point and back.\r\n\r\nEven though it is purported that up to 90% of the Rainbow Rim\'s users are mountain bikers, hikers and horseback riders commonly use this trail as well.','A high elevation, remote, and iconic singletrack trail that hugs the North Rim of the Grand Canyon.',NULL,NULL,NULL,'Leashed',36.4187372,-112.3670160,'Views / Wildflowers / Wildlife',NULL,'USFS - Kaibab National Forest Office','http://www.fs.usda.gov/kaibab',NULL,'Intermediate',7807,7493,NULL,17.8,'Point to Point',NULL,1517,1491,0.03,0.14,NULL,NULL,'https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/gpx/7017190','8013242 | https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/7017190/rainbow-rim-trail-10',1,'2023-09-12 10:12:22','2023-09-19 06:00:30'),(253,'AZT - Passage 40 - Kaibab Plateau South','8013242','hikingproject','7028552',2,NULL,'Needs Adoption',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,36.3377404,-112.0933083,'',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'Easy',9008,8496,NULL,21.4,'Point to Point',NULL,1916,2069,0.04,0.17,NULL,NULL,'https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/gpx/7028552','8013242 | https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/7028552/azt-passage-40-kaibab-plateau-south',1,'2023-09-12 10:12:23','2023-09-19 06:00:30'),(254,'Granite Narrows Route','8013242','hikingproject','7040449',2,'The Granite Narrows Route is an unmaintained (but well-traveled and well-marked) path that connects Tapeats and Deer Creek. It allows for a complete loop of the Thunder River-Tapeats Creek-Deer Creek drainages, which makes for an outstanding multi-day adventure. The route is at the bottom of the canyon with southern exposure and can get quite hot; shade is minimal. Views along the trail are outstanding, and the unique perspective on the Colorado River\'s dramatic Granite Narrows section makes the route well-worth doing.\r\n\r\nGranite Narrows can be traveled from either direction and starts from the confluence of Tapeats Creek and the Colorado River on the east and just above the beginning of Deer Creek Narrows on the west. In general, the route-finding is straightforward and marked by cairns. Along the Colorado River, there is some rock-hopping involved, but more often, the route follows well-worn paths above the river. Two areas may cause some difficulty: 1) just east of Bonita Creek there is a section of steep and loose down-climbing (when headed from east to west) that can be challenging with a pack (a 20-foot hand-line would make this stretch easier); 2) a bit west of Bonita Creek the route splits to a high route and a lower route—the latter is shorter and with less vertical gain and loss, but also involves a 15 foot ledge traverse with considerable exposure. This traverse is not technically difficult and has stable hands and feet, but those not comfortable with exposure will want to take the high route.','An adventurous route that connects Tapeats and Deer Creeks through the Colorado River\'s Granite Narrows.',NULL,NULL,NULL,'Unknown',36.3709333,-112.4692353,'Commonly Backpacked / River/Creek / Views',NULL,'National Park Service - Grand Canyon National Park','http://www.nps.gov/grca/index.htm',NULL,'Difficult',2620,1972,NULL,3.0,'Point to Point',NULL,812,476,0.08,0.30,NULL,NULL,'https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/gpx/7040449','8013242 | https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/7040449/granite-narrows-route',1,'2023-09-12 10:12:26','2023-09-19 06:00:30'),(255,'Granite Narrows - High Route','8013242','hikingproject','7040450',2,'This is an alternative to the lower Granite Narrows Route; it is less direct and climbs farther above the river, but avoids the most exposed section of trail on the lower route.','A higher road through the Granite Narrows that avoids some exposure on the lower trail.',NULL,NULL,NULL,'Unknown',36.3805890,-112.4878124,'Commonly Backpacked / River/Creek / Views',NULL,'National Park Service - Grand Canyon National Park','http://www.nps.gov/grca/index.htm',NULL,'Intermediate/Difficult',2492,2077,NULL,1.4,'Point to Point',NULL,454,39,0.07,0.41,NULL,NULL,'https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/gpx/7040450','8013242 | https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/7040450/granite-narrows-high-route',1,'2023-09-12 10:12:29','2023-09-19 06:00:30'),(256,'Cedar Mountain Map',NULL,'vsu','13670',3,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'https://visitsouthernutah.com/post-trail-maps/cedar-mountain-map/',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'{\"id\":13670,\"date\":\"2023-05-17T13:00:17\",\"date_gmt\":\"2023-05-17T19:00:17\",\"guid\":{\"rendered\":\"https:\\/\\/visitsouthernutah.com\\/?post_type=trail_maps&#038;p=13670\"},\"modified\":\"2023-05-17T13:00:17\",\"modified_gmt\":\"2023-05-17T19:00:17\",\"slug\":\"cedar-mountain-map\",\"status\":\"publish\",\"type\":\"trail_maps\",\"link\":\"https:\\/\\/visitsouthernutah.com\\/post-trail-maps\\/cedar-mountain-map\\/\",\"title\":{\"rendered\":\"Cedar Mountain Map\"},\"content\":{\"rendered\":\"\",\"protected\":false},\"excerpt\":{\"rendered\":\"\",\"protected\":false},\"author\":8,\"featured_media\":13672,\"template\":\"\",\"difficulties\":[],\"trail_types\":[],\"yoast_head\":\"<!-- This site is optimized with the Yoast SEO plugin v18.6 - https:\\/\\/yoast.com\\/wordpress\\/plugins\\/seo\\/ -->\\n<title>Cedar Mountain Map | Visit Southern Utah<\\/title>\\n<meta name=\\\"robots\\\" content=\\\"index, follow, max-snippet:-1, 