being\\u201d<\\/li> <li>\\u201cThe Lighter Side of the Moon\\u201d<\\/li> <li>\\u201cThunder! Lightning! Very, Very Frightening\\u201d<\\/li> <li>\\\"The Geology of the Grand Staircase\\\"<\\/li> <li>Scary Stories to Tell in the Dark: Fire in Bryce Canyon\\\"<\\/li> <li>\\u201cHave a Bryce Day!\\u201d<\\/li> <li>\\u201cAdaptations in the Park\\u201d<\\/li> <\\/ul> <strong>A schedule of daily topics is available in the Visitor Center.<\\/strong>\",\"reservationDescription\":\"\",\"season\":[\"Summer\"],\"topics\":[{\"id\":\"0D00073E-18C3-46E5-8727-2F87B112DDC6\",\"name\":\"Animals\"},{\"id\":\"7F81A0CB-B91F-4896-B9A5-41BE9A54A27B\",\"name\":\"Archeology\"},{\"id\":\"BCE3ACBE-7871-495B-AEA3-5E19BC482405\",\"name\":\"Canyons and Canyonlands\"},{\"id\":\"4DC11D06-00F1-4A01-81D0-89CCCCE4FF50\",\"name\":\"Climate Change\"},{\"id\":\"04A39AB8-DD02-432F-AE5F-BA1267D41A0D\",\"name\":\"Fire\"},{\"id\":\"A266302A-E2AE-4671-8515-B3614D8EF37F\",\"name\":\"Plateaus and Mesas\"},{\"id\":\"41B1A0A3-11FF-4F55-9CB9-034A7E28B087\",\"name\":\"Forests and Woodlands\"},{\"id\":\"762170E5-0747-4836-B837-7F2547D3F733\",\"name\":\"Coniferous Forests\"},{\"id\":\"DE2F0F3C-C0C4-410F-90D3-97EEC33D348E\",\"name\":\"Deciduous Forests\"},{\"id\":\"F6D3A52E-608F-47D6-96DF-1FD64122A2FC\",\"name\":\"Fossils and Paleontology\"},{\"id\":\"F0F97E32-2F29-41B4-AF98-9FBE8DAB36B1\",\"name\":\"Geology\"},{\"id\":\"4BE01DC5-52E6-4F18-8C9A-B22D65965F6D\",\"name\":\"Groundwater\"},{\"id\":\"A1BAF33E-EA84-4608-A888-4CEE9541F027\",\"name\":\"Native American Heritage\"},{\"id\":\"F8C2FE93-DEB3-4B12-9A87-913E3E6B448D\",\"name\":\"Natural Sounds\"},{\"id\":\"A7359FC4-DAD8-45F5-AF15-7FF62F816ED3\",\"name\":\"Night Sky\"},{\"id\":\"E06F3C94-AC8A-4B1C-A247-8EBA8910D5EE\",\"name\":\"Astronomy\"},{\"id\":\"4244F489-6813-4B7A-9D0C-20CE098C8FFF\",\"name\":\"Rock Landscapes and Features\"}],\"durationDescription\":\"\",\"arePetsPermitted\":\"false\",\"timeOfDay\":[\"Dusk\"],\"duration\":\"45-60 Minutes\",\"credit\":\"\"}',1,'2023-09-10 21:06:03','2023-09-10 21:06:32'),(339,'Hoodoo Geology Talk','Sunset Point','nps','96AA9939-AC7B-4AAA-9B4D-107657189FB3',1,'\\r\\n\\r\\nThis is a ranger talk that does not include any walking. It is on a paved surface along the rim of the Bryce Canyon Amphitheater.','Be sure to always check the Calendar or stop by the Visitor Center to find the programs that will be available during your visit.  Typically offered year-round at Sunset Point at 11 a.m.   Have your Bryce moment! Most people experience the view from Sunset Point as a breath-taking event, and for good reason. The amphitheaters filled with mazes of magical hoodoos may prompt you to ask…when…where…why…how?  We have some of the answers! Geologists have spent years studying the unique geologic story of Bryce Canyon and its amphitheaters of hoodoos. Join the Ranger along the rim at Sunset Point to explore the evolution of the hoodoos and the groups of people who have passed through this magical terrain.  Handicap accessible.',NULL,'This talk is accessible. It takes place along the rim and does not require any walking. This talk is standing room only and no seating is available. Please bring your own seating as needed.','20-30 Minutes','1',37.6225683,-112.1659877,NULL,'https://www.nps.gov/thingstodo/hoodoo-geology-talk.htm',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,1,NULL,'{\"id\":\"96AA9939-AC7B-4AAA-9B4D-107657189FB3\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/thingstodo\\/hoodoo-geology-talk.htm\",\"title\":\"Hoodoo Geology Talk\",\"shortDescription\":\"Typically offered year-round at Sunset Point at 11 a.m. Geologists have spent years studying the unique geologic story of Bryce Canyon and its amphitheaters of hoodoos. Join the Ranger along\\u00a0the rim at\\u00a0Sunset Point to explore the evolution of the hoodoos and\\u00a0the groups of people who have passed through this magical terrain.\\u00a0\",\"images\":[{\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/B6DF22C8-EDF1-6712-2C0123D10CDFA1CC.jpg\",\"credit\":\"NPS Photo\",\"altText\":\"A ranger in uniform stands in front of a crowd with a background of red rock formations\",\"title\":\"Hoodoo Geology Talk\",\"description\":\"\",\"caption\":\"Ranger Anne gives a Hoodoo Geology Talk along the rim at Sunset Point.\",\"crops\":[{\"aspectRatio\":\"1.78\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/primary\\/B6DF22C8-EDF1-6712-2C0123D10CDFA1CC.jpg\"},{\"aspectRatio\":\"1\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/secondary\\/B6DF22C8-EDF1-6712-2C0123D10CDFA1CC.jpg\"}]}],\"relatedParks\":[{\"states\":\"UT\",\"parkCode\":\"brca\",\"designation\":\"National Park\",\"fullName\":\"Bryce Canyon National Park\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/brca\\/index.htm\",\"name\":\"Bryce Canyon\"}],\"relatedOrganizations\":[],\"tags\":[\"geology\",\"Bryce Canyon\",\"talks\",\"Hoodoo\"],\"latitude\":\"37.622568295798914\",\"longitude\":\"-112.16598767830192\",\"geometryPoiId\":\"1f535e3a-21f7-4fdf-8632-d26df99149b0\",\"amenities\":[],\"location\":\"Sunset Point\",\"seasonDescription\":\"\",\"accessibilityInformation\":\"This talk is accessible. It takes place along the rim and does not require any walking. This talk is standing room only and no seating is available. Please bring your own seating as needed.\",\"isReservationRequired\":\"false\",\"ageDescription\":\"\",\"petsDescription\":\"Pets are allowed on paved surfaces only.\",\"timeOfDayDescription\":\"Daily at 11:00 a.m.\",\"feeDescription\":\"\",\"age\":\"\",\"arePetsPermittedWithRestrictions\":\"false\",\"activities\":[{\"id\":\"B33DC9B6-0B7D-4322-BAD7-A13A34C584A3\",\"name\":\"Guided Tours\"}],\"activityDescription\":\"This is a ranger talk that does not include any walking. It is on a paved surface along the rim of the Bryce Canyon Amphitheater.\",\"locationDescription\":\"\",\"doFeesApply\":\"false\",\"longDescription\":\"<p><strong>Be sure to always check the\\u00a0<a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/brca\\/planyourvisit\\/calendar.htm\\\">Calendar\\u00a0<\\/a>or stop by the\\u00a0<a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/places\\/000\\/visitor-center.htm\\\">Visitor Center<\\/a>\\u00a0to find the programs that will be available during your visit.<\\/strong><br \\/> <br \\/> Typically offered year-round at Sunset Point at 11 a.m.\\u00a0<br \\/> <br \\/> Have your Bryce moment! Most people experience the view from Sunset Point as a breath-taking event, and for good reason. The amphitheaters filled with mazes of magical hoodoos may prompt you to ask\\u2026when\\u2026where\\u2026why\\u2026how?<br \\/> <br \\/> We have some of the answers! Geologists have spent years studying the unique geologic story of Bryce Canyon and its amphitheaters of hoodoos. Join the Ranger along\\u00a0the rim at\\u00a0Sunset Point to explore the evolution of the hoodoos and\\u00a0the groups of people who have passed through this magical terrain.\\u00a0<\\/p> <p>Handicap accessible.<\\/p>\",\"reservationDescription\":\"\",\"season\":[],\"topics\":[{\"id\":\"FB3641FE-67A3-4EC7-B9C4-0A0867776798\",\"name\":\"Ancient Seas\"},{\"id\":\"7F81A0CB-B91F-4896-B9A5-41BE9A54A27B\",\"name\":\"Archeology\"},{\"id\":\"BCE3ACBE-7871-495B-AEA3-5E19BC482405\",\"name\":\"Canyons and Canyonlands\"},{\"id\":\"A266302A-E2AE-4671-8515-B3614D8EF37F\",\"name\":\"Plateaus and Mesas\"},{\"id\":\"F6D3A52E-608F-47D6-96DF-1FD64122A2FC\",\"name\":\"Fossils and Paleontology\"},{\"id\":\"F0F97E32-2F29-41B4-AF98-9FBE8DAB36B1\",\"name\":\"Geology\"},{\"id\":\"4244F489-6813-4B7A-9D0C-20CE098C8FFF\",\"name\":\"Rock Landscapes and Features\"},{\"id\":\"9C9FCBB6-360B-4743-8155-6F9341CBE01B\",\"name\":\"Scenic Views\"}],\"durationDescription\":\"\",\"arePetsPermitted\":\"true\",\"timeOfDay\":[\"Day\"],\"duration\":\"20-30 Minutes\",\"credit\":\"\"}',1,'2023-09-10 21:06:03','2023-09-10 21:06:32'),(340,'Snowshoe Hikes','Visitor Center','nps','C6784E60-3812-4414-8995-9D69A23B6C5B',1,'Program location TBD. Sign-up will be offered at the Visitor Center the day of the program beginning at 8 a.m. and continuing until full. No advance sign-up or reservations are available.\\r\\n\\r\\nApproximately 1 mile (1.6 km) hike. ','Be sure to always check the Calendar or stop by the Visitor Center to find the programs that will be available during your visit.  The stark white of freshly fallen snow, red rocks, blue sky, and evergreen trees--some say Bryce Canyon is even more beautiful in winter! Here at 8,000 feet (2,438 m) the scenery changes dramatically in the colder months.  Come experience this magical season with a ranger-led snowshoe hike! These hikes are designed for all levels of experience, from beginner to expert. Snowshoes and poles are available for free for those who sign up for these programs, which are provided when snow depth and staffing permit.  Winter clothing, water, and waterproof boots are required. Snowshoes and poles will be provided. Tennis shoes will not be permitted.',NULL,'Due to the nature of this activity, this hike is not ADA accessible.','90-120 Minutes','1',37.5951640,-112.1711922,NULL,'https://www.nps.gov/thingstodo/snowshoe-hikes.htm',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,1,NULL,'{\"id\":\"C6784E60-3812-4414-8995-9D69A23B6C5B\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/thingstodo\\/snowshoe-hikes.htm\",\"title\":\"Snowshoe Hikes\",\"shortDescription\":\"Offered in winter as weather and snow conditions permit. Here at 8,000 feet (2,438 m) the scenery changes dramatically in the colder months. Come experience this magical season with a ranger-led snowshoe hike! These hikes are designed for all levels of experience, from beginner to expert.\",\"images\":[{\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/EBCF1211-96A8-7FE4-578E4A76C915E62B.jpg\",\"credit\":\"NPS Photo\",\"altText\":\"A man in uniform on snowshoes points out a view to a woman on showshoes.\",\"title\":\"Snowshoe Hikes\",\"description\":\"\",\"caption\":\"Snowshoe hikes\",\"crops\":[{\"aspectRatio\":\"1.78\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/primary\\/EBCF1211-96A8-7FE4-578E4A76C915E62B.jpg\"},{\"aspectRatio\":\"1\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/secondary\\/EBCF1211-96A8-7FE4-578E4A76C915E62B.jpg\"}]}],\"relatedParks\":[{\"states\":\"UT\",\"parkCode\":\"brca\",\"designation\":\"National Park\",\"fullName\":\"Bryce Canyon National Park\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/brca\\/index.htm\",\"name\":\"Bryce Canyon\"}],\"relatedOrganizations\":[],\"tags\":[\"snowshoe hike\",\"hiking\",\"winter\",\"snow\",\"Bryce Canyon National Park\"],\"latitude\":\"37.595163972108544\",\"longitude\":\"-112.17119224841173\",\"geometryPoiId\":\"72604fed-5c67-45ff-8312-ff73452608a1\",\"amenities\":[],\"location\":\"Visitor Center\",\"seasonDescription\":\"\",\"accessibilityInformation\":\"Due to the nature of this activity, this hike is not ADA accessible.\",\"isReservationRequired\":\"true\",\"ageDescription\":\"Children must be 8 years old or over to participate in the program.\",\"petsDescription\":\"\",\"timeOfDayDescription\":\"\",\"feeDescription\":\"\",\"age\":\"8\",\"arePetsPermittedWithRestrictions\":\"false\",\"activities\":[{\"id\":\"01D717BC-18BB-4FE4-95BA-6B13AD702038\",\"name\":\"Snowshoeing\"}],\"activityDescription\":\"Approximately 1 mile (1.6 km) hike.\\u00a0\",\"locationDescription\":\"Program location TBD. Sign-up will be offered at the Visitor Center the day of the program beginning at 8 a.m. and continuing until full. No advance sign-up or reservations are available.\",\"doFeesApply\":\"false\",\"longDescription\":\"<strong>Be sure to always check the\\u00a0<a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/brca\\/planyourvisit\\/calendar.htm\\\">Calendar\\u00a0<\\/a>or stop by the\\u00a0<a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/places\\/000\\/visitor-center.htm\\\">Visitor Center<\\/a>\\u00a0to find the programs that will be available during your visit.<\\/strong><br \\/> <br \\/> The stark white of freshly fallen snow, red rocks, blue sky, and evergreen trees--some say Bryce Canyon is even more beautiful in winter! Here at 8,000 feet (2,438 m) the scenery changes dramatically in the colder months.<br \\/> <br \\/> Come experience this magical season with a ranger-led snowshoe hike! These hikes are designed for all levels of experience, from beginner to expert. Snowshoes and poles are available for free for those who sign up for these programs, which are provided when snow depth and staffing permit.<br \\/> <br \\/> Winter clothing, water, and waterproof boots are required. Snowshoes and poles will be provided. Tennis shoes will not be permitted.\",\"reservationDescription\":\"Registration is required as group size is limited and hikes are subject to cancellation.<br \\/> <br \\/> When snow depth and weather permit, <strong>sign-up will be offered at the Visitor Center the day of the program beginning at 8 a.m.<\\/strong> and continuing until full. No advance sign-up or reservations are available.\",\"season\":[\"Winter\"],\"topics\":[{\"id\":\"0D00073E-18C3-46E5-8727-2F87B112DDC6\",\"name\":\"Animals\"},{\"id\":\"BCE3ACBE-7871-495B-AEA3-5E19BC482405\",\"name\":\"Canyons and Canyonlands\"},{\"id\":\"4DC11D06-00F1-4A01-81D0-89CCCCE4FF50\",\"name\":\"Climate Change\"},{\"id\":\"A266302A-E2AE-4671-8515-B3614D8EF37F\",\"name\":\"Plateaus and Mesas\"},{\"id\":\"41B1A0A3-11FF-4F55-9CB9-034A7E28B087\",\"name\":\"Forests and Woodlands\"},{\"id\":\"F0F97E32-2F29-41B4-AF98-9FBE8DAB36B1\",\"name\":\"Geology\"},{\"id\":\"4244F489-6813-4B7A-9D0C-20CE098C8FFF\",\"name\":\"Rock Landscapes and Features\"},{\"id\":\"9C9FCBB6-360B-4743-8155-6F9341CBE01B\",\"name\":\"Scenic Views\"},{\"id\":\"5BE55D7F-BDB6-4E3D-AC35-2D8EBB974417\",\"name\":\"Trails\"}],\"durationDescription\":\"Programs generally last 2 hours.\",\"arePetsPermitted\":\"false\",\"timeOfDay\":[\"Day\"],\"duration\":\"90-120 Minutes\",\"credit\":\"\"}',1,'2023-09-10 21:06:03','2023-09-10 21:06:32'),(341,'Plan a Picnic','CCC Picnic Area','nps','F8EFD5F3-A14B-4C0D-B448-8D22C2E2EB84',1,'The CCC Picnic Area is located at mile 4.75 of the 18 mile Southern Scenic Drive.\\r\\n\\r\\n','Looking for a good place to enjoy a picnic? See below for a list of Bryce Canyon picnic areas. Amphitheater Area (Miles 1-3)  North Campground Picnic Area General Store  Sunset Point  Southern Scenic Drive (Miles 4-18)  CCC Picnic Area Whiteman Bench Picnic Area Rainbow Point  Outside the Park  Bryce Canyon Shuttle Station (Bryce Canyon City, north of the park)   It is a violation of federal law to feed any wildlife in the park and surrounding areas. Be mindful that some of these animals may be seen on or near the roads and trails and should not be approached as they may be dangerous.',NULL,'All picnic areas are unpaved and composed of firm native sediment. While native sediment is generally hard packed, it may be muddy and difficult to navigate after rain or snow. Picnic areas are all adjacent to paved sidewalks or parking areas.',NULL,'1',37.6066939,-112.2004849,NULL,'https://www.nps.gov/thingstodo/plan-a-picnic.htm',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,1,NULL,'{\"id\":\"F8EFD5F3-A14B-4C0D-B448-8D22C2E2EB84\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/thingstodo\\/plan-a-picnic.htm\",\"title\":\"Plan a Picnic\",\"shortDescription\":\"Looking for a good place to enjoy a picnic? Here is a list of Bryce Canyon picnic areas.\",\"images\":[{\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/68DCAD29-9364-E051-10D2D7A897BA87F4.jpg\",\"credit\":\"NPS Photo\",\"altText\":\"A raven sits atop a brown sign that says Rainbow Point Elevation 9115.\",\"title\":\"Rainbow Point\",\"description\":\"\",\"caption\":\"Rainbow Point, at the end of the Southern Scenic Drive, is a great place to stop for a picnic.\",\"crops\":[{\"aspectRatio\":\"1.78\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/primary\\/68DCAD29-9364-E051-10D2D7A897BA87F4.jpg\"},{\"aspectRatio\":\"1\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/secondary\\/68DCAD29-9364-E051-10D2D7A897BA87F4.jpg\"}]}],\"relatedParks\":[{\"states\":\"UT\",\"parkCode\":\"brca\",\"designation\":\"National Park\",\"fullName\":\"Bryce Canyon National Park\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/brca\\/index.htm\",\"name\":\"Bryce Canyon\"}],\"relatedOrganizations\":[],\"tags\":[\"picnic\",\"picnic area\",\"Bryce Canyon National Park\"],\"latitude\":\"37.60669393153801\",\"longitude\":\"-112.20048488943571\",\"geometryPoiId\":\"fb9662ed-d887-46e4-8973-356d60f715a8\",\"amenities\":[],\"location\":\"CCC Picnic Area\",\"seasonDescription\":\"\",\"accessibilityInformation\":\"All picnic areas are unpaved and composed of firm native sediment. While native sediment is generally hard packed, it may be muddy and difficult to navigate after rain or snow. Picnic areas are all adjacent to paved sidewalks or parking areas.\",\"isReservationRequired\":\"false\",\"ageDescription\":\"\",\"petsDescription\":\"Pets are allowed on paved surfaces.\\u00a0\",\"timeOfDayDescription\":\"\",\"feeDescription\":\"\",\"age\":\"\",\"arePetsPermittedWithRestrictions\":\"false\",\"activities\":[{\"id\":\"C6D3230A-2CEA-4AFE-BFF3-DC1E2C2C4BB4\",\"name\":\"Picnicking\"}],\"activityDescription\":\"\",\"locationDescription\":\"The CCC Picnic Area is located at mile 4.75 of the 18 mile Southern Scenic Drive.\",\"doFeesApply\":\"false\",\"longDescription\":\"<p>Looking for a good place to enjoy a picnic? See below\\u00a0for a list of Bryce Canyon picnic areas.<\\/p> <h4>Amphitheater Area (Miles 1-3)<\\/h4> <ul> <li><a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/places\\/000\\/north-campground-picnic-area.htm\\\">North Campground Picnic Area<\\/a><\\/li> <li><a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/places\\/000\\/bryce-canyon-general-store.htm\\\">General Store<\\/a>\\u00a0<\\/li> <li><a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/places\\/000\\/sunset-point.htm\\\">Sunset Point<\\/a><\\/li> <\\/ul> <h4>Southern Scenic Drive (Miles 4-18)<\\/h4> <ul> <li><a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/places\\/000\\/ccc-picnic-area.htm\\\">CCC Picnic Area<\\/a><\\/li> <li><a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/places\\/000\\/whiteman-bench-picnic-area.htm\\\">Whiteman Bench Picnic Area<\\/a><\\/li> <li><a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/places\\/000\\/rainbow-point.htm\\\">Rainbow Point<\\/a><\\/li> <\\/ul> <h4>Outside the Park<\\/h4> <ul> <li><a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/places\\/000\\/bryce-canyon-shuttle-station.htm\\\">Bryce Canyon Shuttle Station<\\/a>\\u00a0(Bryce Canyon City, north of the park)<\\/li> <\\/ul> <br \\/> <strong>It is a violation of federal law to\\u00a0<a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/brca\\/learn\\/nature\\/feedingwildlife.htm\\\">feed any wildlife<\\/a>\\u00a0in the park and surrounding areas. Be mindful that some of these animals may be seen on or near the roads and trails and should not be approached as they may be dangerous.<\\/strong>\",\"reservationDescription\":\"\",\"season\":[],\"topics\":[{\"id\":\"9C9FCBB6-360B-4743-8155-6F9341CBE01B\",\"name\":\"Scenic Views\"}],\"durationDescription\":\"\",\"arePetsPermitted\":\"true\",\"timeOfDay\":[],\"duration\":\"\",\"credit\":\"\"}',1,'2023-09-10 21:06:03','2023-09-10 21:06:32'),(342,'Hat Shop','Hat Shop Trailhead','nps','C8E47D3E-0F92-473F-8720-4AE2733CD3D7',1,'Peekaboo Loop, Hat Shop, and the Under-the-Rim trail can all be accessed from this trailhead at Bryce Point.\\r\\n\\r\\n','The Hat Shop trail descends from Bryce Point into the backcountry using the Under-the-Rim trail, offering views of the colorful cliffs of the Grand Staircase. This strenuous out-and-back hike leads down 1,380 feet (421 meters) over two-miles to a cluster of balanced-rock hoodoos before hikers turn around to return to Bryce Point. These narrow, orange pinnacles wear \"hats\" of large gray boulders, giving them a distinct appearance compared to hoodoos seen in the Bryce Amphitheater to the north.     Trailhead: Bryce Point Parking: At Bryce Point; oversized vehicles are not permitted in this parking area when the shuttle is in operation. Distance: 4.0 miles (6.4 km) Approximate Time: 3-4 hours Elevation Gain: 1,380 feet (421 meters) Difficulty: Strenuous (rapid elevation gain, longer length) Services: None. Shuttle Service: Yes.  During winter, the Hat Shop trail is not advised unless you are very well prepared for ice, deep snow, and other winter conditions. Pets are not allowed on this trail. Service animals are allowed in national parks. What is a service animal?',NULL,'Due to the park\'s natural terrain, this is not considered an accessible trail.   Surface Type: Firm Native Sediment Total Ascent: 1380 feet (421 meters) Length: 4.0 mi (6.4km) roundtrip Typical Grade: 13.0% Maximum Grade: 29.0% Typical Cross Slope: 5% Maximum Cross Slope: 20% Typical Width: 37 inches (94 cm) Minimum Width: 36 inches (cm)  Service animals are allowed in national parks. What is a service animal?','3-4 Hours','1',37.6043349,-112.1568900,NULL,'https://www.nps.gov/thingstodo/hat-shop.htm',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,1,NULL,'{\"id\":\"C8E47D3E-0F92-473F-8720-4AE2733CD3D7\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/thingstodo\\/hat-shop.htm\",\"title\":\"Hat Shop\",\"shortDescription\":\"The Hat Shop trail descends from Bryce Point into the backcountry using the Under-the-Rim trail, offering views of the colorful cliffs of the Grand Staircase. This strenuous out-and-back hike leads down 1,380 feet (421 meters) over two-miles to a cluster of balanced-rock hoodoos before hikers turn around to return to Bryce Point.\\u00a0These narrow, orange pinnacles wear \\\"hats\\\" of large gray boulders, giving them a distinct appearance.\",\"images\":[{\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/092C5324-C49A-E4AB-170044A6F944FC01.jpg\",\"credit\":\"\",\"altText\":\"A landscape of red rocks in the foreground with hazy mountains on the horizon\",\"title\":\"Hat Shop Overlook\",\"description\":\"\",\"caption\":\"Views along the Hat Shop trail.\",\"crops\":[{\"aspectRatio\":\"1.78\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/primary\\/092C5324-C49A-E4AB-170044A6F944FC01.jpg\"},{\"aspectRatio\":\"1\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/secondary\\/092C5324-C49A-E4AB-170044A6F944FC01.jpg\"}]}],\"relatedParks\":[{\"states\":\"UT\",\"parkCode\":\"brca\",\"designation\":\"National Park\",\"fullName\":\"Bryce Canyon National Park\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/brca\\/index.htm\",\"name\":\"Bryce Canyon\"}],\"relatedOrganizations\":[],\"tags\":[\"Bryce Canyon National Park\",\"geology\",\"hike\",\"hiking\",\"Hat Shop Trail\",\"strenuous hike\",\"backcountry\",\"Under the Rim Trail\",\"wilderness\",\"viewpoint\"],\"latitude\":\"37.60433494119063\",\"longitude\":\"-112.15688997255705\",\"geometryPoiId\":\"d8d1a7b0-0979-5c23-9ad4-65ec66ec2af1\",\"amenities\":[],\"location\":\"Hat Shop Trailhead\",\"seasonDescription\":\"\",\"accessibilityInformation\":\"<b>Due to the park\'s\\u00a0natural terrain, this is not considered an accessible trail.\\u00a0<\\/b> <ul> <li><b>Surface Type:\\u00a0<\\/b>Firm Native Sediment<\\/li> <li><b>Total Ascent:\\u00a0<\\/b>1380 feet (421 meters)<\\/li> <li><b>Length:\\u00a0<\\/b>4.0 mi (6.4km) roundtrip<\\/li> <li><b>Typical Grade: <\\/b>13.0%<\\/li> <li><b>Maximum Grade:\\u00a0<\\/b>29.0%<\\/li> <li><b>Typical Cross Slope: <\\/b>5%<\\/li> <li><b>Maximum Cross Slope: <\\/b>20%<\\/li> <li><b>Typical Width<\\/b>: 37\\u00a0inches (94 cm)<\\/li> <li><b>Minimum Width: <\\/b>36\\u00a0inches (cm)<\\/li> <\\/ul> Service animals are allowed in national parks.\\u00a0<a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/cms.nps.gov\\/planyourvisit\\/service-animals.htm\\\">What is a service animal?<\\/a>\",\"isReservationRequired\":\"false\",\"ageDescription\":\"\",\"petsDescription\":\"\",\"timeOfDayDescription\":\"\",\"feeDescription\":\"\",\"age\":\"\",\"arePetsPermittedWithRestrictions\":\"false\",\"activities\":[{\"id\":\"7C37B79B-D02D-49EB-9020-3DB8299B748A\",\"name\":\"Backcountry Hiking\"}],\"activityDescription\":\"\",\"locationDescription\":\"Peekaboo Loop, Hat Shop, and the Under-the-Rim trail can all be accessed from this trailhead at Bryce Point.\",\"doFeesApply\":\"false\",\"longDescription\":\"The Hat Shop trail descends from Bryce Point into the backcountry using the Under-the-Rim trail, offering views of the colorful cliffs of the Grand Staircase. This strenuous out-and-back hike leads down 1,380 feet (421 meters) over two-miles to a cluster of balanced-rock hoodoos before hikers turn around to return to Bryce Point.\\u00a0These narrow, orange pinnacles wear \\\"hats\\\" of large gray boulders, giving them a distinct appearance compared to hoodoos seen in the Bryce Amphitheater to the north.\\u00a0<br \\/> \\u00a0 <ul> <li><b>Trailhead: <\\/b><a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/places\\/000\\/bryce-point.htm\\\">Bryce Point<\\/a><\\/li> <li><b>Parking:<\\/b> At Bryce Point; oversized vehicles are not\\u00a0permitted in this\\u00a0parking area\\u00a0<a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/brca\\/planyourvisit\\/shuttle.htm\\\">when the shuttle is\\u00a0in operation.<\\/a><\\/li> <li><b>Distance:<\\/b>\\u00a04.0 miles (6.4\\u00a0km)<\\/li> <li><b>Approximate Time:<\\/b>\\u00a03-4\\u00a0hours<\\/li> <li><b>Elevation Gain:<\\/b>\\u00a01,380 feet (421 meters)<\\/li> <li><b>Difficulty:<\\/b>\\u00a0Strenuous (rapid elevation gain, longer length)<\\/li> <li><b>Services:\\u00a0<\\/b>None.<\\/li> <li><b>Shuttle Service:<\\/b>\\u00a0<a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/brca\\/planyourvisit\\/shuttle.htm\\\">Yes.\\u00a0<\\/a><\\/li> <li>During winter, the Hat Shop trail is not advised unless you are very well prepared for ice, deep snow, and other winter conditions.<\\/li> <li><strong>Pets are not allowed on this trail.<\\/strong> Service animals are allowed in national parks. <a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/planyourvisit\\/service-animals.htm\\\">What is a service animal?<\\/a><\\/li> <\\/ul>\",\"reservationDescription\":\"\",\"season\":[],\"topics\":[{\"id\":\"BCE3ACBE-7871-495B-AEA3-5E19BC482405\",\"name\":\"Canyons and Canyonlands\"},{\"id\":\"A266302A-E2AE-4671-8515-B3614D8EF37F\",\"name\":\"Plateaus and Mesas\"},{\"id\":\"F0F97E32-2F29-41B4-AF98-9FBE8DAB36B1\",\"name\":\"Geology\"},{\"id\":\"4244F489-6813-4B7A-9D0C-20CE098C8FFF\",\"name\":\"Rock Landscapes and Features\"},{\"id\":\"9C9FCBB6-360B-4743-8155-6F9341CBE01B\",\"name\":\"Scenic Views\"},{\"id\":\"B85866E2-0897-4000-9040-605CA335804F\",\"name\":\"Wilderness\"}],\"durationDescription\":\"\",\"arePetsPermitted\":\"false\",\"timeOfDay\":[],\"duration\":\"3-4 Hours\",\"credit\":\"\"}',1,'2023-09-10 21:06:03','2023-09-10 21:06:32'),(343,'Drive the Southern Scenic Drive',NULL,'nps','0847C602-A853-48F0-A394-67A12607DEDA',1,'\\r\\n\\r\\n<p>This trip provides opportunities to see 9 unique viewpoints along the southern, higher elevation stretches of the main park road. These viewpoints not only overlook unique geologic features, but the grand landscape of the Paria Valley and the Grand Staircase beyond.</p>','Check out the Park App\'s \"Tours\" section for a guided walkthrough of how to take a driving tour of this area.  The main park road is 18 miles (29 km) from the park entrance in the north to the end of the road at Rainbow at Yovimpa Points. While the Bryce Amphitheater area is found along its first 3 miles (4.8 km), the next 15 miles (24 km) to the road\'s end are known as the Southern Scenic Drive.   Along this section of the main road are 9 scenic overlooks that display a lesser-seen beauty of Bryce Canyon. One aspect that makes the Southern Scenic Drive unique is that the park\'s elevations increase the further south you travel. While the Visitor Center is situated at 7,894 ft (2,406 m), Rainbow and Yovimpa Points exceed 9,000 ft (2,743 m). This change in elevation is the result uneven tectonic forces that uplifted this region over the last 20 million years, and creates a dramatic contrast--with exceptionally long views and unique features--between the north and south ends of the park. Winter Closures The high elevations along this section of the main road also lead to temporary winter closures at the Mile 3 gate, so be sure to visit the Conditions page for the latest road status. Planning Your Drive It takes about 40 minutes to drive to the end of the Southern Scenic Drive from the Visitor Center. It is recommended that you begin by first doing this all the way to Rainbow and Yovimpa Points. After enjoying these viewpoints, those you will have passed along the road can be visited on the way back. This way, everything will be on the right-hand side of the road; much safer than pulling across traffic everytime you want to see a viewpoint. Typically a trip of only driving and viewpoints will take around 2-3 hours. Highlights It is easy to enjoy all the views along the road, as none of them require much walking to reach. That said, most visitors would likely agree that Rainbow Point, Yovimpa Point, Agua Canyon and Natural Bridge are not to be missed.  As you drive south and gain elevation, you\'ll notice the park\'s forest community changing from ponderosa forest to a mixture of colorful quaking aspen and denser stands of white fir and Douglas-fir. Wildlife Pull-outs and viewpoints along the Southern Scenic Drive also give opportunities for wildlife watching. Species seen further south in the park may include elk, black bears, mule deer, birds associated with mixed-conifer forests (such as Clark\'s nutcrackers and dusky grouse) and high-elevation cliffs (such as peregine falcons). Walks and Hikes All the viewpoints along the Southern Scenic Drive are located a very short walk from their parking lots, so no hiking is required to enjoy this trip. That said, very little can be seen from the car, so you will want to walk the short distances to the railings to enjoy the view. There are two popular day hikes located along this drive: the easy Bristlecone Loop at Rainbow and Yovimpa Points (usually takes about an hour to hike) and the Swamp Canyon Loop at Swamp Canyon overlook (often takes 3 to 4 hours).  Backcountry hiking options also exist, with the Under-the-Rim Trail running approximately 22.9 miles (36.9 km) from Bryce Point to Rainbow Point. Connecting trails offer access from along the Southern Scenic Drive and intersect the Under-the-Rim Trail at Swamp Canyon, and Whiteman Bench. The Riggs Spring Loop is an 8.6 mile (13.9 km) hike beginning and ending at the Rainbow and Yovimpa Point parking lot at the south end of the Scenic Drive. Either the Yovimpa or Rainbow Point trailhead may be used to access this loop. A backcountry hiking map with mileage is available at the Visitor Center. More information',NULL,'All viewpoints along the Southern Scenic Drive are paved and provide relatively flat access to the views. The exception to this is Piracy Point, which is reached via a flat 1,000 foot (39m) unpaved path that provides good access when dry. Visit our Accessibility page for more information.','2-12 Hours','1',NULL,NULL,NULL,'https://www.nps.gov/thingstodo/drive-the-southern-scenic-drive.htm',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,1,NULL,'{\"id\":\"0847C602-A853-48F0-A394-67A12607DEDA\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/thingstodo\\/drive-the-southern-scenic-drive.htm\",\"title\":\"Drive the Southern Scenic Drive\",\"shortDescription\":\"The main park road is 18 miles (29\\u00a0km) from the park entrance in the north to the end of the road at Rainbow at Yovimpa Points. While the Bryce Amphitheater area is found along its first 3 miles (4.8 km), the next 15 miles (24 km) to the road\'s end are known as the Southern Scenic Drive.\\u00a0Along this section of the main road are 9 scenic overlooks and vehicle pullouts that offer opportunities to see the lesser-seen\\u00a0geology and wildlife of Bryce Canyon.\",\"images\":[{\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/8644FCF8-E1C9-4C45-6CDE52E153F53894.jpg\",\"credit\":\"NPS Photo \\/ Peter Densmore\",\"altText\":\"A large red rock limestone arch stands along a slope above forested cliffs\",\"title\":\"Natural Bridge\",\"description\":\"\",\"caption\":\"Natural Bridge is one of nine viewpoints along the Southern Scenic Drive\",\"crops\":[{\"aspectRatio\":\"1.78\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/primary\\/8644FCF8-E1C9-4C45-6CDE52E153F53894.jpg\"},{\"aspectRatio\":\"1\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/secondary\\/8644FCF8-E1C9-4C45-6CDE52E153F53894.jpg\"}]}],\"relatedParks\":[{\"states\":\"UT\",\"parkCode\":\"brca\",\"designation\":\"National Park\",\"fullName\":\"Bryce Canyon National Park\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/brca\\/index.htm\",\"name\":\"Bryce Canyon\"}],\"relatedOrganizations\":[],\"tags\":[\"Southern Scenic Drive\",\"Bryce Canyon National Park\",\"driving\",\"Scenic Drive\"],\"latitude\":\"\",\"longitude\":\"\",\"geometryPoiId\":\"\",\"amenities\":[],\"location\":\"\",\"seasonDescription\":\"\",\"accessibilityInformation\":\"<p>All viewpoints along the Southern Scenic Drive are paved and provide relatively flat access to the views. The exception to this is Piracy Point, which is reached via a flat 1,000 foot (39m) unpaved path that provides good access when dry. Visit our <a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/brca\\/planyourvisit\\/accessibility.htm\\\">Accessibility page<\\/a> for more information.<\\/p>\",\"isReservationRequired\":\"false\",\"ageDescription\":\"\",\"petsDescription\":\"<ul> <li><strong>Pets are permitted<\\/strong>\\u00a0<strong>on all paved surfaces in the park:<\\/strong>\\u00a0campgrounds, parking lots, paved roads, paved viewpoint areas, on the paved trail between Sunset Point and Sunrise Point, and on the paved Shared Use Path between the park entrance and Inspiration Point.<\\/li> <li><strong>Pets are not permitted<\\/strong>\\u00a0on unpaved trails or viewpoints, in public buildings or on public transportation vehicles. These regulations also apply to pets that are carried.<\\/li> <li><strong>Pets must be on a leash at all times<\\/strong>; the leash must be no longer than 6 feet.<\\/li> <li><strong>Pet owners may not leave pet(s) unattended<\\/strong>\\u00a0or tied to an object. Pets may not be left in vehicles while their owners hike. Be aware that idling and generator use is not permitted in park parking lots.<\\/li> <li><strong>Pets may not make unreasonable noise.<\\/strong><\\/li> <li><strong>Pet owners are required to pick-up after their pets<\\/strong>\\u00a0(excrement and other solid waste).<\\/li> <\\/ul>\",\"timeOfDayDescription\":\"\",\"feeDescription\":\"<p>Park admission provides access to this activity.<\\/p>\",\"age\":\"\",\"arePetsPermittedWithRestrictions\":\"false\",\"activities\":[{\"id\":\"0B4A5320-216D-451A-9990-626E1D5ACE28\",\"name\":\"Scenic Driving\"}],\"activityDescription\":\"<p>This trip provides opportunities to see 9 unique viewpoints along the southern, higher elevation stretches of the main park road. These viewpoints not only overlook unique geologic features, but the grand landscape of the Paria Valley and the Grand Staircase beyond.<\\/p>\",\"locationDescription\":\"\",\"doFeesApply\":\"true\",\"longDescription\":\"<p><b>Check out the <\\/b><a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/subjects\\/digital\\/nps-apps.htm\\\">Park App<\\/a><b>\'s \\\"Tours\\\" section for a guided walkthrough of how to take a driving tour of this area.<\\/b><br \\/> <br \\/> The main park road is 18 miles (29\\u00a0km) from the park entrance in the north to the end of the road at Rainbow at Yovimpa Points. While the Bryce Amphitheater area is found along its first 3 miles (4.8 km), the next 15 miles (24 km) to the road\'s end are known as the Southern Scenic Drive.\\u00a0<br \\/> <br \\/> Along this section of the main road are 9 scenic overlooks that display a lesser-seen\\u00a0beauty of Bryce Canyon. One aspect that makes the Southern Scenic Drive unique is that the park\'s elevations increase the further south you travel. While the Visitor Center is situated at 7,894 ft (2,406 m), Rainbow and Yovimpa Points exceed 9,000 ft (2,743 m).\\u00a0This change in elevation is the result uneven tectonic forces that uplifted this region over the last 20 million years, and creates a dramatic contrast--with exceptionally long views\\u00a0and unique features--between the north and south ends of the park.<\\/p> <h3>Winter Closures<\\/h3> <p>The high elevations along this section of the main road\\u00a0also lead\\u00a0to temporary winter closures\\u00a0at the Mile 3 gate, so be sure to visit the <a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/brca\\/planyourvisit\\/conditions.htm\\\">Conditions page<\\/a> for the latest road status.<\\/p> <h3>Planning Your Drive<\\/h3> <p>It takes about 40 minutes to drive to the end of the Southern Scenic Drive from the Visitor Center. It is recommended that you begin by first doing this all the way to Rainbow and Yovimpa Points. After enjoying these viewpoints, those you will have passed along the road can be visited on the way back. This way, everything will be on the right-hand side of the road; much safer than pulling across traffic everytime you want to see a viewpoint. Typically a trip of only driving and viewpoints will take around 2-3 hours.<\\/p> <h3>Highlights<\\/h3> <p>It is easy to enjoy all the views along the road, as none of them require much walking to reach. That said, most visitors would likely agree that Rainbow Point, Yovimpa Point, Agua Canyon and Natural Bridge are not to be missed.<br \\/> <br \\/> As you drive south and gain elevation, you\'ll notice the park\'s forest community changing from ponderosa forest to a mixture of colorful quaking aspen and denser stands of white fir and Douglas-fir.<\\/p> <h4>Wildlife<\\/h4> <p>Pull-outs and viewpoints along the Southern Scenic Drive also give opportunities for wildlife watching. Species seen further south in the park may include elk, black bears, mule deer, birds associated with mixed-conifer forests (such as Clark\'s nutcrackers and dusky grouse) and high-elevation cliffs (such as peregine falcons).<\\/p> <h3>Walks and Hikes<\\/h3> <p>All the viewpoints along the Southern Scenic Drive are located a very short walk from their parking lots, so no hiking is required to enjoy this trip. That said, very little can be seen from the car, so you will want to walk the short distances to the railings to enjoy the view. There are two popular day hikes located along this drive: the easy Bristlecone Loop at Rainbow and Yovimpa Points (usually takes about an hour to hike) and the Swamp Canyon Loop at Swamp Canyon overlook (often takes 3 to 4 hours).<br \\/> <br \\/> Backcountry hiking options also exist, with the Under-the-Rim Trail running\\u00a0approximately 22.9 miles (36.9 km) from Bryce Point to Rainbow Point. Connecting trails offer access from along the Southern Scenic Drive and intersect the Under-the-Rim Trail at Swamp Canyon, and Whiteman Bench. The Riggs Spring Loop is an 8.6 mile (13.9 km) hike beginning and ending at the Rainbow and Yovimpa Point parking lot at the south end of the Scenic Drive. Either the Yovimpa or Rainbow Point trailhead may be used to access this loop. A backcountry hiking map with mileage is available at the Visitor Center.\\u00a0<a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/brca\\/planyourvisit\\/backcountryinfo.htm\\\">More information<\\/a><\\/p>\",\"reservationDescription\":\"\",\"season\":[\"Winter\",\"Spring\",\"Summer\",\"Fall\"],\"topics\":[{\"id\":\"9C9FCBB6-360B-4743-8155-6F9341CBE01B\",\"name\":\"Scenic Views\"}],\"durationDescription\":\"<p>Depending on whether you just enjoy the drive and the viewpoints or choose to take a hike along the way, the Southern Scenic Drive can be a relatively short excursion or your whole day.<\\/p>\",\"arePetsPermitted\":\"true\",\"timeOfDay\":[\"Day\",\"Night\",\"Dawn\",\"Dusk\"],\"duration\":\"2-12 Hours\",\"credit\":\"\"}',1,'2023-09-10 21:06:03','2023-09-10 21:06:32'),(344,'Get Your Junior Ranger Badge',NULL,'nps','06B03AB7-942B-448D-B55E-70651AEAFC3E',1,'\\r\\n\\r\\n','How can you become a Junior Ranger? At Bryce Canyon National Park, our Junior Rangers must successfully complete two tasks:  Learn about the park by exploring the park museum, attending a presentation or hike given by a Park Ranger, or watching the park movie. Work through an activity booklet completing an age appropriate number of activities (complete as many activities as you are years old).  Upon completing these requirements, Junior Ranger candidates must return to the Visitor Center where they will be inducted as Junior Rangers and receive a free badge. Other unique Junior Ranger souvenirs are available for purchased from the park bookstore. Where do I get my book? Rangers at the Visitor Center front desk will both provide you your free book and reward you with your badge! Download the Bryce Canyon Junior Ranger book here: You can download the Bryce Canyon Junior Ranger book [PDF 6.4 MB] and get started as soon a you arrive to the park. Printed copies of the book are also available at the visitor center. Stuck on an Activity? You can find some hints and helpful suggestions at our Junior Ranger webpage. Completing the Book from Home? Once you\'ve completed your book, please take a photo of your favorite page or activity with an adult\'s signature on that page (certifying you\'ve completed all your activities) to brca_information@nps.gov Be sure to include your mailing address in the e-mail. We would also love to hear about why you enjoyed that page or activity! Please do not mail your book. Mailed books will not be returned.  Once we receive your email, we will mail you an official Junior Ranger sticker badge. Again, please note that due to shipping costs we are unable to mail physical badges and provide a gold foil official Junior Ranger sticker badge instead.  If you would like a wooden badge, you will need to cover shipping by sending the park a pre-paid bubble envelope.The enclosed envelope will need first class package postage, and will need to be a padded bubble envelope (regular envelopes will be rejected by USPS). Badges are 0.2 oz. Your postmaster should be able to tell you what your postage will be from your zip code.  You can mail the pre-paid envelope to the park at: Bryce Canyon National Park PO Box 640201 Bryce, UT 84764  If you get stuck while completing your book, you can e-mail a ranger for help!  Explore. Learn. Protect. More information on other Junior Ranger programs throughout the national park system is also available.',NULL,'A 508-compliant version of the book is available online.','1-4 Hours','1',NULL,NULL,NULL,'https://www.nps.gov/thingstodo/get-your-junior-ranger-badge.htm',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,1,NULL,'{\"id\":\"06B03AB7-942B-448D-B55E-70651AEAFC3E\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/thingstodo\\/get-your-junior-ranger-badge.htm\",\"title\":\"Get Your Junior Ranger Badge\",\"shortDescription\":\"Junior Rangers can be thought of as potential Park Rangers in training. This nationwide program is designed to not only familiarize children with the duties and responsibilities of a park ranger, but also to spark an interest in the whole family for the resources and stories each National Park Service area preserves.\",\"images\":[{\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/20BC259E-05A9-92E0-DC7FD1F62917DD87.jpg\",\"credit\":\"NPS Photo \\/ Keith Moore\",\"altText\":\"Three children and an adult sit on the floor working on an activity book\",\"title\":\"Junior Rangers in Museum\",\"description\":\"\",\"caption\":\"The Visitor Center museum is an excellent place to look for answers for your Junior Ranger book.\",\"crops\":[{\"aspectRatio\":\"1.78\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/primary\\/20BC259E-05A9-92E0-DC7FD1F62917DD87.jpg\"},{\"aspectRatio\":\"1\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/secondary\\/20BC259E-05A9-92E0-DC7FD1F62917DD87.jpg\"}]}],\"relatedParks\":[{\"states\":\"UT\",\"parkCode\":\"brca\",\"designation\":\"National Park\",\"fullName\":\"Bryce Canyon National Park\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/brca\\/index.htm\",\"name\":\"Bryce Canyon\"}],\"relatedOrganizations\":[],\"tags\":[\"Junior Ranger\"],\"latitude\":\"\",\"longitude\":\"\",\"geometryPoiId\":\"\",\"amenities\":[],\"location\":\"\",\"seasonDescription\":\"\",\"accessibilityInformation\":\"<p>A 508-compliant version of the book is <a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/brca\\/learn\\/kidsyouth\\/upload\\/Bryce-Canyon-Junior-Ranger-Book-Reduced-508.pdf\\\">available online<\\/a>.<\\/p>\",\"isReservationRequired\":\"false\",\"ageDescription\":\"<p>Anyone can participate in this program!<\\/p>\",\"petsDescription\":\"<ul> <li><strong>Pets are permitted<\\/strong>\\u00a0<strong>on all paved surfaces in the park:<\\/strong>\\u00a0campgrounds, parking lots, paved roads, paved viewpoint areas, on the paved trail between Sunset Point and Sunrise Point, and on the paved Shared Use Path between the park entrance and Inspiration Point.<\\/li> <li><strong>Pets are not permitted<\\/strong>\\u00a0on unpaved trails or viewpoints, in public buildings or on public transportation vehicles. These regulations also apply to pets that are carried.<\\/li> <li><strong>Pets must be on a leash at all times<\\/strong>; the leash must be no longer than 6 feet.<\\/li> <li><strong>Pet owners may not leave pet(s) unattended<\\/strong>\\u00a0or tied to an object. Pets may not be left in vehicles while their owners hike. Be aware that idling and generator use is not permitted in park parking lots.<\\/li> <li><strong>Pets may not make unreasonable noise.<\\/strong><\\/li> <li><strong>Pet owners are required to pick-up after their pets<\\/strong>\\u00a0(excrement and other solid waste).<\\/li> <\\/ul>\",\"timeOfDayDescription\":\"<p>Junior Ranger books are available for pick up and turn in\\u00a0during\\u00a0<a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/brca\\/planyourvisit\\/hours.htm\\\">Visitor Center hours.<\\/a><\\/p>\",\"feeDescription\":\"\",\"age\":\"\",\"arePetsPermittedWithRestrictions\":\"true\",\"activities\":[{\"id\":\"DF4A35E0-7983-4A3E-BC47-F37B872B0F25\",\"name\":\"Junior Ranger Program\"}],\"activityDescription\":\"\",\"locationDescription\":\"\",\"doFeesApply\":\"true\",\"longDescription\":\"<h2>How can you become a Junior Ranger?<\\/h2> <p>At Bryce Canyon National Park, our Junior Rangers must successfully complete two tasks:<\\/p> <ol> <li>Learn about the park by exploring the park museum, attending a presentation or hike given by a Park Ranger, or watching the park movie.<\\/li> <li>Work through an activity booklet completing an age appropriate number of activities (complete as many activities as you are years old).<\\/li> <\\/ol> <p>Upon completing these requirements, Junior Ranger candidates must return to the Visitor Center where they will be inducted as Junior Rangers and receive a free badge. Other unique Junior Ranger souvenirs are available for purchased from the park bookstore.<\\/p> <h4>Where do I get my book?<\\/h4> <p>Rangers at the Visitor Center front desk will both provide you your free book and reward you with your badge!<\\/p> <h4>Download the Bryce Canyon Junior Ranger book here:<\\/h4> <p>You can\\u00a0<a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/brca\\/learn\\/kidsyouth\\/upload\\/Bryce-Canyon-Junior-Ranger-Book-Reduced-508.pdf\\\">download the Bryce Canyon Junior Ranger book<\\/a>\\u00a0[PDF 6.4 MB] and get started as soon a you arrive to the park. Printed copies of the book are also available at the visitor center.<\\/p> <h2>Stuck on an Activity?<\\/h2> <p>You can find some hints and helpful suggestions at our <a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/brca\\/learn\\/kidsyouth\\/beajuniorranger.htm\\\">Junior Ranger webpage<\\/a>.<\\/p> <h2>Completing the Book from Home?<\\/h2> <p>Once you\'ve completed your book, please take a photo of your favorite page or activity with an adult\'s signature on that page (certifying you\'ve completed all your activities) to\\u00a0<strong>brca_information@nps.gov\\u00a0<\\/strong>Be sure to include your mailing address in the e-mail. We would also love to hear about why you enjoyed that page or activity! Please do not mail your book. Mailed books will not be returned.<br \\/> <br \\/> Once we receive your email, we will mail you an official Junior Ranger sticker badge. <strong>Again, please note that due to shipping costs we are unable to mail physical badges and provide a gold foil official Junior Ranger sticker badge instead.<\\/strong><br \\/> <br \\/> If you would like a wooden badge, you will need to cover shipping by sending\\u00a0the park a pre-paid bubble envelope.The enclosed envelope will need first class package postage, and will need to be a padded bubble envelope (regular envelopes will be rejected by USPS). Badges are 0.2\\u00a0oz. Your postmaster should be able to tell you what your postage will be from your zip code.<br \\/> <br \\/> You can mail the pre-paid envelope to the park at:<br \\/> Bryce Canyon National Park<br \\/> PO Box 640201<br \\/> Bryce, UT 84764<br \\/> <br \\/> If you get stuck while completing your book,\\u00a0<a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/utilities\\/sendmail\\/sendemail.cfm?o=4997D8BAA2DAA1BA9BA91EAEED1EA4A36693569C12B18389&r=\\/brca\\/learn\\/kidsyouth\\/beajuniorranger.htm\\\">you can e-mail a ranger<\\/a>\\u00a0for help!<\\/p> <br \\/> <span style=\\\"color: rgb(34, 34, 34); font-family: inherit; font-size: 2rem; font-weight: 700; letter-spacing: -0.25px;\\\">Explore. Learn. Protect.<\\/span> <p>More information on other\\u00a0<a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/kids\\/junior-rangers.htm\\\">Junior Ranger programs throughout the national park system<\\/a>\\u00a0is also available.<\\/p>\",\"reservationDescription\":\"\",\"season\":[\"Winter\",\"Spring\",\"Summer\",\"Fall\"],\"topics\":[{\"id\":\"3CDB67A9-1EAC-408D-88EC-F26FA35E90AF\",\"name\":\"Schools and Education\"}],\"durationDescription\":\"\",\"arePetsPermitted\":\"true\",\"timeOfDay\":[\"Day\",\"Night\",\"Dawn\",\"Dusk\"],\"duration\":\"1-4 Hours\",\"credit\":\"\"}',1,'2023-09-10 21:06:03','2023-09-10 21:06:33'),(345,'Backcountry Hiking and Camping',NULL,'nps','85CBBD19-1B5A-444E-82BE-F30522DCC31B',1,'\\r\\n\\r\\n<p>Experience the solitude of Bryce\'s forested backcountry on the Under the Rim Trail. Descending from the rim, this 22.9 mile trail meanders through forests & meadows, offering stunning views of rainbow-hued cliffs in the distance. The Under the Rim Trail follows the base of the Paunsaugant Plateau and is largely forested. The hoodoos are part of the distant vistas to the west as you hike the valley floor. To preserve this experience, <strong>backcountry camping is permitted only in designated campsites</strong>. A backcountry <strong>permit must be obtained</strong> at the Visitor Center prior to any overnight trips.</p> <ul> <li>There are 7 campsites on the 22.9 mile (36.9 km) Under-the-Rim Trail <strong>(Sheep Creek is closed)</strong></li> <li>There are 3 campsites on the 8.8 mile (14.2 km) Riggs Spring Loop Trail</li> <li>The trails are strenuous, with multiple changes in elevation ranging from 6,800 feet (2,073 m) to 9,115 feet (2778 m)</li> </ul>','Experience the solitude of Bryce\'s forested backcountry! To preserve this experience, backcountry camping is permitted only in designated campsites. A backcountry permit must be obtained at the Visitor Center prior to any overnight trips.  There are 7 campsites on the 22.9 mile (36.9 km) Under-the-Rim Trail (Sheep Creek is closed) There are 3 campsites on the 8.8 mile (14.2 km) Riggs Spring Loop Trail The trails are strenuous, with multiple changes in elevation ranging from 6,800 feet (2,073 m) to 9,115 feet (2778 m)  Backcountry Trails Under-the-Rim Trail Difficulty: Strenuous Total Distance: 22.9 miles (37 km) Ascent: 5,884 feet (1,793 m) Average time: 2-3 Days This trail may be inaccessible during mid-winter due to snow depths ranging from 2-15 ft (0.7-5 m).  Note: the Agua Connecting Trail is closed until further notice. Southern end at Rainbow Point, northern end at Bryce Point. Connecting trails at Ponderosa Canyon (Agua Canyon Connecting Trail), Whiteman Bench, and Swamp Canyon. Riggs Spring Loop Difficulty: Strenuous Total Distance: 8.8 miles (14.2 km) Ascent:  2,503 feet (763 m) Average time: 4-6 Hours This trail may be inaccessible during mid-winter due to snow depths ranging from 2-15 ft (0.7-5 m). Trailheads located near Rainbow and Yovimpa Points. Maps A backcountry hiking map with mileage is available at the Visitor Center. View the map and more information online. Permits Required A Backcountry Permit is required for all overnight stays in the backcountry. Permits may be purchased at the Visitor Center from 9 am - 12:30 pm and 1:30 pm to 6 pm. Backcountry campsites are limited to 6 people per regular site, and 15 people per group site. No Fires Permitted Though gas-powered cooking stoves are allowed, no open fires are allowed anywhere in the park outside of North and Sunset Campground\'s established fire rings. Bears and Other Wildlife Bryce Canyon is home to many animals including mountain lions, coyotes and black bears. Squirrels, snakes and other small animals may also be present near your campsite. Due to the presence of black bears within Bryce Canyon National Park, the use of bear-resistant food canisters on overnight backcountry hikes is highly recommended. Bear-resistant canisters are available for loan, free of charge, at the Visitor Center. Transportation When available, hikers may reserve seats on the Rainbow Point Tour bus for transportation to Rainbow and Yovimpa Point. However, this service is seasonal in nature. Reservations and detailed information may be obtained by calling (435) 834-5290.',NULL,'Park backcountry trails are very rugged and not considered accessible.',NULL,'1',NULL,NULL,NULL,'https://www.nps.gov/thingstodo/backcountry-hiking-and-camping.htm',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,1,NULL,'{\"id\":\"85CBBD19-1B5A-444E-82BE-F30522DCC31B\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/thingstodo\\/backcountry-hiking-and-camping.htm\",\"title\":\"Backcountry Hiking and Camping\",\"shortDescription\":\"Experience the solitude of Bryce\'s forested backcountry! To preserve this experience,\\u00a0backcountry camping is permitted only in designated campsites. A backcountry\\u00a0permit must be obtained\\u00a0at the Visitor Center prior to any overnight trips.\",\"images\":[{\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/03D12254-EAE1-10CC-CCDDD8D329C0E700.jpg\",\"credit\":\"NPS Photo\\/Peter Densmore\",\"altText\":\"Two hikers wearing backpacks and using poles along a red rock path surrounded by forested cliffs.\",\"title\":\"Bryce Canyon Backcountry Hikers\",\"description\":\"\",\"caption\":\"Hikers along the Under-the-Rim Trail near Rainbow Point.\",\"crops\":[{\"aspectRatio\":\"1.78\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/primary\\/03D12254-EAE1-10CC-CCDDD8D329C0E700.jpg\"},{\"aspectRatio\":\"1\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/secondary\\/03D12254-EAE1-10CC-CCDDD8D329C0E700.jpg\"}]}],\"relatedParks\":[{\"states\":\"UT\",\"parkCode\":\"brca\",\"designation\":\"National Park\",\"fullName\":\"Bryce Canyon National Park\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/brca\\/index.htm\",\"name\":\"Bryce Canyon\"}],\"relatedOrganizations\":[],\"tags\":[\"Under the Rim Trail\",\"Riggs Spring Loop Trail\",\"backcountry\"],\"latitude\":\"\",\"longitude\":\"\",\"geometryPoiId\":\"\",\"amenities\":[],\"location\":\"\",\"seasonDescription\":\"<p><span style=\\\"color:null;\\\">While permitted year-round, these trails\\u00a0may be difficult to access\\u00a0during mid-winter due to snow depths ranging from 2-15 ft (0.7-5 m).\\u00a0<\\/span><\\/p>\",\"accessibilityInformation\":\"<p>Park backcountry trails are very rugged and not considered accessible.<\\/p>\",\"isReservationRequired\":\"true\",\"ageDescription\":\"\",\"petsDescription\":\"\",\"timeOfDayDescription\":\"\",\"feeDescription\":\"<p>$10\\/permit + $5\\/person, ages 16+ to obtain a permit. Cash and card accepted.<\\/p>\",\"age\":\"\",\"arePetsPermittedWithRestrictions\":\"false\",\"activities\":[{\"id\":\"4A58AF13-E8FB-4530-B41A-97DF0B0C77B7\",\"name\":\"Backcountry Camping\"}],\"activityDescription\":\"<p>Experience the solitude of Bryce\'s forested backcountry on the Under the Rim Trail. Descending from the rim, this 22.9 mile trail meanders through forests & meadows, offering stunning views of rainbow-hued cliffs in the distance. The Under the Rim Trail follows the base of the Paunsaugant Plateau and is largely forested. The hoodoos are part of the distant vistas to the west as you hike the valley floor. To preserve this experience,\\u00a0<strong>backcountry camping is permitted only in designated campsites<\\/strong>. A backcountry\\u00a0<strong>permit must be obtained<\\/strong>\\u00a0at the Visitor Center prior to any overnight trips.<\\/p> <ul> <li>There are 7 campsites on the 22.9 mile (36.9 km) Under-the-Rim Trail\\u00a0<strong>(Sheep Creek is closed)<\\/strong><\\/li> <li>There are 3 campsites on the 8.8 mile (14.2 km) Riggs Spring Loop Trail<\\/li> <li>The trails are strenuous, with multiple changes in elevation ranging from 6,800 feet (2,073 m) to 9,115 feet (2778 m)<\\/li> <\\/ul>\",\"locationDescription\":\"\",\"doFeesApply\":\"true\",\"longDescription\":\"<p>Experience the solitude of Bryce\'s forested backcountry! To preserve this experience,\\u00a0<strong>backcountry camping is permitted only in designated campsites<\\/strong>. A backcountry\\u00a0<strong>permit must be obtained<\\/strong>\\u00a0at the Visitor Center prior to any overnight trips.<\\/p> <ul> <li>There are 7 campsites on the 22.9 mile (36.9 km) Under-the-Rim Trail\\u00a0<strong>(Sheep Creek is closed)<\\/strong><\\/li> <li>There are 3 campsites on the 8.8 mile (14.2 km) Riggs Spring Loop Trail<\\/li> <li>The trails are strenuous, with multiple changes in elevation ranging from 6,800 feet (2,073 m) to 9,115 feet (2778 m)<\\/li> <\\/ul> <h3>Backcountry Trails<\\/h3> <h4>Under-the-Rim Trail<\\/h4> <p><strong>Difficulty: Strenuous<br \\/> Total Distance: 22.9\\u00a0miles (37\\u00a0km)<br \\/> Ascent: 5,884\\u00a0feet (1,793\\u00a0m)<br \\/> Average time: 2-3 Days<\\/strong><\\/p> <p><span style=\\\"color:#2980b9;\\\"><strong>This trail may be inaccessible during mid-winter due to snow depths ranging from 2-15 ft (0.7-5 m).\\u00a0<\\/strong><\\/span><br \\/> <span style=\\\"color:#e74c3c;\\\"><strong>Note: the Agua Connecting Trail is closed until further notice.<\\/strong><\\/span><br \\/> Southern end at Rainbow Point, northern end at Bryce Point. Connecting trails at Ponderosa Canyon (Agua Canyon Connecting Trail), Whiteman Bench, and Swamp Canyon.<\\/p> <h4>Riggs Spring Loop<\\/h4> <p><strong>Difficulty: Strenuous<br \\/> Total Distance: 8.8\\u00a0miles (14.2\\u00a0km)<br \\/> Ascent: \\u00a02,503 feet (763\\u00a0m)<br \\/> Average time: 4-6 Hours<\\/strong><\\/p> <p><span style=\\\"color:#2980b9;\\\"><strong>This trail may be inaccessible during mid-winter due to snow depths ranging from 2-15 ft (0.7-5 m).<\\/strong><\\/span><br \\/> Trailheads located near\\u00a0Rainbow and Yovimpa Points.<\\/p> <h3>Maps<\\/h3> <p>A backcountry hiking map with mileage is available at the Visitor Center.\\u00a0<a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/brca\\/planyourvisit\\/backcountryinfo.htm\\\">View the map and more information online.<\\/a><\\/p> <h3>Permits Required<\\/h3> <p>A Backcountry Permit is required for all overnight stays in the backcountry. Permits may be purchased at the Visitor Center from 9 am - 12:30 pm and 1:30 pm to 6 pm. Backcountry campsites are limited to 6 people per regular site, and 15 people per group site.<\\/p> <h3>No Fires Permitted<\\/h3> <p>Though gas-powered cooking stoves are allowed, no open fires are allowed anywhere in the park outside of North and Sunset Campground\'s established fire rings.<\\/p> <h3>Bears and Other Wildlife<\\/h3> <p>Bryce Canyon is home to many animals including mountain lions, coyotes and black bears. Squirrels, snakes and other small animals may also be present near your campsite.<br \\/> Due to the presence of black bears within Bryce Canyon National Park, the use of bear-resistant food canisters on overnight backcountry hikes is highly recommended. Bear-resistant canisters are available for loan, free of charge, at the Visitor Center.<\\/p> <h3>Transportation<\\/h3> <p>When available, hikers may reserve seats on the Rainbow Point Tour bus for transportation to Rainbow and Yovimpa Point. However, this service is seasonal in nature. Reservations and detailed information may be obtained by calling (435) 834-5290.<\\/p>\",\"reservationDescription\":\"<p>Permits are required for backcountry camping and can be reserved in person up to 48 hours in advance.<\\/p>\",\"season\":[\"Winter\",\"Spring\",\"Summer\",\"Fall\"],\"topics\":[{\"id\":\"5BE55D7F-BDB6-4E3D-AC35-2D8EBB974417\",\"name\":\"Trails\"}],\"durationDescription\":\"\",\"arePetsPermitted\":\"false\",\"timeOfDay\":[],\"duration\":\"\",\"credit\":\"\"}',1,'2023-09-10 21:06:03','2023-09-10 21:06:33'),(346,'See the Park Film','Visitor Center','nps','0EDF1023-A410-4E7D-A3E4-76D079B05FB0',1,'\\r\\n\\r\\n','\"A Song of Seasons\" plays in the Visitor Center theater every half hour when the Visitor Center is open. Watch the film online.  The film is captioned and assisted listening devices are available upon request.',NULL,'The film is captioned and assisted listening devices are available upon request. The theater is fully accessible.','24-30 Minutes','1',37.6400770,-112.1696690,NULL,'https://www.nps.gov/thingstodo/see-the-park-film.htm',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,1,NULL,'{\"id\":\"0EDF1023-A410-4E7D-A3E4-76D079B05FB0\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/thingstodo\\/see-the-park-film.htm\",\"title\":\"See the Park Film\",\"shortDescription\":\"\\\"A Song of Seasons\\\" plays in the Visitor Center theater every half hour when the Visitor Center is open.\",\"images\":[{\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/2200EB50-AA8C-95D3-BD621B6B9F925880.jpg\",\"credit\":\"NPS Photo\",\"altText\":\"Title screen for A Song of Seasons film reads Bryce Canyon National Park\",\"title\":\"A Song of Seasons\",\"description\":\"\",\"caption\":\"\\\"A Song of Seasons\\\" explores the park in all four seasons.\",\"crops\":[{\"aspectRatio\":\"1.78\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/primary\\/2200EB50-AA8C-95D3-BD621B6B9F925880.jpg\"},{\"aspectRatio\":\"1\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/secondary\\/2200EB50-AA8C-95D3-BD621B6B9F925880.jpg\"}]}],\"relatedParks\":[{\"states\":\"UT\",\"parkCode\":\"brca\",\"designation\":\"National Park\",\"fullName\":\"Bryce Canyon National Park\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/brca\\/index.htm\",\"name\":\"Bryce Canyon\"}],\"relatedOrganizations\":[],\"tags\":[\"park film\"],\"latitude\":\"37.640077\",\"longitude\":\"-112.169669\",\"geometryPoiId\":\"\",\"amenities\":[],\"location\":\"Visitor Center\",\"seasonDescription\":\"\",\"accessibilityInformation\":\"<p>The film is captioned and assisted listening devices are available upon request. The theater is fully accessible.<\\/p>\",\"isReservationRequired\":\"false\",\"ageDescription\":\"\",\"petsDescription\":\"\",\"timeOfDayDescription\":\"\",\"feeDescription\":\"<p>Park admission provides access to this activity.<\\/p>\",\"age\":\"\",\"arePetsPermittedWithRestrictions\":\"false\",\"activities\":[{\"id\":\"0C0D142F-06B5-4BE1-8B44-491B90F93DEB\",\"name\":\"Park Film\"}],\"activityDescription\":\"\",\"locationDescription\":\"\",\"doFeesApply\":\"true\",\"longDescription\":\"<p>\\\"A Song of Seasons\\\" plays in the Visitor Center theater every half hour when the Visitor Center is open.<br \\/> <a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/www.youtube.com\\/watch?v=v5dQbo4EUCI&feature=youtu.be\\\">Watch the film online<\\/a>.<br \\/> <br \\/> The film is captioned and assisted listening devices are available upon request.<\\/p>\",\"reservationDescription\":\"\",\"season\":[\"Winter\",\"Spring\",\"Summer\",\"Fall\"],\"topics\":[{\"id\":\"3CDB67A9-1EAC-408D-88EC-F26FA35E90AF\",\"name\":\"Schools and Education\"}],\"durationDescription\":\"\",\"arePetsPermitted\":\"false\",\"timeOfDay\":[\"Day\"],\"duration\":\"24-30 Minutes\",\"credit\":\"\"}',1,'2023-09-10 21:06:03','2023-09-10 21:06:33'),(347,'Day Hikes',NULL,'nps','3E505AE7-11A8-46CA-B09C-DF910DEE1BB3',1,'\\r\\n\\r\\n','Visit our Hiking Page for more details on the hikes below. This is a High Elevation Park With most of the park at 8,000 feet (2,438 m) or above, elevation is a very real factor to consider when choosing your hike. At this elevation, there\'s about 70% of the oxygen available at sea-level, and you\'ll certainly feel it if you begin to hike. When you\'re choosing a hike, do yourself a huge favor and don\'t choose something at the edge of your abilities. Take it easy, and rest assured that even short hikes have as much beauty to offer as the longest ones. Elevation-related injuries, such as heart issues, are among the park\'s most common medical emergencies.  UV radiation is also a lot stronger at this elevation. Make sure you cover up, wear sunscreen, or take other precautions to protect your skin and eyes.  Lastly, storms can form quickly here. If thunder roars, go indoors! Lightning--especially during the summer monsoon season--is a deadly threat. Take shelter in your vehicle, a building, a shuttle bus, or if you\'re on a trail find a low lying depression. Both trees and hoodoos can conduct lightning down to you. Water and Footwear With less oxygen available in each breath, you\'re going to be breathing harder, which means that you\'re going to dehydrate more quickly. We recommend a liter of water for every 1-2 hours you plan to hike. There is no water available once you\'re on a trail (backcountry hikes pass seasonal springs and drainages, but even these are rarely reliable), so make sure you pack all that you need.   Our number one cause of injuries in the park? Bad footwear. Trails have a lot of small, loose rock on their otherwise flat surfaces making slipping and twisting an ankle all too easy. Footwear with a good tread and ankle support is highly recommended. In winter, ice creates even more hazardous conditions. We strongly recommend the use of footwear traction devices when ice is present. Easy Hikes Mossy Cave: 0.8 mi (1.3 km) Sunset to Sunrise (along Rim Trail): 1.0 mi (1.6 km) Bristlecone Loop: 1.0 mi (1.6 km) Moderate Hikes Queen\'s Garden: 1.8 mi (2.9 km) Navajo Loop: 1.3 mi (2.2 km) Queen\'s/Navajo Combo Loop: 2.9 mi (4.6 km) Tower Bridge: 3.0 mi (4.8 km) Swamp Canyon Loop: 4.0 mi (6.4 km) Strenuous Hikes Hat Shop: 4.0 mi (6.4 km) Fairyland Loop: 8.0 mi (12.9 km) Peekaboo Loop: 5.5 mi (8.8 km) Navajo/Peekaboo Combo: 4.9 mi (7.8 km) Figure-8 Combo: 6.4 mi (10.2 km) Bryce Amphitheater Traverse: 4.7 mi (7.5 km)',NULL,'The only truly accessible trail here listed is the 0.5 mile (0.8 km) Sunset to Sunrise section of the Rim Trail. Other trails will vary. More details can be found on each individual hike page.',NULL,'1',NULL,NULL,NULL,'https://www.nps.gov/thingstodo/day-hikes.htm',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,1,NULL,'{\"id\":\"3E505AE7-11A8-46CA-B09C-DF910DEE1BB3\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/thingstodo\\/day-hikes.htm\",\"title\":\"Day Hikes\",\"shortDescription\":\"From easy walks along the edge of the famous Bryce Amphitheater using the Rim Trail to more strenuous adventures surrounded by the hoodoos below on the Peekaboo Loopthe park provides a variety of options for a variety of abilities to enjoy the park up close at your own pace. Visit the \\\"Hiking Trails\\\" button on the front page of this app for more trail details.\",\"images\":[{\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/8958F9CD-93EE-2D75-47719B47EF9E364C.jpg\",\"credit\":\"NPS Photo \\/ Peter Densmore\",\"altText\":\"Two hikers pass through a hole carved in limestone feature along a trail lined with trees and rocks\",\"title\":\"Hikers on Queen\'s Garden\",\"description\":\"\",\"caption\":\"The Queen\'s Garden provides an opportunity to descend among the hoodoos of the Bryce Amphitheater\",\"crops\":[{\"aspectRatio\":\"1.78\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/primary\\/8958F9CD-93EE-2D75-47719B47EF9E364C.jpg\"},{\"aspectRatio\":\"1\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/secondary\\/8958F9CD-93EE-2D75-47719B47EF9E364C.jpg\"}]}],\"relatedParks\":[{\"states\":\"UT\",\"parkCode\":\"brca\",\"designation\":\"National Park\",\"fullName\":\"Bryce Canyon National Park\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/brca\\/index.htm\",\"name\":\"Bryce Canyon\"}],\"relatedOrganizations\":[],\"tags\":[\"day hikes\",\"hikes\"],\"latitude\":\"\",\"longitude\":\"\",\"geometryPoiId\":\"\",\"amenities\":[],\"location\":\"\",\"seasonDescription\":\"\",\"accessibilityInformation\":\"<p>The only truly accessible trail here listed is the 0.5 mile (0.8 km) Sunset to Sunrise section of the Rim Trail. Other trails will vary. More details can be found on each individual hike page.<\\/p>\",\"isReservationRequired\":\"false\",\"ageDescription\":\"\",\"petsDescription\":\"<ul> <li><strong>Pets are permitted<\\/strong>\\u00a0<strong>on all paved surfaces in the park:<\\/strong>\\u00a0campgrounds, parking lots, paved roads, paved viewpoint areas, on the paved trail between Sunset Point and Sunrise Point, and on the paved Shared Use Path between the park entrance and Inspiration Point.<\\/li> <li><strong>Pets are not permitted<\\/strong>\\u00a0on unpaved trails or viewpoints, in public buildings or on public transportation vehicles. These regulations also apply to pets that are carried.<\\/li> <li><strong>Pets must be on a leash at all times<\\/strong>; the leash must be no longer than 6 feet.<\\/li> <li><strong>Pet owners may not leave pet(s) unattended<\\/strong>\\u00a0or tied to an object. Pets may not be left in vehicles while their owners hike. Be aware that idling and generator use is not permitted in park parking lots.<\\/li> <li><strong>Pets may not make unreasonable noise.<\\/strong><\\/li> <li><strong>Pet owners are required to pick-up after their pets<\\/strong>\\u00a0(excrement and other solid waste).<\\/li> <\\/ul>\",\"timeOfDayDescription\":\"\",\"feeDescription\":\"<p>Park admission provides access to this activity.<\\/p> <p>\\u00a0<\\/p>\",\"age\":\"\",\"arePetsPermittedWithRestrictions\":\"false\",\"activities\":[{\"id\":\"BFF8C027-7C8F-480B-A5F8-CD8CE490BFBA\",\"name\":\"Hiking\"}],\"activityDescription\":\"\",\"locationDescription\":\"\",\"doFeesApply\":\"true\",\"longDescription\":\"<p><strong>Visit our <\\/strong><a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/brca\\/planyourvisit\\/day-hikes.htm\\\">Hiking Page<\\/a><strong>\\u00a0for more details on the hikes below.<\\/strong><\\/p> <h3>This is a High Elevation Park<\\/h3> <p>With most of the park at 8,000 feet (2,438 m) or above, elevation is a very real factor to consider when choosing your hike. At this elevation, there\'s about 70% of the oxygen available at sea-level, and you\'ll certainly feel it if you begin to hike. When you\'re choosing a hike, do yourself a huge favor and don\'t choose something at the edge of your abilities. Take it easy, and rest assured that even short hikes have as much beauty to offer as the longest ones. Elevation-related injuries, such as heart issues, are among the park\'s most common medical emergencies.<br \\/> <br \\/> UV radiation is also a lot stronger at this elevation. Make sure you cover up, wear sunscreen, or take other precautions to protect your skin and eyes.<br \\/> <br \\/> Lastly, storms can form quickly here. If thunder roars, go indoors! Lightning--especially during the summer monsoon season--is a deadly threat. Take shelter in your vehicle, a building, a shuttle bus, or if you\'re on a trail find a low lying depression. Both trees and hoodoos can conduct lightning down to you.<\\/p> <h3>Water and Footwear<\\/h3> <p>With less oxygen available in each breath, you\'re going to be breathing harder, which means that you\'re going to dehydrate more quickly. We recommend a liter of water for every 1-2 hours you plan to hike. There is no water available once you\'re on a trail (backcountry hikes pass seasonal springs and drainages, but even these are rarely reliable), so make sure you pack all that you need.\\u00a0<br \\/> <br \\/> Our number one cause of injuries in the park? Bad footwear. Trails have a lot of small, loose rock on their otherwise flat surfaces making slipping and twisting an ankle all too easy. Footwear with a good tread and ankle support is highly recommended. In winter, ice creates even more hazardous conditions. We <strong>strongly recommend<\\/strong> the use of footwear traction devices when ice is present.<\\/p> <h4>Easy Hikes<\\/h4> <p>Mossy Cave: 0.8 mi (1.3 km)<br \\/> Sunset to Sunrise (along Rim Trail): 1.0 mi (1.6 km)<br \\/> Bristlecone Loop: 1.0 mi (1.6 km)<\\/p> <h4>Moderate Hikes<\\/h4> <p>Queen\'s Garden: 1.8 mi (2.9 km)<br \\/> Navajo Loop: 1.3 mi (2.2 km)<br \\/> Queen\'s\\/Navajo Combo Loop: 2.9 mi (4.6 km)<br \\/> Tower Bridge: 3.0 mi (4.8 km)<br \\/> Swamp Canyon Loop: 4.0 mi (6.4 km)<\\/p> <h4>Strenuous Hikes<\\/h4> <p>Hat Shop: 4.0 mi (6.4 km)<br \\/> Fairyland Loop: 8.0 mi (12.9 km)<br \\/> Peekaboo Loop: 5.5 mi (8.8 km)<br \\/> Navajo\\/Peekaboo Combo: 4.9 mi (7.8 km)<br \\/> Figure-8 Combo: 6.4 mi (10.2 km)<br \\/> Bryce Amphitheater Traverse: 4.7 mi (7.5 km)<\\/p>\",\"reservationDescription\":\"\",\"season\":[\"Winter\",\"Spring\",\"Summer\",\"Fall\"],\"topics\":[{\"id\":\"5BE55D7F-BDB6-4E3D-AC35-2D8EBB974417\",\"name\":\"Trails\"}],\"durationDescription\":\"\",\"arePetsPermitted\":\"false\",\"timeOfDay\":[\"Day\",\"Night\",\"Dawn\",\"Dusk\"],\"duration\":\"\",\"credit\":\"\"}',1,'2023-09-10 21:06:03','2023-09-10 21:06:33'),(348,'Get a Passport Stamp','Bryce Canyon Visitor Center','nps','E7822199-B756-4FF4-A4BF-58CD3CA88B4E',1,'Passport counter located in back right corner of Visitor Center\\r\\n\\r\\n','Are you a \"Passport to Your National Parks\" cancellation collector? Bryce Canyon has a cancellation stamp to commemorate your visit in your passport book.',NULL,'The Bryce Canyon Visitor Center is fully accessible.',NULL,'1',37.6403874,-112.1696423,NULL,'https://www.nps.gov/thingstodo/get-a-passport-stamp.htm',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,1,NULL,'{\"id\":\"E7822199-B756-4FF4-A4BF-58CD3CA88B4E\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/thingstodo\\/get-a-passport-stamp.htm\",\"title\":\"Get a Passport Stamp\",\"shortDescription\":\"Are you a \\\"Passport to Your National Parks\\\" cancellation collector? Bryce Canyon has a cancellation stamp to commemorate your visit in your passport book.\",\"images\":[{\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/5A7643DF-002D-7358-7758FD98DFFA3FA1.jpg\",\"credit\":\"NPS Photo \\/ Peter Densmore\",\"altText\":\"A merchandise display between two windows reads Passports with many stickers and stamps.\",\"title\":\"Stamp Your Passport\",\"description\":\"\",\"caption\":\"Stamp your \\\"Passport to Your National Parks\\\" book in the Bryce Canyon Visitor Center.\",\"crops\":[{\"aspectRatio\":\"1.78\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/primary\\/5A7643DF-002D-7358-7758FD98DFFA3FA1.jpg\"},{\"aspectRatio\":\"1\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/secondary\\/5A7643DF-002D-7358-7758FD98DFFA3FA1.jpg\"}]}],\"relatedParks\":[{\"states\":\"UT\",\"parkCode\":\"brca\",\"designation\":\"National Park\",\"fullName\":\"Bryce Canyon National Park\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/brca\\/index.htm\",\"name\":\"Bryce Canyon\"}],\"relatedOrganizations\":[],\"tags\":[\"passport stamp\",\"passport\"],\"latitude\":\"37.6403873677938\",\"longitude\":\"-112.169642330719\",\"geometryPoiId\":\"c0f3639f-2049-49ed-9b85-78f10e4586ee\",\"amenities\":[],\"location\":\"Bryce Canyon Visitor Center\",\"seasonDescription\":\"\",\"accessibilityInformation\":\"The Bryce Canyon Visitor Center is fully accessible.\",\"isReservationRequired\":\"false\",\"ageDescription\":\"\",\"petsDescription\":\"\",\"timeOfDayDescription\":\"During Visitor Center hours.\",\"feeDescription\":\"Included with park admission.\",\"age\":\"\",\"arePetsPermittedWithRestrictions\":\"false\",\"activities\":[{\"id\":\"467DC8B8-0B7D-436D-A026-80A22358F615\",\"name\":\"Bookstore and Park Store\"}],\"activityDescription\":\"\",\"locationDescription\":\"Passport counter located in back right corner of Visitor Center\",\"doFeesApply\":\"false\",\"longDescription\":\"Are you a \\\"Passport to Your National Parks\\\" cancellation collector? Bryce Canyon has a cancellation stamp to commemorate your visit in your passport book.\",\"reservationDescription\":\"\",\"season\":[\"Winter\",\"Spring\",\"Summer\",\"Fall\"],\"topics\":[{\"id\":\"BCE3ACBE-7871-495B-AEA3-5E19BC482405\",\"name\":\"Canyons and Canyonlands\"}],\"durationDescription\":\"\",\"arePetsPermitted\":\"false\",\"timeOfDay\":[\"Day\"],\"duration\":\"\",\"credit\":\"\"}',1,'2023-09-10 21:06:03','2023-09-10 21:06:33'),(349,'Hike the Hoodoos',NULL,'nps','96177421-E0B1-4983-BD5A-C8B509E0525D',1,'\\r\\n\\r\\n','Hike the Hoodoos Program To encourage visitors to stay healthy while having fun, Bryce Canyon National Park has developed an activity called \"Hike the Hoodoos!\" Part hike, part scavenger hunt, visitors must hike at least 3 miles on specially-marked trails and find \"Hike the Hoodoos\" benchmark survey markers along the way. Visitors may either obtain rubbings of the benchmarks, or take pictures of themselves with the benchmarks, to prove they hiked the required distance. Upon showing their rubbings or photos to the Visitor Center ranger, they are then presented with a special reward. Where to Hike There are nine \"Hike the Hoodoos!\" benchmarks located along eight different hiking trails in the park. Although only 3-miles of hiking (and finding benchmarks) are required to earn a reward, some visitors are opting to attain rubbings/photos of all nine benchmarks - for a total of 18.4 miles of hiking! Quite an accomplishment, indeed! Medallions can be found on the following trails:    Trail Distance Number of Medallions   Mossy Cave 0.8 mi (1.3 km) 1   Bristlecone Loop 1.0 mi (1.6 km) 1   Queen\'s Garden* 1.8 mi (2.9 km) 1   Navajo Loop* 1.3 mi (2.2 km) 1   Tower Bridge 3.0 mi (4.8 km) 1   Swamp Canyon Loop 4.0 mi (6.4 km) 1   Peekaboo Loop 5.5 mi (8.8 km) 2   Fairyland Loop  8.0 mi (12.9 km) 2     *Hikers often combine the Queen\'s Garden and Navajo Loop into the 3 mile (4.6 km) Queen\'s/Navajo Loop to get two medallions and claim the prize. Thanks The \"Hike the Hoodoos!\" program was made possible, in part, by a grant from the National Park Foundation through the generous support of the Coca-Cola Foundation.  ',NULL,'Service animals are permitted on all of the included trails. Trail surfaces are not paved and include some steep grades and loose rocks. Not accessible for wheelchairs. ','2-6 Hours','1',NULL,NULL,NULL,'https://www.nps.gov/thingstodo/hike-the-hoodoos.htm',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,1,NULL,'{\"id\":\"96177421-E0B1-4983-BD5A-C8B509E0525D\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/thingstodo\\/hike-the-hoodoos.htm\",\"title\":\"Hike the Hoodoos\",\"shortDescription\":\"To encourage visitors to stay healthy while having fun, Bryce Canyon National Park has developed an activity called \\\"Hike the Hoodoos!\\\" Part hike, part scavenger hunt, visitors must hike at least 3 miles on specially-marked trails and find \\\"Hike the Hoodoos\\\" benchmark survey markers along the way. Visitors may either obtain rubbings of the benchmarks, or take pictures of themselves with the benchmarks, to prove they hiked the required distance.\",\"images\":[{\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/C3CE7CB1-93FB-5BEB-A7625CDCB3331597.jpg\",\"credit\":\"NPS Photo\",\"altText\":\"An interpretive wayside display shows a peregrine falcon beside text near a wooden gazebo\",\"title\":\"Bristlecone Loop Hike the Hoodoos Gazebo\",\"description\":\"\",\"caption\":\"The Bristlecone Loop has one medallion along its 1.0 mile (1.6 km) trail.\",\"crops\":[{\"aspectRatio\":\"1.78\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/primary\\/C3CE7CB1-93FB-5BEB-A7625CDCB3331597.jpg\"},{\"aspectRatio\":\"1\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/secondary\\/C3CE7CB1-93FB-5BEB-A7625CDCB3331597.jpg\"}]}],\"relatedParks\":[{\"states\":\"UT\",\"parkCode\":\"brca\",\"designation\":\"National Park\",\"fullName\":\"Bryce Canyon National Park\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/brca\\/index.htm\",\"name\":\"Bryce Canyon\"}],\"relatedOrganizations\":[],\"tags\":[\"hike the hoodoos\"],\"latitude\":\"\",\"longitude\":\"\",\"geometryPoiId\":\"\",\"amenities\":[],\"location\":\"\",\"seasonDescription\":\"\",\"accessibilityInformation\":\"Service animals are permitted on all of the included trails. Trail surfaces are not paved and include some steep grades and loose rocks. Not accessible for wheelchairs.\\u00a0\",\"isReservationRequired\":\"false\",\"ageDescription\":\"\",\"petsDescription\":\"\",\"timeOfDayDescription\":\"\",\"feeDescription\":\"Access to the activity comes with park admission.\",\"age\":\"\",\"arePetsPermittedWithRestrictions\":\"false\",\"activities\":[{\"id\":\"BFF8C027-7C8F-480B-A5F8-CD8CE490BFBA\",\"name\":\"Hiking\"}],\"activityDescription\":\"\",\"locationDescription\":\"\",\"doFeesApply\":\"true\",\"longDescription\":\"<h2>Hike the Hoodoos Program<\\/h2> <p>To encourage visitors to stay healthy while having fun, Bryce Canyon National Park has developed an activity called \\\"Hike the Hoodoos!\\\" Part hike, part scavenger hunt, visitors must hike at least 3 miles on specially-marked trails and find \\\"Hike the Hoodoos\\\" benchmark survey markers along the way. Visitors may either obtain rubbings of the benchmarks, or take pictures of themselves with the benchmarks, to prove they hiked the required distance. Upon showing their rubbings or photos to the Visitor Center ranger, they are then presented with a special reward.<\\/p> <h3>Where to Hike<\\/h3> <p>There are nine \\\"Hike the Hoodoos!\\\" benchmarks located along eight different hiking trails in the park. Although only 3-miles of hiking (and finding benchmarks) are required to earn a reward, some visitors are opting to attain rubbings\\/photos of all nine benchmarks - for a total of 18.4 miles of hiking! Quite an accomplishment, indeed!<br \\/> Medallions can be found on the following trails:<\\/p> <table border=\\\"1\\\" cellpadding=\\\"1\\\" cellspacing=\\\"1\\\" style=\\\"width:500px;\\\"> <tbody> <tr> <td style=\\\"text-align: center;\\\"><strong>Trail<\\/strong><\\/td> <td style=\\\"text-align: center;\\\"><strong>Distance<\\/strong><\\/td> <td style=\\\"text-align: center;\\\"><strong>Number of Medallions<\\/strong><\\/td> <\\/tr> <tr> <td style=\\\"text-align: center;\\\">Mossy Cave<\\/td> <td style=\\\"text-align: center;\\\">0.8 mi (1.3 km)<\\/td> <td style=\\\"text-align: center;\\\">1<\\/td> <\\/tr> <tr> <td style=\\\"text-align: center;\\\">Bristlecone Loop<\\/td> <td style=\\\"text-align: center;\\\">1.0 mi (1.6 km)<\\/td> <td style=\\\"text-align: center;\\\">1<\\/td> <\\/tr> <tr> <td style=\\\"text-align: center;\\\">Queen\'s Garden*<\\/td> <td style=\\\"text-align: center;\\\">1.8 mi (2.9 km)<\\/td> <td style=\\\"text-align: center;\\\">1<\\/td> <\\/tr> <tr> <td style=\\\"text-align: center;\\\">Navajo Loop*<\\/td> <td style=\\\"text-align: center;\\\">1.3 mi (2.2 km)<\\/td> <td style=\\\"text-align: center;\\\">1<\\/td> <\\/tr> <tr> <td style=\\\"text-align: center;\\\">Tower Bridge<\\/td> <td style=\\\"text-align: center;\\\">3.0 mi (4.8 km)<\\/td> <td style=\\\"text-align: center;\\\">1<\\/td> <\\/tr> <tr> <td style=\\\"text-align: center;\\\">Swamp Canyon Loop<\\/td> <td style=\\\"text-align: center;\\\">4.0 mi (6.4 km)<\\/td> <td style=\\\"text-align: center;\\\">1<\\/td> <\\/tr> <tr> <td style=\\\"text-align: center;\\\">Peekaboo Loop<\\/td> <td style=\\\"text-align: center;\\\">5.5 mi (8.8 km)<\\/td> <td style=\\\"text-align: center;\\\">2<\\/td> <\\/tr> <tr> <td style=\\\"text-align: center;\\\">Fairyland Loop\\u00a0<\\/td> <td style=\\\"text-align: center;\\\">8.0 mi (12.9 km)<\\/td> <td style=\\\"text-align: center;\\\">2<\\/td> <\\/tr> <\\/tbody> <\\/table> <p><br \\/> *Hikers often combine the Queen\'s Garden and Navajo Loop into the 3\\u00a0mile (4.6 km) Queen\'s\\/Navajo Loop to get two medallions and claim the prize.<\\/p> <h3>Thanks<\\/h3> <p>The \\\"Hike the Hoodoos!\\\" program was made possible, in part, by a grant from the National Park Foundation through the generous support of the Coca-Cola Foundation.<\\/p> <p>\\u00a0<\\/p>\",\"reservationDescription\":\"\",\"season\":[\"Winter\",\"Spring\",\"Summer\",\"Fall\"],\"topics\":[{\"id\":\"5BE55D7F-BDB6-4E3D-AC35-2D8EBB974417\",\"name\":\"Trails\"}],\"durationDescription\":\"\",\"arePetsPermitted\":\"false\",\"timeOfDay\":[],\"duration\":\"2-6 Hours\",\"credit\":\"\"}',1,'2023-09-10 21:06:03','2023-09-10 21:06:33'),(350,'View Wildlife',NULL,'nps','BD32ADD4-7B3F-4DE2-8982-B615E49B0CBC',1,'\\r\\n\\r\\n','The canyons and plateau of Bryce Canyon National Park are home to many animals. Park boundaries mean little to the migratory hummingbirds, nesting Peregrine Falcon, Rocky Mountain Elk and Pronghorn which daily cross through the forested plateau and amphitheaters. The search for food and water leads them to the best place to find sustenance and shelter. Many animals share habitats. Ebb and flow of populations is interdependent on all the members of the wildlife community. Mammals/Reptile List   Safety and Respect   Treat wildlife with respect - you are a visitor in their home.   Never feed wild animals - even a chipmunk can inflict a painful bite and possibly spread disease. Feeding them human food is also akin to animal cruelty in its likelihood to lead to maladaptive behaviors or even death.   Keep a safe distance - use binoculars or, for photography, telephoto lenses.   When venturing into venomous snake territory wear boots and loose-fitting pants.   Stay on trails, and watch where you place your hands and feet – especially when sitting, climbing, or stepping on or over fences, large rocks, and logs.   Hang food, garbage and anything with strong odors (toothpaste, bug repellent, soap, etc.) at least 10 feet off the ground and 6 feet from the trunk or use a bear-proof container. Never store food inside your tent.   If a bear or Mountain Lion approaches you back away slowly. Avoid direct eye contact with a bear but maintain eye contact with a cat. In either case do not run. Raise your arms above your head to make yourself look larger than normal, make noise, and throw rocks or sticks.   Seasons Spring comes late at these high elevations, with snow often remaining into April. Many of the park\'s inhabitants either hibernate or move to lower, less snow-covered elevations. While large mammals like Mule Deer can be seen year-round, pronghorn typically depart in fall and aren\'t seen again until snow has begun to melt in spring. Utah Prairie Dogs typically begin to depart underground for hibernation by November, and the first males are often seen reemerging in late February or early March. Other species are extremely uncommon--mountain lions are present but seldom seen, as are the park\'s black bears, which overwinter in dens and spend summers foraging in less-travelled forests. In warmer months, you\'re almost guaranteed to be confronted by a Unita chipmunk or golden-mantled ground squirrel scavenging near viewpoints or trail junctions. Remember to never feed or approach any wildlife in the park.  Where to Look  Utah Prairie Dog Colonies: A threatened species, in the 1970s the Utah Prairie Dog was expected to go extinct by the year 2000. Thankfully reintroduction efforts here in the park in the 1980s have stabliized the largest population of Utah\'s only endemic (only found here) mammal. The smallest of the prairie dogs, 8,000 feet (2,438 m) is about as high as they\'ll live. From March through October you can pretty realiably find them near the entrances to their underground burrows in the meadow that crosses the main road immediately south of the Visitor Center. Another colony is found on the right-hand side of the road around Mile 2 of the park road. A third colony can be seen on the way to Sunrise Point after taking the second left turn past the Visitor Center. Be sure to observe them from road pull-offs but never enter these meadows. Not only do prairie dogs potentially carry fleas infected with plague, they can deliver a very powerful bite! Prairie dogs provide food for most predators in the park, so observing a prairie dog colony is often the best way to see other species too. Listen for when the prairie dogs begin to chitter and \"bark\"--this may alert you to a predator along the treeline or soaring above.  Southern Scenic Drive: Travelling to Rainbow and Yovimpa Points at the southern end of the park takes you up to elevations of over 9,000 feet (2,743 m). At these high elevations, the park\'s ponderosa forests transition to mixed conifer woodlands of white fir, limber pine, douglas-fir, quaking aspen, and bristlecone pine. Snow levels are much higher here in winter, linger longer into spring, and provide more moisture for a dense understory. Wildlife is most often seen in meadows leading up to these higher views, such as the meadow around Mile 4 of the 18 mile park road. More often than not, wildlife sightings here are large mammals seen crossing the road.   Bryce Amphitheater: The Bryce Amphitheater is defined by its ponderosa forests and the mixed juniper and ponderosa woodland below the rim. Here one sees chipmunks and ground squirrels in warmer months, and often small families of mule deer foraging above and below the rim. If you\'re out stargazing, you may be so lucky as to spot a ringtail--they\'ve most often been seen near the Sunset Point parking lot.',NULL,'Viewpoints provide paved, flat surfaces accessible to all. Trails will vary. Visit our Accessibility page for more information.',NULL,'1',NULL,NULL,NULL,'https://www.nps.gov/thingstodo/view-wildlife.htm',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,1,NULL,'{\"id\":\"BD32ADD4-7B3F-4DE2-8982-B615E49B0CBC\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/thingstodo\\/view-wildlife.htm\",\"title\":\"View Wildlife\",\"shortDescription\":\"The canyons and plateau of Bryce Canyon National Park are home to many animals. Park boundaries mean little to the migratory hummingbirds, nesting Peregrine Falcon, Rocky Mountain Elk and Pronghorn which daily cross through the forested plateau and\\u00a0amphitheaters. The search for food and water leads them to the best place to find sustenance and shelter. Many animals share habitats. Ebb and flow of populations is interdependent on all the members of the wildlife community.\",\"images\":[{\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/237E16CB-B105-30A6-EDCBA0489CF8F2B4.jpg\",\"credit\":\"Kadi Franson\",\"altText\":\"Three male pronghorns stand together in a field of grass\",\"title\":\"Pronghorn\",\"description\":\"\",\"caption\":\"Pronghorn are seen in warmer months in meadows within and around the park.\",\"crops\":[{\"aspectRatio\":\"1.78\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/primary\\/237E16CB-B105-30A6-EDCBA0489CF8F2B4.jpg\"},{\"aspectRatio\":\"1\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/secondary\\/237E16CB-B105-30A6-EDCBA0489CF8F2B4.jpg\"}]}],\"relatedParks\":[{\"states\":\"UT\",\"parkCode\":\"brca\",\"designation\":\"National Park\",\"fullName\":\"Bryce Canyon National Park\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/brca\\/index.htm\",\"name\":\"Bryce Canyon\"}],\"relatedOrganizations\":[],\"tags\":[\"wildlife\"],\"latitude\":\"\",\"longitude\":\"\",\"geometryPoiId\":\"\",\"amenities\":[],\"location\":\"\",\"seasonDescription\":\"\",\"accessibilityInformation\":\"<p>Viewpoints provide paved, flat surfaces accessible to all. Trails will vary.\\u00a0Visit our\\u00a0<a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/brca\\/planyourvisit\\/accessibility.htm\\\">Accessibility page<\\/a>\\u00a0for more information.<\\/p>\",\"isReservationRequired\":\"false\",\"ageDescription\":\"\",\"petsDescription\":\"<ul> <li><strong>Pets are permitted<\\/strong>\\u00a0<strong>on all paved surfaces in the park:<\\/strong>\\u00a0campgrounds, parking lots, paved roads, paved viewpoint areas, on the paved trail between Sunset Point and Sunrise Point, and on the paved Shared Use Path between the park entrance and Inspiration Point.<\\/li> <li><strong>Pets are not permitted<\\/strong>\\u00a0on unpaved trails or viewpoints, in public buildings or on public transportation vehicles. These regulations also apply to pets that are carried.<\\/li> <li><strong>Pets must be on a leash at all times<\\/strong>; the leash must be no longer than 6 feet.<\\/li> <li><strong>Pet owners may not leave pet(s) unattended<\\/strong>\\u00a0or tied to an object. Pets may not be left in vehicles while their owners hike. Be aware that idling and generator use is not permitted in park parking lots.<\\/li> <li><strong>Pets may not make unreasonable noise.<\\/strong><\\/li> <li><strong>Pet owners are required to pick-up after their pets<\\/strong>\\u00a0(excrement and other solid waste).<\\/li> <\\/ul>\",\"timeOfDayDescription\":\"\",\"feeDescription\":\"<p>Park admission provides access to this activity.<\\/p>\",\"age\":\"\",\"arePetsPermittedWithRestrictions\":\"true\",\"activities\":[{\"id\":\"0B685688-3405-4E2A-ABBA-E3069492EC50\",\"name\":\"Wildlife Watching\"}],\"activityDescription\":\"\",\"locationDescription\":\"\",\"doFeesApply\":\"true\",\"longDescription\":\"<p>The canyons and plateau of Bryce Canyon National Park are home to many animals. Park boundaries mean little to the migratory hummingbirds, nesting Peregrine Falcon, Rocky Mountain Elk and Pronghorn which daily cross through the forested plateau and\\u00a0amphitheaters. The search for food and water leads them to the best place to find sustenance and shelter. Many animals share habitats. Ebb and flow of populations is interdependent on all the members of the wildlife community.<\\/p> <p><a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/brca\\/forteachers\\/upload\\/mrachecklistweb.pdf\\\">Mammals\\/Reptile List<\\/a><\\/p> <p>\\u00a0<\\/p> <h3>Safety and Respect<\\/h3> <ul> <li> <p>Treat wildlife with respect - you are a visitor in their home.<\\/p> <\\/li> <li> <p><strong>Never feed wild animals<\\/strong> - even a chipmunk can inflict a painful bite and possibly spread disease. Feeding them human food is also akin to animal cruelty in its likelihood to lead to maladaptive behaviors or even death.<\\/p> <\\/li> <li> <p>Keep a safe distance - use binoculars or, for photography, telephoto lenses.<\\/p> <\\/li> <li> <p>When venturing into venomous snake territory wear boots and loose-fitting pants.<\\/p> <\\/li> <li> <p>Stay on trails, and watch where you place your hands and feet \\u2013 especially when sitting, climbing, or stepping on or over fences, large rocks, and logs.<\\/p> <\\/li> <li> <p>Hang food, garbage and anything with strong odors (toothpaste, bug repellent, soap, etc.) at least 10 feet off the ground and 6 feet from the trunk or use a bear-proof container. Never store food inside your tent.<\\/p> <\\/li> <li> <p>If a bear or Mountain Lion approaches you back away slowly. Avoid direct eye contact with a bear but maintain eye contact with a cat. In either case do not run. Raise your arms above your head to make yourself look larger than normal, make noise, and throw rocks or sticks.<\\/p> <\\/li> <\\/ul> <h3>Seasons<\\/h3> <p>Spring comes late at these high elevations, with snow often remaining into April. Many of the park\'s inhabitants either hibernate or move to lower, less snow-covered elevations. While large mammals\\u00a0like Mule Deer can be seen year-round, pronghorn typically depart in fall and aren\'t seen again until snow has begun to melt in spring. Utah Prairie Dogs typically begin to depart underground for hibernation by November, and the first males are often seen reemerging in late February or early March. Other species are extremely uncommon--mountain lions are present but seldom seen, as are the park\'s black bears, which overwinter in dens and spend summers foraging in less-travelled forests. In warmer months, you\'re almost guaranteed to be confronted by a Unita chipmunk or golden-mantled ground squirrel scavenging near viewpoints or trail junctions. Remember to never feed or approach any wildlife in the park.\\u00a0<\\/p> <h3>Where to Look<\\/h3> <p><br \\/> <strong>Utah Prairie Dog Colonies:\\u00a0<\\/strong>A threatened species, in the 1970s the Utah Prairie Dog was\\u00a0expected to go extinct by the year 2000. Thankfully reintroduction efforts here in the park in the 1980s have stabliized the largest population of Utah\'s only endemic (only found here) mammal. The smallest of the prairie dogs, 8,000 feet (2,438 m) is about as high as they\'ll live. From March through October you can pretty realiably find them near the entrances to their underground burrows in the meadow that crosses the main road immediately south of the Visitor Center. Another colony is found on the right-hand side of the road around Mile 2 of the park road. A third colony can be seen on the way to Sunrise Point after taking the second left turn past the Visitor Center. Be sure to observe them from road pull-offs but never enter these meadows. Not only do prairie dogs potentially carry fleas infected with plague, they can deliver a very powerful bite!<br \\/> Prairie dogs provide food for most predators in the park, so observing a prairie dog colony is often the best way to see other species too. Listen for when the prairie dogs begin to chitter and \\\"bark\\\"--this may alert you to a predator along the treeline or soaring above.<br \\/> <br \\/> <strong>Southern Scenic Drive:\\u00a0<\\/strong>Travelling to Rainbow and Yovimpa Points at the southern end of the park takes you up to elevations of over 9,000 feet (2,743 m). At these high elevations, the park\'s ponderosa forests transition to mixed conifer woodlands of white fir, limber pine, douglas-fir, quaking aspen, and bristlecone pine. Snow levels are much higher here in winter, linger longer into spring, and provide more moisture for a dense understory. Wildlife is most often seen in meadows leading up to these higher views, such as the meadow around Mile 4 of the 18 mile park road. More often than not, wildlife sightings here are large mammals seen crossing the road.\\u00a0<br \\/> <br \\/> <strong>Bryce Amphitheater:\\u00a0<\\/strong>The Bryce Amphitheater is defined by its ponderosa forests and the mixed juniper and ponderosa woodland below the rim. Here one sees chipmunks and ground squirrels in warmer months, and often small families of mule deer foraging above and below the rim. If you\'re out stargazing, you may be so lucky as to spot a ringtail--they\'ve most often been seen near the Sunset Point parking lot.<\\/p>\",\"reservationDescription\":\"\",\"season\":[\"Winter\",\"Spring\",\"Summer\",\"Fall\"],\"topics\":[{\"id\":\"0D00073E-18C3-46E5-8727-2F87B112DDC6\",\"name\":\"Animals\"}],\"durationDescription\":\"\",\"arePetsPermitted\":\"true\",\"timeOfDay\":[\"Day\",\"Night\",\"Dawn\",\"Dusk\"],\"duration\":\"\",\"credit\":\"\"}',1,'2023-09-10 21:06:03','2023-09-10 21:06:33'),(351,'Birdwatching',NULL,'nps','A3A40059-56B9-4403-BA59-DB350F57AF8E',1,'\\r\\n\\r\\n','175 different species of birds have been documented to frequent Bryce Canyon National Park. Some are just passing through. Others stay for an entire season. Fewer still make this their year-round home, but those that do are charming and charismatic. Check out a species checklist for the park or recent eBird sightings within the park. Planning a visit in December? Consider joining us for our annual Christmas Bird Count!  Visual Guide for Common Songbirds Visual Guide for Additional Songbirds and Other Birds Visual Guide for Raptors and Large Birds Seasons Spring comes late at these high elevations, with snow often remaining into April. But by late February and early March western bluebirds and American robins are usually returning to the park from lower elevations. Warblers come a little later, often in late April or May, when yellow-rumped warblers, Grace\'s warblers, black-throated gray warblers and others begin to be heard. May through July is the busy breeding season within the park, and depending on the year various hummingbirds, such as black-chinned, broad-tailed, and rufuous can be seen visiting thistles and other flowers. The melodious songs of hermit thrushes, the ribbits of western tanagers, or the shreiks of green-tailed towhees or wood-pewees and flycatchers can be heard in ponderosa forests, but often fade by mid-August. The violet-green swallows and cliff swallows seen swooping over the Bryce Amphitheater have disappeared by October in most years. Eruptions of crossbills and Cassin\'s finches occur every few years in late summer and last through the winter. Come the colder months, year-round residents such as Townsend\'s solitaires, nuthatches, chickadees, juncos, woodpeckers, corvids, and raptors are most commonly seen. Where to Look Some species are common in all areas of the park. These include common ravens, Steller\'s jays, white-breasted nuthatches, Townsend\'s solitaires, downy and hairy woodpeckers, and dark-eyed juncos.  Southern Scenic Drive: Travelling to Rainbow and Yovimpa Points at the southern end of the park takes you up to elevations of over 9,000 feet (2,743 m). At these high elevations, the park\'s ponderosa forests transition to mixed conifer woodlands of white fir, limber pine, douglas-fir, quaking aspen, and bristlecone pine. Snow levels are much higher here in winter, linger longer into spring, and provide more moisture for a dense understory. Species seen more often in this area and along the Bristlecone Loop Trail include dusky grouse, peregrine falcon, Clark\'s nutcracker, green-tailed towhee, red-breasted nuthatches, pine siskins, and Cassin\'s finches.   Bryce Amphitheater: The Bryce Amphitheater is defined by its ponderosa forests. These provide a year-round home for pygmy nuthatches, which travel from tree to tree like squeaking fleas. Those with a sharp ear for higher frequencies and cryptic feathers may spot brown creepers here. In winter mixed flocks of chickadees, nuthatches, juncos, and creepers are not uncommon. In summer, violet-green swallows, white-throated swifts, and cliff swallows can be see swooping along the plateau rim. Owls, such as northern pygmy owls, reward those willing to wander the rim trail or quieter viewpoints like Paria View listening for the chiding chorus of songbirds. Below the rim, wrens, juncos, jays, and small flocks of bushtits can be found.  Mossy Cave: Mossy Cave provides one of the park\'s only consistent areas of water. Not a wetland, but essentially an irrigation stream travelling through the park, this area nevertheless protects a sometimes bird-rich area near Mossy Cave itself. Turn left at the waterfall junction and keep an eye out in the Gambel oak and Nootka rose for flycatchers and warblers.  Tropic Trail: A best-kept secret in the park, the Tropic Trail provides a quiet, densely vegetated area in the lowest elevations of the Bryce Amphitheater to encounter towhees, jays, nuthatches, juncos, and sometimes unexpected lower-elevation birds (our 2020 Christmas Bird Count encountered a beautiful acorn woodpecker here).  Outside the park: Check out the Rodeo, open fields, and waterpond areas in Bryce Canyon City just north of the park for an abundance of other species including larks, raptors, and sometimes sage grouse.',NULL,'Viewpoints provide paved, flat surfaces accessible to all. Trails will vary. Visit our Accessibility page for more information.',NULL,'1',NULL,NULL,NULL,'https://www.nps.gov/thingstodo/birdwatching.htm',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,1,NULL,'{\"id\":\"A3A40059-56B9-4403-BA59-DB350F57AF8E\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/thingstodo\\/birdwatching.htm\",\"title\":\"Birdwatching\",\"shortDescription\":\"175 different species of birds have been documented to frequent Bryce Canyon National Park. Some are just passing through. Others stay for an entire season. Fewer still make this their year-round home, but those that do are charming and charismatic.\",\"images\":[{\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/E486B36C-A6DC-C5C1-2A6317A61B20EE6F.jpg\",\"credit\":\"NPS Photo \\/ Keith Moore\",\"altText\":\"A blue and black bird with a black crest sits perched on a pine branch among green pine needles\",\"title\":\"Steller\'s Jay\",\"description\":\"\",\"caption\":\"Steller\'s Jays are one of the park\'s most common year-round bird species.\",\"crops\":[{\"aspectRatio\":\"1.78\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/primary\\/E486B36C-A6DC-C5C1-2A6317A61B20EE6F.jpg\"},{\"aspectRatio\":\"1\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/secondary\\/E486B36C-A6DC-C5C1-2A6317A61B20EE6F.jpg\"}]}],\"relatedParks\":[{\"states\":\"UT\",\"parkCode\":\"brca\",\"designation\":\"National Park\",\"fullName\":\"Bryce Canyon National Park\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/brca\\/index.htm\",\"name\":\"Bryce Canyon\"}],\"relatedOrganizations\":[],\"tags\":[\"birding\"],\"latitude\":\"\",\"longitude\":\"\",\"geometryPoiId\":\"\",\"amenities\":[],\"location\":\"\",\"seasonDescription\":\"\",\"accessibilityInformation\":\"<p>Viewpoints provide paved, flat surfaces accessible to all. Trails will vary.\\u00a0Visit our <a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/brca\\/planyourvisit\\/accessibility.htm\\\">Accessibility page<\\/a> for more information.<\\/p>\",\"isReservationRequired\":\"false\",\"ageDescription\":\"\",\"petsDescription\":\"<ul> <li><strong>Pets are permitted<\\/strong>\\u00a0<strong>on all paved surfaces in the park:<\\/strong>\\u00a0campgrounds, parking lots, paved roads, paved viewpoint areas, on the paved trail between Sunset Point and Sunrise Point, and on the paved Shared Use Path between the park entrance and Inspiration Point.<\\/li> <li><strong>Pets are not permitted<\\/strong>\\u00a0on unpaved trails or viewpoints, in public buildings or on public transportation vehicles. These regulations also apply to pets that are carried.<\\/li> <li><strong>Pets must be on a leash at all times<\\/strong>; the leash must be no longer than 6 feet.<\\/li> <li><strong>Pet owners may not leave pet(s) unattended<\\/strong>\\u00a0or tied to an object. Pets may not be left in vehicles while their owners hike. Be aware that idling and generator use is not permitted in park parking lots.<\\/li> <li><strong>Pets may not make unreasonable noise.<\\/strong><\\/li> <li><strong>Pet owners are required to pick-up after their pets<\\/strong>\\u00a0(excrement and other solid waste).<\\/li> <\\/ul>\",\"timeOfDayDescription\":\"\",\"feeDescription\":\"<p>Park admission provides access to these areas.<\\/p>\",\"age\":\"\",\"arePetsPermittedWithRestrictions\":\"true\",\"activities\":[{\"id\":\"5A2C91D1-50EC-4B24-8BED-A2E11A1892DF\",\"name\":\"Birdwatching\"}],\"activityDescription\":\"\",\"locationDescription\":\"\",\"doFeesApply\":\"true\",\"longDescription\":\"<p>175 different species of birds have been documented to frequent Bryce Canyon National Park. Some are just passing through. Others stay for an entire season. Fewer still make this their year-round home, but those that do are charming and charismatic.<br \\/> <a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/brca\\/learn\\/nature\\/upload\\/birdlistweb.pdf\\\">Check out a species checklist for the park<\\/a>\\u00a0or recent <a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/ebird.org\\/hotspot\\/L268297\\\">eBird sightings within the park<\\/a>. Planning a visit in December? Consider joining us for our annual <a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/brca\\/learn\\/nature\\/brcacbc.htm\\\">Christmas Bird Count<\\/a>!<br \\/> <br \\/> <a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/thingstodo\\/upload\\/FieldGuide_Songbirds_BRCA_Page_1.jpg\\\">Visual Guide for Common Songbirds<\\/a><br \\/> <a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/thingstodo\\/upload\\/FieldGuide_Additional_Birds_BRCA_Page_1.jpg\\\">Visual Guide for Additional Songbirds and Other Birds<\\/a><br \\/> <a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/thingstodo\\/upload\\/FieldGuide_Raptors_BRCA_Page_1.jpg\\\">Visual Guide for Raptors and Large Birds<\\/a><\\/p> <h3>Seasons<\\/h3> <p>Spring comes late at these high elevations, with snow often remaining into April. But\\u00a0by late February and early March western bluebirds and American robins are usually returning to the park from lower elevations. Warblers come a little later, often in late April or May, when yellow-rumped warblers, Grace\'s warblers, black-throated gray warblers and others begin to be heard. May through July is the busy breeding season within the park, and depending on the year various hummingbirds, such as black-chinned, broad-tailed, and rufuous can be seen visiting thistles and other flowers. The melodious songs of hermit thrushes, the ribbits of western tanagers, or the shreiks of green-tailed towhees or wood-pewees and flycatchers can be heard in ponderosa forests, but often fade by mid-August. The violet-green swallows and cliff swallows seen swooping over the Bryce Amphitheater have disappeared by October in most years. Eruptions of crossbills and Cassin\'s finches occur every few years in late summer and last through the winter. Come the colder months, year-round residents such as Townsend\'s solitaires, nuthatches, chickadees, juncos, woodpeckers, corvids, and raptors are most commonly seen.<\\/p> <h3>Where to Look<\\/h3> <p>Some species are common in all areas of the park. These include common ravens, Steller\'s jays, white-breasted nuthatches, Townsend\'s solitaires, downy and hairy woodpeckers, and dark-eyed juncos.<br \\/> <br \\/> <strong>Southern Scenic Drive: <\\/strong>Travelling to Rainbow and Yovimpa Points at the southern end of the park takes you up to elevations of over 9,000 feet (2,743 m). At these high elevations, the park\'s ponderosa forests transition to mixed conifer woodlands of white fir, limber pine, douglas-fir, quaking aspen, and bristlecone pine. Snow levels are much higher here in winter, linger longer into spring, and provide more moisture for a dense understory. Species seen more often in this area and along the Bristlecone Loop Trail include dusky grouse, peregrine falcon, Clark\'s nutcracker, green-tailed towhee, red-breasted nuthatches, pine siskins, and Cassin\'s finches.\\u00a0<br \\/> <br \\/> <strong>Bryce Amphitheater: <\\/strong>The Bryce Amphitheater is defined by its ponderosa forests. These provide a year-round home for pygmy nuthatches, which travel from tree to tree like squeaking fleas. Those with a sharp ear for higher frequencies and cryptic feathers may spot brown creepers here. In winter mixed flocks of chickadees, nuthatches, juncos, and creepers are not uncommon. In summer, violet-green swallows, white-throated swifts, and cliff swallows can be see swooping along the plateau rim. Owls, such as northern pygmy owls, reward those willing to wander the rim trail or quieter viewpoints like Paria View listening for the chiding chorus of songbirds. Below the rim, wrens, juncos, jays, and small flocks of bushtits can be found.<br \\/> <br \\/> <strong>Mossy Cave: <\\/strong>Mossy Cave provides one of the park\'s only consistent areas of water. Not a wetland, but essentially an irrigation stream travelling through the park, this area nevertheless protects a sometimes bird-rich area near Mossy Cave itself. Turn left at the waterfall junction and keep an eye out in the Gambel oak and Nootka rose for flycatchers and warblers.<br \\/> <br \\/> <strong>Tropic Trail: <\\/strong>A best-kept secret in the park, the Tropic Trail provides a quiet, densely vegetated area in the lowest elevations of the Bryce Amphitheater to encounter towhees, jays, nuthatches, juncos, and sometimes unexpected lower-elevation birds (our 2020 Christmas Bird Count encountered a beautiful acorn woodpecker here).<br \\/> <br \\/> <strong>Outside the park:<\\/strong> Check out the Rodeo, open fields, and waterpond areas in Bryce Canyon City just north of the park for an abundance of other species including larks, raptors, and sometimes sage grouse.<\\/p>\",\"reservationDescription\":\"\",\"season\":[\"Winter\",\"Spring\",\"Summer\",\"Fall\"],\"topics\":[{\"id\":\"957EF2BD-AC6C-4B7B-BD9A-87593ADC6691\",\"name\":\"Birds\"}],\"durationDescription\":\"\",\"arePetsPermitted\":\"true\",\"timeOfDay\":[\"Day\",\"Night\",\"Dawn\",\"Dusk\"],\"duration\":\"\",\"credit\":\"\"}',1,'2023-09-10 21:06:03','2023-09-10 21:06:33'),(352,'Go Snowshoeing',NULL,'nps','C1645ADA-D194-4FA7-AE46-D4729A732CAD',1,'\\r\\n\\r\\n','When the park has at least 12 inches of snow, snowshoeing is an excellent way to explore it. Snowshoeing is allowed throughout the park on all trails. But be aware that though snowshoes make it possible to travel through deep powdery snow, snowshoeing is still a highly strenuous activity. Most snowshoers enjoy walks along the Rim Trail, Bristlecone Loop, Fairyland Road, and Paria Road.  Don\'t have your own snowshoes? Join a Ranger Program! Ranger-led snowshoe hikes are held when the park has at least 12-14\" of snow and are designed for all levels of experience, from beginner to expert. Snowshoes and poles are available for free for those who sign up for these programs, which are provided when snow depth and staffing permit. Ranger-led Snowshoe Hike Basics     Time   Usually 2 hours     Duration   Approximately 1 mile (1.6 km)     Attendance Fee   Free     Minimum Age   8 years old +     Required Equipment   Winter clothing, water, and waterproof boots (Snowshoes, poles provided). Tennis shoes will not be permitted.      How to Sign Up When programs are offered, registration is required as group size is limited and hikes are subject to cancellation. When snow depth and weather permit, sign-up will be offered at the Visitor Center the day of the program beginning at 8 a.m. and continuing until full. No advance sign-up or reservations are available. Snowshoe and Cross-country Ski Rentals Snowshoes and crosscountry skis are available for rental outside the park at Ruby\'s Inn in Bryce Canyon City. Full Moon Snoeshoe Hikes One of the rarest ranger programs is a full moon snowshoe hike. Both the full moon date and the weather have to cooperate to create the necessary conditions. Review the Required Equipment for a snowshoe hike, then check out the full moon hike calendar.',NULL,'This is not an inherently accessible activity, but any and all possible accomodations will be made.',NULL,'1',NULL,NULL,NULL,'https://www.nps.gov/thingstodo/go-snowshoeing.htm',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,1,NULL,'{\"id\":\"C1645ADA-D194-4FA7-AE46-D4729A732CAD\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/thingstodo\\/go-snowshoeing.htm\",\"title\":\"Go Snowshoeing\",\"shortDescription\":\"Snowshoeing is allowed throughout the park on all trails. But be aware that though snowshoes make it possible to travel through deep powdery snow, snowshoeing is still a highly strenuous activity. Most snowshoers enjoy walks along the Rim Trail, Bristlecone Loop, Fairyland Road, and Paria Road.\",\"images\":[{\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/22E975E1-D726-16A7-30636AA0D90FFE31.jpg\",\"credit\":\"NPS Photo \\/ Peter Densmore\",\"altText\":\"A group of people in snowshoes stand on the rim of a limestone spire-lined canyon\",\"title\":\"Paria Snowshoe Hike\",\"description\":\"\",\"caption\":\"Ranger-led Snowshoe hikes provide you with snowshoes and a deeper understanding of the park.\",\"crops\":[{\"aspectRatio\":\"1.78\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/primary\\/22E975E1-D726-16A7-30636AA0D90FFE31.jpg\"},{\"aspectRatio\":\"1\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/secondary\\/22E975E1-D726-16A7-30636AA0D90FFE31.jpg\"}]}],\"relatedParks\":[{\"states\":\"UT\",\"parkCode\":\"brca\",\"designation\":\"National Park\",\"fullName\":\"Bryce Canyon National Park\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/brca\\/index.htm\",\"name\":\"Bryce Canyon\"}],\"relatedOrganizations\":[],\"tags\":[\"snowshoeing\"],\"latitude\":\"\",\"longitude\":\"\",\"geometryPoiId\":\"\",\"amenities\":[],\"location\":\"\",\"seasonDescription\":\"\",\"accessibilityInformation\":\"<p>This is not an inherently accessible activity, but any and all possible accomodations will be made.<\\/p>\",\"isReservationRequired\":\"true\",\"ageDescription\":\"<p>While there is no age limit to go snowshoeing in the park yourself, ranger-led snowshoe hikes are for ages 8 and older.<\\/p>\",\"petsDescription\":\"\",\"timeOfDayDescription\":\"\",\"feeDescription\":\"<p>Park admission provides access to this activity. Snowshoe rentals outside the park rates may vary, but snowshoes on ranger-led snowshoe hikes are provided for free.<\\/p>\",\"age\":\"8\",\"arePetsPermittedWithRestrictions\":\"false\",\"activities\":[{\"id\":\"01D717BC-18BB-4FE4-95BA-6B13AD702038\",\"name\":\"Snowshoeing\"}],\"activityDescription\":\"\",\"locationDescription\":\"\",\"doFeesApply\":\"true\",\"longDescription\":\"<p>When the park has at least 12 inches of snow, snowshoeing is an excellent way to explore it. Snowshoeing is allowed throughout the park on all trails. But be aware that though snowshoes make it possible to travel through deep powdery snow, snowshoeing is still a highly strenuous activity.<br \\/> Most snowshoers enjoy walks along the <strong>Rim Trail, Bristlecone Loop, Fairyland Road, and Paria Road.<\\/strong><br \\/> <br \\/> Don\'t have your own snowshoes? Join a\\u00a0<a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/brca\\/planyourvisit\\/ranger-programs.htm\\\">Ranger Program<\\/a>! Ranger-led snowshoe hikes are held when the park has at least 12-14\\\" of snow and are designed for all levels of experience, from beginner to expert. Snowshoes and poles are available for free for those who sign up for these programs, which are provided when snow depth and staffing permit.<\\/p> <h4>Ranger-led Snowshoe Hike Basics<\\/h4> <table border=\\\"1\\\" cellpadding=\\\"2\\\" cellspacing=\\\"2\\\" summary=\\\"Table Summary\\\" width=\\\"100%\\\"> <tbody> <tr> <td> <p><strong>Time<\\/strong><\\/p> <\\/td> <td> <p>Usually 2 hours<\\/p> <\\/td> <\\/tr> <tr> <td> <p><strong>Duration<\\/strong><\\/p> <\\/td> <td> <p>Approximately 1 mile (1.6 km)<\\/p> <\\/td> <\\/tr> <tr> <td> <p><strong>Attendance Fee<\\/strong><\\/p> <\\/td> <td> <p>Free<\\/p> <\\/td> <\\/tr> <tr> <td> <p><strong>Minimum Age<\\/strong><\\/p> <\\/td> <td> <p>8 years old +<\\/p> <\\/td> <\\/tr> <tr> <td> <p><strong>Required Equipment<\\/strong><\\/p> <\\/td> <td> <p>Winter clothing, water, and waterproof boots (Snowshoes, poles provided). Tennis shoes will not be permitted.<\\/p> <\\/td> <\\/tr> <\\/tbody> <\\/table> <p><br \\/> <strong>How to Sign Up<\\/strong><\\/p> <p><a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/brca\\/planyourvisit\\/calendar.htm\\\">When programs are offered<\\/a>, registration is required as group size is limited and hikes are subject to cancellation. When snow depth and weather permit, sign-up will be offered at the Visitor Center the day of the program beginning at 8 a.m. and continuing until full. No advance sign-up or reservations are available.<\\/p> <h4>Snowshoe and Cross-country Ski Rentals<\\/h4> <p>Snowshoes and crosscountry skis are available for rental outside the park at Ruby\'s Inn in Bryce Canyon City.<\\/p> <h4>Full Moon Snoeshoe Hikes<\\/h4> <p>One of the rarest ranger programs is a full moon snowshoe hike. Both the full moon date and the weather have to cooperate to create the necessary conditions. Review the Required Equipment for a snowshoe hike, then check out the\\u00a0<a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/brca\\/planyourvisit\\/fullmoonhikes.htm\\\">full moon hike calendar<\\/a>.<\\/p>\",\"reservationDescription\":\"<p>Sign up for ranger-led snowshoe hikes the day of the hike beginning at 8 a.m. at the Visitor Center. Sign-up closes at noon for 1 p.m. hikes.<\\/p>\",\"season\":[\"Winter\"],\"topics\":[{\"id\":\"5BE55D7F-BDB6-4E3D-AC35-2D8EBB974417\",\"name\":\"Trails\"}],\"durationDescription\":\"\",\"arePetsPermitted\":\"false\",\"timeOfDay\":[\"Day\",\"Night\",\"Dawn\",\"Dusk\"],\"duration\":\"\",\"credit\":\"\"}',1,'2023-09-10 21:06:03','2023-09-10 21:06:33'),(353,'Explore the Visitor Center Museum','Visitor Center','nps','01483EED-F911-4FF8-B773-0E7D877F63A9',1,'\\r\\n\\r\\n','The park museum is found within the Visitor Center and contains exhibits focused on scales of time: Geological, Ecological, Human, and with special exhibits on the Night Sky and Air Quality. Jr. Ranger If you\'re working on your Jr. Ranger book, we highly recommend a visit to the museum. Answers to many activities (especially the Prairie Dog crossword) can be found here! Native Perspectives Video In the Human History area of the museum a 24 minute video plays sharing Native perspectives of this area. Watch the video.',NULL,'Two moderately sloping ramps lead from the parking lot to the Visitor Center. The building is equipped with a lowered information desk and fully accessible auditorium with a captioned 24-minute film. Assisted Listening and Audio Description devices are available at the front desk upon request. Restrooms have fully-accessible stalls.','15-60 Minutes','1',37.6400770,-112.1696690,NULL,'https://www.nps.gov/thingstodo/explore-the-visitor-center-museum.htm',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,1,NULL,'{\"id\":\"01483EED-F911-4FF8-B773-0E7D877F63A9\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/thingstodo\\/explore-the-visitor-center-museum.htm\",\"title\":\"Explore the Visitor Center Museum\",\"shortDescription\":\"The park museum is found within the Visitor Center and contains exhibits focused on scales of time: Geological, Ecological, Human, and with special exhibits on the Night Sky and Air Quality.\",\"images\":[{\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/1FDF5B85-091D-F31D-3AE78CD6503B8440.jpg\",\"credit\":\"NPS Photo \\/ Peter Densmore\",\"altText\":\"A couple stands among other people examining a relief map within the park museum\",\"title\":\"Visitors in Museum\",\"description\":\"\",\"caption\":\"The museum provides an opportunity to explore different scales of time in the park.\",\"crops\":[{\"aspectRatio\":\"1.78\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/primary\\/1FDF5B85-091D-F31D-3AE78CD6503B8440.jpg\"},{\"aspectRatio\":\"1\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/secondary\\/1FDF5B85-091D-F31D-3AE78CD6503B8440.jpg\"}]}],\"relatedParks\":[{\"states\":\"UT\",\"parkCode\":\"brca\",\"designation\":\"National Park\",\"fullName\":\"Bryce Canyon National Park\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/brca\\/index.htm\",\"name\":\"Bryce Canyon\"}],\"relatedOrganizations\":[],\"tags\":[\"Museum\"],\"latitude\":\"37.640077\",\"longitude\":\"-112.169669\",\"geometryPoiId\":\"\",\"amenities\":[],\"location\":\"Visitor Center\",\"seasonDescription\":\"\",\"accessibilityInformation\":\"<p>Two moderately sloping ramps lead from the parking lot to the Visitor Center. The building is equipped with a lowered information desk and fully accessible auditorium with a captioned 24-minute film. Assisted Listening and Audio Description devices are available at the front desk upon request. Restrooms have fully-accessible stalls.<\\/p>\",\"isReservationRequired\":\"false\",\"ageDescription\":\"\",\"petsDescription\":\"\",\"timeOfDayDescription\":\"<p>Museum is open during <a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/brca\\/planyourvisit\\/hours.htm\\\">Visitor Center hours.<\\/a><\\/p>\",\"feeDescription\":\"<p>Park admission provides access to this activity.<\\/p>\",\"age\":\"\",\"arePetsPermittedWithRestrictions\":\"false\",\"activities\":[{\"id\":\"C8F98B28-3C10-41AE-AA99-092B3B398C43\",\"name\":\"Museum Exhibits\"}],\"activityDescription\":\"\",\"locationDescription\":\"\",\"doFeesApply\":\"true\",\"longDescription\":\"<p>The park museum is found within the Visitor Center and contains exhibits focused on scales of time: Geological, Ecological, Human, and with special exhibits on the Night Sky and Air Quality.<\\/p> <h4>Jr. Ranger<\\/h4> <p>If you\'re working on your Jr. Ranger book, we highly recommend a visit to the museum. Answers to many activities (especially the Prairie Dog crossword) can be found here!<\\/p> <h4>Native Perspectives Video<\\/h4> <p>In the Human History area of the museum a 24 minute video plays sharing Native perspectives of this area. <a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/brca\\/learn\\/historyculture\\/americanindianhistory.htm\\\">Watch the video.<\\/a><\\/p>\",\"reservationDescription\":\"\",\"season\":[\"Winter\",\"Spring\",\"Summer\",\"Fall\"],\"topics\":[{\"id\":\"0D00073E-18C3-46E5-8727-2F87B112DDC6\",\"name\":\"Animals\"},{\"id\":\"F0F97E32-2F29-41B4-AF98-9FBE8DAB36B1\",\"name\":\"Geology\"},{\"id\":\"A1BAF33E-EA84-4608-A888-4CEE9541F027\",\"name\":\"Native American Heritage\"},{\"id\":\"F8C2FE93-DEB3-4B12-9A87-913E3E6B448D\",\"name\":\"Natural Sounds\"},{\"id\":\"A7359FC4-DAD8-45F5-AF15-7FF62F816ED3\",\"name\":\"Night Sky\"}],\"durationDescription\":\"\",\"arePetsPermitted\":\"false\",\"timeOfDay\":[\"Day\"],\"duration\":\"15-60 Minutes\",\"credit\":\"\"}',1,'2023-09-10 21:06:03','2023-09-10 21:06:33'),(354,'Walk the Rim Trail','Sunset Point','nps','286F2EC1-CE0F-4D59-A62E-243F3D71F87C',1,'Access to the Rim Trail is found at Bryce Point, Inspiration Point, Sunset Point, Sunrise Point, and Fairyland Point.\\r\\n\\r\\n','The Rim Trail stretches from its beginning in the south at Bryce Point 5.5 miles (8.9 km) north to Fairyland Point. As it follows the edge of the Bryce Amphitheater area of the park, it connects to Inspiration Point, Sunset Point, and Sunrise Point providing a relatively easy hike (depending on how much of it you do) that offers spectacular views of the park\'s most popular area. Recommended: Sunset to Sunrise The easiest hike in the park is the section of the Rim Trail that connects Sunset and Sunrise Points. It is 0.5 miles (0.8 km) long and walkers can begin from either Sunrise or Sunset Point. Elevation gain is 34 feet (10 meters). We recommend hiking from one point and back for a very easy 1 mile (1.6 km) walk.  Winter Closures In winter the 1.5 mile (2.4 km) section of the Rim Trail from Bryce Point to Inspiration Point is regularly closed. Section Lengths Difficulty: Varies (Easy to Moderate) Total Distance: 5.5 miles (8.9 km) Ascent/Descent (South to North): 1,330 feet (405 m) / 1,825 feet (556 m) Average time: 3-4 hours  Bryce to Inspiration: 1.5 miles (2.4 km) Inspiration to Sunset: 0.7 miles (1.1 km) Sunset to Sunrise: 0.5 miles (0.8 km) Sunrise to Fairyland 2.8 miles (4.5 km)  Riding the Shuttle During shuttle season, hikers may use stops at Bryce, Inspiration, Sunset, and Sunrise Points to complete one-way hikes. Fairyland Point has no shuttle service.',NULL,'Because of the park\'s natural terrain, only the 0.5 mile (0.8 km) section of the Rim Trail between Sunset and Sunrise Points is wheelchair accessible.','1-6 Hours','1',37.6225820,-112.1659620,NULL,'https://www.nps.gov/thingstodo/walk-the-rim-trail.htm',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,1,NULL,'{\"id\":\"286F2EC1-CE0F-4D59-A62E-243F3D71F87C\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/thingstodo\\/walk-the-rim-trail.htm\",\"title\":\"Walk the Rim Trail\",\"shortDescription\":\"The Rim Trail stretches from its beginning in the south at Bryce Point 5.5 miles (8.9 km) north to Fairyland Point. As it follows the edge of the Bryce Amphitheater area of the park,\\u00a0it connects to Inspiration Point, Sunset Point, and Sunrise Point providing a relatively easy hike (depending on how much of it you do) that offers spectacular views of the park\'s most popular area.\",\"images\":[{\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/7E526C54-E05E-B9EF-9E62A9BCBEE1FAF8.jpg\",\"credit\":\"NPS Photo \\/ Peter Densmore\",\"altText\":\"Two hikers pause along a path to view a red rock landscape of badlands and spires at a cliff\'s edge\",\"title\":\"HIkers along the Rim Trail\",\"description\":\"\",\"caption\":\"The Rim Trail provides views all along the edge of the Bryce Amphitheater and connects viewpoints\",\"crops\":[{\"aspectRatio\":\"1.78\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/primary\\/7E526C54-E05E-B9EF-9E62A9BCBEE1FAF8.jpg\"},{\"aspectRatio\":\"1\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/secondary\\/7E526C54-E05E-B9EF-9E62A9BCBEE1FAF8.jpg\"}]}],\"relatedParks\":[{\"states\":\"UT\",\"parkCode\":\"brca\",\"designation\":\"National Park\",\"fullName\":\"Bryce Canyon National Park\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/brca\\/index.htm\",\"name\":\"Bryce Canyon\"}],\"relatedOrganizations\":[],\"tags\":[\"rim trail\"],\"latitude\":\"37.622582\",\"longitude\":\"-112.165962\",\"geometryPoiId\":\"\",\"amenities\":[],\"location\":\"Sunset Point\",\"seasonDescription\":\"<p>Those visiting in winter may find icy or snowy conditions and should dress accordingly. Footwear traction devices are highly recommended for all areas of the park in winter.<\\/p>\",\"accessibilityInformation\":\"<p>Because of the park\'s natural terrain, only the 0.5 mile (0.8 km) section of the Rim Trail between Sunset and Sunrise Points is wheelchair accessible.<\\/p>\",\"isReservationRequired\":\"false\",\"ageDescription\":\"\",\"petsDescription\":\"<ul> <li><strong>Pets are permitted<\\/strong>\\u00a0<strong>on all paved surfaces in the park:<\\/strong>\\u00a0campgrounds, parking lots, paved roads, paved viewpoint areas, on the paved trail between Sunset Point and Sunrise Point, and on the paved Shared Use Path between the park entrance and Inspiration Point.<\\/li> <li><strong>Pets are not permitted<\\/strong>\\u00a0on unpaved trails or viewpoints, in public buildings or on public transportation vehicles. These regulations also apply to pets that are carried.<\\/li> <li><strong>Pets must be on a leash at all times<\\/strong>; the leash must be no longer than 6 feet.<\\/li> <li><strong>Pet owners may not leave pet(s) unattended<\\/strong>\\u00a0or tied to an object. Pets may not be left in vehicles while their owners hike. Be aware that idling and generator use is not permitted in park parking lots.<\\/li> <li><strong>Pets may not make unreasonable noise.<\\/strong><\\/li> <li><strong>Pet owners are required to pick-up after their pets<\\/strong>\\u00a0(excrement and other solid waste).<\\/li> <\\/ul>\",\"timeOfDayDescription\":\"\",\"feeDescription\":\"<p>Park admission provides access to this activity.<\\/p>\",\"age\":\"\",\"arePetsPermittedWithRestrictions\":\"false\",\"activities\":[{\"id\":\"BFF8C027-7C8F-480B-A5F8-CD8CE490BFBA\",\"name\":\"Hiking\"}],\"activityDescription\":\"\",\"locationDescription\":\"<p>Access to the Rim Trail is found at Bryce Point, Inspiration Point, Sunset Point, Sunrise Point, and Fairyland Point.<\\/p>\",\"doFeesApply\":\"true\",\"longDescription\":\"<p>The Rim Trail stretches from its beginning in the south at Bryce Point 5.5 miles (8.9 km) north to Fairyland Point. As it follows the edge of the Bryce Amphitheater area of the park,\\u00a0it connects to Inspiration Point, Sunset Point, and Sunrise Point providing a relatively easy hike (depending on how much of it you do) that offers spectacular views of the park\'s most popular area.<\\/p> <h3>Recommended: Sunset to Sunrise<\\/h3> <p>The easiest hike in the park is the section of the Rim Trail that connects Sunset and Sunrise Points. It is 0.5 miles (0.8 km) long and walkers can begin from either Sunrise or Sunset Point. Elevation gain is 34 feet (10 meters). We recommend hiking from one point and back for a very easy 1 mile (1.6 km) walk.\\u00a0<\\/p> <h3>Winter Closures<\\/h3> <p>In winter the 1.5 mile (2.4 km) section of the Rim Trail from Bryce Point to Inspiration Point is\\u00a0regularly closed.<\\/p> <h3>Section Lengths<\\/h3> <p><em><strong>Difficulty: Varies (Easy to Moderate)<br \\/> Total Distance: 5.5\\u00a0miles (8.9\\u00a0km)<br \\/> Ascent\\/Descent (South to North): 1,330\\u00a0feet (405\\u00a0m) \\/ 1,825 feet (556 m)<br \\/> Average time: 3-4 hours<\\/strong><\\/em><\\/p> <ul> <li>Bryce to Inspiration: 1.5 miles (2.4 km)<\\/li> <li>Inspiration to Sunset: 0.7\\u00a0miles (1.1\\u00a0km)<\\/li> <li>Sunset to Sunrise: 0.5 miles\\u00a0(0.8 km)<\\/li> <li>Sunrise to Fairyland 2.8 miles (4.5 km)<\\/li> <\\/ul> <h3>Riding the Shuttle<\\/h3> <p>During shuttle season, hikers may use stops at Bryce,\\u00a0Inspiration, Sunset, and Sunrise Points to complete one-way hikes. Fairyland Point has no shuttle service.<\\/p>\",\"reservationDescription\":\"\",\"season\":[\"Winter\",\"Spring\",\"Summer\",\"Fall\"],\"topics\":[{\"id\":\"5BE55D7F-BDB6-4E3D-AC35-2D8EBB974417\",\"name\":\"Trails\"}],\"durationDescription\":\"<p>Most visitors will enjoy about an hour walk by travelling the flat surface from Sunrise to Sunset Point (or vice versa) and back.<\\/p>\",\"arePetsPermitted\":\"true\",\"timeOfDay\":[],\"duration\":\"1-6 Hours\",\"credit\":\"\"}',1,'2023-09-10 21:06:03','2023-09-10 21:06:33'),(355,'Hike the South Rim Trail','Point Supreme','nps','37AEC41C-7261-4E82-B65A-83D1E1835B84',7,'The South Rim Trail begins at the Point Supreme parking lot.\\r\\n\\r\\nThe South Rim Trail offers one of the best hiking experiences at Cedar Breaks. The trail follows the winding edge of the amphitheater offering one of a kind opportunities to see hoodoos, arches, waterfalls, and much more.','A hike along the South Rim Trail can be one of the best experiences at Cedar Breaks National Monument. The trail follows the rim of the Cedar Breaks Amphitheater from Point Supreme to the Bartizan. Along the way, there are several scenic viewpoints that offer unique perspectives of the formations within the amphitheater.',NULL,'The trail is a narrow dirt path with frequently changing elevation including steep portions which water-breaks or small stairs are built to limit erosion.','1-4 Hours','1',NULL,NULL,NULL,'https://www.nps.gov/thingstodo/south-rim-trail.htm',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,1,NULL,'{\"id\":\"37AEC41C-7261-4E82-B65A-83D1E1835B84\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/thingstodo\\/south-rim-trail.htm\",\"title\":\"Hike the South Rim Trail\",\"shortDescription\":\"The South Rim Trail is the a moderate to hard trail, featuring several different scenic viewpoints.\",\"images\":[{\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/87883164-99FA-A5C5-4DBFBDC70D348500.jpg\",\"credit\":\"NPS\\/Everitt\",\"altText\":\"A dirt trail winds along the rim of a geologic amphitheater to the left.\",\"title\":\"South Rim Trail\",\"description\":\"\",\"caption\":\"The 5-mile (roundtrip) South Rim Trail is one of the best ways to experience the breaks.\",\"crops\":[{\"aspectRatio\":\"1.78\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/primary\\/87883164-99FA-A5C5-4DBFBDC70D348500.jpg\"},{\"aspectRatio\":\"1\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/secondary\\/87883164-99FA-A5C5-4DBFBDC70D348500.jpg\"}]}],\"relatedParks\":[{\"states\":\"UT\",\"parkCode\":\"cebr\",\"designation\":\"National Monument\",\"fullName\":\"Cedar Breaks National Monument\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/cebr\\/index.htm\",\"name\":\"Cedar Breaks\"}],\"relatedOrganizations\":[],\"tags\":[\"cedar breaks national monument\",\"cedar breaks\",\"trail\",\"hiking\",\"Utah\"],\"latitude\":\"\",\"longitude\":\"\",\"geometryPoiId\":\"\",\"amenities\":[],\"location\":\"Point Supreme\",\"seasonDescription\":\"The South Rim Trail is best experienced during the summer. Cool temperatures at high elevation keep this trail and the park cooler than surrounding hiking opportunities. During the spring and fall, snow and ice are possible which can make the trail hazardous and muddy. During the winter, heavy snowfall covers the park - visitors are strongly encouraged to stay away from the rim of the amphitheater during the winter for their safety.\",\"accessibilityInformation\":\"The trail is a narrow dirt path with frequently changing elevation including steep portions which water-breaks or small stairs are built to limit erosion.\",\"isReservationRequired\":\"false\",\"ageDescription\":\"All ages are welcome on this trail, but minors must be with an adult parent or guardian as the trail features steep drop-offs at several locations.\",\"petsDescription\":\"Pets are not permitted on the South Rim Trail. Please visit our <a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/cebr\\/planyourvisit\\/pets.htm\\\">Pets page<\\/a> for more information.\",\"timeOfDayDescription\":\"\",\"feeDescription\":\"There is an entrance fee for Cedar Breaks National Monument that all visitors are required to pay upon entry, please visit our <a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/cebr\\/planyourvisit\\/fees.htm\\\">Fees & Passes page<\\/a> for more information.\",\"age\":\"\",\"arePetsPermittedWithRestrictions\":\"false\",\"activities\":[{\"id\":\"BFF8C027-7C8F-480B-A5F8-CD8CE490BFBA\",\"name\":\"Hiking\"}],\"activityDescription\":\"The South Rim Trail offers one of the best hiking experiences at Cedar Breaks. The trail follows the winding edge of the amphitheater offering one of a kind opportunities to see hoodoos, arches, waterfalls, and much more.\",\"locationDescription\":\"The South Rim Trail begins at the Point Supreme parking lot.\",\"doFeesApply\":\"true\",\"longDescription\":\"A hike along the South Rim Trail can be one of the best experiences at Cedar Breaks National Monument. The trail follows the rim of the Cedar Breaks Amphitheater from Point Supreme to the Bartizan. Along the way, there are several scenic viewpoints that offer unique perspectives of the formations within the amphitheater.\",\"reservationDescription\":\"\",\"season\":[\"Summer\"],\"topics\":[{\"id\":\"F0F97E32-2F29-41B4-AF98-9FBE8DAB36B1\",\"name\":\"Geology\"},{\"id\":\"4244F489-6813-4B7A-9D0C-20CE098C8FFF\",\"name\":\"Rock Landscapes and Features\"},{\"id\":\"72E87FCE-B48F-48E8-8761-4849A79736AF\",\"name\":\"Arches\"},{\"id\":\"9C9FCBB6-360B-4743-8155-6F9341CBE01B\",\"name\":\"Scenic Views\"},{\"id\":\"5BE55D7F-BDB6-4E3D-AC35-2D8EBB974417\",\"name\":\"Trails\"}],\"durationDescription\":\"Depending on how much time you have, there are several different options for hiking the South Rim Trail. Just getting up to the rim and walking for a short bit will provide you with a completely different perspective than the roadside overlooks. There are three primary points of interest you can use as mile-markers or decision points. <ol> <li>Hiking to Spectra Point and back to the trailhead is two miles round trip.<\\/li> <li>Hiking to the Ramparts Overlook and back to the trailhead is four miles round trip.<\\/li> <li>Hiking to the end of the trail at the Bartizen is the longest option at five miles round trip.<\\/li> <\\/ol>\",\"arePetsPermitted\":\"false\",\"timeOfDay\":[\"Day\"],\"duration\":\"1-4 Hours\",\"credit\":\"\"}',1,'2023-09-10 21:06:04','2023-09-10 21:06:33');
INSERT INTO `trails` (`id`, `name`, `name_alt`, `source_type`, `source_id`, `area_id`, `description`, `description_alt`, `directions`, `accessibility`, `duration`, `pets_allowed`, `latitude`, `longitude`, `features`, `url`, `contact_url`, `contact_name`, `season`, `difficulty`, `elevation_high_ft`, `elevation_low_ft`, `vehicle`, `distance_miles`, `distance_type`, `average_hours`, `incline_ft`, `decline_ft`, `average_grade_pt`, `max_grade_pt`, `reservation`, `fee`, `gpx_url`, `raw_data`, `is_usable`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (356,'Driving the Hole in the Rock Road','Hole-in-the-Rock Road, Escalante District','nps','B474045A-78F4-4557-8D3F-71219B8CC00B',4,'Starts at the Junction of Us Hwy 12 and Hole-in-the-Rock Rd south of Escalante UT. Mile by Mile Guide This guide will help point out areas of historical and scenic interest along the road. Mileages indicated do not allow for side trips. Be aware that your odometer may not register exactly the same mileage listed here. Please keep this in mind as you drive. Mile 0.0, Junction of Hole-in-the-Rock road and Highway 12: The town of Escalante, a few miles west of this junction, provided the last chance for the pioneers to obtain food and supplies and to make repairs on their equipment. Mile 4.2, Ten Mile Spring: A small seep where the first major camp was established after departing from Escalante. Mile 12.0, Devil\'s Garden: About 1/2 mile southwest from this road, one can observe spectacular rock formations. This site has been designated as an Outstanding Natural Area by the Bureau of Land Management. Always carry water when walking in the desert! Mile 14.0, Twenty Mile Spring: At this site, now called Collett Wash, water was so scarce the pioneers had to dig in the sand to obtain sufficient quantities for survival. Mile 36.4, Dance Hall Rock: This is a large, solid sandstone amphitheater. The pioneers set up a base camp near here and held dances in the natural theater, accompanied by violins, to keep the morale up. Mile 37.1, Forty Mile Spring: This area served as a base camp and rendezvous point, since the well-used road ended here. A member of the advance party of scouts, Platt D. Lyman, commented on the terrain that lay directly ahead: \"It is certainly the worst country I ever saw . . . \" Mile 39.1, Carcass Wash: A major obstacle for the expedition, as well as the site of a terrible accident that took the lives of 13 Boy Scouts on June 10, 1963. Brake failure on a truck was blamed. Mile 45.7, Glen Canyon National Recreation Area Boundary: You have left Bureau of Land Management lands and are now within Glen Canyon National Recreation Area, a National Park Service area. From here to the end, the condition of the road worsens and is recommended for four-wheel drive only. Mile 48.7, Fifty Mile Spring: A major mission camp was located in this general vicinity at one of these three springs. The first child born on this journey was born here. Mile 50.1, Hole-in-the-Rock Arch: A commemorative plaque is mounted in stone next to the road, pointing out a natural arch at the top of the Kaiparowits Plateau. Careful observation will reveal faint remains of wagon tracks to the side of the road. Mile 55.3, Hole-in-the-Rock: The expedition of members of the Church of Jesus Christ of Latter-day Saints made use of the Hole-in-the-Rock cut to cross the Colorado River in 1880. Construction of this passageway was very difficult. The workers were plagued by lack of wood, forage for cattle, bitter cold, and diminishing food supplies. Blasting powder and picks were used to widen and/or fill various sections of the crevice. At the lower part of the Hole, a road was constructed on the side of a sheer cliff wall. Although a three-foot shelf had already existed, an extension to the shelf was formed by driving two-foot stakes into the rock and piling vegetation and rocks on top. This portion of the trail was nicknamed \"Uncle Ben\'s Dugway\" in honor of its engineer, Benjamin Perkins. After six weeks of picking, chiseling, drilling, blasting, and digging, the Hole-in-the-Rock road had been completed.  \\r\\n\\r\\n<p>This is no Sunday drive. In good weather, most of the road is passable to high-clearance, two-wheel drive vehicles in dry weather. The last few miles within Glen Canyon are best traveled by foot, bicycle, or four-wheel drive vehicle. There are numerous side-roads that leave this main road. Nearly all of these are only recommended for four-wheel drive.<br /> <br /> All day- and overnight hikes into the Esclante region of Glen Canyon are access by this drive. Trailheads can be found for Coyote Gulch and many other side canyons. Read up on any specific hikes you may want to attempt before you go. Depanding on the weather, the time of year, and your physical abilities, you may need to bring in and pack out much more gear and water than you think.</p>','This 62 mile (100 km) drive (one way) runs from Escalante, Utah to the actual Hole-in-the-Rock on the western shore of Lake Powell following the general route of of the original Hole-in-the-Rock Expedition. Most of the road is in Grand Staircase-Escalante National Monument, however the last approximately 5 miles are within the boundaries of Glen Canyon National Recreation Area. Most of the road is passable to high-clearance, two-wheel drive vehicles in dry weather. The last few miles within Glen Canyon are best traveled by foot, bicycle, or four-wheel drive vehicle. There are numerous side-roads that leave this main road. Nearly all of these are only recommended for four-wheel drive. Persons traveling this road should carry plenty of water (at least one gallon--4 liters--per person per day) and be equipped to get themselves out of any difficulty they might encounter. This road is not routinely patrolled by any agency. Temperatures can range over 100° F (38°C) in summer to near 0° F (-17°C) in winter. Sudden heavy rains, especially in summer months may make this road impassable. If you are caught near the end of the road during a heavy storm, you may not be able to make it back to the paved highway, even with a four-wheel drive. For latest road conditions and travel information, call the Escalante Interagency Visitor Center at 435-826-5499 or check the most recent Road Report. If you are having an emergency, call 911. Tips for a safe and enjoyable trip  Pack out what you pack in. Carry out all trash and food scraps. Help keep wildlife wild by not feeding them human food. Always take plenty of drinking water with you. One gallon per person per day is recommended. Make sure to eat to balance electrolyte loss and bring snacks. Flash floods happen in an instant here. A storm can appear out of nowhere, sending water flooding into a canyon. If you see or hear a threat of rain or storms, DO NOT RISK your safety for the scenery. Never enter washes or narrow canyons when it is raining or threatening to rain. Check with a ranger or official for road and weather conditions before starting your trip. Warning: there is limited cell service in this area. Leave your trip itinerary with someone so you can be located in case of car trouble or other mishaps. Leave the scenery as it is. Do not write or carve on rocks, do not disturb plants or wildlife. Take only pictures, visit with respect and leave no trace.  This scenic drive is the west portion of the road left by the San Juan Mission. There are other sections of the historic Hole-in-the-Rock trail on the east side of Lake Powell as well, where the expedition\'s journey continued. For the most part, these eastern sections are much rougher, requiring well-equipped four-wheel drive vehicles.',NULL,'This is a rough dirt road that can usually be driven by high-clearance two-wheel drive. The last seven miles are recommended four-wheel drive. There are no restroom facilities or shade structures along the road. None of the side hikes are paved, evenly graded, or fitted with railings or other assistive structures.','1-14 Days','1',37.7270448,-111.5313615,NULL,'https://www.nps.gov/thingstodo/driving-the-hole-in-the-rock-road.htm',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,1,NULL,'{\"id\":\"B474045A-78F4-4557-8D3F-71219B8CC00B\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/thingstodo\\/driving-the-hole-in-the-rock-road.htm\",\"title\":\"Driving the Hole in the Rock Road\",\"shortDescription\":\"The Hole-in-the-Rock road is an historic pioneer trail turned path to adventure. This 62 mile (100 km) one-way section runs from Escalante, UT to the western edge of Lake Powell. This road is rough, impassable in inclement weather, and 4WD only for the last 7 miles. Along the way are quite a few trailheads for the Coyote Gulch and other Escalante River trails in Glen Canyon.\",\"images\":[{\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/ED05AE61-B94A-1365-E4746C363F662D8B.jpg\",\"credit\":\"NPS\",\"altText\":\"A dirt road with large sandstone boulder\",\"title\":\"The Hole-in-the-Rock Road\",\"description\":\"\",\"caption\":\"Check the weather report before heading out on this road\",\"crops\":[{\"aspectRatio\":\"1.78\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/primary\\/ED05AE61-B94A-1365-E4746C363F662D8B.jpg\"},{\"aspectRatio\":\"1\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/secondary\\/ED05AE61-B94A-1365-E4746C363F662D8B.jpg\"}]}],\"relatedParks\":[{\"states\":\"AZ,UT\",\"parkCode\":\"glca\",\"designation\":\"National Recreation Area\",\"fullName\":\"Glen Canyon National Recreation Area\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/glca\\/index.htm\",\"name\":\"Glen Canyon\"}],\"relatedOrganizations\":[],\"tags\":[\"Glen Canyon National Recreation Area\",\"grand staircase escalante national monument\",\"mormon pioneer\",\"dirt road\",\"Escalante\",\"Lake Powell\",\"hole in the rock\",\"recreate responsibly\",\"backcountry\",\"wilderness area\",\"scenic drives\"],\"latitude\":\"37.727044771225515\",\"longitude\":\"-111.53136151639262\",\"geometryPoiId\":\"\",\"amenities\":[],\"location\":\"Hole-in-the-Rock Road, Escalante District\",\"seasonDescription\":\"<p>The weather in Glen Canyon National Recreation Area is usually typical for the high deserts. Summers are extremely hot with little, if any, shade. Winters are moderately cold with night time lows often below freezing. Spring weather is highly variable and unpredictable with extended periods of winds. Fall weather is usually nice and mild, a great time to beat the heat.<\\/p> <p>Temperatures can range from 110\\u00b0 F (38\\u00b0C) in June & July to 0\\u00b0 F (-16\\u00b0C) in December & January. Precipitation is generally light (less than 6 inches [15.2cm] annually) though heavy rains and flash flooding is possible during the summer and fall \\\"monsoons.\\\" <strong>The road will not be passable in inclement weather.<\\/strong><\\/p>\",\"accessibilityInformation\":\"<p>This is a rough dirt road that can usually be driven by high-clearance two-wheel drive. The last seven miles are recommended four-wheel drive. There are no restroom facilities or shade structures along the road. None of the side hikes are paved, evenly graded, or fitted with railings or other assistive structures.<\\/p>\",\"isReservationRequired\":\"false\",\"ageDescription\":\"\",\"petsDescription\":\"<p>Pets are allowed in <strong>most<\\/strong> parts of the recreation area. Pets must be on a leash that is no longer than six feet in length. Owners\\u00a0<strong>must<\\/strong>\\u00a0clean up solid pet waste.<\\/p> <h4>Areas Closed to Pets<\\/h4> <p>Pets are\\u00a0<strong>NOT<\\/strong>\\u00a0allowed in the following areas:<\\/p> <ul> <li>All archeological sites.<\\/li> <li>The portion of Cathedral Wash between the road and the Colorado River.<\\/li> <li><strong>Dogs and other companion animals are prohibited from entering Coyote Gulch within the boundaries of Glen Canyon National Recreation Area. (This prohibition does not apply to guide dogs accompanying visual impaired persons or to hearing ear dogs accompanying hearing impaired persons.) This prohibition includes all dogs or other pet animals used to carry or transport recreational equipment or food.<\\/strong><\\/li> <\\/ul>\",\"timeOfDayDescription\":\"<p>This wilderness area is best traveled during daylight hours - the night sky is beautiful but very dark, and the road does end at a long drop into Lake Powell. Enjoy the views when you can see them.<\\/p>\",\"feeDescription\":\"<p>There is no extra fee beyond park entrance.<\\/p>\",\"age\":\"\",\"arePetsPermittedWithRestrictions\":\"false\",\"activities\":[{\"id\":\"0B4A5320-216D-451A-9990-626E1D5ACE28\",\"name\":\"Scenic Driving\"}],\"activityDescription\":\"<p>This is no Sunday drive. In good weather,\\u00a0most of the road is passable to high-clearance, two-wheel drive vehicles in dry weather. The last few miles within Glen Canyon are best traveled by foot, bicycle, or four-wheel drive vehicle. There are numerous side-roads that leave this main road. Nearly all of these are only recommended for four-wheel drive.<br \\/> <br \\/> All day- and overnight hikes into the Esclante region of Glen Canyon are access by this drive. Trailheads can be found for Coyote Gulch and many other side canyons. Read up on any specific hikes you may want to attempt before you go. Depanding on the weather, the time of year, and your physical abilities, you may need to bring in and pack out much more gear and water than you think.<\\/p>\",\"locationDescription\":\"<p><strong>Starts at the Junction of Us Hwy 12 and Hole-in-the-Rock Rd south of Escalante UT.<\\/strong><\\/p> <h2>Mile by Mile Guide<\\/h2> <p>This guide will help point out areas of historical and scenic interest along the road. Mileages indicated do not allow for side trips. Be aware that your odometer may not register exactly the same mileage listed here. Please keep this in mind as you drive.<\\/p> <p><strong>Mile 0.0, Junction of Hole-in-the-Rock road and Highway 12:<\\/strong><br \\/> The town of Escalante, a few miles west of this junction, provided the last chance for the pioneers to obtain food and supplies and to make repairs on their equipment.<\\/p> <p><strong>Mile 4.2, Ten Mile Spring:<\\/strong><br \\/> A small seep where the first major camp was established after departing from Escalante.<\\/p> <p><strong>Mile 12.0, Devil\'s Garden:<\\/strong><br \\/> About 1\\/2 mile southwest from this road, one can observe spectacular rock formations. This site has been designated as an Outstanding Natural Area by the Bureau of Land Management. Always carry water when walking in the desert!<\\/p> <p><strong>Mile 14.0, Twenty Mile Spring:<\\/strong><br \\/> At this site, now called Collett Wash, water was so scarce the pioneers had to dig in the sand to obtain sufficient quantities for survival.<\\/p> <p><strong>Mile 36.4, Dance Hall Rock:<\\/strong><br \\/> This is a large, solid sandstone amphitheater. The pioneers set up a base camp near here and held dances in the natural theater, accompanied by violins, to keep the morale up.<\\/p> <p><strong>Mile 37.1, Forty Mile Spring:<\\/strong><br \\/> This area served as a base camp and rendezvous point, since the well-used road ended here. A member of the advance party of scouts, Platt D. Lyman, commented on the terrain that lay directly ahead: \\\"It is certainly the worst country I ever saw . . . \\\"<\\/p> <p><strong>Mile 39.1, Carcass Wash:<\\/strong><br \\/> A major obstacle for the expedition, as well as the site of a terrible accident that took the lives of 13 Boy Scouts on June 10, 1963. Brake failure on a truck was blamed.<\\/p> <p><strong>Mile 45.7, Glen Canyon National Recreation Area Boundary:<\\/strong><br \\/> You have left Bureau of Land Management lands and are now within Glen Canyon National Recreation Area, a National Park Service area. From here to the end, the condition of the road worsens and is recommended for four-wheel drive only.<\\/p> <p><strong>Mile 48.7, Fifty Mile Spring:<\\/strong><br \\/> A major mission camp was located in this general vicinity at one of these three springs. The first child born on this journey was born here.<\\/p> <p><strong>Mile 50.1, Hole-in-the-Rock Arch:<\\/strong><br \\/> A commemorative plaque is mounted in stone next to the road, pointing out a natural arch at the top of the Kaiparowits Plateau. Careful observation will reveal faint remains of wagon tracks to the side of the road.<\\/p> <p><strong>Mile 55.3, Hole-in-the-Rock:<\\/strong><br \\/> The expedition of members of the Church of Jesus Christ of Latter-day Saints made use of the\\u00a0<a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/glca\\/learn\\/historyculture\\/holeintherock.htm\\\">Hole-in-the-Rock<\\/a>\\u00a0cut to cross the Colorado River in 1880. Construction of this passageway was very difficult. The workers were plagued by lack of wood, forage for cattle, bitter cold, and diminishing food supplies. Blasting powder and picks were used to widen and\\/or fill various sections of the crevice. At the lower part of the Hole, a road was constructed on the side of a sheer cliff wall. Although a three-foot shelf had already existed, an extension to the shelf was formed by driving two-foot stakes into the rock and piling vegetation and rocks on top. This portion of the trail was nicknamed \\\"Uncle Ben\'s Dugway\\\" in honor of its engineer, Benjamin Perkins. After six weeks of picking, chiseling, drilling, blasting, and digging, the Hole-in-the-Rock road had been completed.<\\/p> <p>\\u00a0<\\/p>\",\"doFeesApply\":\"false\",\"longDescription\":\"<p>This 62 mile (100 km) drive (one way) runs from Escalante, Utah to the actual Hole-in-the-Rock on the western shore of Lake Powell following the general route of of the original\\u00a0<a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/glca\\/learn\\/historyculture\\/holeintherock.htm\\\">Hole-in-the-Rock Expedition<\\/a>. Most of the road is in Grand Staircase-Escalante National Monument, however the last approximately 5 miles are within the boundaries of Glen Canyon National Recreation Area. Most of the road\\u00a0is passable to high-clearance, two-wheel drive vehicles in dry weather. The last few miles within Glen Canyon are best traveled by foot, bicycle, or four-wheel drive vehicle. There are numerous side-roads that leave this main road. Nearly all of these are only recommended for four-wheel drive.<\\/p> <p>Persons traveling this road should carry plenty of water (at least one gallon--4 liters--per person per day) and be equipped to get themselves out of any difficulty they might encounter. This road is not routinely patrolled by any agency. Temperatures can range over 100\\u00b0 F (38\\u00b0C) in summer to near 0\\u00b0 F (-17\\u00b0C) in winter. Sudden heavy rains, especially in summer months may make this road impassable. If you are caught near the end of the road during a heavy storm, you may not be able to make it back to the paved highway, even with a four-wheel drive.<\\/p> <p>For latest road conditions and travel information, call the Escalante Interagency Visitor Center at 435-826-5499 or check the most recent\\u00a0<a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/glca\\/learn\\/news\\/road-conditions.htm\\\">Road Report<\\/a>. If you are having an emergency, call 911.<\\/p> <h4>Tips for a safe and enjoyable trip<\\/h4> <ul> <li>Pack out what you pack in. Carry out all trash and food scraps. Help keep wildlife wild by not feeding them human food.<\\/li> <li>Always take plenty of drinking water with you. One gallon per person per day is recommended. Make sure to eat to balance electrolyte loss and bring snacks.<\\/li> <li>Flash floods happen in an instant here. A storm can appear out of nowhere, sending water flooding into a canyon. If you see or hear a threat of rain or storms, DO NOT RISK your safety for the scenery. Never enter washes or narrow canyons when it is raining or threatening to rain.<\\/li> <li>Check with a ranger or official for road and weather conditions before starting your trip.<\\/li> <li>Warning: there is limited cell service in this area. Leave your trip itinerary with someone so you can be located in case of car trouble or other mishaps.<\\/li> <li>Leave the scenery as it is. Do not write or carve on rocks, do not disturb plants or wildlife. Take only pictures, visit with respect and leave no trace.<\\/li> <\\/ul> <p>This scenic drive is the west portion of the road left by the San Juan Mission. There are other sections of the historic Hole-in-the-Rock trail on the east side of Lake Powell as well, where the expedition\'s journey continued. For the most part, these eastern sections are much rougher, requiring well-equipped four-wheel drive vehicles.<\\/p>\",\"reservationDescription\":\"<p>No reservations are needed. Check the road report ahead to make sure road is passable. Check in at the Escalante Interagency Visitor Center if you plan to stay overnight to get a free permit.<\\/p>\",\"season\":[\"Winter\",\"Spring\",\"Summer\",\"Fall\"],\"topics\":[{\"id\":\"BCE3ACBE-7871-495B-AEA3-5E19BC482405\",\"name\":\"Canyons and Canyonlands\"},{\"id\":\"7F12224B-217A-4B07-A4A2-636B1CE7F221\",\"name\":\"Colonization and Settlement\"},{\"id\":\"F8C2FE93-DEB3-4B12-9A87-913E3E6B448D\",\"name\":\"Natural Sounds\"},{\"id\":\"A7359FC4-DAD8-45F5-AF15-7FF62F816ED3\",\"name\":\"Night Sky\"},{\"id\":\"039CDC0F-6408-473D-9C9F-83B3B8A1E069\",\"name\":\"Missions\"},{\"id\":\"A155238F-0DD2-4610-9B87-05FCE1C59283\",\"name\":\"River and Riparian\"},{\"id\":\"4244F489-6813-4B7A-9D0C-20CE098C8FFF\",\"name\":\"Rock Landscapes and Features\"},{\"id\":\"9C9FCBB6-360B-4743-8155-6F9341CBE01B\",\"name\":\"Scenic Views\"},{\"id\":\"5BE55D7F-BDB6-4E3D-AC35-2D8EBB974417\",\"name\":\"Trails\"},{\"id\":\"596BAA6D-F66B-4348-8433-FEFF35A8005E\",\"name\":\"Water Trails\"},{\"id\":\"0BBD4A42-2B3D-4E82-B5C4-1A3874C8682E\",\"name\":\"Roads, Routes and Highways\"},{\"id\":\"B85866E2-0897-4000-9040-605CA335804F\",\"name\":\"Wilderness\"}],\"durationDescription\":\"<p>Check at the Escalante Interagency Visitor Center for a free permit to stay overnight.<\\/p>\",\"arePetsPermitted\":\"true\",\"timeOfDay\":[\"Day\",\"Dawn\",\"Dusk\"],\"duration\":\"1-14 Days\",\"credit\":\"\"}',1,'2023-09-10 21:06:04','2023-09-10 21:06:34'),(357,'Driving to Alstrom Point','Alstrom Point (tag is at the end of the drive)','nps','53CD8A54-6E79-47D9-B938-79FB7D0175CF',4,'Driving Directions to Alstrom Point Mileage and GPS locations were to the best of our knowledge, and did not account for any side quests. Cellular coverage is spotty along the drive, so some points may vary from device to device.  From Carl Hayden Visitor Center at Glen Canyon Dam: Drive north on US89 approx 12 miles to Big Water UT.  Turn right across from the entrance to Grand Staircase Escalante Big Water Visitor Center entrance onto Ethan Allen Rd. Reset your odometer.  Mile 0.25: TURN RIGHT at sign attached to chain-link fence for “Glen Canyon National Recreation Area and State Highway 12” onto the Smokey Mountain/Croton Rd (Rte 230).  Mile 0.80 (N37°4’51” W111°39’6”): Cross Wahweap Creek and continue on a semi-improved road (sometimes paved, sometimes not). If the creek is running too fast or high to cross, your trip ends here. Running water only six inches deep can move a vehicle if it is flowing fast enough. Turn around, don’t drown. Come back another day.   Mile 3.7 (N37°4’4” W111°36’21”) This is the unimproved parking area for people who want to hike Resurrection Canyon on the right; or go view balanced rocks on the left.   Mile 4.9 (N37°4’39” W111°35’23”) parking area/trailhead for Wiregrass Canyon. Wiregrass Canyon is a partially improved, partially technical hike through a canyon that is wider in some parts and narrow in others. This sandy wash takes you through a small canyon and requires some scrambling and detours. Look for cairns (small stacks of rocks that act as trail markers) to lead you out. Wayfinding required. At current lake levels, the trail does not reach the water. Round-trip distance 4 miles (6.4 km)   Mile 9.2 This is the turnoff to Crosby Canyon, which leads to Warm Creek Bay (Rte 231). At current lake levels, the shoreline at Warm Creek is closed to vehicles. Keep straight.  Mile 12.9 (N37°7’49” W111°29’43”) This is the intersection of Smokey Hollow (left fork) and Smokey Mountain/Croton Road (the road you are on). The signs indicate some history and recreation opportunities in Grand Staircase-Escalante National Monument. Bear right and keep going.  Mile 12.9 (N36°44’5” W111°35’24”) Another intersection. At this one, the left branch is the continuation of Smokey Mountain Road, which eventually climbs up the cliffs in the distance and ends up at Escalante, UT. Bear right, past the Grand Staircase-Escalante National Monument sign on the Croton Road.   Mile 18.2 (N37°7’50” W111°24’53”) This is the turnoff for the Alstrom Point road. It is significantly less maintained than the Smokey Mountain/Croton roads you have been on. Now would be a good time to turn on the 4WD. The sign at the turn indicates that this is Rte 264 and the Alstrom Point overlook is in five miles. If you somehow miss this sign and continue on Croton Road, you will soon cross a cattle guard. This means you have gone too far. Turn around and come back.  About a mile before this turnoff, there was another road that can technically go to Alstrom Point, but it is not recommended for highway vehicles.  The road gets sketchy: it may disappear into sandstone, there are a few social trails that lead in various directions, there are a few minor hills to climb. On the last stretch, try to follow the largest of the road tracks to minimize impact on the landscape. There are several places with great views; you can walk the last couple of miles if it is too rough, or keep going until you can\'t go any further. GPS for the end of the road: N37°03’54” W111°21’83”  You will be on sandy rocks overlooking 1,000\' cliffs. Sunrise and sunset are best times, but interesting weather may make for great photos at any time of day.   Return to the highway the way you came.\\r\\n\\r\\n<ul> <li> <p>Only attempt in dry weather forecast or when road has dried after rain (keep your return trip in mind too. If it starts raining while you are out there, you are staying out there). High clearance 4WD are necessary for the last few miles, and high clearance 2WD is recommended for the entire drive. Most of the Smokey Mountain/Croton Road is graded periodically, but not regularly.</p> </li> <li> <p>Driving time is a minimum of 2 hours each way. It is unsafe to attempt the drive at night. Bring camping gear for sunset/sunrise photography.</p> </li> <li> <p>Leave your trip itinerary with someone back in the real world so you can be located in case of car trouble or other mishap. Cell service is unreliable. Be prepared for emergencies.</p> </li> <li> <p>Alstrom Point Is an unimproved location with no facilities. Bring water and seasonally appropriate outdoor gear. Pack out everything you bring in, most importantly <strong>pack out all human waste and trash. </strong>Take about wag bags?</p> </li> <li> <p>Leave the scenery as it is. Do not write or carve on rocks; do not disturb plants or wildlife. Take only pictures; leave behind nothing</p> </li> </ul>','Travel Glen Canyon’s Moonscape Backcountry On The Way to Alstrom Point  Located at the southwest region of Glen Canyon National Recreation Area along the border with Grand Staircase-Escalante National Monument, the 46 mile (74 km) long drive to Alstrom Point offers excitement for the adventurous explorer. Views of features in the Tropic Shale, where marine reptiles once swam the Cretaceous Seas, then up on the Romana Sandstone with panoramic views of the ever changing canyons of Lake Powell in Glen Canyon, await the traveler who wishes to explore this interesting road. There are side hikes at Wiregrass Canyon and Resurrection Canyon, side drives at Crosby Canyon, any hoodoo formations to stop and see, and a number of side roads that lead up Smokey Mountain and onward to Escalante UT.  To fully enjoy the journey always be well prepared. Make sure you have plenty of water, an emergency car kit, proper footwear, sunscreen, a hat and a means of communication. There is no shade anywhere on Romana Mesa including Alstrom Point. Be confident that your vehicle can drive on these roads. If the vehicle has high enough clearance, it can. If you can\'t clear that first creek crossing, your vehicle cannot handle the drive. For latest road conditions and travel information, check in at the nearest Visitor Center or check the most recent Road Report. If you are having an emergency, call 911. Tips for a safe and enjoyable trip  Pack out what you pack in. Carry out all trash and food scraps. Help keep wildlife wild by not feeding them human food. Always take plenty of drinking water with you. One gallon per person per day is recommended. Make sure to eat to balance electrolyte loss and bring snacks. Flash floods happen in an instant here. A storm can appear out of nowhere, sending water flooding into a canyon (Specifically, into Wahweap Creek, and if you are on the other side of it, you are camping there until the creek calms down). If you see or hear a threat of rain or storms, DO NOT RISK your safety for the scenery. Never enter washes or narrow canyons when it is raining or threatening to rain. Check with a ranger or official for road and weather conditions before starting your trip. Warning: there is limited cell service in this area. Leave your trip itinerary with someone so you can be located in case of car trouble or other mishaps. Leave the scenery as it is. Do not write or carve on rocks, do not disturb plants or wildlife. Take only pictures, visit with respect and leave no trace. Pack out everything you bring in, most importantly pack out all human waste and trash. This is the desert, and mostly rock, so burying waste will be almost impossible, and the waste will not decompose.  Please note Although in dry weather the Burr Trail is easily accessible to passenger cars, wet weather may make the road impassable even for 4-wheel drive vehicles. Check with rangers or local officials for weather and road conditions. Recreational Vehicles and trailers are not recommended.',NULL,'Although in dry weather the roads to Alstrom Point are easily accessible to high clearance passenger cars, wet weather may make the road impassable even for 4-wheel drive vehicles. Check with rangers or local officials for weather and road conditions. Recreational Vehicles and trailers are not recommended.','4-48 Hours','1',37.0589958,-111.3643312,NULL,'https://www.nps.gov/thingstodo/driving-to-alstrom-point.htm',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,1,NULL,'{\"id\":\"53CD8A54-6E79-47D9-B938-79FB7D0175CF\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/thingstodo\\/driving-to-alstrom-point.htm\",\"title\":\"Driving to Alstrom Point\",\"shortDescription\":\"Drive the semi-improved roads to Alstrom Point, in the southwest region of Glen Canyon National recreation Area along the border with Grand Staircase-Escalante National Monument near Big Water, UT. Distance: 46 miles round-trip, about 4 hours if you do not stop to look at the scenery Mix of paved and dirt roads, impassable when wet Four-wheel drive recommended for the final five miles and required at the last parts of the road. No RVs.\",\"images\":[{\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/D6ABB6EB-B0C2-55DE-56A07B9EAD5C78C3.jpg\",\"credit\":\"NPS\",\"altText\":\"Sandstone cliffs, buttes, and mesas. Just a bit of a lake in the distance.\",\"title\":\"View of Gunsight Butte from Romana Mesa\",\"description\":\"\",\"caption\":\"The view of Gunsight Butte from Romana Mesa on the way to Alstrom Point\",\"crops\":[{\"aspectRatio\":\"1.78\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/primary\\/D6ABB6EB-B0C2-55DE-56A07B9EAD5C78C3.jpg\"},{\"aspectRatio\":\"1\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/secondary\\/D6ABB6EB-B0C2-55DE-56A07B9EAD5C78C3.jpg\"}]}],\"relatedParks\":[{\"states\":\"AZ,UT\",\"parkCode\":\"glca\",\"designation\":\"National Recreation Area\",\"fullName\":\"Glen Canyon National Recreation Area\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/glca\\/index.htm\",\"name\":\"Glen Canyon\"}],\"relatedOrganizations\":[],\"tags\":[\"Glen Canyon National Recreation Area\",\"grand staircase escalante national monument\",\"scenic drives\",\"wilderness\",\"hiking\",\"ORV\",\"recreate responsibly\",\"Utah\"],\"latitude\":\"37.05899575510198\",\"longitude\":\"-111.36433124542236\",\"geometryPoiId\":\"\",\"amenities\":[],\"location\":\"Alstrom Point (tag is at the end of the drive)\",\"seasonDescription\":\"<p>The weather on the drive to Alstrom Point is usually typical for the high deserts. Summers are extremely hot with no shade. Winters are moderately cold with night time lows often below freezing. Spring weather is highly variable and unpredictable with extended periods of winds and possible snowmelt flash floods. Fall weather is usually nice and mild, a great time to beat the heat (and the crowds!).<\\/p> <p>Temperatures can range from 110\\u00b0 F (38\\u00b0C) in June & July to 0\\u00b0 F (-16\\u00b0C) in December & January. Precipitation is generally light (less than 6 inches [15.2cm] annually) though heavy rains and flash flooding is possible during the summer and fall \\\"monsoons.\\\" Parts of the road may be impassable in inclement weather.<\\/p>\",\"accessibilityInformation\":\"Although in dry weather the roads to Alstrom Point are easily accessible to high clearance passenger cars, wet weather may make the road impassable even for 4-wheel drive vehicles. Check with rangers or local officials for weather and road conditions.\\u00a0Recreational Vehicles and trailers are not recommended.\",\"isReservationRequired\":\"false\",\"ageDescription\":\"\",\"petsDescription\":\"<p>Pets are allowed in most parts of Glen Canyon National Recreation Area. Pets must be on a leash that is no longer than six feet in length. Owners\\u00a0<strong>must<\\/strong>\\u00a0clean up solid pet waste.<\\/p>\",\"timeOfDayDescription\":\"<p>We do not recommend driving this winding road at night. However, the most magnificent views will be sunrise and sunset, so plan ahead to camp overnight and get the best evening\\/morning views.<\\/p>\",\"feeDescription\":\"<p>There is no additional fee to drive to Alstrom Point.<\\/p>\",\"age\":\"\",\"arePetsPermittedWithRestrictions\":\"false\",\"activities\":[{\"id\":\"0B4A5320-216D-451A-9990-626E1D5ACE28\",\"name\":\"Scenic Driving\"}],\"activityDescription\":\"<ul> <li> <p>Only attempt in dry weather forecast or when road has dried after rain (keep your return trip in mind too. If it starts raining while you are out there, you are staying out there). High clearance 4WD are necessary for the last few miles, and high clearance 2WD is recommended for the entire drive. Most of the Smokey Mountain\\/Croton Road is graded periodically, but not regularly.<\\/p> <\\/li> <li> <p>Driving time is a minimum of 2 hours each way. It is unsafe to attempt the drive at night. Bring camping gear for sunset\\/sunrise photography.<\\/p> <\\/li> <li> <p>Leave your trip itinerary with someone back in the real world so you can be located in case of car trouble or other mishap. Cell service is unreliable. Be prepared for emergencies.<\\/p> <\\/li> <li> <p>Alstrom Point Is an unimproved location with no facilities. Bring water and seasonally appropriate outdoor gear. Pack out everything you bring in, most importantly\\u00a0<strong>pack out all human waste and trash. <\\/strong>Take about wag bags?<\\/p> <\\/li> <li> <p>Leave the scenery as it is. Do not write or carve on rocks; do not disturb plants or wildlife. Take only pictures; leave behind nothing<\\/p> <\\/li> <\\/ul>\",\"locationDescription\":\"<h2>Driving Directions to Alstrom Point<\\/h2> <p>Mileage and GPS locations were to the best of our knowledge, and did not account for any side quests. Cellular coverage is spotty along the drive, so some points may vary from device to device.<br \\/> <br \\/> From Carl Hayden Visitor Center at Glen Canyon Dam: Drive north on US89 approx 12 miles to Big Water UT.<br \\/> <br \\/> <strong>Turn right<\\/strong> across from the entrance to Grand Staircase Escalante Big Water Visitor Center entrance onto Ethan Allen Rd. Reset your odometer.<br \\/> <br \\/> <strong>Mile 0.25<\\/strong>: TURN RIGHT at sign attached to chain-link fence for \\u201cGlen Canyon National Recreation Area and State Highway 12\\u201d onto the Smokey Mountain\\/Croton Rd (Rte 230).<br \\/> <br \\/> <strong>Mile 0.80<\\/strong> (N37\\u00b04\\u201951\\u201d W111\\u00b039\\u20196\\u201d): Cross Wahweap Creek and continue on a semi-improved road (sometimes paved, sometimes not). If the creek is running too fast or high to cross, your trip ends here. Running water only six inches deep can move a vehicle if it is flowing fast enough. Turn around, don\\u2019t drown. Come back another day.\\u00a0<br \\/> <br \\/> <strong>Mile 3.7<\\/strong> (N37\\u00b04\\u20194\\u201d W111\\u00b036\\u201921\\u201d) This is the unimproved parking area for people who want to hike Resurrection Canyon on the right; or go view balanced rocks on the left.\\u00a0<br \\/> <br \\/> <strong>Mile 4.9<\\/strong> (N37\\u00b04\\u201939\\u201d W111\\u00b035\\u201923\\u201d) parking area\\/trailhead for Wiregrass Canyon. Wiregrass Canyon is a partially improved, partially technical hike through a canyon that is wider in some parts and narrow in others. This sandy wash takes you through a small canyon and requires some scrambling and detours. Look for cairns (small stacks of rocks that act as trail markers) to lead you out. Wayfinding required. At current lake levels, the trail does not reach the water. Round-trip distance 4 miles (6.4 km)\\u00a0<br \\/> <br \\/> <strong>Mile 9.2<\\/strong> This is the turnoff to Crosby Canyon, which leads to Warm Creek Bay (Rte 231). At current lake levels, the shoreline at Warm Creek is closed to vehicles. Keep straight.<br \\/> <br \\/> <strong>Mile 12.9<\\/strong> (N37\\u00b07\\u201949\\u201d W111\\u00b029\\u201943\\u201d) This is the intersection of Smokey Hollow (left fork) and Smokey Mountain\\/Croton Road (the road you are on). The signs indicate some history and recreation opportunities in Grand Staircase-Escalante National Monument. Bear right and keep going.<br \\/> <br \\/> <strong>Mile 12.9<\\/strong> (N36\\u00b044\\u20195\\u201d W111\\u00b035\\u201924\\u201d) Another intersection. At this one, the left branch is the continuation of Smokey Mountain Road, which eventually climbs up the cliffs in the distance and ends up at Escalante, UT. Bear right, past the Grand Staircase-Escalante National Monument sign on the Croton Road.\\u00a0<br \\/> <br \\/> <strong>Mile 18.2<\\/strong> (N37\\u00b07\\u201950\\u201d W111\\u00b024\\u201953\\u201d) This is the turnoff for the Alstrom Point road. It is significantly less maintained than the Smokey Mountain\\/Croton roads you have been on. Now would be a good time to turn on the 4WD. The sign at the turn indicates that this is Rte 264 and the Alstrom Point overlook is in five miles. If you somehow miss this sign and continue on Croton Road, you will soon cross a cattle guard. This means you have gone too far. Turn around and come back.<br \\/> <br \\/> About a mile before this turnoff, there was another road that can technically go to Alstrom Point, but it is not recommended for highway vehicles.<br \\/> <br \\/> The road gets sketchy: it may disappear into sandstone, there are a few social trails that lead in various directions, there are a few minor hills to climb. On the last stretch, try to follow the largest of the road tracks to minimize impact on the landscape. There are several places with great views; you can walk the last couple of miles if it is too rough, or keep going until you can\'t go any further. GPS for the end of the road: N37\\u00b003\\u201954\\u201d W111\\u00b021\\u201983\\u201d<br \\/> <br \\/> You will be on sandy rocks overlooking 1,000\' cliffs. Sunrise and sunset are best times, but interesting weather may make for great photos at any time of day.\\u00a0<br \\/> <br \\/> Return to the highway the way you came.<\\/p>\",\"doFeesApply\":\"false\",\"longDescription\":\"<h4>Travel Glen Canyon\\u2019s Moonscape Backcountry On The Way to Alstrom Point<\\/h4> <p><br \\/> Located at the southwest region of Glen Canyon National Recreation Area along the border with Grand Staircase-Escalante National Monument, the 46 mile (74 km) long\\u00a0drive to Alstrom Point offers excitement for the adventurous explorer. Views of features in the Tropic Shale, where marine reptiles once swam the Cretaceous Seas, then up on the Romana Sandstone with panoramic views of the ever changing canyons of Lake Powell in Glen Canyon,\\u00a0await the traveler who wishes to explore this interesting road. There are side hikes at Wiregrass Canyon and Resurrection Canyon, side drives at Crosby Canyon, any hoodoo formations to stop and see, and a number of side roads that lead up Smokey Mountain and onward to Escalante UT.<br \\/> <br \\/> To fully enjoy the journey always be well prepared. Make sure you have plenty of water, an emergency car kit, proper footwear, sunscreen, a hat and a means of communication. There is no shade anywhere on Romana Mesa including Alstrom Point. Be confident that your vehicle can drive on these roads. If the vehicle has high enough clearance, it can. If you can\'t clear that first creek crossing, your vehicle cannot handle the drive.<\\/p> <p>For latest road conditions and travel information, check in at the nearest Visitor Center or check the most recent\\u00a0<a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/glca\\/learn\\/news\\/road-conditions.htm\\\">Road Report<\\/a>. If you are having an emergency, call 911.<\\/p> <h4>Tips for a safe and enjoyable trip<\\/h4> <ul> <li>Pack out what you pack in. Carry out all trash and food scraps. Help keep wildlife wild by not feeding them human food.<\\/li> <li>Always take plenty of drinking water with you. One gallon per person per day is recommended. Make sure to eat to balance electrolyte loss and bring snacks.<\\/li> <li>Flash floods happen in an instant here. A storm can appear out of nowhere, sending water flooding into a canyon (Specifically, into Wahweap Creek, and if you are on the other side of it, you are camping there until the creek calms down). If you see or hear a threat of rain or storms, DO NOT RISK your safety for the scenery. Never enter washes or narrow canyons when it is raining or threatening to rain.<\\/li> <li>Check with a ranger or official for road and weather conditions before starting your trip.<\\/li> <li>Warning: there is limited cell service in this area. Leave your trip itinerary with someone so you can be located in case of car trouble or other mishaps.<\\/li> <li>Leave the scenery as it is. Do not write or carve on rocks, do not disturb plants or wildlife. Take only pictures, visit with respect and leave no trace.\\u00a0Pack out everything you bring in, most importantly\\u00a0<strong>pack out all human waste and trash. <\\/strong>This is the desert, and mostly rock, so burying waste will be almost impossible, and the waste will not decompose.<\\/li> <\\/ul> <h4>Please note<\\/h4> <p>Although in dry weather the Burr Trail is easily accessible to passenger cars, wet weather may make the road impassable even for 4-wheel drive vehicles. Check with rangers or local officials for weather and road conditions.\\u00a0<strong>Recreational Vehicles and trailers are not recommended.<\\/strong><\\/p>\",\"reservationDescription\":\"\",\"season\":[\"Winter\",\"Spring\",\"Summer\",\"Fall\"],\"topics\":[{\"id\":\"FB3641FE-67A3-4EC7-B9C4-0A0867776798\",\"name\":\"Ancient Seas\"},{\"id\":\"9BD60DC0-C82B-42BA-A170-456B7423429D\",\"name\":\"Photography\"},{\"id\":\"BCE3ACBE-7871-495B-AEA3-5E19BC482405\",\"name\":\"Canyons and Canyonlands\"},{\"id\":\"4DC11D06-00F1-4A01-81D0-89CCCCE4FF50\",\"name\":\"Climate Change\"},{\"id\":\"E4E84C87-6737-4C37-9157-5962648A35F2\",\"name\":\"Ranches\"},{\"id\":\"F6D3A52E-608F-47D6-96DF-1FD64122A2FC\",\"name\":\"Fossils and Paleontology\"},{\"id\":\"F0F97E32-2F29-41B4-AF98-9FBE8DAB36B1\",\"name\":\"Geology\"},{\"id\":\"A7359FC4-DAD8-45F5-AF15-7FF62F816ED3\",\"name\":\"Night Sky\"},{\"id\":\"4244F489-6813-4B7A-9D0C-20CE098C8FFF\",\"name\":\"Rock Landscapes and Features\"},{\"id\":\"DC053312-4F5B-430E-831F-B939FA6E8E8E\",\"name\":\"Buttes\"},{\"id\":\"9C9FCBB6-360B-4743-8155-6F9341CBE01B\",\"name\":\"Scenic Views\"},{\"id\":\"5BE55D7F-BDB6-4E3D-AC35-2D8EBB974417\",\"name\":\"Trails\"},{\"id\":\"0BBD4A42-2B3D-4E82-B5C4-1A3874C8682E\",\"name\":\"Roads, Routes and Highways\"},{\"id\":\"83DDFE66-914E-4402-AD3E-ACA731AB2537\",\"name\":\"The Gold Rush\"},{\"id\":\"B85866E2-0897-4000-9040-605CA335804F\",\"name\":\"Wilderness\"}],\"durationDescription\":\"<p>It does take around four hours to just drive to Alstrom Point and back, but there are places to stop, walk around, and camp to the night if you wish.<\\/p>\",\"arePetsPermitted\":\"true\",\"timeOfDay\":[\"Day\",\"Dawn\",\"Dusk\"],\"duration\":\"4-48 Hours\",\"credit\":\"\"}',1,'2023-09-10 21:06:05','2023-09-10 21:06:34'),(358,'Day Trip or Backpack Hike to Coyote Gulch','Coyote Gulch','nps','160E1FC6-A60F-4B5F-AE58-10C66931BB6F',4,'Coyote Gulch is a tributary of the Escalante River. Multiple hiking entry points are located off of the Hole-in-the-Rock Road in Escalante, Utah. The trail may be enjoyed as an overnight backpacking trip or as a long day hike. The mileage and length of time required will depend on your point of entry into the canyon. Please check road conditions before your trip to a trailhead.  Red Well - Follow the Hole-in-the-Rock road 30 miles to the signed junction, then drive 1.5 miles to the trailhead. Hurricane Wash - Follow the Hole-in-the-Rock road 33 miles to the parking area beside the road. The trailhead is located across the road. Fortymile Ridge: Water Tank- Follow Hole-in-the-Rock road 35 miles to the signed junction, then drive 4 miles to the trailhead. This route requires 100+ feet of class 5 friction climbing. Fortymile Ridge: Crack in the Wall - Follow Hole-in-the-Rock road 35 miles to the signed junction, then drive 7 miles to the trailhead. The last two miles of this road is through deep sand and is not suitable for low-clearance vehicles. This route contains a large sand dune (700 feet of elevation) that can be strenuous to climb.    Water Availability Coyote Gulch contains a number of seeps and springs in addition to a perennial stream. One particularly good spring flows from the canyon wall just downstream from Jacob Hamblin Arch. Be Ready to Get Wet The perennial stream in Coyote Gulch begins about one mile from the Red Well trailhead. The stream in Hurricane Wash begins about 3.5 miles from the trailhead. The stream is encountered upon reaching the canyon bottom from the other two trailheads. Most of the hike includes many stream crossings or walking in the stream. Your feet will get wet. From Red Well or Hurricane Wash trailheads, the canyondevelops from wide, sandy washes to a narrow canyon with towering walls as you travel toward the confluence with the Escalante River. In canyon country, always be Flash Flood Aware! Flash flooding can result from heavy rains and thunderstorms. Even if the skies above you are clear, a storm miles away can trigger a flash flood in your area. Check weather conditions before hiking, and especially before entering a slot canyon. Group Size Large groups create large impacts in the backcountry. The group size limit for Coyote Gulch is 12 people. Groups larger than 12 people must break up into smaller groups and maintain a minimum distance of ½ mile from each other while hiking and camping. Group size limits are strictly enforced. Permits will be denied and violators will be cited if limits are exceeded. Leave No Trace Make every attempt to leave the backcountry nicer than you found it. “Take only pictures and leave only footprints” is a good reminder. Do not remove anything from the canyon. Leave the flowers, rocks and everything else for others to enjoy. Do not feed wildlife. Food and trash should be stored in a manner impervious to entry by birds and other wildlife.Carry all of your trash out of the canyon, including toilet paper and other hygiene items. Do not burn or bury it. Preserve the SoundscapeYour voice carries farther than you think in canyon country. Respect other visitors by keeping your group quiet and not playing amplified music. If you must have music in the backcountry wear headphones. Human Waste Disposal  Human waste must be removed from Coyote Gulch. With the increase of recreational use, the National Park Service has introduced a human waste disposal program in the Escalante River corridor and its tributaries within Glen Canyon National Recreation Area. All human body waste solids shall be contained and carried out using a portable toilet or a specifically engineered bag waste containment system in Coyote Gulch. Use of a plastic or paper bag as a receptacle for solid human waste and/or for disposal of solid human waste is prohibited unless part of a specifically engineered bag waste containment system containing enzymes and polymers to treat human solid waste, capable of being sealed securely and state approved for disposal in ordinary trash receptacles. Visitors are responsible for providing their own removal system that is adequate for the size of their group and length of stay. There are many “portable toilet” human waste bags available on the market. Some examples are Restop II Bags, Wag Bags and Biffy Bags. These products can be found at many outdoor product retailers. Homemade PVC “poop tubes” or other hard sided screw top containers can be used directly or in conjunction with a bag system.The pit toilets by Jacob Hamblin Arch have been closed. The composting toilet by Big Spring Alcove is currently open. Please do not leave trash at this facility. Fire Fires are not permitted in Coyote Gulch. Rock Cairns Do not build rock cairns.They can mislead other visitors who rely on landmarks for wayfinding. Building them causes resource damage. Rely on your map and compass and know your route.   \\r\\n\\r\\n','Nestled in the heart of Escalante’s canyon country, Coyote Gulch contains two arches, a natural bridge, and several waterfalls. This concentration of outstanding natural features, relatively easy route finding, and water availability make Coyote Gulch the most popular backpacking destination of all the canyons of the Escalante. To ensure this area of proposed wilderness remains a special place for future generations, please follow all regulations and plan accordingly. Thorough trip planning is not only your first step in having a safe and enjoyable trip, but also in protecting the natural and cultural resource in the area. When hiking in Coyote Gulch plan on encountering other visitors, particularly in the spring and fall. If solitude is one of your primary objectives, consider another route. Water Availability  Coyote Gulch contains a number of seeps and springs in addition to a perennial stream. One particularly good spring flows from the canyon wall just downstream from Jacob Hamblin Arch. Be Ready to Get Wet The perennial stream in Coyote Gulch begins about one mile from the Red Well trailhead. The stream in Hurricane Wash begins about 3.5 miles from the trailhead. The stream is encountered upon reaching the canyon bottom from the other two trailheads. Most of the hike includes many stream crossings or walking in the stream. Your feet will get wet. From Red Well or Hurricane Wash trailheads, the canyon develops from wide, sandy washes to a narrow canyon with towering walls as you travel toward the confluence with the Escalante River.  Flash Floods—In canyon country, always be Flash Flood Aware! Flash flooding can result from heavy rains and thunderstorms. Even if the skies above you are clear, a storm miles away can trigger a flash flood in your area. Check weather conditions before hiking, and especially before entering a slot canyon. Group Size Limit Large groups create large impacts in the backcountry. The group size limit for Coyote Gulch is 12 people. Groups larger than 12 people must break up into smaller groups and maintain a minimum distance of ½ mile from each other while hiking and camping. Group size limits are strictly enforced. Permits will be denied and violators will be cited if limits are exceeded. Leave No Trace Make every attempt to leave the backcountry nicer than you found it. “Take only pictures and leave only footprints” is a good reminder. Do not remove anything from the canyon. Leave the flowers, rocks and everything else for others to enjoy. Do not feed wildlife. Food and trash should be stored in a manner impervious to entry by birds and other wildlife. Carry all of your trash out of the canyon, including toilet paper and other hygiene items. Do not burn or bury it.Your voice carries farther than you think in canyon country. Respect other visitors by keeping your group quiet and not playing amplified music. If you must have music in the backcountry wear headphones. Human Waste Disposal  Human waste must be removed from Coyote Gulch. With the increase of recreational use, the National Park Service has introduced a human waste disposal program in the Escalante River corridor and its tributaries within Glen Canyon National Recreation Area. All human body waste solids shall be contained and carried out using a portable toilet or a specifically engineered bag waste containment system in Coyote Gulch. Use of a plastic or paper bag as a receptacle for solid human waste and/or for disposal of solid human waste is prohibited unless part of a specifically engineered bag waste containment system containing enzymes and polymers to treat human solid waste, capable of being sealed securely and state approved for disposal in ordinary trash receptacles. Visitors are responsible for providing their own removal system that is adequate for the size of their group and length of stay. There are many “portable toilet” human waste bags available on the market. Some examples are Restop II Bags, Wag Bags and Biffy Bags. These products can be found at many outdoor product retailers. Homemade PVC “poop tubes” or other hard sided screw top containers can be used directly or in conjunction with a bag system. The pit toilets by Jacob Hamblin Arch have been closed. The composting toilet by Big Spring Alcove is currently open. Please do not leave trash at this facility. Fires Fires are not permitted in Coyote Gulch. Rock Cairns Do not build rock cairns.They can mislead other visitors who rely on landmarks for wayfinding. Building them causes resource damage. Rely on your map and compass and know your route. ',NULL,'Coyote Gulch is not recommended to people with low mobility.  The road to access any of the trailheads is rough dirt and does not have accessible parking.  The route is along an unpaved riverbed. Along the route, one may encounter deep sand, slickrock, wet slickrock, sheer cliffs that may require some technical gear, and water.','1-14 Days','1',37.4327944,-111.1360532,NULL,'https://www.nps.gov/thingstodo/day-trip-or-backpack-hike-to-coyote-gulch.htm',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,1,NULL,'{\"id\":\"160E1FC6-A60F-4B5F-AE58-10C66931BB6F\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/thingstodo\\/day-trip-or-backpack-hike-to-coyote-gulch.htm\",\"title\":\"Day Trip or Backpack Hike to Coyote Gulch\",\"shortDescription\":\"Open year-round but most popular in Spring and Fall, Coyote Gulch is a tributary of the Escalante River with several entry points for day hiking or backpacking in the Escalante Canyons of Glen Canyon National recreation Area and Grand Staircase National Monument. This is a wilderness area, prepare to pack out everything you bring in.\",\"images\":[{\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/A1A3EA69-E3C6-20EF-905E6674746654B8.jpg\",\"credit\":\"NPS\",\"altText\":\"Sandstone canyon and butte with autumn trees.\",\"title\":\"Canyon and Butte, Coyote Gulch\",\"description\":\"\",\"caption\":\"Buttes line the way along Coyote Gulch\",\"crops\":[{\"aspectRatio\":\"1.78\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/primary\\/A1A3EA69-E3C6-20EF-905E6674746654B8.jpg\"},{\"aspectRatio\":\"1\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/secondary\\/A1A3EA69-E3C6-20EF-905E6674746654B8.jpg\"}]}],\"relatedParks\":[{\"states\":\"AZ,UT\",\"parkCode\":\"glca\",\"designation\":\"National Recreation Area\",\"fullName\":\"Glen Canyon National Recreation Area\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/glca\\/index.htm\",\"name\":\"Glen Canyon\"}],\"relatedOrganizations\":[],\"tags\":[\"Glen Canyon National Recreation Area\",\"grand staircase national monument\",\"Escalante\",\"gulch\",\"Hole-in-the-Rock\",\"dirt road\",\"trailhead\",\"hiking\",\"backpacking\",\"Rock Climbing\",\"backcountry camping\",\"wilderness\",\"recreate responsibly\",\"scenic drives\"],\"latitude\":\"37.432794397125726\",\"longitude\":\"-111.13605320214266\",\"geometryPoiId\":\"6d61b9d7-4a66-4fdf-91a5-ee3ebb085952\",\"amenities\":[],\"location\":\"Coyote Gulch\",\"seasonDescription\":\"<p>Hikes along Coyote Gulch are more popular in the spring and fall, as it is too cold in the winter and too hot in the summer to truly enjoy the hike. But the area is open year-round. Check weather conditions and water availability before you go.\\u00a0<\\/p>\",\"accessibilityInformation\":\"<p>Coyote Gulch is not recommended to people with low mobility.<br \\/> <br \\/> The road to access any of the trailheads is rough dirt and does not have accessible parking.<br \\/> <br \\/> The route is along an unpaved riverbed. Along the route, one may encounter deep sand, slickrock, wet slickrock, sheer cliffs that may require some technical gear, and water.<\\/p>\",\"isReservationRequired\":\"true\",\"ageDescription\":\"<p>This is wilderness. Minors should be accompanied with an adult.<\\/p>\",\"petsDescription\":\"<p>Pets are not permitted in Coyote Gulch. This includes pack animals such as horses, llamas and goats.<\\/p>\",\"timeOfDayDescription\":\"<p>You can hike along the route any time, but we suggest camping during the night and watching the stars.<\\/p>\",\"feeDescription\":\"<p>Hiking and overnight permits are free, but make sure you get yours before you start hiking!<\\/p>\",\"age\":\"\",\"arePetsPermittedWithRestrictions\":\"false\",\"activities\":[{\"id\":\"7C37B79B-D02D-49EB-9020-3DB8299B748A\",\"name\":\"Backcountry Hiking\"}],\"activityDescription\":\"\",\"locationDescription\":\"<p>Coyote Gulch is a tributary of the Escalante River. Multiple hiking entry points are located off of the Hole-in-the-Rock Road in Escalante, Utah. The trail may be enjoyed as an overnight backpacking trip or as a long day hike. The mileage and length of time required will depend on your point of entry into the canyon. Please check <a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/glca\\/learn\\/news\\/road-conditions.htm\\\">road conditions<\\/a> before your trip to a trailhead.<\\/p> <ol> <li>Red Well - Follow the Hole-in-the-Rock road 30 miles to the signed junction, then drive 1.5 miles to the trailhead.<\\/li> <li>Hurricane Wash - Follow the Hole-in-the-Rock road 33 miles to the parking area beside the road. The trailhead is located across the road.<\\/li> <li>Fortymile Ridge: Water Tank- Follow Hole-in-the-Rock road 35 miles to the signed junction, then drive 4 miles to the trailhead. <strong>This route requires 100+ feet of class 5 friction climbing.<\\/strong><\\/li> <li>Fortymile Ridge: Crack in the Wall - Follow Hole-in-the-Rock road 35 miles to the signed junction, then drive 7 miles to the trailhead. The last two miles of this road is through deep sand and is not suitable for low-clearance vehicles. <strong>This route contains a large sand dune (700 feet of elevation) that can be strenuous to climb.<\\/strong><\\/li> <\\/ol> <h2>\\u00a0<\\/h2> <h4>Water Availability<\\/h4> <p>Coyote Gulch contains a number of seeps and springs in addition to a perennial stream. One particularly good spring flows from the canyon wall just downstream from Jacob Hamblin Arch.<\\/p> <p><strong>Be Ready to Get Wet<\\/strong><br \\/> The perennial stream in Coyote Gulch begins about one mile from the Red Well trailhead. The stream in Hurricane Wash begins about 3.5 miles from the trailhead. The stream is encountered upon reaching the canyon bottom from the other two trailheads. Most of the hike includes many stream crossings or walking in the stream. Your feet will get wet. From Red Well or Hurricane Wash trailheads, the canyondevelops from wide, sandy washes to a narrow canyon with towering walls as you travel toward the confluence with the Escalante River.\\u00a0<strong>In canyon country, always be Flash Flood Aware! Flash flooding can result from heavy rains and thunderstorms. Even if the skies above you are clear, a storm\\u00a0miles away can trigger a flash flood in your area. Check weather conditions before hiking, and especially before entering a slot canyon.<\\/strong><\\/p> <h4>Group Size<\\/h4> <p>Large groups create large impacts in the backcountry. The group size limit for Coyote Gulch is 12 people. Groups larger than 12 people must break up into smaller groups and maintain a minimum distance of \\u00bd mile from each other while hiking and camping. Group size limits are strictly enforced. Permits will be denied and violators will be cited if limits are exceeded.<\\/p> <h4>Leave No Trace<\\/h4> <p>Make every attempt to leave the backcountry nicer than you found it. \\u201cTake only pictures and leave only footprints\\u201d is a good reminder. Do not remove anything from the canyon. Leave the flowers, rocks and everything else for others to enjoy. Do not feed wildlife. Food and trash should be stored in a manner impervious to entry by birds and other wildlife.Carry <strong>all<\\/strong> of your trash out of the canyon, including toilet paper and other hygiene items. Do not burn or bury it.\\u00a0<span>Preserve the Soundscape<\\/span>Your voice carries farther than you think in canyon country. Respect other visitors by keeping your group quiet and not playing amplified music. If you must have music in the backcountry wear headphones.<\\/p> <h4>Human Waste Disposal\\u00a0<\\/h4> <p>Human waste must be removed from Coyote Gulch. With the increase of recreational use, the National Park Service has introduced a human waste disposal program in the Escalante River corridor and its tributaries within Glen Canyon National Recreation Area. <strong>All human body waste solids shall be contained and carried out using a portable toilet or a specifically engineered bag waste containment system in Coyote Gulch.<\\/strong> Use of a plastic or paper bag as a receptacle for solid human waste and\\/or for disposal of solid human waste is prohibited unless part of a specifically engineered bag waste containment system containing enzymes and polymers to treat human solid waste, capable of being sealed securely and state approved for disposal in ordinary trash receptacles. Visitors are responsible for providing their own removal system that is adequate for the size of their group and length of stay. There are many \\u201cportable toilet\\u201d human waste bags available on the market. Some examples are Restop II Bags, Wag Bags and Biffy Bags. These products can be found at many outdoor product retailers. Homemade PVC \\u201cpoop tubes\\u201d or other hard sided screw top containers can be used directly or in conjunction with a bag system.<em>The pit toilets by Jacob Hamblin Arch have been closed. The composting toilet by Big Spring Alcove is currently open. Please do not leave trash at this facility.<\\/em><\\/p> <h4>Fire<\\/h4> <p>Fires are not permitted in Coyote Gulch.<\\/p> <h4>Rock Cairns<\\/h4> Do not build rock cairns.They can mislead other visitors who rely on landmarks for wayfinding. Building them causes\\u00a0resource damage. Rely on your map and compass and know your route.\\u00a0 <h4>\\u00a0<\\/h4>\",\"doFeesApply\":\"false\",\"longDescription\":\"<p>Nestled in the heart of Escalante\\u2019s canyon country, Coyote Gulch contains two arches, a natural bridge, and several waterfalls. This concentration of outstanding natural features, relatively easy route finding, and water availability make Coyote Gulch the most popular backpacking destination of all the canyons of the Escalante.<\\/p> <p>To ensure this area of proposed wilderness remains a special place for future generations, please follow all regulations and plan accordingly. Thorough trip planning is not only your first step in having a\\u00a0safe\\u00a0and enjoyable trip, but also in protecting the natural and cultural resource in the area.<\\/p> <p>When hiking in Coyote Gulch plan on encountering other visitors, particularly in the spring and fall. If solitude is one of your primary objectives, consider another route.<\\/p> <h4><strong>Water Availability\\u00a0<\\/strong><\\/h4> <p>Coyote Gulch contains a number of seeps and springs in addition to a perennial stream. One particularly good spring flows from the canyon wall just downstream from Jacob Hamblin Arch.<\\/p> <p><strong>Be Ready to Get Wet<\\/strong><br \\/> The perennial stream in Coyote Gulch begins about one mile from the Red Well trailhead. The stream in Hurricane Wash begins about 3.5 miles from the trailhead. The stream is encountered upon reaching the canyon bottom from the other two trailheads. Most of the hike includes many stream crossings or walking in the stream. Your feet will get wet. From Red Well or Hurricane Wash trailheads, the canyon develops from wide, sandy washes to a narrow canyon with towering walls as you travel toward the confluence with the Escalante River.<br \\/> <br \\/> <span><strong>Flash Floods<\\/strong>\\u2014<\\/span>In canyon country, always be Flash Flood Aware! Flash flooding can result from heavy rains and thunderstorms. Even if the skies above you are clear, a storm\\u00a0miles away can trigger a flash flood in your area. Check weather conditions before hiking, and especially before entering a slot canyon.<\\/p> <h4>Group Size Limit<\\/h4> <p>Large groups create large impacts in the backcountry. The group size limit for Coyote Gulch is 12 people. Groups larger than 12 people must break up into smaller groups and maintain a minimum distance of \\u00bd mile from each other while hiking and camping. Group size limits are strictly enforced. Permits will be denied and violators will be cited if limits are exceeded.<\\/p> <h4>Leave No Trace<\\/h4> <p>Make every attempt to leave the backcountry nicer than you found it. \\u201cTake only pictures and leave only footprints\\u201d is a good reminder. Do not remove anything from the canyon. Leave the flowers, rocks and everything else for others to enjoy.\\u00a0Do not feed wildlife. Food and trash should be stored in a manner impervious to entry by birds and other wildlife. Carry <strong>all<\\/strong> of your trash out of the canyon, including toilet paper and other hygiene items. Do not burn or bury it.Your voice carries farther than you think in canyon country. Respect other visitors by keeping your group quiet and not playing amplified music. If you must have music in the backcountry wear headphones.<\\/p> <h4>Human Waste Disposal\\u00a0<\\/h4> <p>Human waste must be removed from Coyote Gulch. With the increase of recreational use, the National Park Service has introduced a human waste disposal program in the Escalante River corridor and its tributaries within Glen Canyon National Recreation Area. <strong>All human body waste solids shall be contained and carried out using a portable toilet or a specifically engineered bag waste containment system in Coyote Gulch.<\\/strong> Use of a plastic or paper bag as a receptacle for solid human waste and\\/or for disposal of solid human waste is prohibited unless part of a specifically engineered bag waste containment system containing enzymes and polymers to treat human solid waste, capable of being sealed securely and state approved for disposal in ordinary trash receptacles. Visitors are responsible for providing their own removal system that is adequate for the size of their group and length of stay. There are many \\u201cportable toilet\\u201d human waste bags available on the market. Some examples are Restop II Bags, Wag Bags and Biffy Bags. These products can be found at many outdoor product retailers. Homemade PVC \\u201cpoop tubes\\u201d or other hard sided screw top containers can be used directly or in conjunction with a bag system.<br \\/> <em>The pit toilets by Jacob Hamblin Arch have been closed. The composting toilet by Big Spring Alcove is currently open. Please do not leave trash at this facility.<\\/em><\\/p> <h4>Fires<\\/h4> <p>Fires are not permitted in Coyote Gulch.<\\/p> <h4>Rock Cairns<\\/h4> <p>Do not build rock cairns.They can mislead other visitors who rely on landmarks for wayfinding. Building them causes\\u00a0resource damage. Rely on your map and compass and know your route.\\u00a0<\\/p>\",\"reservationDescription\":\"<p>Backcountry permits are required for all overnight stays in the Escalante District of Glen Canyon National Recreation Area. Obtain permits at the Escalante Interagency Visitor Center in the town of Escalante or at one of the entry trailheads. Day use does not require a backcountry permit, but please sign the trail register.<br \\/> <br \\/> Large groups create large impacts in the backcountry. The group size limit for Coyote Gulch is 12 people. Groups larger than 12 people must break up into smaller groups and maintain a minimum distance of \\u00bd mile from each other while hiking and camping. Group size limits are strictly enforced. Permits will be denied and violators will be cited if limits are exceeded.<\\/p>\",\"season\":[\"Winter\",\"Spring\",\"Summer\",\"Fall\"],\"topics\":[{\"id\":\"BCE3ACBE-7871-495B-AEA3-5E19BC482405\",\"name\":\"Canyons and Canyonlands\"},{\"id\":\"F0F97E32-2F29-41B4-AF98-9FBE8DAB36B1\",\"name\":\"Geology\"},{\"id\":\"0E1A04CC-EB51-4F18-93D4-EC0B0B4EC1E3\",\"name\":\"Freshwater Springs\"},{\"id\":\"F8C2FE93-DEB3-4B12-9A87-913E3E6B448D\",\"name\":\"Natural Sounds\"},{\"id\":\"A7359FC4-DAD8-45F5-AF15-7FF62F816ED3\",\"name\":\"Night Sky\"},{\"id\":\"A155238F-0DD2-4610-9B87-05FCE1C59283\",\"name\":\"River and Riparian\"},{\"id\":\"4244F489-6813-4B7A-9D0C-20CE098C8FFF\",\"name\":\"Rock Landscapes and Features\"},{\"id\":\"72E87FCE-B48F-48E8-8761-4849A79736AF\",\"name\":\"Arches\"},{\"id\":\"DC053312-4F5B-430E-831F-B939FA6E8E8E\",\"name\":\"Buttes\"},{\"id\":\"9C9FCBB6-360B-4743-8155-6F9341CBE01B\",\"name\":\"Scenic Views\"}],\"durationDescription\":\"<p>From any given trailhead, Your hike may last two miles to two days. Prepare for the trailhead you will be using, and which route you will be taking.<\\/p> <h4>Hiking Distances:<\\/h4> <ul> <li> <p>From Red Well trailhead to the Escalante River - 13 miles (one way)<\\/p> <\\/li> <li> <p>Hurricane Wash trailhead to the Escalante River - 12.3 miles (one way)<\\/p> <\\/li> <li> <p>Fortymile Ridge: Water Tank to Coyote Gulch- 2 miles (one way)<\\/p> <\\/li> <li> <p>Fortymile Ridge: Crack in the Wall to Coyote Gulch\\u2013 2.5 miles (one way)<\\/p> <\\/li> <\\/ul>\",\"arePetsPermitted\":\"false\",\"timeOfDay\":[\"Day\",\"Night\",\"Dawn\",\"Dusk\"],\"duration\":\"1-14 Days\",\"credit\":\"\"}',1,'2023-09-10 21:06:05','2023-09-10 21:06:34'),(359,'Driving the Burr Trail','Burr Trail','nps','D7CF6BF9-7C85-4B07-8124-BCBC66AF4AB1',4,'Mile by Mile Guide to the Burr Trail Whether traveling to Bullfrog or Boulder, this mile by mile guide will help point out junctions, trailheads, side roads, and scenic views, as well as provide some interesting information about the geology, plants, and human history of the area. Please note: Mileages indicated here do not allow for side trips. Your odometer may not reflect the exact distance listed here. Please keep this in mind as you look for roads, trails and scenic features.  Miles From Bullfrog: 0.0 Miles From Boulder: 67.0 Burr Trail Joins Utah Highway 276 To the south, Highway 276 continues to Bullfrog. From Bullfrog, one can take the Charles Hall Ferry across Bullfrog Bay (part of Lake Powell) to Halls Crossing, if it is operational. Check before you come with Utah Department of Transportation at 435-893-4747. To the north, Highway 276 intersects Highway 95 northwest of Hite.  Miles From Bullfrog: 2.0 Miles From Boulder: 65.0 Bullfrog South Primitive Camping Area (Currently closed)  Miles From Bullfrog: 3.6 Miles From Boulder: 63.4 Bullfrog North Primitive Camping Area (Currently closed)  Miles From Bullfrog: 4.8 Miles From Boulder: 62.2 Pedestal Alley Trail This 3-mile (4.8km) roundtrip hike is suitable for all skill levels and leads to interesting pedestal rocks. The trail is marked with cairns (small rock piles), and there is no shade or water. Spring and fall are the best seasons for this hike. Take sufficient water and wear a hat when hiking this trail. The parking area is located on the south side of the road and the trailhead is found across the road on the north side.  Miles From Bullfrog: 5.1 Miles From Boulder: 61.9 Bullfrog Creek Crossing When Lake Powell is at full pool, water can extend up the wash as far as the road! After a rain, the wash can be wet and muddy. Use caution when entering this wash if the streambed is wet.  Miles From Bullfrog: 7.9 Miles From Boulder: 59.2 Glen Canyon National Recreation Area Boundary This area between Glen Canyon National Recreation Area and Capitol Reef National Park is a section of Grand Staircase-Escalante National Monument. To the north is an excellent view of Clay Canyon.  Miles From Bullfrog: 9.6 Miles From Boulder: 57.3 View of Navajo Mountain To the southwest is the rounded form of Navajo Mountain. Known geologically as a laccolith, this mountain was formed by igneous magma (molten rock) which pushed up under the already existing sedimentary layers. To the west is the Waterpocket Fold, a colorful eroded fold of rock layers nearly 100 miles (162 km) long but only a few miles wide. To the south, Upper Bullfrog Bay and Upper Halls Creek Bay can be seen.  Miles From Bullfrog: 12.4 Miles From Boulder: 55.3 View of the Henry Mountains Like Navajo Mountain, the Henry Mountains are also laccoliths, formed when magma pushed overlying sedimentary rock up into a dome. Much of the sedimentary rock has since been eroded away, exposing the igneous rock underneath. Five distinct peaks make up the Henry Mountains, from west to east they are: Mt. Ellen:  11,615 ft (3540 m) elevation Mt. Pennell:  11,371 ft (3466 m) elevation Mt. Hillers:  10,723 ft (3268 m) elevation Mt. Ellsworth:  8,235 ft (2510 m) elevation Mt. Holmes: 7,930 ft (2417 m) elevation The Henry Mountains were among the last mountain ranges in the continental United States to be named. When Major John Wesley Powell saw them in 1869, during his exploration of the Green and Colorado Rivers, they did not appear on any official map. Powell first called the mountains the “Unknown Mountains” but later named them for Professor Joseph Henry, who was Secretary of the Smithsonian Institute at the time. Each of the peaks were named for the members of the Powell expedition, and Ellen was John Wesley Powell’s wife.  Miles From Bullfrog: 12.6 Miles From Boulder: 54.5 Halls Creek Overlook via BLM Road 12200 This road, located on the west side of the Burr Trail, provides another access route to Halls Creek Overlook. It is 7.1 miles (11.4 km) to the overlook. Although longer it may be less rough for passenger cars, however it is still a fairly rough backcountry road and may have rocky and steep grades in certain areas. When it is wet, the road may be impassable for all types of vehicles.  Miles From Bullfrog: 18.8 Miles From Boulder: 48.3 Halls Creek Overlook/Halls Creek Narrows Halls Creek Overlook road branches off to the west. It is 3 miles (5 km) to the overlook. The road is very rough and may not always be passable for passenger cars, high clearance and 4-wheel drive are recommended. When it is wet, the road is impassable for all types of vehicles. At the overlook, there is a superb view of the Waterpocket Fold, Brimhall Natural Bridge, and the large, colorful expanse of the desert environment. Beginning 50 to 70 million years ago, the Waterpocket Fold formed from tilted sandstone layers that were later eroded into cliffs, arches, and Brimhall Natural Bridge is an unusual double arch, named for Dr. Dean Brimhall, an authority on prehistoric Native rock art in Utah’s rugged canyon country. A rough 2.5 mile (4 km) trail descends the cliffs and leads across the valley floor and into the canyon to Brimhall Bridge. The trail is difficult to hike and involves wading. It should be attempted by experienced hikers only! Experienced hikers can continue their adventure by hiking through Halls Creek Narrows. Please be aware this is an unmarked route that is 22.4 miles total roundtrip, requires wading and scrambling, is subject to hazards such as flash floods, rock falls and canyon obstacles, and should be completed over three to four days with the aid of map reading and navigation experience. A backcountry permit is required for overnight trips, available at the Bullfrog Visitor Center.  Miles From Bullfrog: 19.7 Miles From Boulder: 47.3 Junction with Starr Springs Road Starr Springs Road gives access to the Henry Mountains; a high clearance 4-wheel drive vehicle is needed. Starr Springs is 21 miles (33.8 km) to the east, on the east side of the Henry Mountains. There is a Bureau of Land Management campground at Starr Springs, and from there the road connects in approximately 5 miles (8 km) with UT Hwy 276.  Miles From Bullfrog: 27.5 Miles From Boulder: 39.6 Capitol Reef National Park Boundary Here is a distant view of the Waterpocket Fold, a 100-mile (161 km) long bend in the earth’s crust, extending from Thousand Lake Mountain to the north to Lake Powell to the south. The formation to the north is composed of Bentonite clay. When wet, this clay can make the road impassable.  Miles From Bullfrog: 28.5 Miles From Boulder: 38.6 The Post/ Lower Muley Twist Trailhead This was once the site of a roundup corral and a cabin, but is now merely a point of reference. To the south is a spur road leading to a parking area. A 6.5 mile (10.5 km) one way trail from the parking area leads to Lower Muley Twist Canyon. Within a few miles of the parking area are some of the largest water pockets found in the Waterpocket Fold.  Miles From Bullfrog: 29.0 Miles From Boulder: 38.1 Surprise Canyon Trailhead Surprise Canyon is a short, 2 mile (3.2 km) roundtrip hike that crosses a wash and leads into a short canyon in the Waterpocket Fold.  Miles From Bullfrog: 30.8 Miles From Boulder: 36.2 Junction with Notom Road The road to the north parallels the Waterpocket Fold, gives access to several canyons and washes which offer good hiking possibilities, passes through the town of Notom and meets Hwy 24 just east of Capitol Reef National Park. There are two primitive campgrounds along the road: Cedar Mesa and Bitter Creek.  Miles From Bullfrog: 30.9 Miles From Boulder: 36.1 Peek-a-Boo Arch This arch is visible on the skyline to the northwest. Some say this arch resembles an unblinking eye in the horned head of a giant lizard stretched out along the sandstone slope of the Waterpocket Fold.  Miles From Bullfrog: 31.6 Miles From Boulder: 35.5 Bottom of the Switchbacks in Burr Canyon Here, in the Burr Canyon, the Navajo sandstone has been completely eroded away, leaving a huge notch in the Waterpocket Fold. The Burr Trail switchbacks steeply up through the canyon. Originally, only the switchbacks were named the Burr Trail, a route used by cattleman in the late 19th century to move cattle back and forth between winter and summer ranges. Today, the entire road from Bullfrog to Boulder is known as the Burr Trail. Although the views are spectacular, do not stop on the switchbacks. Cars ascending the switchbacks have the right of way.  Miles From Bullfrog: 32.8 Miles From Boulder: 34.3 Top of the Switchbacks/ Lower Muley Twist Canyon Trailhead The scenic view to the east is superb. Just to the west is a picnic area and the trailhead to Lower Muley Twist Canyon. A hike through the Lower Muley Twist Canyon can be done as a long day hike or as an overnight trip by starting and ending at The Post parking area. Hiking the entire canyon from the trailhead on the Burr Trail Road and back is best done as a two to three day trip. A backcountry permit is required for overnight trips, available at the Bullfrog Visitor Center.This route is not an official, maintained trail. Route conditions including obstacles in canyons change frequently due to weather, flash floods, rock falls and other hazards may be present. Route finding, navigation, and map reading skills are critical. Backpacking experience is a must. Maps are available at the Capitol Reef and Bullfrog Visitor Centers. The highlight of the hike is a deep, narrow, twisting canyon with large alcoves. Beginning at the trailhead on the Burr Trail Road and hiking down canyon to The Post trailhead necessitates leaving a vehicle at each end. If you do not have two vehicles, turn around when you get to the sign indicating The Post cutoff trail.  Miles From Bullfrog: 33.0 Miles From Boulder: 34.0 Wash Crossing This crossing can be difficult or impossible if the wash is wet.  Miles From Bullfrog: 34.0 Miles From Boulder: 33.2 Upper Muley Twist Canyon Trailhead This route is not an official, maintained trail. Route conditions including obstacles in canyons change frequently due to weather, flash floods, rock falls and other hazards may be present. Although you will find cairns along this hike, a topographic map is highly recommended. Route finding, navigation, and map reading skills are critical. Backpacking experience is a must. Maps are available at the Capitol Reef and Bullfrog Visitor Centers. A backcountry permit is required for overnight trips, available at the Bullfrog Visitor Center.This hike provides narrow canyons, multiple arches, expansive vistas and a clear view of the Waterpocket Fold. The trailhead is located on the west side of the Burr Trail Road, 2-wheel drive vehicles will be able to navigate the .3 miles (.5 km) past the trailhead, after that a high clearance, 4-wheel drive vehicle is necessary for the remaining 2.9 miles to the Strike Valley Overlook parking area. From the parking area it is a 1.7 mile (2.7 km) hike up the wash to Saddle Arch where the rim route is marked by a sign. This route is a loop trail. From the loop trail, you are able to connect to the Narrows route. The Narrows are 2.3 miles (3.7 km) beyond Saddle Arch, marked by cairns. Hiking through the Narrows requires difficult climbing using hand and toe holds. Continuing up the canyon where the trail drops into the bottom of a wash, an NPS sign marks where the trail exits the canyon.  Miles From Bullfrog: 36.0 Miles From Boulder: 31.4 Piñon/Juniper Forest Here, at an elevation of 6,000 ft (1892 m), is a “cold desert zone” which has different plants and animals than lower-elevation desert ecosystems. This zone is characterized by such plants as sumac, sagebrush, blackbrush, piñon pine and juniper and animals such as bobcat, mountain lion, deer, and bighorn sheep. Both piñon pine and juniper trees were utilized by Native Americans and early settlers. Today these trees are protected, and vital to the habitat of the area. Do not remove them.  Miles From Bullfrog: 36.2 Miles From Boulder: 30.8 Grand Staircase-Escalante National Monument Boundary Here the unpaved portion of the road ends if you are traveling towards Boulder, or begins if you are heading towards Bullfrog. To the east is a panoramic view of the jagged, brilliantly colored west side of the Waterpocket Fold. All five peaks of the Henry Mountains can be seen from this viewpoint, and far in the distance are the red mesas and buttes of Glen Canyon National Recreation Area. Surrounding this area are the red Circle Cliffs. The great oval arena within the uplifted circle of cliffs eroded from the center, exposing the red sandstone cliffs which enclose it.  Miles From Bullfrog: 37.9 Miles From Boulder: 29.2 Wolverine Loop Road This 28 mile (45 km) loop road leads to a beautiful but remote and rugged area near Wolverine Canyon. Many side roads lead from the Wolverine Loop Road and the road is not well marked. 4-wheel drive vehicles may access some of these side roads; others are accessible only by foot or horseback. This area features stunning examples of petrified wood, but collection of petrified wood is not allowed on National Park Service lands or within Grand Staircase-Escalante National Monument.  Miles From Bullfrog: 42.6 Miles From Boulder: 24.6 Road to the Lampstand This road leads 5 miles (8 km) one way to the Lampstand, an unusually shaped butte.  Miles From Bullfrog: 45.0 Miles From Boulder: 22.1 Road to the Lampstand This road also leads to the Lampstand  Miles From Bullfrog: 48.7 Miles From Boulder: 18.6 Wolverine Loop Road This is the other access point for the 28 mile (45 km) loop road located on the western side of the Burr Trail.  Miles From Bullfrog: 50.1 Miles From Boulder: 17.3 Entrance to Long Canyon Here, the road enters Long Canyon, and will follow this colorful canyon for the next 7 miles (11 km). The lighter, golden sandstone crowning the red cliff is known as the Diadem. As you enter or exit the canyon there is a pull out with scenic views to the southeast.  Miles From Bullfrog: 55.0 Miles From Boulder: 12.4 Long Canyon This narrow canyon is enclosed by sheer walls of Wingate sandstone which tower hundreds of feet above the road. The sandstone has fractured and eroded, forming alcoves. Some of the dark red sandstone has been leached by water to a whitish shade, and black desert varnish stains the vertical walls.  Miles From Bullfrog: 55.5 Miles From Boulder: 12.9 Singing Canyon This quick and easy slot canyon is known locally for its acoustic properties. Be careful, at the end of the canyon is a steep dry fall. The pullout for parking is located on the east side of the road.  Miles From Bullfrog: 56.8 Miles From Boulder: 10.2 The Gulch The Burr Trail leaves Long Canyon here at its confluence with its sister gorge, The Gulch, and climbs onto the lower slopes of Boulder Mountain.  Miles From Bullfrog: 60.7 Miles From Boulder: 6.7 Deer Creek Campground This small campground is located at 5,800 ft on Bureau of Land Management land. There are seven campsites ($10.00 per night) and no potable water. Due to size constraints it does not accommodate travel trailers.  Miles From Bullfrog: 61.2 Miles From Boulder: 6.0 Sandstone Sand Landscape Sand dunes, created millions of years ago by wind-blown sand, were covered over by other layers of sand and pressed into stone. Gradually, these dunes were again exposed as the overlying sandstone was eroded away, creating the appearance of hardened, petrified dunes. Today, the light-colored Navajo sandstone, rounded into domes and hills, resembles the sand dunes from long ago.  Miles From Bullfrog: 67.3 Miles From Boulder: 0.0 Junction with Utah State Route 12/Boulder  The Burr Trail ends (or begins) here at Boulder, Utah on State Route 12. Located in Boulder are services such as food, fuel and lodging as well as Anasazi State Park. Thirty-two miles (51.5 km) south is the town of Escalante; 40 miles (25 km) north over Boulder Mountain is the town of Torrey.\\r\\n\\r\\n<p>Although in dry weather the Burr Trail is easily accessible to passenger cars, wet weather may make the road impassable even for 4-wheel drive vehicles. Check with rangers or local officials for weather and road conditions. Recreational Vehicles and trailers are not recommended. Read ahead about the area if you wish to do some hikes along the route.</p>','Travel Utah’s Beautiful Backcountry Along the Burr Trail  Located just outside the northeast region of Glen Canyon National Recreation Area in Bullfrog, the 67 mile (108 km) long Burr Trail offers excitement for the adventurous explorer. Views of features like the Henry Mountains, Waterpocket Fold, the red Circle Cliffs, Long Canyon, and Pedestal Alley await the traveler who wishes to explore this interesting road. To fully enjoy the journey always be well prepared. Make sure you have plenty of water, a first aid kit, proper footwear, sunscreen, a hat and a means of communication. For latest road conditions and travel information, call the Escalante Interagency Visitor Center at 435-826-5499 or check the most recent Road Report. If you are having an emergency, call 911. Tips for a safe and enjoyable trip  Pack out what you pack in. Carry out all trash and food scraps. Help keep wildlife wild by not feeding them human food. Always take plenty of drinking water with you. One gallon per person per day is recommended. Make sure to eat to balance electrolyte loss and bring snacks. Flash floods happen in an instant here. A storm can appear out of nowhere, sending water flooding into a canyon. If you see or hear a threat of rain or storms, DO NOT RISK your safety for the scenery. Never enter washes or narrow canyons when it is raining or threatening to rain. Check with a ranger or official for road and weather conditions before starting your trip. Warning: there is limited cell service in this area. Leave your trip itinerary with someone so you can be located in case of car trouble or other mishaps. Leave the scenery as it is. Do not write or carve on rocks, do not disturb plants or wildlife. Take only pictures, visit with respect and leave no trace.  Driving the Burr Trail The Burr Trail connects Bullfrog and Boulder, passing through Glen Canyon National Recreation Area, Capitol Reef National Park, and Grand Staircase-Escalante National Monument.  Please note: Although in dry weather the Burr Trail is easily accessible to passenger cars, wet weather may make the road impassable even for 4-wheel drive vehicles. Check with rangers or local officials for weather and road conditions. Recreational Vehicles and trailers are not recommended.',NULL,'Although in dry weather the Burr Trail is easily accessible to passenger cars, wet weather may make the road impassable even for 4-wheel drive vehicles. Check with rangers or local officials for weather and road conditions. Recreational Vehicles and trailers are not recommended.','1-14 Days','1',37.5751050,-110.7133024,NULL,'https://www.nps.gov/thingstodo/driving-the-burr-trail.htm',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,1,NULL,'{\"id\":\"D7CF6BF9-7C85-4B07-8124-BCBC66AF4AB1\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/thingstodo\\/driving-the-burr-trail.htm\",\"title\":\"Driving the Burr Trail\",\"shortDescription\":\"Drive the scenic Burr Trail from Glen Canyon\'s Bullfrog district, along the bottom of Capitol Reef, through Grand Staircase, to Boulder, UT. Distance: 67 miles (108 km) one-way Mix of paved and dirt roads, impassable when wet Four-wheel drive sometimes required. No RVs.\",\"images\":[{\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/F0D97CBA-EF3B-5CBD-B70DEEF83437D436.jpg\",\"credit\":\"NPS \\/ Brent&Dawn Davis\",\"altText\":\"Dirt road that curves around a large rock. Road sign indicating steep incline ahead.\",\"title\":\"Burr Trail\",\"description\":\"\",\"caption\":\"The winding switchbacks and steep inclines of the Burr trail are not for every vehicle.\",\"crops\":[{\"aspectRatio\":\"1.78\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/primary\\/F0D97CBA-EF3B-5CBD-B70DEEF83437D436.jpg\"},{\"aspectRatio\":\"1\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/secondary\\/F0D97CBA-EF3B-5CBD-B70DEEF83437D436.jpg\"}]}],\"relatedParks\":[{\"states\":\"UT\",\"parkCode\":\"care\",\"designation\":\"National Park\",\"fullName\":\"Capitol Reef National Park\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/care\\/index.htm\",\"name\":\"Capitol Reef\"},{\"states\":\"AZ,UT\",\"parkCode\":\"glca\",\"designation\":\"National Recreation Area\",\"fullName\":\"Glen Canyon National Recreation Area\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/glca\\/index.htm\",\"name\":\"Glen Canyon\"}],\"relatedOrganizations\":[],\"tags\":[\"Glen Canyon National Recreation Area\",\"Capitol Reef National Park\",\"grand staircase escalante national monument\",\"scenic drives\",\"wilderness\",\"recreate responsibly\",\"Utah\"],\"latitude\":\"37.575104984681445\",\"longitude\":\"-110.7133024208033\",\"geometryPoiId\":\"\",\"amenities\":[],\"location\":\"Burr Trail\",\"seasonDescription\":\"<p>The weather in Glen Canyon National Recreation Area is usually typical for the high deserts. Summers are extremely hot with little, if any, shade. Winters are moderately cold with night time lows often below freezing. Spring weather is highly variable and unpredictable with extended periods of winds. Fall weather is usually nice and mild, a great time to beat the heat (and the crowds!).<\\/p> <p>Temperatures can range from 110\\u00b0 F (38\\u00b0C) in June & July to O\\u00b0 F (-16\\u00b0C) in December & January. Precipitation is generally light (less than 6 inches [15.2cm] annually) though heavy rains and flash flooding is possible during the summer and fall \\\"monsoons.\\\" Parts of the road may be impassable in inclement weather.<\\/p>\",\"accessibilityInformation\":\"<p>Although in dry weather the Burr Trail is easily accessible to passenger cars, wet weather may make the road impassable even for 4-wheel drive vehicles. Check with rangers or local officials for weather and road conditions.\\u00a0Recreational Vehicles and trailers are not recommended.<\\/p>\",\"isReservationRequired\":\"false\",\"ageDescription\":\"\",\"petsDescription\":\"<p>Pets are allowed in most parts of Glen Canyon National Recreation Area. Pets must be on a leash that is no longer than six feet in length. Owners\\u00a0<strong>must<\\/strong>\\u00a0clean up solid pet waste.<\\/p>\",\"timeOfDayDescription\":\"<p>We do not recommend driving this winding road at night.<\\/p>\",\"feeDescription\":\"<p>There is no additional fee to drive the Burr Trail.<\\/p>\",\"age\":\"\",\"arePetsPermittedWithRestrictions\":\"false\",\"activities\":[{\"id\":\"0B4A5320-216D-451A-9990-626E1D5ACE28\",\"name\":\"Scenic Driving\"}],\"activityDescription\":\"<p>Although in dry weather the Burr Trail is easily accessible to passenger cars, wet weather may make the road impassable even for 4-wheel drive vehicles. Check with rangers or local officials for weather and road conditions.\\u00a0Recreational Vehicles and trailers are not recommended. Read ahead about the area if you wish to do some hikes along the route.<\\/p>\",\"locationDescription\":\"<h4>Mile by Mile Guide to the Burr Trail<\\/h4> <p>Whether traveling to Bullfrog or Boulder, this mile by mile guide will help point out junctions, trailheads, side roads, and scenic views, as well as provide some interesting information about the geology, plants, and human history of the area.<\\/p> <p><strong>Please note<\\/strong>: Mileages indicated here do not allow for side trips. Your odometer may not reflect the exact distance listed here. Please keep this in mind as you look for roads, trails and scenic features.<br \\/> <br \\/> <strong>Miles From Bullfrog: 0.0 Miles From Boulder: 67.0 Burr Trail Joins Utah Highway 276<\\/strong><br \\/> To the south, Highway 276 continues to Bullfrog. From Bullfrog, one can take the Charles Hall Ferry across Bullfrog Bay (part of Lake Powell) to Halls Crossing, if it is operational. Check before you come with Utah Department of Transportation at 435-893-4747. To the north, Highway 276 intersects Highway 95 northwest of Hite.<br \\/> <br \\/> <strong>Miles From Bullfrog: 2.0 Miles From Boulder: 65.0 Bullfrog South Primitive Camping Area\\u00a0(Currently closed)<\\/strong><br \\/> <br \\/> <strong>Miles From Bullfrog: 3.6 Miles From Boulder: 63.4 Bullfrog North Primitive Camping Area\\u00a0(Currently closed)<\\/strong><br \\/> <br \\/> <strong>Miles From Bullfrog: 4.8 Miles From Boulder: 62.2 Pedestal Alley Trail<\\/strong><br \\/> This 3-mile (4.8km) roundtrip hike is suitable for all skill levels and leads to interesting pedestal rocks. The trail is marked with cairns (small rock piles), and there is no shade or water. Spring and fall are the best seasons for this hike. Take sufficient water and wear a hat when hiking this trail. The parking area is located on the south side of the road and the trailhead is found across the road on the north side.<br \\/> <br \\/> <strong>Miles From Bullfrog: 5.1 Miles From Boulder: 61.9 Bullfrog Creek Crossing<\\/strong><br \\/> When Lake Powell is at full pool, water can extend up the wash as far as the road! After a rain, the wash can be wet and muddy. Use caution when entering this wash if the streambed is wet.<br \\/> <br \\/> <strong>Miles From Bullfrog: 7.9 Miles From Boulder: 59.2 Glen Canyon National Recreation Area Boundary<\\/strong><br \\/> This area between Glen Canyon National Recreation Area and Capitol Reef National Park is a section of Grand Staircase-Escalante National Monument. To the north is an excellent view of Clay Canyon.<br \\/> <br \\/> <strong>Miles From Bullfrog: 9.6 Miles From Boulder: 57.3 View of Navajo Mountain<\\/strong><br \\/> To the southwest is the rounded form of Navajo Mountain. Known geologically as a laccolith, this mountain was formed by igneous magma (molten rock) which pushed up under the already existing sedimentary layers. To the west is the Waterpocket Fold, a colorful eroded fold of rock layers nearly 100 miles (162 km) long but only a few miles wide. To the south, Upper Bullfrog Bay and Upper Halls Creek Bay can be seen.<br \\/> <br \\/> <strong>Miles From Bullfrog: 12.4 Miles From Boulder: 55.3 View of the Henry Mountains<\\/strong><br \\/> Like Navajo Mountain, the Henry Mountains are also laccoliths, formed when magma pushed overlying sedimentary rock up into a dome. Much of the sedimentary rock has since been eroded away, exposing the igneous rock underneath. Five distinct peaks make up the Henry Mountains, from west to east they are:<br \\/> Mt. Ellen: \\u00a011,615 ft (3540 m) elevation<br \\/> Mt. Pennell: \\u00a011,371 ft (3466 m) elevation<br \\/> Mt. Hillers: \\u00a010,723 ft (3268 m) elevation<br \\/> Mt. Ellsworth: \\u00a08,235 ft (2510 m) elevation<br \\/> Mt. Holmes: 7,930 ft (2417 m) elevation<br \\/> The Henry Mountains were among the last mountain ranges in the continental United States to be named. When Major John Wesley Powell saw them in 1869, during his exploration of the Green and Colorado Rivers, they did not appear on any official map. Powell first called the mountains the \\u201cUnknown Mountains\\u201d but later named them for Professor Joseph Henry, who was Secretary of the Smithsonian Institute at the time. Each of the peaks were named for the members of the Powell expedition, and Ellen was John Wesley Powell\\u2019s wife.<br \\/> <br \\/> <strong>Miles From Bullfrog: 12.6 Miles From Boulder: 54.5 Halls Creek Overlook via BLM Road 12200<\\/strong><br \\/> This road, located on the west side of the Burr Trail, provides another access route to Halls Creek Overlook. It is 7.1 miles (11.4 km) to the overlook. Although longer it may be less rough for passenger cars, however it is still a fairly rough backcountry road and may have rocky and steep grades in certain areas. When it is wet, the road may be impassable for all types of vehicles.<br \\/> <br \\/> <strong>Miles From Bullfrog: 18.8 Miles From Boulder: 48.3 Halls Creek Overlook\\/Halls Creek Narrows<\\/strong><br \\/> Halls Creek Overlook road branches off to the west. It is 3 miles (5 km) to the overlook. The road is very rough and may not always be passable for passenger cars, high clearance and 4-wheel drive are recommended. When it is wet, the road is impassable for all types of vehicles. At the overlook, there is a superb view of the Waterpocket Fold, Brimhall Natural Bridge, and the large, colorful expanse of the desert environment. Beginning 50 to 70 million years ago, the Waterpocket Fold formed from tilted sandstone layers that were later eroded into cliffs, arches, and Brimhall Natural Bridge is an unusual double arch, named for Dr. Dean Brimhall, an authority on prehistoric Native rock art in Utah\\u2019s rugged canyon country. A rough 2.5 mile (4 km) trail descends the cliffs and leads across the valley floor and into the canyon to Brimhall Bridge. The trail is difficult to hike and involves wading. It should be attempted by experienced hikers only!<br \\/> Experienced hikers can continue their adventure by hiking through Halls Creek Narrows. Please be aware this is an unmarked route that is 22.4 miles total roundtrip, requires wading and scrambling, is subject to hazards such as flash floods, rock falls and canyon obstacles, and should be completed over three to four days with the aid of map reading and navigation experience. A backcountry permit is required for overnight trips, available at the Bullfrog Visitor Center.<br \\/> <br \\/> <strong>Miles From Bullfrog: 19.7 Miles From Boulder: 47.3 Junction with Starr Springs Road<\\/strong><br \\/> Starr Springs Road gives access to the Henry Mountains; a high clearance 4-wheel drive vehicle is needed. Starr Springs is 21 miles (33.8 km) to the east, on the east side of the Henry Mountains. There is a Bureau of Land Management campground at Starr Springs, and from there the road connects in approximately 5 miles (8 km) with UT Hwy 276.<br \\/> <br \\/> <strong>Miles From Bullfrog: 27.5 Miles From Boulder: 39.6 Capitol Reef National Park Boundary<\\/strong><br \\/> Here is a distant view of the Waterpocket Fold, a 100-mile (161 km) long bend in the earth\\u2019s crust, extending from Thousand Lake Mountain to the north to Lake Powell to the south. The formation to the north is composed of Bentonite clay. When wet, this clay can make the road impassable.<br \\/> <br \\/> <strong>Miles From Bullfrog: 28.5 Miles From Boulder: 38.6 The Post\\/ Lower Muley Twist Trailhead<\\/strong><br \\/> This was once the site of a roundup corral and a cabin, but is now merely a point of reference.\\u00a0To the south is a spur road leading to a parking area. A 6.5 mile (10.5 km) one way trail from the parking area leads to Lower Muley Twist Canyon. Within a few miles of the parking area are some of the largest water pockets found in the Waterpocket Fold.<br \\/> <br \\/> <strong>Miles From Bullfrog: 29.0 Miles From Boulder: 38.1 Surprise Canyon Trailhead<\\/strong><br \\/> Surprise Canyon is a short, 2 mile (3.2 km) roundtrip hike that crosses a wash and leads into a short canyon in the Waterpocket Fold.<br \\/> <br \\/> <strong>Miles From Bullfrog: 30.8 Miles From Boulder: 36.2 Junction with Notom Road<\\/strong><br \\/> The road to the north parallels the Waterpocket Fold, gives access to several canyons and washes which offer good hiking possibilities, passes through the town of Notom and meets Hwy 24 just east of Capitol Reef National Park. There are two primitive campgrounds along the road: Cedar Mesa and Bitter Creek.<br \\/> <br \\/> <strong>Miles From Bullfrog: 30.9 Miles From Boulder: 36.1 Peek-a-Boo Arch<\\/strong><br \\/> This arch is visible on the skyline to the northwest. Some say this arch resembles an unblinking eye in the horned head of a giant lizard stretched out along the sandstone slope of the Waterpocket Fold.<br \\/> <br \\/> <strong>Miles From Bullfrog: 31.6 Miles From Boulder: 35.5 Bottom of the Switchbacks in Burr Canyon<\\/strong><br \\/> Here, in the Burr Canyon, the Navajo sandstone has been completely eroded away, leaving a huge notch in the Waterpocket Fold. The Burr Trail switchbacks steeply up through the canyon.\\u00a0Originally, only the switchbacks were named the Burr Trail, a route used by cattleman in the late 19th century to move cattle back and forth between winter and summer ranges. Today, the entire road from Bullfrog to Boulder is known as the Burr Trail.<br \\/> Although the views are spectacular, do not stop on the switchbacks. Cars ascending the switchbacks have the right of way.<br \\/> <br \\/> <strong>Miles From Bullfrog: 32.8 Miles From Boulder: 34.3 Top of the Switchbacks\\/ Lower Muley Twist Canyon Trailhead<\\/strong><br \\/> The scenic view to the east is superb. Just to the west is a picnic area and the trailhead to Lower Muley Twist Canyon. A hike through the Lower Muley Twist Canyon can be done as a long day hike or as an overnight trip by starting and ending at The Post parking area. Hiking the entire canyon from the trailhead on the Burr Trail Road and back is best done as a two to three day trip. A backcountry permit is required for overnight trips, available at the Bullfrog Visitor Center.This route is not an official, maintained trail. Route conditions including obstacles in canyons change frequently due to weather, flash floods, rock falls and other hazards may be present. Route finding, navigation, and map reading skills are critical. Backpacking experience is a must. Maps are available at the Capitol Reef and Bullfrog Visitor Centers. The highlight of the hike is a deep, narrow, twisting canyon with large alcoves. Beginning at the trailhead on the Burr Trail Road and hiking down canyon to The Post trailhead necessitates leaving a vehicle at each end. If you do not have two vehicles, turn around when you get to the sign indicating The Post cutoff trail.<br \\/> <br \\/> <strong>Miles From Bullfrog: 33.0 Miles From Boulder: 34.0 Wash Crossing<\\/strong><br \\/> This crossing can be difficult or impossible if the wash is wet.<br \\/> <br \\/> <strong>Miles From Bullfrog: 34.0 Miles From Boulder: 33.2 Upper Muley Twist Canyon Trailhead<\\/strong><br \\/> This route is not an official, maintained trail. Route conditions including obstacles in canyons change frequently due to weather, flash floods, rock falls and other hazards may be present. Although you will find cairns along this hike, a topographic map is highly recommended. Route finding, navigation, and map reading skills are critical. Backpacking experience is a must. Maps are available at the Capitol Reef and Bullfrog Visitor Centers. A backcountry permit is required for overnight trips, available at the Bullfrog Visitor Center.This hike provides narrow canyons, multiple arches, expansive vistas and a clear view of the Waterpocket Fold. The trailhead is located on the west side of the Burr Trail Road, 2-wheel drive vehicles will be able to navigate the .3 miles (.5 km) past the trailhead, after that a high clearance, 4-wheel drive vehicle is necessary for the remaining 2.9 miles to the Strike Valley Overlook parking area.\\u00a0From the parking area it is a 1.7 mile (2.7 km) hike up the wash to Saddle Arch where the rim route is marked by a sign. This route is a loop trail. From the loop trail, you are able to connect to the Narrows route. The Narrows are 2.3 miles (3.7 km) beyond Saddle Arch, marked by cairns. Hiking through the Narrows requires difficult climbing using hand and toe holds. Continuing up the canyon where the trail drops into the bottom of a wash, an NPS sign marks where the trail exits the canyon.<br \\/> <br \\/> <strong>Miles From Bullfrog: 36.0 Miles From Boulder: 31.4 Pi\\u00f1on\\/Juniper Forest<\\/strong><br \\/> Here, at an elevation of 6,000 ft (1892 m), is a \\u201ccold desert zone\\u201d which has different plants and animals than lower-elevation desert ecosystems. This zone is characterized by such plants as sumac, sagebrush, blackbrush, pi\\u00f1on pine and juniper and animals such as bobcat, mountain lion, deer, and bighorn sheep.<br \\/> Both pi\\u00f1on pine and juniper trees were utilized by Native Americans and early settlers. Today these trees are protected, and vital to the habitat of the area. Do not remove them.<br \\/> <br \\/> <strong>Miles From Bullfrog: 36.2 Miles From Boulder: 30.8 Grand Staircase-Escalante National Monument Boundary<\\/strong><br \\/> Here the unpaved portion of the road ends if you are traveling towards Boulder, or begins if you are heading towards Bullfrog.\\u00a0To the east is a panoramic view of the jagged, brilliantly colored west side of the Waterpocket Fold. All five peaks of the Henry Mountains can be seen from this viewpoint, and far in the distance are the red mesas and buttes of Glen Canyon National Recreation Area.\\u00a0Surrounding this area are the red Circle Cliffs. The great oval arena within the uplifted circle of cliffs eroded from the center, exposing the red sandstone cliffs which enclose it.<br \\/> <br \\/> <strong><strong>Miles From Bullfrog: 37.9 Miles From Boulder: 29.2 Wolverine Loop Road<\\/strong><\\/strong><br \\/> This 28 mile (45 km) loop road leads to a beautiful but remote and rugged area near Wolverine Canyon. Many side roads lead from the Wolverine Loop Road and the road is not well marked. 4-wheel drive vehicles may access some of these side roads; others are accessible only by foot or horseback.\\u00a0This area features stunning examples of petrified wood, but collection of petrified wood is not allowed on National Park Service lands or within Grand Staircase-Escalante National Monument.<br \\/> <br \\/> <strong><strong>Miles From Bullfrog: 42.6 Miles From Boulder: 24.6 Road to the<\\/strong>\\u00a0Lampstand<\\/strong><br \\/> This road leads 5 miles (8 km) one way to the Lampstand, an unusually shaped butte.<br \\/> <br \\/> <strong><strong>Miles From Bullfrog: 45.0 Miles From Boulder: 22.1 Road to the <\\/strong>Lampstand<\\/strong><br \\/> This road also leads to the Lampstand<br \\/> <br \\/> <strong><strong>Miles From Bullfrog: 48.7 Miles From Boulder: 18.6 Wolverine Loop Road<\\/strong><\\/strong><br \\/> This is the other access point for the 28 mile (45 km) loop road located on the western side of the Burr Trail.<br \\/> <br \\/> <strong><strong>Miles From Bullfrog: 50.1 Miles From Boulder: 17.3 Entrance to Long Canyon<\\/strong><\\/strong><br \\/> Here, the road enters Long Canyon, and will follow this colorful canyon for the next 7 miles (11 km). The lighter, golden sandstone crowning the red cliff is known as the Diadem.\\u00a0As you enter or exit the canyon there is a pull out with scenic views to the southeast.<br \\/> <br \\/> <strong><strong>Miles From Bullfrog: 55.0 Miles From Boulder: 12.4 Long Canyon<\\/strong><\\/strong><br \\/> This narrow canyon is enclosed by sheer walls of Wingate sandstone which tower hundreds of feet above the road. The sandstone has fractured and eroded, forming alcoves. Some of the dark red sandstone has been leached by water to a whitish shade, and black desert varnish stains the vertical walls.<br \\/> <br \\/> <strong><strong>Miles From Bullfrog: 55.5 Miles From Boulder: 12.9 Singing Canyon<\\/strong><\\/strong><br \\/> This quick and easy slot canyon is known locally for its acoustic properties. Be careful, at the end of the canyon is a steep dry fall. The pullout for parking is located on the east side of the road.<br \\/> <br \\/> <strong><strong>Miles From Bullfrog: 56.8 Miles From Boulder: 10.2 The Gulch<\\/strong><\\/strong><br \\/> The Burr Trail leaves Long Canyon here at its confluence with its sister gorge, The Gulch, and climbs onto the lower slopes of Boulder Mountain.<br \\/> <br \\/> <strong><strong>Miles From Bullfrog: 60.7 Miles From Boulder: 6.7 Deer Creek Campground<\\/strong><\\/strong><br \\/> This small campground is located at 5,800 ft on Bureau of Land Management land. There are seven campsites ($10.00 per night) and no potable water. Due to size constraints it does not accommodate travel trailers.<br \\/> <br \\/> <strong><strong>Miles From Bullfrog: 61.2 Miles From Boulder: 6.0 Sandstone Sand Landscape<\\/strong><\\/strong><br \\/> Sand dunes, created millions of years ago by wind-blown sand, were covered over by other layers of sand and pressed into stone. Gradually, these dunes were again exposed as the overlying sandstone was eroded away, creating the appearance of hardened, petrified dunes. Today, the light-colored Navajo sandstone, rounded into domes and hills, resembles the sand dunes from long ago.<br \\/> <br \\/> <strong><strong>Miles From Bullfrog: 67.3 Miles From Boulder: 0.0 Junction with Utah State Route 12\\/Boulder\\u00a0<\\/strong><\\/strong><br \\/> The Burr Trail ends (or begins) here at Boulder, Utah on State Route 12. Located in Boulder are services such as food, fuel and lodging as well as Anasazi State Park. Thirty-two miles (51.5 km) south is the town of Escalante; 40 miles (25 km) north over Boulder Mountain is the town of Torrey.<\\/p>\",\"doFeesApply\":\"false\",\"longDescription\":\"<h4>Travel Utah\\u2019s Beautiful Backcountry Along the Burr Trail<\\/h4> <p><br \\/> Located just outside the northeast region of Glen Canyon National Recreation Area in Bullfrog, the 67 mile (108 km) long\\u00a0Burr Trail offers excitement for the adventurous explorer. Views of features like the Henry Mountains, Waterpocket Fold, the red Circle Cliffs, Long Canyon, and Pedestal Alley await the traveler who wishes to explore this interesting road. To fully enjoy the journey always be well prepared. Make sure you have plenty of water, a first aid kit, proper footwear, sunscreen, a hat and a means of communication.<\\/p> <p>For latest road conditions and travel information, call the Escalante Interagency Visitor Center at 435-826-5499 or check the most recent\\u00a0<a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/glca\\/learn\\/news\\/road-conditions.htm\\\">Road Report<\\/a>. If you are having an emergency, call 911.<\\/p> <h4>Tips for a safe and enjoyable trip<\\/h4> <ul> <li>Pack out what you pack in. Carry out all trash and food scraps. Help keep wildlife wild by not feeding them human food.<\\/li> <li>Always take plenty of drinking water with you. One gallon per person per day is recommended. Make sure to eat to balance electrolyte loss and bring snacks.<\\/li> <li>Flash floods happen in an instant here. A storm can appear out of nowhere, sending water flooding into a canyon. If you see or hear a threat of rain or storms, DO NOT RISK your safety for the scenery. Never enter washes or narrow canyons when it is raining or threatening to rain.<\\/li> <li>Check with a ranger or official for road and weather conditions before starting your trip.<\\/li> <li>Warning: there is limited cell service in this area. Leave your trip itinerary with someone so you can be located in case of car trouble or other mishaps.<\\/li> <li>Leave the scenery as it is. Do not write or carve on rocks, do not disturb plants or wildlife. Take only pictures, visit with respect and leave no trace.<\\/li> <\\/ul> <h4>Driving the Burr Trail<\\/h4> <p>The Burr Trail connects Bullfrog and Boulder, passing through Glen Canyon National Recreation Area, Capitol Reef National Park, and Grand Staircase-Escalante National Monument.<br \\/> <br \\/> Please note: Although in dry weather the Burr Trail is easily accessible to passenger cars, wet weather may make the road impassable even for 4-wheel drive vehicles. Check with rangers or local officials for weather and road conditions.\\u00a0<strong>Recreational Vehicles and trailers are not recommended.<\\/strong><\\/p>\",\"reservationDescription\":\"\",\"season\":[\"Winter\",\"Spring\",\"Summer\",\"Fall\"],\"topics\":[{\"id\":\"BCE3ACBE-7871-495B-AEA3-5E19BC482405\",\"name\":\"Canyons and Canyonlands\"},{\"id\":\"4244F489-6813-4B7A-9D0C-20CE098C8FFF\",\"name\":\"Rock Landscapes and Features\"},{\"id\":\"9C9FCBB6-360B-4743-8155-6F9341CBE01B\",\"name\":\"Scenic Views\"},{\"id\":\"0BBD4A42-2B3D-4E82-B5C4-1A3874C8682E\",\"name\":\"Roads, Routes and Highways\"},{\"id\":\"B85866E2-0897-4000-9040-605CA335804F\",\"name\":\"Wilderness\"}],\"durationDescription\":\"<p>It does take less than a day to just drive the length of the road, but there are places to stop, walk around, and camp to the night if you wish.<\\/p>\",\"arePetsPermitted\":\"true\",\"timeOfDay\":[\"Day\"],\"duration\":\"1-14 Days\",\"credit\":\"\"}',1,'2023-09-10 21:06:05','2023-09-10 21:06:34'),(360,'From Your Boat To The Bridge','The hike from the docks','nps','CB3A5DD8-67E9-4E12-B194-F2AF51079354',4,'Once you disembark, there is one trail you can take to Rainbow Bridge and to the other side of the bridge. Most other areas are closed for revegetation or for your safety. Please stay on the trail.   \\r\\n\\r\\n<p>This is the hike from the docks to and around Rainbow Bridge. There is no overnight camping at Rainbow Bridge, so unless you have a permit to keep hiking across the Navajo Nation lands, you must come back to your boat before sundown.</p>','Take a hike - from your boat up the trail to the observation at Rainbow Bridge. If you have the time, continue on the trail around to the commemorative plaques and beyond!  The trail’s length, difficulty and time it takes to complete varies depending on lake levels. Expect to walk anywhere from 0.5 to 1.5 miles (0.8 to 2.4km) each way. Proceed carefully and at your own risk. There is considerable elevation gain from the courtesy dock to the bridge itself. Follow the dock to the trailhead. Walk the rough trail along the wall next to Bridge Creek. After the second viewing area, a section of trail completed in 2017, extending the existing path entirely around the bridge. Visitors who decide to take this new part of the trail can now see the bridge from multiple angles while honoring the sacred nature of the bridge by not walking directly under it. The new section of trail also gives visitors the opportunity to see the two large metal plaques located on the other side of the bridge commemorating Nasja Begay and Jim Mike for their help in the official discovery of Rainbow Bridge in 1909. Take extra water and plan to hike another 20-30 minutes round trip.The section of trail from the upper viewing area onward narrows considerably and transverses a steep section of slick rock sandstone.  Extreme Heat Warning: You must bring WATER! There is very little to no shade along the trail and summer temperatures can be above 100°F/37°C  Things to Know Before You Go    Use caution in the extreme heat.   Leave your pets and drones at home.  No swimming at the Rainbow Bridge courtesy dock, or within the boundaries of Rainbow Bridge national Monument. Let the animals eat animal food, not people food. There is no restroom after you start up the trail. Take care of your needs before you leave the docks. Respect the vegetation and biotic soil crusts by staying on the trail.  Rainbow Bridge is a sacred site to several Native American tribes. Please respect these long-standing traditions by not approaching or walking under the bridge.  Things to ponder as you hike the trail (hopefully you\'ll know when you reach each of these spots).  1. Hanging Garden: How does it feel to see so much green in the desert? Hanging gardens are spring-fed colonies of plants that cling to the vertical wall of a cliff in an alcove or “glen”, protected from the direct force of the desert sun. Hanging gardens support an incredible amount of diverse water loving plant species, including the Ryberg’s thistle (Cirsium rydbergii) and the Maidenhair fern (rydbergii Adiantum-capillus). As you continue up the trail and around corner, notice how the plant and animal life quickly changes as you leave the shade and moisture of the hanging garden.   2. Lower Viewing area: What gives you hope? As you continue to ascend, you will begin to see a massive mountain in the distance. This is Navajo Mountain. At 10,388 feet, Navajo Mountain lies entirely inside the Navajo Nation. The harsh and hidden landscape around Navajo Mt and Rainbow Bridge once served as a safe haven for the Navajo people. Protecting a small group of people from the Long Walk of 1864; the forced removal of the Navajo from their homeland in Arizona and New Mexico to Fort Sumner, some 300 miles away at Boseque Redondo. The juxtaposition of Rainbow Bridge and Navajo Mountain, serves as symbol of resilience and hope for the future for the Navajo people.  3. Upper Viewing Area: Does it look like a rainbow to you? Standing 290 feet from its base to the top of its arch - nearly as tall as the Statute of Liberty – and spanning 275 feet across the stream below, Rainbow Bridge is one of the largest natural bridges in the world. The top of the arch measures 42 feet thick and 33 feet wide. Throughout time the span has undoubtedly inspired people from the neighboring Native American tribes who consider Rainbow Bridge sacred, to the thousands of people from around the world who visit it each year. As an active sacred site please visit with respect by voluntarily not approaching or walking under the bridge. Interested in learning more? Ask a Park Ranger to tell you more about the cultural significance of the rainbow turned to stone.  4. A Sidelong View: How does water shape your life? A natural bridge is created by flowing water. The stream flows around the site where the bridge will be, and slowly eats away at the stone until a hole forms. The hole gets bigger, and a natural bridge is born. A natural arch is created by wind, ice, and water forces which never flowed under the span; rather they chipped away at the rock itself to create an opening.   5. Nasja Begay and Jim Mike: What will you be remembered for?  Late in the afternoon on August the 14th, 1909, two groups of Anglo explorers led by Paiute guides Nasja Begay and Jim Mike, saw Rainbow Bridge for the first time. In May of 1910, President William Howard Taft created Rainbow Bridge National Monument. Commemorated here in the plaques mounted high in the sandstone are Nasja Begay and Jim Mike for their help in sharing Rainbow Bridge with the world. Today thousands of people from all over the world come to experience the scenic beauty, cultural significance, and history preserved in Rainbow Bridge National Monument.   This concludes the trail. We hope this guide helped you slow down, enjoy a closer look at the landscape, and learn about the bridge’s history and what Rainbow Bridge means to you.',NULL,'The trail is a combination of boat dock, mud, concrete, packed dirt, loose dirt, rocks, and some uphill/downhill walking. It is not recommended for people with low mobility. Service animals are permitted on the trail only.  The trail length changes throughout the year, all dependent on water levels. It is always at least 1 mile one-way. If water levels are low, traversing up and down the bank and crossing a shallow creek may be required to reach the main trail. Soft sandy soil and muddy creek bed present.  If water levels are high, the main trail is hard pack dirt/sand with sections of varying elevation gain and slick rock sandstone.','2-8 Hours','1',37.0788994,-110.9621964,NULL,'https://www.nps.gov/thingstodo/from-your-boat-to-the-bridge.htm',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,1,NULL,'{\"id\":\"CB3A5DD8-67E9-4E12-B194-F2AF51079354\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/thingstodo\\/from-your-boat-to-the-bridge.htm\",\"title\":\"From Your Boat To The Bridge\",\"shortDescription\":\"There is one trail within the boundaries of Rainbow Bridge National Monument. It runs the length of the monument, from the boat dock on one side, to the border with the Navajo Nation on the other. We ask that you stay on this trail when you visit Rainbow Bridge for your safety and out of respect for the local Native American tribes that hold this place special to them.\",\"images\":[{\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/5994C2F1-92F8-41B0-8F4A16BB997EEB4A.jpg\",\"credit\":\"NPS\",\"altText\":\"Paved trail in sandstone canyon\",\"title\":\"On the trail from the dock to the bridge\",\"description\":\"\",\"caption\":\"Once you disembark, take the path through the canyon to the observation areas for Rainbow Bridge\",\"crops\":[{\"aspectRatio\":\"1.78\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/primary\\/5994C2F1-92F8-41B0-8F4A16BB997EEB4A.jpg\"},{\"aspectRatio\":\"1\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/secondary\\/5994C2F1-92F8-41B0-8F4A16BB997EEB4A.jpg\"}]}],\"relatedParks\":[{\"states\":\"AZ,UT\",\"parkCode\":\"glca\",\"designation\":\"National Recreation Area\",\"fullName\":\"Glen Canyon National Recreation Area\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/glca\\/index.htm\",\"name\":\"Glen Canyon\"},{\"states\":\"UT\",\"parkCode\":\"rabr\",\"designation\":\"National Monument\",\"fullName\":\"Rainbow Bridge National Monument\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/rabr\\/index.htm\",\"name\":\"Rainbow Bridge\"}],\"relatedOrganizations\":[],\"tags\":[\"Rainbow Bridge National Monument\",\"Glen Canyon National Recreation Area\",\"Lake Powell\",\"boating\",\"hiking\",\"scenic view\",\"hanging garden\",\"Historic Marker\",\"native american heritage\"],\"latitude\":\"37.07889938354492\",\"longitude\":\"-110.96219635009766\",\"geometryPoiId\":\"\",\"amenities\":[],\"location\":\"The hike from the docks\",\"seasonDescription\":\"<p>While Rainbow Bridge National Monument is open all year, it is most heavily visited in the summer months. Despite the lack of shade in 100+ degree temps, visiting Rainbow Bridge in the summer is easiest because boat travel is easiest.<\\/p>\",\"accessibilityInformation\":\"<p>The trail is a combination of boat dock, mud, concrete, packed dirt, loose dirt, rocks, and some uphill\\/downhill walking. It is not recommended for people with low mobility. Service animals are permitted on the trail only.<br \\/> <br \\/> The trail length changes throughout the year, all dependent on water levels. It is always at least 1 mile one-way.<br \\/> If water levels are low, traversing up and down the bank and crossing a shallow creek may be required to reach the main trail. Soft sandy soil and muddy creek bed present.\\u00a0<br \\/> If water levels are high, the main trail is hard pack dirt\\/sand with sections of varying elevation gain and slick rock sandstone.<\\/p>\",\"isReservationRequired\":\"false\",\"ageDescription\":\"<p>All ages are welcome at Rainbow Bridge, but there are no comfort facilities, restrooms, benches, or shade along the trail or at the observation areas. Know your families\' limits when walking the trail.<\\/p>\",\"petsDescription\":\"<p>Pets are not allowed at Rainbow Bridge National Monument. If you choose to take the walk to Rainbow Bridge, your pet must remain on your boat. Do not leave your pet unattended.<\\/p>\",\"timeOfDayDescription\":\"<p>Rainbow Bridge National Monument is closed from sunset to sunrise.<\\/p>\",\"feeDescription\":\"<p>Rainbow Bridge has no entrance fee. You will be traveling through Glen Canyon National Recreation Area to get to Rainbow Bridge, which does charge an entrance fee. Rainbow Bridge is only accessible from Glen Canyon by boat, so if you do not have a boat of your own, you will have to rent a boat or take a boat tour.<\\/p>\",\"age\":\"\",\"arePetsPermittedWithRestrictions\":\"false\",\"activities\":[{\"id\":\"45261C0A-00D8-4C27-A1F8-029F933A0D34\",\"name\":\"Front-Country Hiking\"}],\"activityDescription\":\"<p>This is the hike from the docks to and around Rainbow Bridge. There is no overnight camping at Rainbow Bridge, so unless you have a permit to keep hiking across the Navajo Nation lands, you must come back to your boat before sundown.<\\/p>\",\"locationDescription\":\"<p>Once you disembark, there is one trail you can take to Rainbow Bridge and to the other side of the bridge. Most other areas are closed for revegetation or for your safety. Please stay on the trail.<\\/p> <br \\/> \\u00a0\",\"doFeesApply\":\"false\",\"longDescription\":\"<h4>Take a hike - from your boat up the trail to the observation at Rainbow Bridge. If you have the time, continue on the trail around to the commemorative plaques and beyond!<\\/h4> <p><br \\/> The trail\\u2019s length, difficulty and time it takes to complete varies depending on lake levels. Expect to walk anywhere from 0.5 to 1.5 miles (0.8 to 2.4km) each way. Proceed carefully and at your own risk. There is considerable elevation gain from the courtesy dock to the bridge itself. Follow the dock to the trailhead. Walk the rough trail along the wall next to Bridge Creek. After the second viewing area, a section of trail\\u00a0completed in 2017, extending the existing path entirely around the bridge. Visitors who decide to take this new part of the trail can now see the bridge from multiple angles while honoring the sacred nature of the bridge by not walking directly under it. The new section of trail also gives visitors the opportunity to see the two large metal plaques located on the other side of the bridge commemorating Nasja Begay and Jim Mike for their help in the official discovery of Rainbow Bridge in 1909. Take extra water and plan to hike another 20-30 minutes round trip.The section of trail from the upper viewing area onward narrows considerably and transverses a steep section of slick rock sandstone.\\u00a0<\\/p> <h4>Extreme Heat Warning: You must bring WATER! There is very little to no shade along the trail and summer temperatures can be above 100\\u00b0F\\/37\\u00b0C\\u00a0<\\/h4> <h4>Things to Know Before You Go\\u00a0<\\/h4> <ul> <li> <p>Use caution in the extreme heat.<\\/p> <\\/li> <li> <p>Leave your pets and drones at home.<\\/p> <\\/li> <li>No swimming\\u00a0at the Rainbow Bridge courtesy dock, or within the boundaries of Rainbow Bridge national Monument.<\\/li> <li>Let the animals eat animal food, not people food.<\\/li> <li>There is no restroom after you start up the trail. Take care of your needs before you leave the docks.<\\/li> <li>Respect the vegetation and biotic soil crusts by staying on the trail.<\\/li> <\\/ul> <h4>Rainbow Bridge is a sacred site to several Native American tribes. Please respect these long-standing traditions by not approaching or walking under the bridge.<br \\/> <br \\/> Things to ponder as you hike the trail (hopefully you\'ll know when you reach each of these spots).<\\/h4> <p><br \\/> <strong>1. Hanging Garden: <em>How does it feel to see so much green in the desert?<\\/em><\\/strong><br \\/> Hanging gardens are spring-fed colonies of plants that cling to the vertical wall of a cliff in an alcove or \\u201cglen\\u201d, protected from the direct force of the desert sun. Hanging gardens support an incredible amount of diverse water loving plant species, including the Ryberg\\u2019s thistle (Cirsium rydbergii)\\u00a0and the Maidenhair fern (rydbergii Adiantum-capillus). As you continue up the trail and around corner, notice how the plant and animal life quickly changes as you leave the shade and moisture of the hanging garden.\\u00a0<br \\/> <br \\/> <strong>2. Lower Viewing area: <em>What gives you hope?<\\/em><\\/strong><br \\/> As you continue to ascend, you will begin to see a massive mountain in the distance. This is Navajo Mountain. At 10,388 feet, Navajo Mountain lies entirely inside the Navajo Nation. The harsh and hidden landscape around Navajo Mt and Rainbow Bridge once served as a safe haven for the Navajo people. Protecting a small group of people from the Long Walk of 1864; the forced removal of the Navajo from their homeland in Arizona and New Mexico to Fort Sumner, some 300 miles away at Boseque Redondo. The juxtaposition of Rainbow Bridge and Navajo Mountain, serves as symbol of resilience and hope for the future for the Navajo people.<br \\/> <br \\/> <strong>3. Upper Viewing Area: <em>Does it look like a rainbow to you?<\\/em><\\/strong><br \\/> Standing 290 feet from its base to the top of its arch - nearly as tall as the Statute of Liberty \\u2013 and spanning 275 feet across the stream below, Rainbow Bridge is one of the largest natural bridges in the world. The top of the arch measures 42 feet thick and 33 feet wide. Throughout time the span has undoubtedly inspired people from the neighboring Native American tribes who consider Rainbow Bridge sacred, to the thousands of people from around the world who visit it each year. As an active sacred site please visit with respect by voluntarily not approaching or walking under the bridge. Interested in learning more? Ask a Park Ranger to tell you more about the cultural significance of the rainbow turned to stone.<br \\/> <br \\/> <strong>4. A Sidelong View: <em>How does water shape your life?<\\/em><\\/strong><br \\/> A natural bridge is created by flowing water. The stream flows around the site where the bridge will be, and slowly eats away at the stone until a hole forms. The hole gets bigger, and a natural bridge is born.<br \\/> A natural arch is created by wind, ice, and water forces which never flowed under the span; rather they chipped away at the rock itself to create an opening.<\\/p> <p>\\u00a0<br \\/> <strong>5. Nasja Begay and Jim Mike: <em>What will you be remembered for?\\u00a0<\\/em><\\/strong><br \\/> Late in the afternoon on August the 14th, 1909, two groups of Anglo explorers led by Paiute guides Nasja Begay and Jim Mike, saw Rainbow Bridge for the first time. In May of 1910, President William Howard Taft created Rainbow Bridge National Monument. Commemorated here in the plaques mounted high in the sandstone are Nasja Begay and Jim Mike for their help in sharing Rainbow Bridge with the world. Today thousands of people from all over the world come to experience the scenic beauty, cultural significance, and history preserved in Rainbow Bridge National Monument.\\u00a0<br \\/> <br \\/> <em>This concludes the trail. We hope this guide helped you slow down, enjoy a closer look at the landscape, and learn about the bridge\\u2019s history and what Rainbow Bridge means to you.<\\/em><\\/p>\",\"reservationDescription\":\"<p>While no reservations are required, consider tour times and popular visitation times to ensure a spot to tie up on the dock.<\\/p>\",\"season\":[\"Winter\",\"Spring\",\"Summer\",\"Fall\"],\"topics\":[{\"id\":\"BCE3ACBE-7871-495B-AEA3-5E19BC482405\",\"name\":\"Canyons and Canyonlands\"},{\"id\":\"F0F97E32-2F29-41B4-AF98-9FBE8DAB36B1\",\"name\":\"Geology\"},{\"id\":\"A1BAF33E-EA84-4608-A888-4CEE9541F027\",\"name\":\"Native American Heritage\"},{\"id\":\"F8C2FE93-DEB3-4B12-9A87-913E3E6B448D\",\"name\":\"Natural Sounds\"},{\"id\":\"4244F489-6813-4B7A-9D0C-20CE098C8FFF\",\"name\":\"Rock Landscapes and Features\"},{\"id\":\"72E87FCE-B48F-48E8-8761-4849A79736AF\",\"name\":\"Arches\"},{\"id\":\"9C9FCBB6-360B-4743-8155-6F9341CBE01B\",\"name\":\"Scenic Views\"},{\"id\":\"5BE55D7F-BDB6-4E3D-AC35-2D8EBB974417\",\"name\":\"Trails\"}],\"durationDescription\":\"<p>The length of this trail changes every day. As the water levels on Lake Powell rise and fall, the docks have to be moved so they will reach the shore.<br \\/> <br \\/> The amount of time it takes to enjoy this hikes depends on how you got here. If you joined a boat tour, you have to leave when your boat does.<br \\/> <br \\/> If you brought your own boat, you have to be gone by sundown. The trail is usually about a mile and a half one way, but does involve walking uphill, on some sandy and rocky areas, in extreme desert weather. You can choose when to turn around and go back to your boat.<\\/p>\",\"arePetsPermitted\":\"false\",\"timeOfDay\":[\"Day\"],\"duration\":\"2-8 Hours\",\"credit\":\"\"}',1,'2023-09-10 21:06:05','2023-09-10 21:06:35'),(361,'Scramble down Cathedral Wash Trail','How to get to Cathedral Wash Trail Head','nps','4C392174-7152-4403-9347-A521A751EB5C',4,'Lees Ferry is 42 miles (61 km) from Page via Hwy 89 south and Hwy 89A west. It is 85 miles (125 km) from the North Rim of the Grand Canyon via Hwy 89A and Hwy 67. The Lees Ferry Junction and Park Entrance is in Marble Canyon, just west of Navajo Bridge Interpretive Center.  After you turn off Highway 89A onto the Lees Ferry access road, drive 1.3 miles. The trailhead is at the pull out on your left. You can access both upper and lower Cathedral Wash from this parking area.\\r\\n\\r\\n','Canyon walls rise as you follow a mostly dry wash to the Colorado River. This adventure has no major technical challenges, but scrambling and careful decision-making are needed to choose safe routes over ledges and drop-offs. Named after church-like Cathedral Rock at the head of this small canyon, your journey will take you deeper and deeper through Kaibab Limestone and Toroweap Formations until ending at the river, where canyon debris created Cathedral Wash Rapid.',NULL,'Steep trail descends multiple times onto uneven rock surface. Rock scrabbles, narrow ledges, soft sand and puddles found along the trail leading to a soft sand riverbank. ','3-4 Hours','1',36.8341605,-111.6397996,NULL,'https://www.nps.gov/thingstodo/scramble-down-cathedral-wash-trail.htm',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,1,NULL,'{\"id\":\"4C392174-7152-4403-9347-A521A751EB5C\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/thingstodo\\/scramble-down-cathedral-wash-trail.htm\",\"title\":\"Scramble down Cathedral Wash Trail\",\"shortDescription\":\"Follow Cathedral Wash trail as it climbs down to meet the Colorado River on its sandy bank. The 3 miles (4.8 km) round-trip moderate hike descend into the rocky wash, traveling through the Kaibab Limestone and Toroweap Formations of the Grand Canyon Group. Some rock scrambling required.\",\"images\":[{\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/5EF6A468-90B9-9CF9-6D70AD44735ACE5E.jpg\",\"credit\":\"NPS\",\"altText\":\"Two hikers descend steep lawyers of reddish brown rock on the wash trail.\",\"title\":\"Cathedral Wash\",\"description\":\"\",\"caption\":\"Hikers descend the steep trail at Cathedral Wash.\",\"crops\":[{\"aspectRatio\":\"1.78\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/primary\\/5EF6A468-90B9-9CF9-6D70AD44735ACE5E.jpg\"},{\"aspectRatio\":\"1\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/secondary\\/5EF6A468-90B9-9CF9-6D70AD44735ACE5E.jpg\"}]}],\"relatedParks\":[{\"states\":\"AZ,UT\",\"parkCode\":\"glca\",\"designation\":\"National Recreation Area\",\"fullName\":\"Glen Canyon National Recreation Area\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/glca\\/index.htm\",\"name\":\"Glen Canyon\"}],\"relatedOrganizations\":[],\"tags\":[\"Glen Canyon National Recreation Area\",\"Lees Ferry\",\"hiking\",\"hike\",\"wash\",\"desert\",\"Colorado River\",\"geology\",\"scenic view\"],\"latitude\":\"36.83416048019832\",\"longitude\":\"-111.63979959378752\",\"geometryPoiId\":\"\",\"amenities\":[],\"location\":\"How to get to Cathedral Wash Trail Head\",\"seasonDescription\":\"During summer months temperatures can be extreme, reaching over\\u00a0<strong>100\\u00a0\\u00b0 F (38\\u00b0C)<\\/strong>\\u00a0by early afternoon.<strong>\\u00a0<\\/strong>Do not hike during the warmest parts of the day. Bring plenty of water. Be aware of flash floods.\\u00a0\",\"accessibilityInformation\":\"Steep trail descends multiple times onto uneven rock surface. Rock scrabbles, narrow ledges, soft sand and puddles found along the trail leading to a soft sand riverbank.\\u00a0\",\"isReservationRequired\":\"true\",\"ageDescription\":\"All ages welcome.\\u00a0\",\"petsDescription\":\"\",\"timeOfDayDescription\":\"\",\"feeDescription\":\"\",\"age\":\"\",\"arePetsPermittedWithRestrictions\":\"false\",\"activities\":[{\"id\":\"45261C0A-00D8-4C27-A1F8-029F933A0D34\",\"name\":\"Front-Country Hiking\"}],\"activityDescription\":\"\",\"locationDescription\":\"Lees Ferry is 42 miles (61 km) from Page via Hwy 89 south and Hwy 89A west. It is 85 miles (125 km) from the North Rim of the Grand Canyon via Hwy 89A and Hwy 67. The Lees Ferry Junction and Park Entrance is in Marble Canyon, just west of Navajo Bridge Interpretive Center. \\u00a0After you turn off Highway 89A onto the Lees Ferry access road, drive 1.3 miles. The trailhead is at the pull out on your left. You can access both upper and lower Cathedral Wash from this parking area.\",\"doFeesApply\":\"false\",\"longDescription\":\"<p>Canyon walls rise as you follow a mostly dry wash to the Colorado River. This adventure has no major technical challenges, but scrambling and careful decision-making are needed to choose safe routes over ledges and drop-offs. Named after church-like Cathedral Rock at the head of this small canyon, your journey will take you deeper and deeper through Kaibab Limestone and Toroweap Formations until ending at the river, where canyon debris created Cathedral Wash Rapid.<\\/p>\",\"reservationDescription\":\"\",\"season\":[\"Winter\",\"Spring\",\"Summer\",\"Fall\"],\"topics\":[{\"id\":\"F0F97E32-2F29-41B4-AF98-9FBE8DAB36B1\",\"name\":\"Geology\"},{\"id\":\"A155238F-0DD2-4610-9B87-05FCE1C59283\",\"name\":\"River and Riparian\"},{\"id\":\"9C9FCBB6-360B-4743-8155-6F9341CBE01B\",\"name\":\"Scenic Views\"},{\"id\":\"5BE55D7F-BDB6-4E3D-AC35-2D8EBB974417\",\"name\":\"Trails\"}],\"durationDescription\":\"This time does now account for breaks, sightseeing, or picnics along the river.\\u00a0\",\"arePetsPermitted\":\"false\",\"timeOfDay\":[\"Day\"],\"duration\":\"3-4 Hours\",\"credit\":\"\"}',1,'2023-09-10 21:06:05','2023-09-10 21:06:34'),(362,'Lonely Dell Ranch Historic Site','Lonely Dell Historic Ranch','nps','3686F305-964B-4338-86DB-4C9CD60EA675',4,'Lees Ferry is 42 miles (61 km) from Page via Hwy 89 south and Hwy 89A west. It is 85 miles (125 km) from the North Rim of the Grand Canyon via Hwy 89A and Hwy 67. The Lees Ferry Junction and Park Entrance is in Marble Canyon, just west of Navajo Bridge Interpretive Center. A paved road leads 5 miles (8 km) to the Ferry area.  \\r\\n\\r\\n<p>Walk the 1 mile (1.6 km) round trip self-guided walking tour of the orchard, log cabins, stone ranch house, and pioneer cemetery beginning 700 feet (213 m) up the dirt road from the trail head. The walk consist of mixed terrain includes gravel, sandy, and rocky uneven surfaces with normal elevation change. </p>','To the pioneer missionary, it was an isolated settlement offering small comfort during the Great Mormon Migration deep into Arizona. To the families who lived here, the ranch was the centerpiece of daily life where hard work was required to tame and survive the rugged yet beautiful desert. For some it offered employment in a hardscrabble setting, and for later owners it promised rustic peace and quiet. Today, Lonely Dell Ranch still serves its main purpose as a place for the traveler to rest a while, enjoy variety of nature, and bask in the shade of the trees. Lonely Dell Orchard Walk among the fruit trees in the orchard and search for a ripe snack. Collection of fruit at Lonely Dell Ranch Orchard is permitted for personal consumption only and not for purposes of sale or exchange. Fruit collected at Lonely Dell Ranch Orchard shall not exceed 5 gallons per person per day.',NULL,'The road leading back to Lonely Dell Ranch is a improved dirt road. Two uneven dirt trails lead down into the orchard and main ranch building areas. The orchard has a hand dug irrigation system. Feel free to walk through but watch your step. The road leading to the pioneer cemetery and Paria Canyon Wilderness trailhead are uneven and exposed to direct sun. ','1-8 Hours','1',36.8680221,-111.5958995,NULL,'https://www.nps.gov/thingstodo/lonely-dell-ranch-historic-site.htm',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,1,NULL,'{\"id\":\"3686F305-964B-4338-86DB-4C9CD60EA675\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/thingstodo\\/lonely-dell-ranch-historic-site.htm\",\"title\":\"Lonely Dell Ranch Historic Site\",\"shortDescription\":\"Hidden between the shade of trees near the muddy Paria River sits Lonely Dell Ranch. This historic site was once home to those who operated the river crossing at Lees Ferry. Life was challenging on the homestead, but the families who chose to live on this secluded outpost left behind a story of hard work and perseverance.\",\"images\":[{\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/961BF237-C836-D93F-569D9F5DE86A3BD4.jpg\",\"credit\":\"NPS\",\"altText\":\"Rustic log cabin sits in the shade of tall trees surrounding by red rock canyon walls.\",\"title\":\"Lonely Dell Ranch\",\"description\":\"\",\"caption\":\"Samantha\'s cabin at Lonely Dell Ranch\",\"crops\":[{\"aspectRatio\":\"1.78\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/primary\\/961BF237-C836-D93F-569D9F5DE86A3BD4.jpg\"},{\"aspectRatio\":\"1\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/secondary\\/961BF237-C836-D93F-569D9F5DE86A3BD4.jpg\"}]}],\"relatedParks\":[{\"states\":\"AZ,UT\",\"parkCode\":\"glca\",\"designation\":\"National Recreation Area\",\"fullName\":\"Glen Canyon National Recreation Area\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/glca\\/index.htm\",\"name\":\"Glen Canyon\"}],\"relatedOrganizations\":[],\"tags\":[\"Glen Canyon National Recreation Area\",\"Lees Ferry\",\"Lonely Dell Ranch\",\"Guided Walking Tour\",\"front country trails\",\"hiking\",\"walking\",\"fruit trees\",\"Orchard\",\"mormon pioneer\"],\"latitude\":\"36.86802210387851\",\"longitude\":\"-111.59589954877987\",\"geometryPoiId\":\"\",\"amenities\":[],\"location\":\"Lonely Dell Historic Ranch\",\"seasonDescription\":\"During summer months temperatures can be extreme, reaching over <strong>100\\u00a0\\u00b0 F (38\\u00b0C)<\\/strong> by early afternoon.<strong>\\u00a0<\\/strong>Do not hike during the warmest parts of the day. Bring plenty of water, Take breaks in the shade near the main ranch house.\\u00a0 <p>Be flash flood aware if you continue hiking up the Paria River Trail.<\\/p>\",\"accessibilityInformation\":\"The road leading back to Lonely Dell Ranch is a improved dirt road. Two uneven dirt trails lead down into the orchard and main ranch building areas. The orchard has a hand dug irrigation system. Feel free to walk through but watch your step. The road leading to the pioneer cemetery and Paria Canyon Wilderness trailhead are uneven and exposed to direct sun.\\u00a0\",\"isReservationRequired\":\"false\",\"ageDescription\":\"All ages welcome.\",\"petsDescription\":\"Pets must be on a leash six feet or shorter. Pick up after your pet.\",\"timeOfDayDescription\":\"\",\"feeDescription\":\"\",\"age\":\"\",\"arePetsPermittedWithRestrictions\":\"false\",\"activities\":[{\"id\":\"45261C0A-00D8-4C27-A1F8-029F933A0D34\",\"name\":\"Front-Country Hiking\"}],\"activityDescription\":\"<p>Walk the 1 mile\\u00a0(1.6 km) round trip\\u00a0self-guided walking tour of the orchard, log cabins, stone ranch house, and pioneer cemetery beginning 700 feet (213 m) up the dirt road from the trail head. The walk consist of mixed terrain includes gravel, sandy, and rocky uneven surfaces with normal elevation change.\\u00a0<\\/p>\",\"locationDescription\":\"Lees Ferry is 42 miles (61 km) from Page via Hwy 89 south and Hwy 89A west. It is 85 miles (125 km) from the North Rim of the Grand Canyon via Hwy 89A and Hwy 67. The Lees Ferry Junction and Park Entrance is in Marble Canyon, just west of Navajo Bridge Interpretive Center. A paved road leads 5 miles (8 km) to the Ferry area.\\u00a0\\u00a0\",\"doFeesApply\":\"false\",\"longDescription\":\"<p>To the pioneer missionary, it was an isolated settlement offering small comfort during the Great Mormon Migration deep into Arizona. To the families who lived here, the ranch was the centerpiece of daily life where hard work was required to tame and survive the rugged yet beautiful desert. For some it offered employment in a hardscrabble setting, and for later owners it promised rustic peace and quiet.\\u00a0Today,\\u00a0Lonely Dell Ranch still serves its main purpose as a place for the traveler to rest a while, enjoy variety of nature, and bask in the shade of the trees.<\\/p> <h4><span style=\\\"color: rgb(70, 70, 70); font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif; font-size: 14px;\\\">Lonely Dell Orchard<\\/span><\\/h4> <p>Walk among the fruit trees in the orchard and search for a ripe snack. Collection of fruit at Lonely Dell Ranch Orchard is permitted for personal consumption only and not for purposes of sale or exchange. Fruit collected at Lonely Dell Ranch Orchard shall not exceed 5 gallons per person per day.<\\/p>\",\"reservationDescription\":\"\",\"season\":[\"Winter\",\"Spring\",\"Summer\",\"Fall\"],\"topics\":[{\"id\":\"A155238F-0DD2-4610-9B87-05FCE1C59283\",\"name\":\"River and Riparian\"},{\"id\":\"9C9FCBB6-360B-4743-8155-6F9341CBE01B\",\"name\":\"Scenic Views\"}],\"durationDescription\":\"From sunrise to sunset you can spend as little or as much time as you would like there. No overnight camping.\",\"arePetsPermitted\":\"true\",\"timeOfDay\":[\"Day\",\"Dawn\",\"Dusk\"],\"duration\":\"1-8 Hours\",\"credit\":\"\"}',1,'2023-09-10 21:06:05','2023-09-10 21:06:34'),(363,'Contemplate Your Place in the Landscape At The Big Map','Glen Canyon outside of Glen Canyon','nps','D508A211-DD31-4849-8AA9-FB24D69AB146',4,'In the City of Page, our park partner\'s headquarters is at 12 Lake Powell Blvd. Our friends at Glen Canyon Conservancy host the big relief map in the corner of their flagship store.\\r\\n\\r\\nFind your place in the landscape. Contemplate your place in the world. It\'s easy to get lost in this desert canyon landscape, but much easier to find your way out of here than from the real canyons!<br /> <br /> This terrain relief model of the Glen Canyon National Recreation Area and vicinity can only be described as precisely accurate. The topography shown was based on both topographic maps and aerial photographs. Everything on the map actually exists just as it appears to, considering the vertical scale of the model. When it was installed at the visitor center at Glen Canyon Dam, is was oriented to real life. You could use it for wayfinding. You could use it for mind wandering.<br /> <br /> <strong>Bonus secret:</strong> Bob Miller created the map with such accuracy, he included 12 natural bridges and arches in the topography. How many can you find?','During the construction of Glen Canyon Dam, The U.S. Bureau of Reclamation commissioned a scale model relief map of what would be Lake Powell and the surrounding area. Model map builder Bob Miller used information from topographic maps and overflights of the area to construct the model in his basement. It took over a year to complete. He cut the map into seven pieces to be transported to the opening of the Glen Canyon Visitor Center.  In 2017, during the installation of new exhibits at Carl Hayden Visitor Center, the relief map was cut into the original seven pieces and removed from the building. Two years later park partners Glen Canyon Conservancy retrieved the pieces and reassembled the map at their Flagship office and retail store for the public to once again enjoy and plan trips. Almost 55 years later, aside from a few new roads, the map still accurately depicts the landscape of and around Glen Canyon National Recreation Area.',NULL,'The relief map is about three feet high, 12 feet long. A railing surrounds it, leaving room for wheelchair approach.  Service animals are permitted in the building.','1-480 Minutes','1',36.9195732,-111.4605718,NULL,'https://www.nps.gov/thingstodo/the-big-map.htm',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,1,NULL,'{\"id\":\"D508A211-DD31-4849-8AA9-FB24D69AB146\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/thingstodo\\/the-big-map.htm\",\"title\":\"Contemplate Your Place in the Landscape At The Big Map\",\"shortDescription\":\"The Glen Canyon and Surrounding Area Relief Map sits in a corner of the Glen Canyon Conservancy\'s Flagship office and retail store. Use it to help plan your Glen Canyon adventure, or pretend you are in space looking down at the landscape. Whoa.\",\"images\":[{\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/02384632-A541-CFF6-C4933E0AE78DAC1D.jpeg\",\"credit\":\"Glen Canyon Conservancy\",\"altText\":\"Relief map of canyon landscape.\",\"title\":\"The big map\",\"description\":\"\",\"caption\":\"The big relief map of Glen Canyon and surrounding areas\",\"crops\":[{\"aspectRatio\":\"1.78\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/primary\\/02384632-A541-CFF6-C4933E0AE78DAC1D.jpeg\"},{\"aspectRatio\":\"1\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/secondary\\/02384632-A541-CFF6-C4933E0AE78DAC1D.jpeg\"}]}],\"relatedParks\":[{\"states\":\"AZ,UT\",\"parkCode\":\"glca\",\"designation\":\"National Recreation Area\",\"fullName\":\"Glen Canyon National Recreation Area\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/glca\\/index.htm\",\"name\":\"Glen Canyon\"},{\"states\":\"UT\",\"parkCode\":\"rabr\",\"designation\":\"National Monument\",\"fullName\":\"Rainbow Bridge National Monument\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/rabr\\/index.htm\",\"name\":\"Rainbow Bridge\"}],\"relatedOrganizations\":[],\"tags\":[\"Glen Canyon National Recreation Area\",\"glen canyon conservancy\",\"Bureau of Reclamation\",\"Relief map\",\"historic map\",\"Lake Powell\",\"trip planning\",\"contemplation\"],\"latitude\":\"36.919573171487016\",\"longitude\":\"-111.46057176708823\",\"geometryPoiId\":\"\",\"amenities\":[],\"location\":\"Glen Canyon outside of Glen Canyon\",\"seasonDescription\":\"\",\"accessibilityInformation\":\"The relief map is about three feet high, 12 feet long. A railing surrounds it, leaving room for wheelchair approach.<br \\/> <br \\/> Service animals are permitted in the building.\",\"isReservationRequired\":\"false\",\"ageDescription\":\"All ages can view the map, but please no not touch or try to get on it.\",\"petsDescription\":\"\",\"timeOfDayDescription\":\"Please visit <a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/www.canyonconservancy.org\\/\\\">Glen Canyon Conservancy\'s website<\\/a> for office hours.\",\"feeDescription\":\"\",\"age\":\"\",\"arePetsPermittedWithRestrictions\":\"false\",\"activities\":[{\"id\":\"C8F98B28-3C10-41AE-AA99-092B3B398C43\",\"name\":\"Museum Exhibits\"}],\"activityDescription\":\"Find your place in the landscape. Contemplate your place in the world. It\'s easy to get lost in this desert canyon landscape, but much easier to find your way out of here than from the real canyons!<br \\/> <br \\/> This terrain relief model of the Glen Canyon National Recreation Area and vicinity can only be described as precisely accurate. The topography shown was based on both topographic maps and aerial photographs. Everything on the map actually exists just as it appears to, considering the vertical scale of the model. When it was installed at the visitor center at Glen Canyon Dam, is was oriented to real life. You could use it for wayfinding. You could use it for mind wandering.<br \\/> <br \\/> <strong>Bonus secret:<\\/strong> Bob Miller created the map with such accuracy, he included 12 natural bridges and arches in the topography. How many can you find?\",\"locationDescription\":\"In the City of Page, our park partner\'s headquarters is at 12 Lake Powell Blvd. Our friends at <a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/www.canyonconservancy.org\\/\\\">Glen Canyon Conservancy<\\/a> host the big relief map in the corner of their flagship store.\",\"doFeesApply\":\"false\",\"longDescription\":\"During the construction of Glen Canyon Dam, The U.S. Bureau of Reclamation commissioned a scale model relief map of what would be Lake Powell and the surrounding area. Model map builder Bob Miller used information from topographic maps and overflights of the area to construct the model\\u00a0in his basement. It took over a year to complete. He cut the map into seven pieces to be transported to the opening of the Glen Canyon Visitor Center.<br \\/> <br \\/> In 2017, during the installation of new exhibits at Carl Hayden Visitor Center, the relief map was cut into the original seven pieces and removed from the building. Two years later park partners Glen Canyon Conservancy retrieved the pieces and reassembled the map at their Flagship office and retail store for the public to once again enjoy and plan trips. Almost 55 years later, aside from a few new roads, the map still accurately depicts the landscape of and around Glen Canyon National Recreation Area.\",\"reservationDescription\":\"\",\"season\":[\"Winter\",\"Spring\",\"Summer\",\"Fall\"],\"topics\":[{\"id\":\"0DE2D6B3-46DE-44B1-BB5F-E9E8874630D5\",\"name\":\"Sculpture\"},{\"id\":\"BCE3ACBE-7871-495B-AEA3-5E19BC482405\",\"name\":\"Canyons and Canyonlands\"},{\"id\":\"DAAD7F5E-9112-45F2-9A27-DA51B639F27E\",\"name\":\"Dams\"},{\"id\":\"F0F97E32-2F29-41B4-AF98-9FBE8DAB36B1\",\"name\":\"Geology\"},{\"id\":\"1CF1F6BB-A037-445B-8CF2-81428E50CE52\",\"name\":\"Lakes\"},{\"id\":\"101F4D51-F99D-45A6-BBB6-CD481E5FACED\",\"name\":\"Mountains\"},{\"id\":\"A155238F-0DD2-4610-9B87-05FCE1C59283\",\"name\":\"River and Riparian\"},{\"id\":\"4244F489-6813-4B7A-9D0C-20CE098C8FFF\",\"name\":\"Rock Landscapes and Features\"},{\"id\":\"72E87FCE-B48F-48E8-8761-4849A79736AF\",\"name\":\"Arches\"},{\"id\":\"DC053312-4F5B-430E-831F-B939FA6E8E8E\",\"name\":\"Buttes\"},{\"id\":\"9C9FCBB6-360B-4743-8155-6F9341CBE01B\",\"name\":\"Scenic Views\"},{\"id\":\"5BE55D7F-BDB6-4E3D-AC35-2D8EBB974417\",\"name\":\"Trails\"},{\"id\":\"1015393C-D7B0-47F3-86FB-786F30368CA2\",\"name\":\"Bridges\"},{\"id\":\"0BBD4A42-2B3D-4E82-B5C4-1A3874C8682E\",\"name\":\"Roads, Routes and Highways\"}],\"durationDescription\":\"Take a second glance, take an afternoon. Our friends welcome visitors to take their time (while the building is open) and see what they can find on the map.\",\"arePetsPermitted\":\"false\",\"timeOfDay\":[\"Day\"],\"duration\":\"1-480 Minutes\",\"credit\":\"\"}',1,'2023-09-10 21:06:05','2023-09-10 21:06:35'),(364,'Lake Powell Shoreline Primitive Camping','Undeveloped shoreline of Lake Powell','nps','418A12F8-889A-4D9E-9AFB-DD1B82A7A48A',4,'Nearly 2000 miles of shoreline on Lake Powell are in undeveloped areas (outside of marinas) and you can beach your boat there for day trips or camping.\\r\\n\\r\\n<p>\"Backcountry\" Shoreline camping on Lake Powell can range from toughing out the desert winter from your kayak to \"roughing it\" in a multimillion dollar houseboat and any level of camping/glamping in between. Know your boat before you go - check our <a href=\"https://www.nps.gov/glca/planyourvisit/boating.htm\">boating webpage</a> for safety tips and regulations.</p>','By far the most popular activity in Glen Canyon National Recreation Area is boating. On a private boat or a rental, almost two million visitors enjoy the deep turquoise waters of Lake Powell every year. If you already have a boat, why not stay a night or two on your own beach under the stars? You can camp anywhere on the shorelines of Lake Powell. When planning a camping trip by boat in Glen Canyon, it is best to buy a map beforehand. These show the side canyons, good hiking spots, points of interest and marinas, explain the navigation system, and may provide fishing information. In an emergency you will need to report your location. The main channel varies in depth from 100 to 600 feet. It is recommended that you anchor on a beach for the night, as high winds can move boats into rocks and cause damage. There are no motor vehicles, off-road vehicles or bicycles allowed in Glen Canyon\'s roadless areas. All campsites are required to have a portable toilet unless toilets are available on the vessel or within 200 yards of the campsite. Regular water-quality checks are conducted to ensure compliance with sanitation laws. Pets are allowed on beaches as long as waste is cleaned up. Dispose of waste properly. Burying waste of any kind on the beach is prohibited. Waste may not be contained in a plastic bag unless it is an NPS-approved Waste Bag Containment System, which must be deposited in the trash.  Do not camp under overhanging rocks, as downpouring rain can sink a vessel.  Ground fires are permitted along Lake Powell\'s shoreline below high-water level (3,700 feet), except within developed areas. Check for fire restrictions in effect due to high fire danger conditions. Such restrictions are a regular part of hot, dry summer months, but can occur any time of year. Do not leave trash in fire rings. Fireworks are illegal.  Quiet hours are 10 pm to 6 am, unless otherwise posted.  There is no camping allowed in Rainbow Bridge National Monument or within/on archeological sites.',NULL,'It is the boat owner/renter\'s responsibility to bring themselves to any beach, there is no transport service to a beach campsite. Beaches are sandy and rocky, not even or paved. There are no roads to these backcountry area. Depending on your boat, you will be staying in a fully-equipped houseboat, a cabin cruiser, a tent on the beach next to a kayak, or any combination of those.  The beaches on Lake Powell are considered primitive areas, with no running water or facilities. There are no toilets provided on beaches, boaters must bring their own.  Pets and service animals are permitted on leash. Please pick up after them.','1-14 Days','1',NULL,NULL,NULL,'https://www.nps.gov/thingstodo/lake-powell-shoreline-primitive-camping.htm',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,1,NULL,'{\"id\":\"418A12F8-889A-4D9E-9AFB-DD1B82A7A48A\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/thingstodo\\/lake-powell-shoreline-primitive-camping.htm\",\"title\":\"Lake Powell Shoreline Primitive Camping\",\"shortDescription\":\"\\\"Backcountry\\\" Shoreline camping on Lake Powell can range from toughing out the desert winter from your kayak to \\\"roughing it\\\" in a multimillion dollar houseboat and any level of camping\\/glamping in between. Enjoy the beaches of Lake Powell the way that best suits you.\",\"images\":[{\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/E76E6222-CCFD-3756-6A2CC5BE948EE0B6.jpg\",\"credit\":\"NPS \\/ B Moffit\",\"altText\":\"houseboat beached on lakeshore at dawn\",\"title\":\"Backcountry Lakeshore Camping\",\"description\":\"\",\"caption\":\"Amazing sunrises are a bonus part of boat camping on Lake Powell\",\"crops\":[{\"aspectRatio\":\"1.78\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/primary\\/E76E6222-CCFD-3756-6A2CC5BE948EE0B6.jpg\"},{\"aspectRatio\":\"1\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/secondary\\/E76E6222-CCFD-3756-6A2CC5BE948EE0B6.jpg\"}]}],\"relatedParks\":[{\"states\":\"AZ,UT\",\"parkCode\":\"glca\",\"designation\":\"National Recreation Area\",\"fullName\":\"Glen Canyon National Recreation Area\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/glca\\/index.htm\",\"name\":\"Glen Canyon\"}],\"relatedOrganizations\":[],\"tags\":[\"Glen Canyon National Recreation Area\",\"Lake Powell\",\"camping\",\"boating\",\"houseboating\",\"beach camping\",\"recreate responsibly\"],\"latitude\":\"\",\"longitude\":\"\",\"geometryPoiId\":\"\",\"amenities\":[],\"location\":\"Undeveloped shoreline of Lake Powell\",\"seasonDescription\":\"<p>The weather in Glen Canyon National Recreation Area is usually typical for the high deserts. Summers are extremely hot with little, if any, shade. Winters are moderately cold with night time lows often below freezing. Spring weather is highly variable and unpredictable with extended periods of winds. Fall weather is usually nice and mild, a great time to beat the heat (and the crowds!).<\\/p> <p>Temperatures can range from 110\\u00b0 F (38\\u00b0C) in June & July to 0\\u00b0 F (-16\\u00b0C) in December & January. Precipitation is generally light (less than 6 inches [15.2cm] annually) though heavy rains and flash flooding is possible during the summer and fall \\\"monsoons.\\\"<\\/p> <p>We recommend lightweight, light colored clothing for summer, including a hat. Layers of clothing are best for other times of the year.<\\/p>\",\"accessibilityInformation\":\"It is the boat owner\\/renter\'s responsibility to bring themselves to any beach, there is no transport service to a beach campsite.\\u00a0Beaches are sandy and rocky, not even or paved. There are no roads to these backcountry area. Depending on your boat, you will be staying in a fully-equipped houseboat, a cabin cruiser, a tent on the beach next to a kayak, or any combination of those.<br \\/> <br \\/> The beaches on Lake Powell are considered primitive areas, with no running water or facilities. There are no toilets provided on beaches, boaters must bring their own.<br \\/> <br \\/> Pets and service animals are permitted on leash. Please pick up after them.\",\"isReservationRequired\":\"false\",\"ageDescription\":\"<p>Camping on Lake Powell has long been an every age family activity. Make sure you keep track of everyone in your group, and know the <a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/glca\\/planyourvisit\\/safety.htm\\\">safety regulations<\\/a> for children.<\\/p>\",\"petsDescription\":\"<p>Pets are permitted on leash everywhere in Glen Canyon except:<\\/p> <ul> <li> <p>All archeological sites.<\\/p> <\\/li> <li> <p>All marinas, docks, walkways, and launch ramps, except when proceeding directly to or from a boat.<\\/p> <\\/li> <li> <p>Along the San Juan River from Clay Hills Crossing upstream to the Glen Canyon National Recreation Area boundary as designated by required permit available through the Bureau of Land Management.<\\/p> <\\/li> <li> <p>The portion of Cathedral Wash between the road and the Colorado River.<\\/p> <\\/li> <li> <p>Orange Cliffs special permit area.<\\/p> <\\/li> <li> <p>Rainbow Bridge NM, except for pets in vessels at the courtesy docks.<\\/p> <\\/li> <li> <p>On the Colorado River downstream from the dam to the boundary of Grand Canyon National Park.<\\/p> <\\/li> <li> <p>Dogs and other companion animals are prohibited from entering Coyote Gulch within the boundaries of Glen Canyon National Recreation Area. (This prohibition does not apply to guide dogs accompanying visual impaired persons or to hearing ear dogs accompanying hearing impaired persons.) This prohibition includes all dogs or other pet animals used to carry or transport recreational equipment or food.<\\/p> <\\/li> <\\/ul>\",\"timeOfDayDescription\":\"\",\"feeDescription\":\"<p>There is no camping fee or permit required to camp on the lake in undeveloped areas. However, entrance fees and vessel use fees apply.<\\/p>\",\"age\":\"\",\"arePetsPermittedWithRestrictions\":\"false\",\"activities\":[{\"id\":\"4A58AF13-E8FB-4530-B41A-97DF0B0C77B7\",\"name\":\"Backcountry Camping\"}],\"activityDescription\":\"<p>\\\"Backcountry\\\" Shoreline camping on Lake Powell can range from toughing out the desert winter from your kayak to \\\"roughing it\\\" in a multimillion dollar houseboat and any level of camping\\/glamping in between. Know your boat before you go - check our <a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/glca\\/planyourvisit\\/boating.htm\\\">boating webpage<\\/a> for safety tips and regulations.<\\/p>\",\"locationDescription\":\"<p>Nearly 2000 miles of shoreline on Lake Powell are in undeveloped areas (outside of marinas) and you can beach your boat there for day trips or camping.<\\/p>\",\"doFeesApply\":\"false\",\"longDescription\":\"<p>By far the most popular activity in Glen Canyon National Recreation Area is boating. On a private boat or a rental, almost two million visitors enjoy the deep turquoise waters of Lake Powell every year. If you already have a boat, why not stay a night or two on your own beach under the stars? You can camp anywhere on the shorelines of Lake Powell.<\\/p> <p>When planning a camping trip by boat\\u00a0in Glen Canyon, it is best to buy a map beforehand. These show the side canyons, good hiking spots, points of interest and marinas, explain the navigation system, and may provide fishing information. In an emergency you will need to report your location.<\\/p> <p>The main channel varies in depth from 100 to 600 feet. It is recommended that you anchor on a beach for the night, as high winds can move boats into rocks and cause damage. There are no motor vehicles, off-road vehicles or bicycles allowed in Glen Canyon\'s roadless areas.<\\/p> <p><strong>All campsites are required to have a portable toilet unless toilets are available on the vessel or within 200 yards of the campsite.<\\/strong>\\u00a0Regular water-quality checks are conducted to ensure compliance with sanitation laws. Pets are allowed on beaches as long as waste is cleaned up. Dispose of waste properly. Burying waste of any kind on the beach is prohibited. Waste may not be contained in a plastic bag unless it is an NPS-approved Waste Bag Containment System, which must be deposited in the trash.<br \\/> <br \\/> Do not camp under overhanging rocks, as downpouring rain can sink a vessel.<br \\/> <br \\/> Ground fires are permitted along Lake Powell\'s shoreline below high-water level (3,700 feet), except within developed areas. Check for fire restrictions in effect due to high fire danger conditions. Such restrictions are a regular part of hot, dry summer months, but can occur any time of year. Do not leave trash in fire rings. Fireworks are illegal.<br \\/> <br \\/> Quiet hours are 10 pm to 6 am, unless otherwise posted.<br \\/> <br \\/> There is no camping allowed in Rainbow Bridge National Monument or within\\/on archeological sites.<\\/p>\",\"reservationDescription\":\"\",\"season\":[\"Winter\",\"Spring\",\"Summer\",\"Fall\"],\"topics\":[{\"id\":\"9C9FCBB6-360B-4743-8155-6F9341CBE01B\",\"name\":\"Scenic Views\"}],\"durationDescription\":\"<p>Camping is limited to 14 consecutive days and no more than 30 days total in the park in any calendar year.<\\/p>\",\"arePetsPermitted\":\"true\",\"timeOfDay\":[\"Day\",\"Night\",\"Dawn\",\"Dusk\"],\"duration\":\"1-14 Days\",\"credit\":\"\"}',1,'2023-09-10 21:06:05','2023-09-10 21:06:34'),(365,'Take a stroll to Horseshoe Bend Overlook','Where is Horseshoe Bend?','nps','8E4DCEB8-C379-4C1B-B8D2-855AC68B5A54',4,'Horseshoe Bend has been featured as a landmark for Glen Canyon National Recreation Area, Grand Canyon National Park, the Cities of Page AZ, Kanab UT, St George UT, and the Navajo Nation. Boundary lines are very close in some places. Horseshoe Bend itself, and that part of the Colorado River, are a part of Glen Canyon National Recreation Area. About nine miles downstream is where Grand Canyon National Park begins. US Highway 89, the land north of the trail to the Horseshoe Bend overlook, and the parking area for the trailhead are in the City of Page. The land south of the parking area and the trail and overlook are on the Navajo Nation. This mixture of jurisdictions provides a unique opportunity in the management of this magnificent trail and overlook.\\r\\n\\r\\n<p>The hike to the overlook is 1.5 miles (2.4 km) round-trip over a hardened path. The trail is accessible under the regulations of the Americans with Disabilities Act. There are two shade structures along the length of the trail, but not at the overlook. There are railings at the edge of the overlook\'s viewing platform, but not along the trail.</p> <p>As with any hike, be prepared with plenty of water, sturdy footwear, sunscreen, and other basic hiking essentials. Know that there is little shade along the Horseshoe Bend Trail. Avoid hiking during the hottest part of the day.</p>','A social media darling, Horseshoe Bend has become one of the most recognized and visited places in Glen Canyon National Recreation Area. The parking lot and trailhead is located off US Highway 89, approximately 5 miles (8 km) south of the Carl Hayden Visitor Center. Hiking The hike to the overlook is 1.5 miles (2.4 km) round-trip over a hardened path. The trail is accessible under the regulations of the Americans with Disabilities Act. There are two shade structures along the length of the trail, but not at the overlook. There are railings at the edge of the overlook\'s viewing platform, but not along the trail. Parking Fees Horseshoe Bend itself is in the park, but the parking lot is on city land. The City of Page requires visitors to pay for parking at the Horseshoe Bend trailhead. National Park Service passes do not apply for the parking lot. Contact the City of Page for questions about the parking at Horseshoe Bend at their website here.   PLEASE NOTE: On Holidays and busy weekends, the City of Page may require all visitors to use a shuttle to visit once the parking lot is full. Check with them if you plan to visit during busy times.',NULL,'The trail from the parking lot to the overlook is made of a sand-based mixture hardened with salts, in compliance with Americans with Disabilities Act regulations for low mobility accessibility, while maintaining the desert aesthetic look. The trail is lined with native rocks, no railings. There are two shade structures along the length of the trail, but no shade structure at the overlook viewing platform.  The viewing deck at the end of the trail is flat and has railings at the edge. it is compliant with the Architectural Barriers Act. Beyond the viewing deck, the edge is rocky and has no railings.','1-8 Hours','1',36.8768778,-111.5073586,NULL,'https://www.nps.gov/thingstodo/take-a-stroll-to-horseshoe-bend-overlook.htm',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,1,NULL,'{\"id\":\"8E4DCEB8-C379-4C1B-B8D2-855AC68B5A54\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/thingstodo\\/take-a-stroll-to-horseshoe-bend-overlook.htm\",\"title\":\"Take a stroll to Horseshoe Bend Overlook\",\"shortDescription\":\"Below the rim, the Colorado River makes a wide sweep around a sandstone escarpment. Here at Horseshoe Bend, the Colorado River created a roughly 1,000 foot (305 m) deep, 270\\u00ba horseshoe-shaped bend in Glen Canyon. Take an easy walk to look over the edge at this wonder.\",\"images\":[{\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/F7CF610E-9145-6CA4-DC028A949D9A4597.jpg\",\"credit\":\"NPS \\/ Brent&Dawn Davis\",\"altText\":\"River in almost circular curve at bottom of sandstone canyon\",\"title\":\"Horseshoe Bend on the Colorado River\",\"description\":\"\",\"caption\":\"The Colorado take a wide turn just six miles downstream of Glen Canyon Dam.\",\"crops\":[{\"aspectRatio\":\"1.78\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/primary\\/F7CF610E-9145-6CA4-DC028A949D9A4597.jpg\"},{\"aspectRatio\":\"1\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/secondary\\/F7CF610E-9145-6CA4-DC028A949D9A4597.jpg\"}]}],\"relatedParks\":[{\"states\":\"AZ,UT\",\"parkCode\":\"glca\",\"designation\":\"National Recreation Area\",\"fullName\":\"Glen Canyon National Recreation Area\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/glca\\/index.htm\",\"name\":\"Glen Canyon\"}],\"relatedOrganizations\":[],\"tags\":[\"Glen Canyon National Recreation Area\",\"City of Page\",\"Arizona\",\"Navajo Nation\",\"Horseshoe Bend\",\"Colorado River\",\"glen canyon dam\",\"geologic feature\",\"hiking\",\"walking\",\"desert\",\"overlook\",\"railing\",\"no railing\",\"ADA accessible\"],\"latitude\":\"36.87687775825146\",\"longitude\":\"-111.50735855576683\",\"geometryPoiId\":\"\",\"amenities\":[],\"location\":\"Where is Horseshoe Bend?\",\"seasonDescription\":\"\",\"accessibilityInformation\":\"The trail from the parking lot to the overlook is made of a sand-based mixture hardened with salts, in compliance with Americans with Disabilities Act regulations for low mobility accessibility, while maintaining the desert aesthetic look. The trail is lined with native rocks, no railings. There are two shade structures along the length of the trail, but no shade structure at the overlook viewing platform.<br \\/> <br \\/> The viewing deck at the end of the trail is flat and has railings at the edge. it is compliant with the Architectural Barriers Act. Beyond the viewing deck, the edge is rocky and has no railings.\",\"isReservationRequired\":\"false\",\"ageDescription\":\"All ages are welcome. The trail is stroller and wheelchair friendly.\",\"petsDescription\":\"Pets must be on a leash six feet or shorter. Pick up after your pet. During the summer season, the parking lot and trail is too hot for bare feet and bare paws. Consider booties for your furry friend. Do NOT leave your pet in your vehicle for any amount of time without you there. Bring enough water for you and your dog.\",\"timeOfDayDescription\":\"The parking lot and overlook is open from sunrise to sunset.\",\"feeDescription\":\"Horseshoe Bend itself is in the park, but the parking lot is on city land. The\\u00a0<a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/cityofpage.org\\/hsb\\\">City of Page<\\/a>\\u00a0requires visitors to pay for parking at the Horseshoe Bend trailhead. National Park Service passes do not apply for the parking lot. Contact the City of Page for questions about the parking at Horseshoe Bend at their website\\u00a0<a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/cityofpage.org\\/hsb\\\">here<\\/a>.\\u00a0<br \\/> <br \\/> <strong>PLEASE NOTE<\\/strong>: On Holidays and busy weekends, the City of Page may require all visitors to use a shuttle to visit once the parking lot is full. Check with them if you plan to visit during busy times.\",\"age\":\"\",\"arePetsPermittedWithRestrictions\":\"false\",\"activities\":[{\"id\":\"45261C0A-00D8-4C27-A1F8-029F933A0D34\",\"name\":\"Front-Country Hiking\"}],\"activityDescription\":\"<p>The hike to the overlook is 1.5 miles (2.4 km) round-trip over a hardened path. The trail is accessible under the regulations of the Americans with Disabilities Act. There are two shade structures along the length of the trail, but not at the overlook. There are railings at the edge of the overlook\'s viewing platform, but not along the trail.<\\/p> <p>As with any hike, be prepared with plenty of water, sturdy footwear, sunscreen, and other basic hiking essentials. Know that there is little shade along the Horseshoe Bend Trail. Avoid hiking during the hottest part of the day.<\\/p>\",\"locationDescription\":\"<p>Horseshoe Bend has been featured as a landmark for Glen Canyon National Recreation Area, Grand Canyon National Park, the Cities of Page AZ, Kanab UT, St George UT, and the Navajo Nation. Boundary lines are very close in some places. Horseshoe Bend itself, and that part of the Colorado River, are a part of Glen Canyon National Recreation Area. About nine miles downstream is where Grand Canyon National Park begins. US Highway 89, the land north of the trail to the Horseshoe Bend overlook, and the parking area for the trailhead are in the City of Page. The land south of the parking area and the trail and overlook are on the Navajo Nation. This mixture of jurisdictions provides a unique opportunity in the management of this magnificent trail and overlook.<\\/p>\",\"doFeesApply\":\"true\",\"longDescription\":\"<p>A social media darling, Horseshoe Bend has become one of the most recognized and visited places in Glen Canyon National Recreation Area. The parking lot and trailhead is located off US\\u00a0Highway 89, approximately 5 miles (8 km) south of the Carl Hayden Visitor Center.<\\/p> <h4>Hiking<\\/h4> <p>The hike to the overlook is 1.5 miles (2.4 km) round-trip over a hardened path. The trail is accessible under the regulations of the Americans with Disabilities Act. There are two shade structures along the length of the trail, but not at the overlook. There are railings at the edge of the overlook\'s viewing platform, but not along the trail.<\\/p> <h4>Parking Fees<\\/h4> <p and=\\\"\\\" area=\\\"\\\" bend=\\\"\\\" by=\\\"\\\" canyon=\\\"\\\" city=\\\"\\\" glen=\\\"\\\" horseshoe=\\\"\\\" in=\\\"\\\" is=\\\"\\\" located=\\\"\\\" lot=\\\"\\\" managed=\\\"\\\" national=\\\"\\\" of=\\\"\\\" page.=\\\"\\\" park=\\\"\\\" parking=\\\"\\\" recreation=\\\"\\\" service.=\\\"\\\" the=\\\"\\\" within=\\\"\\\">Horseshoe Bend itself is in the park, but the parking lot is on city land. The\\u00a0<a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/cityofpage.org\\/hsb\\\">City of Page<\\/a>\\u00a0requires visitors to pay for parking at the Horseshoe Bend trailhead. National Park Service passes do not apply for the parking lot. Contact the City of Page for questions about the parking at Horseshoe Bend at their website\\u00a0<a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/cityofpage.org\\/hsb\\\">here<\\/a>.\\u00a0<br \\/> <br \\/> <strong>PLEASE NOTE<\\/strong>: On Holidays and busy weekends, the City of Page may require all visitors to use a shuttle to visit once the parking lot is full. Check with them if you plan to visit during busy times.<\\/p>\",\"reservationDescription\":\"\",\"season\":[\"Winter\",\"Spring\",\"Summer\",\"Fall\"],\"topics\":[{\"id\":\"F0F97E32-2F29-41B4-AF98-9FBE8DAB36B1\",\"name\":\"Geology\"},{\"id\":\"A155238F-0DD2-4610-9B87-05FCE1C59283\",\"name\":\"River and Riparian\"},{\"id\":\"4244F489-6813-4B7A-9D0C-20CE098C8FFF\",\"name\":\"Rock Landscapes and Features\"},{\"id\":\"9C9FCBB6-360B-4743-8155-6F9341CBE01B\",\"name\":\"Scenic Views\"},{\"id\":\"5BE55D7F-BDB6-4E3D-AC35-2D8EBB974417\",\"name\":\"Trails\"}],\"durationDescription\":\"The Horseshoe Bend trail and overlook is only open during daylight hours. From sunrise to sunset you can spend as little or as much time as you would like there. There is no overnight camping.\",\"arePetsPermitted\":\"true\",\"timeOfDay\":[\"Day\",\"Dawn\",\"Dusk\"],\"duration\":\"1-8 Hours\",\"credit\":\"\"}',1,'2023-09-10 21:06:05','2023-09-10 21:06:34'),(366,'2 pm: Geology Talk — Thursday through Monday - S. Rim Village','Yavapai Geology Museum at Yavapai Point','nps','C5E855A8-C57D-4E89-87C0-363268657BC3',2,'Yavapai Point is located on Village Loop Road, in between Market Plaza and the Grand Caynon Visitor Center. From the far end of the Yavapai Point parking lot, by the shuttle bus stop, walk the paved path up the hill on your left to the stone musuem building, perched on the edge of the canyon rim. Meet the ranger at 9 am, just outside of the building. \\r\\n\\r\\n<p>In a free 30 minute presentation, a park ranger will discus how Grand Canyon formed, along with some of the science behind the scenery. </p>','Enhance your afternoon with quick insights into the geologic story of Grand Canyon at our \"Geology Talk\" ranger program - presented Thursday through Monday at 2 pm. Meet in front of Yavapai Geology Museum. Learn how this immense canyon formed and the science behind the landscape. The program is 30 minutes long and is accessible. Presented Thursday through Monday. No program on Tuesday and Wednesday.  Grand Canyon is one-mile deep, up to 18 miles wide, 277 river miles long, and displays more than 20 layers of rock The layers tell stories of oceans, deserts, mountains, and swamps that were once here, brought into existence, then swept away over the vast expanse of time.   Grand Canyon is a geologically young landscape being sculpted from very old rocks. Knowing some of the stories encoded in the rock layers enriches your understanding of our planet and enhances your experience at Grand Canyon.  The program is free of charge. Children must be accompanied by an adult.  The Geology Talk is subject to cancellation due to inclement weather or when lightning danger is present.',NULL,'The wide, paved path, up the hill from the parking lot (and shuttle bus stop) to the museum, is accessible. The Kaibab Rim (Orange) Route shuttle bus provides service from Grand Canyon Visitor Center to Yavapai Point and Geology Museum.  Park Shuttle buses are equipped with ramps and space to carry passengers in wheelchairs. Please note: wheelchairs larger than 30 inches wide by 48 inches long (76 cm x 122 cm) cannot be accommodated on shuttle buses and most motorized scooters will not fit.','25-30 Minutes','1',36.0660834,-112.1175501,NULL,'https://www.nps.gov/thingstodo/grand-canyon-geology-glimpse-ranger-program.htm',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,1,NULL,'{\"id\":\"C5E855A8-C57D-4E89-87C0-363268657BC3\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/thingstodo\\/grand-canyon-geology-glimpse-ranger-program.htm\",\"title\":\"2 pm: Geology Talk \\u2014 Thursday through Monday - S. Rim Village\",\"shortDescription\":\"Geology Talk Ranger Program - 2 pm: Offered Thursday through Monday. Gain insight into Grand Canyon\\u2019s unique geologic story. Meet in front of Yavapai Geology Museum. Program duration: 30 minutes. Presented daily. Learn how this immense canyon formed and the science behind the landscape. Knowing some of the stories encoded in the rock layers will enrich your understanding and enhance your experience at Grand Canyon.\",\"images\":[{\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/82734006-904A-9333-1B971B5544BC7C2C.jpg\",\"credit\":\"NPS\\/Bryan Maul\",\"altText\":\"At a scenic overlook with railings, a park ranger is presenting a talk to a group of visitors.\",\"title\":\"Ranger\'s Choice Program 1\",\"description\":\"\",\"caption\":\"Learn how Grand Canyon was formed during our 2 pm geology talk \\u2014presented Thursday through Monday\",\"crops\":[{\"aspectRatio\":\"1.78\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/primary\\/82734006-904A-9333-1B971B5544BC7C2C.jpg\"},{\"aspectRatio\":\"1\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/secondary\\/82734006-904A-9333-1B971B5544BC7C2C.jpg\"}]}],\"relatedParks\":[{\"states\":\"AZ\",\"parkCode\":\"grca\",\"designation\":\"National Park\",\"fullName\":\"Grand Canyon National Park\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/grca\\/index.htm\",\"name\":\"Grand Canyon\"}],\"relatedOrganizations\":[],\"tags\":[\"geology\",\"geology activities\",\"geology talk\",\"Time travel\",\"paleontology\",\"Colorado River\",\"Grand Canyon\",\"Arizona\"],\"latitude\":\"36.06608335897417\",\"longitude\":\"-112.1175500777901\",\"geometryPoiId\":\"48e55f61-b589-4ba4-8ea1-3e78530e045d\",\"amenities\":[],\"location\":\"Yavapai Geology Museum at Yavapai Point\",\"seasonDescription\":\"\",\"accessibilityInformation\":\"<p>The wide, paved path, up the hill from the parking lot (and shuttle bus stop) to the museum, is accessible.<\\/p> <p dir=\\\"ltr\\\" height=\\\"1\\\">The Kaibab Rim (Orange) Route shuttle bus provides service from Grand Canyon Visitor Center to Yavapai Point and Geology Museum.<br \\/> <br \\/> Park Shuttle buses are equipped with ramps and space to carry passengers in\\u00a0wheelchairs. Please note:\\u00a0wheelchairs larger than 30 inches wide by 48 inches long (76 cm x 122 cm) cannot be accommodated on shuttle buses and most\\u00a0motorized scooters\\u00a0will not fit.<\\/p>\",\"isReservationRequired\":\"false\",\"ageDescription\":\"All ages are welcome, but minors must be with an adult parent or guardian.\\u00a0\",\"petsDescription\":\"Pets are allowed, however they must be on a leash.\",\"timeOfDayDescription\":\"The Geology Talk ranger program is presented Thursday through Monday at 2 pm. The program is not presented on Tuesday and Wednesday.\",\"feeDescription\":\"The program is free and open to the public\",\"age\":\"\",\"arePetsPermittedWithRestrictions\":\"false\",\"activities\":[{\"id\":\"09DF0950-D319-4557-A57E-04CD2F63FF42\",\"name\":\"Arts and Culture\"}],\"activityDescription\":\"<p>In a free 30 minute presentation, a park ranger will discus how Grand Canyon formed, along with some of the science behind the scenery.\\u00a0<\\/p>\",\"locationDescription\":\"<p>Yavapai Point is located on Village Loop Road, in between Market Plaza and the Grand Caynon Visitor Center. From the far\\u00a0end of the Yavapai Point parking lot, by the shuttle bus stop,\\u00a0walk the paved path up the hill on your left\\u00a0to the stone musuem building, perched\\u00a0on the\\u00a0edge of the canyon rim. Meet the ranger at 9 am, just\\u00a0outside of the\\u00a0building.\\u00a0<\\/p>\",\"doFeesApply\":\"false\",\"longDescription\":\"<p>Enhance your afternoon with quick insights into the geologic story of Grand Canyon at our \\\"Geology Talk\\\" ranger program - <strong>presented Thursday through Monday at 2 pm.<\\/strong> Meet in front of Yavapai Geology Museum. Learn how this immense canyon formed and the science behind the landscape. The program is 30 minutes long and is accessible. Presented Thursday through Monday. No program on Tuesday and Wednesday.<br \\/> <br \\/> Grand Canyon is one-mile deep, up to 18 miles wide, 277 river miles long, and displays more than 20 layers of rock The layers tell stories of oceans, deserts, mountains, and swamps that were once here, brought into existence, then swept away over the vast expanse of time.\\u00a0<br \\/> <br \\/> Grand Canyon is a geologically young landscape being sculpted from very old rocks. Knowing some of the stories encoded in the rock layers enriches your understanding of our planet and enhances your experience at Grand Canyon.<br \\/> <br \\/> The program is free of charge. Children must be accompanied by an adult. \\u00a0The Geology Talk is subject to cancellation due to inclement weather or when lightning danger is present.<\\/p>\",\"reservationDescription\":\"No reservations are required.\",\"season\":[\"Summer\"],\"topics\":[{\"id\":\"F6D3A52E-608F-47D6-96DF-1FD64122A2FC\",\"name\":\"Fossils and Paleontology\"},{\"id\":\"F0F97E32-2F29-41B4-AF98-9FBE8DAB36B1\",\"name\":\"Geology\"},{\"id\":\"4244F489-6813-4B7A-9D0C-20CE098C8FFF\",\"name\":\"Rock Landscapes and Features\"}],\"durationDescription\":\"<p>The Geology Talk\\u00a0program is presented Thursday through Monday\\u00a0at 2 pm, and lasts 30 minutes. The program is not presented on Tuesday and Wednesday.<\\/p>\",\"arePetsPermitted\":\"true\",\"timeOfDay\":[\"Day\"],\"duration\":\"25-30 Minutes\",\"credit\":\"\"}',1,'2023-09-10 21:06:05','2023-09-10 21:06:35'),(367,'Shuttle Bus and Walking Tour of Scenic Hermit Road','Hermit Road Interchange','nps','D7062B6B-2FF8-4D6C-82DE-4D6106A21BA7',2,'Scenic Hermit Road begins at the interchange, on the west side of the Village Historic District, near Bright Angel Lodge. Just beyond the gate arms is the Hermit Road Shuttle Transfer Station. You may board the shuttle here, or continue walking along the paved, Canyon Rim Trail.   From the interchange, the road climbs a steep hill, then follows the canyon rim for 7 miles (11 km) out to Hermits Rest, and the Hermit Trailhead.\\r\\n\\r\\n<p>Riding the free Hermits Rest (Red) Route shuttle bus to tour a scenic road with bus stops at 9 outstanding scenic overlooks. <br />  </p> <ul> <li>The shuttle buses are great when you use them in conjunction with the rim trail - you can get off at one stop, enjoy a scenic walk along the rim, and then get back on the bus at the next stop. </li> </ul>   <ul> <li>The <a href=\"https://www.nps.gov/grca/planyourvisit/upload/RimTrail.pdf\">Canyon Rim Trail</a> follows the rim of the canyon for 7.8 miles (12.6 km) alongside Hermit Road. <em><strong>Hermit Road </strong></em><em>is also accessed throughout the year by</em><em> <strong>hikers, runners and </strong><b>bicyclists</b></em><strong><em>.</em></strong></li> </ul>   <ul> <li><strong>Shuttle bus bike racks can accommodate two or three bicycles</strong>, with wheels greater than 16 inches (41 cm). Riders must load and unload their bicycles.</li> </ul> <p><br />  </p>','Hermit Road is a scenic route along the west end of Grand Canyon Village on the South Rim which follows the rim for 7 miles (11 km) out to Hermits Rest.  This extremely popular road is accessed by free park shuttle bus, foot, bicycle, or commercial bus tour most of the year, with private vehicles allowed only during winter months.  Along the rim are shuttle bus stops at nine designated viewpoints . During most of the day, the shuttles run every 15 minutes. The Canyon Rim Trail also follows the rim of the canyon for 7.8 miles (12.6 km) alongside Hermit Road and offers the opportunity for short or long walks between viewpoints on both paved and dirt trails.    Hermit Road provides some of the best Grand Canyon views that you can see anywhere. Once you get out to Hopi Point, the Colorado River comes into view, and on a quiet day at Pima Point, you can hear the roar of the Colorado River as it crashes through Granite Rapid.     The shuttle buses are great when you use them in conjunction with the rim trail - you can get off at one stop, enjoy a scenic walk along the rim, and then get back on the bus at the next stop.      Hermit Road is closed to private vehicles between March 1 and November 30. During this 9 month period, most visitors access the road via the free Hermits Rest (Red) Route Shuttle >       Commercial Bus Tours are available throughout the year.     Hermit Road is only open to private vehicles during the winter months of December, January and February. There is no shuttle bus service during these months. Parking is limited along Hermit Road, especially during Christmas and New Year\'s weeks.',NULL,'Shuttle buses are equipped with ramps and space to carry passengers in wheelchairs. Please note: wheelchairs larger than 30 inches wide by 48 inches long (76 cm x 122 cm) cannot be accommodated on shuttle buses and most motorized scooters will not fit. Collapse strollers before entering the shuttle bus. No oversized or jogging strollers. Remove baby-back carriers when seated.    Shuttle buses can accommodate two or three bicycles, but not tag alongs, baby trailers, or children\'s bicycles with wheels less than 16 inches (41 cm). Riders must load and unload their bicycles    Many of the paths between shuttle stops and scenic overlooks meet accessibility guidelines for outdoor developed areas.   Vault toilets are available at Hopi Point and Hermits Rest.   Water and a snackbar are available at Hermits Rest.   All shuttle buses are wheelchair accessible, however, the park does offer a Scenic Drive Accessibility Permit, which allows entry for visitors with mobility issues to some areas closed to public traffic. The permit is available at entrance stations and National Park Service visitor centers.','90-360 Minutes','1',36.0570776,-112.1447504,NULL,'https://www.nps.gov/thingstodo/grca-tour-scenic-hermit-road-on-free-shuttle-buses.htm',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,1,NULL,'{\"id\":\"D7062B6B-2FF8-4D6C-82DE-4D6106A21BA7\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/thingstodo\\/grca-tour-scenic-hermit-road-on-free-shuttle-buses.htm\",\"title\":\"Shuttle Bus and Walking Tour of Scenic Hermit Road\",\"shortDescription\":\"Hermit Road\\u00a0is a scenic road beginning on the west side of Grand Canyon Village. The road follows the canyon rim for 7 miles (11 km) out to Hermits Rest, and the Hermit Trailhead. Along the canyon rim are\\u00a09 designated viewpoints. The\\u00a0Canyon Rim Trail\\u00a0also follows the rim of the canyon for 7.8 miles (12.6 km) alongside Hermit road, and offers opportunities for short or long walks between viewpoints\\u00a0\\u2014\\u00a0on both paved and dirt trails.\",\"images\":[{\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/9477539A-D844-ADCC-CDC1D0629B558EC8.jpg\",\"credit\":\"Photo courtesy of Aerial Filmworks (all rights reserved)\",\"altText\":\"A paved scenic road in a forested area on the edge of a mile deep canyon filled with colorful peaks\",\"title\":\"Aerial View of Hermit Road\",\"description\":\"\",\"caption\":\"Scenic Hermit Road is open to private vehicles only during December, January and February.\",\"crops\":[{\"aspectRatio\":\"1.78\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/primary\\/9477539A-D844-ADCC-CDC1D0629B558EC8.jpg\"},{\"aspectRatio\":\"1\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/secondary\\/9477539A-D844-ADCC-CDC1D0629B558EC8.jpg\"}]}],\"relatedParks\":[{\"states\":\"AZ\",\"parkCode\":\"grca\",\"designation\":\"National Park\",\"fullName\":\"Grand Canyon National Park\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/grca\\/index.htm\",\"name\":\"Grand Canyon\"}],\"relatedOrganizations\":[],\"tags\":[\"tour\",\"walking\",\"Hermit Road\",\"grand canyon. south rim\",\"Grand Canyon\",\"Touring\",\"historic road\",\"Scenic Road\",\"hermit trail\",\"bus tour\",\"bicycle tour\",\"bicycle trail\"],\"latitude\":\"36.05707755677959\",\"longitude\":\"-112.14475039172362\",\"geometryPoiId\":\"\",\"amenities\":[],\"location\":\"Hermit Road Interchange\",\"seasonDescription\":\"<p><strong>The Hermits Rest\\u00a0(Red) Route shuttle is in operation between March 1, and November 30. During those nine months, Hermit Road is closed to private vehicles.\\u00a0<\\/strong><br \\/> <br \\/> Hermit Road is only open to private vehicles during the three winter months of December, January and February, when there is no shuttle service.\\u00a0<\\/p>\",\"accessibilityInformation\":\"<ul dir=\\\"ltr\\\"> <li height=\\\"1\\\"><strong>Shuttle buses are equipped with ramps and space to carry passengers in\\u00a0wheelchairs.\\u00a0<\\/strong>Please note:\\u00a0wheelchairs larger than 30 inches wide by 48 inches long (76 cm x 122 cm) cannot be accommodated on shuttle buses and most\\u00a0motorized scooters\\u00a0will not fit.<\\/li> <li height=\\\"1\\\"><strong>Collapse strollers<\\/strong>\\u00a0before entering the shuttle bus. No oversized or jogging strollers. Remove baby-back carriers when seated. \\u00a0<\\/li> <li dir=\\\"ltr\\\"> <p dir=\\\"ltr\\\" height=\\\"1\\\"><strong>Shuttle buses can accommodate two or three bicycles<\\/strong>, but not tag alongs, baby trailers, or children\'s bicycles with wheels less than 16 inches (41 cm). Riders must load and unload their bicycles \\u00a0<\\/p> <\\/li> <li height=\\\"1\\\"><strong>Many of the paths\\u00a0between shuttle\\u00a0stops and scenic overlooks meet\\u00a0accessibility guidelines for outdoor developed areas.<\\/strong> \\u00a0<\\/li> <li height=\\\"1\\\">Vault toilets are available at Hopi Point and Hermits Rest. \\u00a0<\\/li> <li height=\\\"1\\\">Water and a snackbar are available at Hermits Rest. \\u00a0<\\/li> <li height=\\\"1\\\"><strong>All shuttle buses are wheelchair accessible, however, the park does offer a Scenic Drive Accessibility Permit, which allows entry for visitors with mobility issues to some areas closed to public traffic.<\\/strong>\\u00a0The permit is available at entrance stations and National Park Service visitor centers.<\\/li> <\\/ul>\",\"isReservationRequired\":\"false\",\"ageDescription\":\"<p>All ages are welcome, however,\\u00a0minors must be with an adult parent or guardian to be able\\u00a0walk the trails.\\u00a0<\\/p>\",\"petsDescription\":\"<p><strong>Pets are not allowed on the shuttle buses.<\\/strong>\\u00a0<br \\/> <br \\/> Service dogs are permitted (as defined by the\\u00a0Americans With Disabilities Act)<br \\/> <br \\/> If you only plan to walk along the Canyon Rim Trail, leashed pets are allowed at the scenic overlooks and along the trail. Pets are not allowed on trails the descend into the depths of Grand Canyon.\\u00a0<\\/p>\",\"timeOfDayDescription\":\"<p>During\\u00a0<strong>summer thunderstorms\\u00a0<\\/strong>or other inclement weather,\\u00a0<strong>shuttle bus service will be temporarily suspended on Hermit Road.<\\/strong><\\/p> <p><br \\/> During winter months, snow storms can close Hermit Road for days at a time. it is best to plan on touring the road during the day, when there is greater visibility and warmer temperatures.\\u00a0<br \\/> \\u00a0<\\/p>\",\"feeDescription\":\"<p>The is no fee or toll required to board the Hermits Rest (Red) Route shuttle bus and experience Hermit Road.<\\/p>\",\"age\":\"\",\"arePetsPermittedWithRestrictions\":\"false\",\"activities\":[{\"id\":\"3DE599E2-22ED-40BF-B383-EDA073563C1E\",\"name\":\"Bus\\/Shuttle Guided Tour\"}],\"activityDescription\":\"<p>Riding the\\u00a0free Hermits Rest (Red) Route shuttle bus to tour a scenic road with bus stops at 9 outstanding scenic overlooks.\\u00a0<br \\/> \\u00a0<\\/p> <ul> <li>The shuttle buses are great when you use them in conjunction with the rim trail - you can get off at one stop, enjoy a scenic walk along the rim, and then get back on the bus at the next stop.\\u00a0<\\/li> <\\/ul> \\u00a0 <ul> <li>The\\u00a0<a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/grca\\/planyourvisit\\/upload\\/RimTrail.pdf\\\">Canyon Rim Trail<\\/a>\\u00a0follows the rim of the canyon for 7.8 miles (12.6 km) alongside Hermit Road.\\u00a0<em><strong>Hermit Road\\u00a0<\\/strong><\\/em><em>is also accessed throughout the year by<\\/em><em>\\u00a0<strong>hikers, runners and\\u00a0<\\/strong><b>bicyclists<\\/b><\\/em><strong><em>.<\\/em><\\/strong><\\/li> <\\/ul> \\u00a0 <ul> <li><strong>Shuttle bus bike racks\\u00a0can accommodate two or three bicycles<\\/strong>, with wheels greater than 16 inches (41 cm). Riders must load and unload their bicycles.<\\/li> <\\/ul> <p><br \\/> \\u00a0<\\/p>\",\"locationDescription\":\"<p><strong>Scenic Hermit Road<\\/strong>\\u00a0begins at the interchange, on the west side of the Village Historic District, near Bright Angel Lodge.<b>\\u00a0Just beyond the gate arms is the Hermit Road Shuttle Transfer Station. You may board the shuttle here, or continue walking along the paved, Canyon Rim Trail.\\u00a0<\\/b><br \\/> <br \\/> <strong>From the interchange, the road climbs a steep hill, then\\u00a0follows the canyon rim for 7 miles (11 km) out to Hermits Rest, and the Hermit Trailhead.<\\/strong><\\/p>\",\"doFeesApply\":\"false\",\"longDescription\":\"<p><strong>Hermit Road is a scenic route along the west end of Grand Canyon Village on the South Rim which follows the rim for 7 miles (11 km) out to Hermits Rest.<\\/strong><br \\/> <br \\/> <strong>This extremely popular road\\u00a0is accessed by free park shuttle bus, foot, bicycle, or commercial bus tour most of the year,<\\/strong> with private vehicles allowed only during winter months.<br \\/> <br \\/> <strong>Along the rim are shuttle bus stops at nine designated viewpoints .<\\/strong>\\u00a0During most of the day, the shuttles run every 15 minutes. The Canyon Rim Trail also follows the rim of the canyon for 7.8 miles (12.6 km) alongside Hermit Road and offers the opportunity for short or long walks between viewpoints on both paved and dirt trails.<br \\/> \\u00a0<\\/p> <ul> <li>Hermit Road provides\\u00a0some of the best Grand Canyon views that you can see anywhere. Once you get out to Hopi Point, the\\u00a0<strong>Colorado River<\\/strong>\\u00a0comes into view, and on a quiet day at Pima Point, you can hear the roar of the Colorado River as it crashes through Granite Rapid.<\\/li> <\\/ul> \\u00a0 <ul> <li>The shuttle buses are great when you use them in conjunction with the rim trail - you can get off at one stop, enjoy a scenic walk along the rim, and then get back on the bus at the next stop.\\u00a0<\\/li> <\\/ul> \\u00a0 <ul> <li><em><strong>Hermit Road is closed to private vehicles between March 1 and November 30.\\u00a0<\\/strong>During this 9 month period, most visitors access the road via the free\\u00a0<a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/grca\\/planyourvisit\\/hermit-red-route.htm\\\">Hermits Rest (Red)\\u00a0Route\\u00a0Shuttle ><\\/a><\\/em> \\u00a0<\\/li> <\\/ul> \\u00a0 <ul> <li><em><a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/www.grandcanyonlodges.com\\/plan\\/interpretive-bus-tours\\/\\\">Commercial\\u00a0Bus Tours<\\/a><strong>\\u00a0<\\/strong>are available throughout the year.<\\/em><\\/li> <\\/ul> \\u00a0 <ul> <li><em><strong>Hermit Road is only open to private vehicles<\\/strong><\\/em><em><strong>\\u00a0<\\/strong>during the winter months of\\u00a0<\\/em><em><strong>December, January and February.\\u00a0<\\/strong>There is no shuttle bus service during these months.\\u00a0<strong>Parking is limited along Hermit Road, <\\/strong>especially during Christmas and New Year\'s weeks.<\\/em><\\/li> <\\/ul>\",\"reservationDescription\":\"<p>No reservations are required, however, <strong>during midday, <\\/strong>in spring and summer,<strong>\\u00a0the shuttle boarding line can be long at the Hermit Road Transfer Station \\u2013 you may have to wait for two to three buses, between 30 minutes and one hour.<\\/strong><\\/p>\",\"season\":[\"Spring\",\"Summer\",\"Fall\"],\"topics\":[{\"id\":\"BCE3ACBE-7871-495B-AEA3-5E19BC482405\",\"name\":\"Canyons and Canyonlands\"},{\"id\":\"F0F97E32-2F29-41B4-AF98-9FBE8DAB36B1\",\"name\":\"Geology\"},{\"id\":\"A7359FC4-DAD8-45F5-AF15-7FF62F816ED3\",\"name\":\"Night Sky\"},{\"id\":\"4244F489-6813-4B7A-9D0C-20CE098C8FFF\",\"name\":\"Rock Landscapes and Features\"},{\"id\":\"9C9FCBB6-360B-4743-8155-6F9341CBE01B\",\"name\":\"Scenic Views\"},{\"id\":\"5BE55D7F-BDB6-4E3D-AC35-2D8EBB974417\",\"name\":\"Trails\"},{\"id\":\"0BBD4A42-2B3D-4E82-B5C4-1A3874C8682E\",\"name\":\"Roads, Routes and Highways\"}],\"durationDescription\":\"<p>If you don\'t get off the bus, a round-trip takes 80 minutes.\\u00a0<br \\/> <br \\/> Allow between 90 minutes and 4 hours, depending how many stops you make along the road to get out to stretch, experience the scenic views, and to walk portions of the Canyon Rim Trail between the bus stops.\\u00a0\\u00a0\\u00a0<\\/p>\",\"arePetsPermitted\":\"false\",\"timeOfDay\":[\"Day\",\"Dawn\",\"Dusk\"],\"duration\":\"90-360 Minutes\",\"credit\":\"\"}',1,'2023-09-10 21:06:05','2023-09-10 21:06:35'),(368,'Weeping Rock','Weeping Rock Trailhead','nps','71BB3562-AC4B-438D-94E0-784C0671C6C7',7,'Weeping Rock Trailhead, Shuttle Stop 7\\r\\n\\r\\n','Short but steep! From the parking area, you\'ll cross the bridge and head to the left onto a paved trail to Weeping Rock. Along the way, look for small trailside exhibits and watch for minor drop-offs. The trail ends at a set of steps leading you to a rock alcove with dripping springs.  Pit toilets are available at the parking area.',NULL,'Short but steep paved trail that gains 98ft (30m) of elevation in 0.4mi (0.6km). Minor drop-offs. Trail includes several stairs.','15-45 Minutes','1',NULL,NULL,NULL,'https://www.nps.gov/thingstodo/weeping-rock.htm',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,1,NULL,'{\"id\":\"71BB3562-AC4B-438D-94E0-784C0671C6C7\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/thingstodo\\/weeping-rock.htm\",\"title\":\"Weeping Rock\",\"shortDescription\":\"Short but steep! From the parking area, you\'ll cross the bridge and head to the left onto a paved trail to Weeping Rock. Along the way, look for small trailside exhibits and watch for minor drop-offs. The trail ends at a set of steps leading you to a rock alcove with dripping springs.\",\"images\":[{\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/CB137FB1-BD88-59D0-BAF97CE53CE3EC59.jpg\",\"credit\":\"NPS\\/Abi Farish\",\"altText\":\"A large, arch shaped alcove streaked with dark shades of orange on red rock.\",\"title\":\"Weeping Rock\",\"description\":\"\",\"caption\":\"View of Weeping Rock from above\",\"crops\":[{\"aspectRatio\":\"1.78\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/primary\\/CB137FB1-BD88-59D0-BAF97CE53CE3EC59.jpg\"},{\"aspectRatio\":\"1\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/secondary\\/CB137FB1-BD88-59D0-BAF97CE53CE3EC59.jpg\"}]}],\"relatedParks\":[{\"states\":\"UT\",\"parkCode\":\"zion\",\"designation\":\"National Park\",\"fullName\":\"Zion National Park\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/zion\\/index.htm\",\"name\":\"Zion\"}],\"relatedOrganizations\":[],\"tags\":[\"Weeping Rock\",\"Spring\",\"seep\",\"hanging garden\",\"Zion National Park\",\"short hike\",\"Easy hike\",\"Steep\",\"water\"],\"latitude\":\"\",\"longitude\":\"\",\"geometryPoiId\":\"\",\"amenities\":[],\"location\":\"Weeping Rock Trailhead\",\"seasonDescription\":\"\",\"accessibilityInformation\":\"Short but steep paved trail that gains 98ft (30m) of elevation in 0.4mi (0.6km). Minor drop-offs. Trail includes several stairs.\",\"isReservationRequired\":\"false\",\"ageDescription\":\"\",\"petsDescription\":\"Pets are not allowed on any trail other than the Pa\'rus Trail.\",\"timeOfDayDescription\":\"\",\"feeDescription\":\"Park entrance fees apply.\",\"age\":\"\",\"arePetsPermittedWithRestrictions\":\"false\",\"activities\":[{\"id\":\"BFF8C027-7C8F-480B-A5F8-CD8CE490BFBA\",\"name\":\"Hiking\"}],\"activityDescription\":\"\",\"locationDescription\":\"<p><span style=\\\"color:#000000;\\\">Weeping Rock Trailhead, Shuttle Stop 7<\\/span><\\/p>\",\"doFeesApply\":\"false\",\"longDescription\":\"<h4><span style=\\\"color: rgb(70, 70, 70); font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif; font-size: 14px; font-weight: normal; text-align: var(--bs-body-text-align);\\\">Short but steep! From the parking area, you\'ll cross the bridge and head to the left onto a paved trail to Weeping Rock. Along the way, look for small trailside exhibits and watch for minor drop-offs. The trail ends at a set of steps leading you to a rock alcove with dripping springs.<\\/span><\\/h4> <br \\/> Pit toilets are available at the parking area.\",\"reservationDescription\":\"\",\"season\":[\"Winter\",\"Spring\",\"Summer\",\"Fall\"],\"topics\":[{\"id\":\"5BE55D7F-BDB6-4E3D-AC35-2D8EBB974417\",\"name\":\"Trails\"}],\"durationDescription\":\"\",\"arePetsPermitted\":\"false\",\"timeOfDay\":[\"Day\",\"Night\",\"Dawn\",\"Dusk\"],\"duration\":\"15-45 Minutes\",\"credit\":\"\"}',1,'2023-09-10 21:06:07','2023-09-10 21:06:36'),(369,'Zion Ranger-led Activities','Zion Canyon Visitor Center','nps','4E694781-7E95-452D-9EA6-04974841F8DC',7,'This is shuttle stop #1. \\r\\n\\r\\n','Join a park ranger to learn about what makes Zion National Park such a special place! We have a variety of ranger-led activities throughout the year, and you can check the schedule on our Events Calendar. Topics range from geology and wildlife to art and human history, and everything inbetween. All ranger-led programs are free.',NULL,'Assistive listening devices are available at the Zion Canyon Visitor Center, as well as supplemental information for park programs.   All ranger-led talks and evening programs currently take place outdoors. Talks at the Visitor Center take place under a shade structure with sandstone benches for seating. Space is available for mobility devices and wheelchairs.   The Ride with A Ranger program takes place on Zion Canyon shuttle buses, which are wheelchair accessible.   Ranger walks on the Pa\'rus Trail are wheelchair accessible, but hikes on the Watchman Trail are not.   ','20-120 Minutes','1',37.2004112,-112.9873091,NULL,'https://www.nps.gov/thingstodo/zion-ranger-led-activities.htm',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,1,NULL,'{\"id\":\"4E694781-7E95-452D-9EA6-04974841F8DC\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/thingstodo\\/zion-ranger-led-activities.htm\",\"title\":\"Zion Ranger-led Activities\",\"shortDescription\":\"Join a park ranger to learn about what makes Zion National Park such a special place! We have a variety of ranger-led activities throughout the year, and you can check the schedule on our Events Calendar. Topics range from geology and\\u00a0wildlife to art and\\u00a0human history, and everything inbetween. All ranger-led programs are free.\",\"images\":[{\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/694B395C-9537-E7C1-F2A6D46A2D7868BE.jpg\",\"credit\":\"NPS\\/Abi Farish\",\"altText\":\"Four NPS rangers stand under a shade structure holding various props.\",\"title\":\"Zion Ranger-led Programs\",\"description\":\"\",\"caption\":\"Ranger-led programs are a great way to learn about the park\",\"crops\":[{\"aspectRatio\":\"1.78\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/primary\\/694B395C-9537-E7C1-F2A6D46A2D7868BE.jpg\"},{\"aspectRatio\":\"1\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/secondary\\/694B395C-9537-E7C1-F2A6D46A2D7868BE.jpg\"}]}],\"relatedParks\":[{\"states\":\"UT\",\"parkCode\":\"zion\",\"designation\":\"National Park\",\"fullName\":\"Zion National Park\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/zion\\/index.htm\",\"name\":\"Zion\"}],\"relatedOrganizations\":[],\"tags\":[\"ranger program\",\"Ranger-led program\",\"Ranger Talks\",\"talk\",\"family activity\",\"families\",\"Program\",\"interpretation\",\"interpretation program\",\"interpretive\",\"Zion Canyon\",\"Zion National Park\"],\"latitude\":\"37.20041122732317\",\"longitude\":\"-112.98730909824371\",\"geometryPoiId\":\"\",\"amenities\":[],\"location\":\"Zion Canyon Visitor Center\",\"seasonDescription\":\"Ranger-led programs are available throughout the year. More programs are offered in the summer than the winter months, so check the scheudle at park visitor centers or on our <a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/zion\\/planyourvisit\\/calendar.htm\\\">events calendar<\\/a>.\",\"accessibilityInformation\":\"Assistive listening devices are available at the Zion Canyon Visitor Center, as well as supplemental information for park programs.\\u00a0<br \\/> <br \\/> All ranger-led talks and evening programs currently take place outdoors. Talks at the Visitor Center take place under a shade structure with sandstone benches for seating. Space is available for mobility devices and wheelchairs.\\u00a0<br \\/> <br \\/> The Ride with A Ranger program takes place on Zion Canyon shuttle buses, which are wheelchair accessible.\\u00a0<br \\/> <br \\/> Ranger walks on the Pa\'rus Trail are wheelchair accessible, but hikes on the Watchman Trail are not.<br \\/> <br \\/> \\u00a0\",\"isReservationRequired\":\"false\",\"ageDescription\":\"Children must be accompanied by an adult.\\u00a0\",\"petsDescription\":\"Pets may only join their owners to ranger-led talks, evening programs, and walks on the Pa\'rus Trail. Pets are not allowed on the Ride with a Ranger program or on the Watchman Trail.\\u00a0\",\"timeOfDayDescription\":\"\",\"feeDescription\":\"Park entrance fees apply.\",\"age\":\"\",\"arePetsPermittedWithRestrictions\":\"true\",\"activities\":[{\"id\":\"B33DC9B6-0B7D-4322-BAD7-A13A34C584A3\",\"name\":\"Guided Tours\"}],\"activityDescription\":\"\",\"locationDescription\":\"This is shuttle stop #1.\\u00a0\",\"doFeesApply\":\"false\",\"longDescription\":\"Join a park ranger to learn about what makes Zion National Park such a special place! We have a variety of ranger-led activities throughout the year, and you can check the schedule on our <a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/zion\\/planyourvisit\\/calendar.htm\\\">Events Calendar<\\/a>. Topics range from geology and\\u00a0wildlife to art and\\u00a0human history, and everything inbetween. All ranger-led programs are free.\",\"reservationDescription\":\"Due to limited space, some programs may require a free reservation be made in advance at the Zion Canyon Visitor Center.\\u00a0\",\"season\":[\"Winter\",\"Spring\",\"Summer\",\"Fall\"],\"topics\":[{\"id\":\"A1BAF33E-EA84-4608-A888-4CEE9541F027\",\"name\":\"Native American Heritage\"},{\"id\":\"F8C2FE93-DEB3-4B12-9A87-913E3E6B448D\",\"name\":\"Natural Sounds\"},{\"id\":\"A7359FC4-DAD8-45F5-AF15-7FF62F816ED3\",\"name\":\"Night Sky\"},{\"id\":\"A155238F-0DD2-4610-9B87-05FCE1C59283\",\"name\":\"River and Riparian\"},{\"id\":\"4244F489-6813-4B7A-9D0C-20CE098C8FFF\",\"name\":\"Rock Landscapes and Features\"},{\"id\":\"3CDB67A9-1EAC-408D-88EC-F26FA35E90AF\",\"name\":\"Schools and Education\"},{\"id\":\"4C0EBDE7-EB73-4252-84D9-0650974EC3E5\",\"name\":\"Conservation Movement\"},{\"id\":\"78E4F4AC-AF97-435A-8C2C-7FB8D67516ED\",\"name\":\"Unique Species\"},{\"id\":\"BA12B386-49EA-46B0-9121-FCACACC47538\",\"name\":\"Watersheds\"},{\"id\":\"C9C749E3-39C3-45F7-BCC5-9A609E30AA05\",\"name\":\"Westward Expansion\"},{\"id\":\"B85866E2-0897-4000-9040-605CA335804F\",\"name\":\"Wilderness\"},{\"id\":\"7DA81DAB-5045-4953-9C20-36590AD9FA95\",\"name\":\"Women\'s History\"}],\"durationDescription\":\"Time will vary depending on which ranger-led activity you join.\",\"arePetsPermitted\":\"true\",\"timeOfDay\":[\"Day\",\"Dawn\",\"Dusk\"],\"duration\":\"20-120 Minutes\",\"credit\":\"\"}',1,'2023-09-10 21:06:07','2023-09-10 21:06:36'),(370,'Upper Emerald Pools Trail','Emerald Pools Trailhead','nps','FD003EF3-8DA2-4FCB-9B49-7BFFB097CB76',7,'Upper Emerald Pools Trail can be accessed from the Emerald Pools Trailhead at Zion Lodge (shuttle stop #5) or from The Grotto (shuttle stop #6).   Zion Lodge Follow the sidewalk past the lodge lawn to the crosswalk. Cross the road then continue over the bridge to begin. From that point, hikers can either take the easier Lower Emerald Pool Trail on the right, or take the moderate Middle Emerald Pools Trail on the left.  The Grotto To get to Upper Emerald Pools Trail from The Grotto, hike the moderate 2 mi (3.2 km) Kayenta Trail until it merges with the Emerald Pools Trails. \\r\\n\\r\\n','Upper Emerald Pools Trail is a moderate, 1 mile (1.6 km) trail that can be accessed via the Kayenta Trail, Lower Emerald Pools Trail, or Middle Emerald Pools Trail. Climb up stone steps to a sandy path that leads through pinyon-juniper woodlands to the base of towering canyon walls which boast majestic, seasonal waterfalls. Enjoy views of canyon landmarks such as Lady Mountain and The Great White Throne along the way. The trail ends at the sandy shore of Upper Emerald Pool. The pool is fragile and an essential water source for wildlife. Do not go into the water. Connects to the other Emerald Pools trails and the Kayenta Trail.  Restrooms and water filling stations are available at Zion Lodge and The Grotto.',NULL,'Wide, but unpaved, sandy trail with 200 ft (61 m) of elevation gain. Multiple sections of sandstone steps with some drop-offs. ','1-2 Hours','1',37.2510389,-112.9586687,NULL,'https://www.nps.gov/thingstodo/hike-upper-emerald-pools.htm',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,1,NULL,'{\"id\":\"FD003EF3-8DA2-4FCB-9B49-7BFFB097CB76\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/thingstodo\\/hike-upper-emerald-pools.htm\",\"title\":\"Upper Emerald Pools Trail\",\"shortDescription\":\"Upper Emerald Pools Trail is a moderate, 1 mile (1.6 km) trail that can be accessed via the Kayenta Trail, Lower Emerald Pools Trail, or Middle Emerald Pools Trail. Climb up stone steps to a sandy path that leads through pinyon-juniper woodlands to the\\u00a0base of towering canyon walls which boast majestic, seasonal waterfalls. The trail ends at the sandy shore of Upper Emerald Pool.\\u00a0No swimming.\",\"images\":[{\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/6720A4AD-AF48-3DDC-30F6E59461D8CEAF.jpg\",\"credit\":\"NPS\\/Jonathan Shafer\",\"altText\":\"A mound of ice and snow surrounded by red sandstone rocks.\",\"title\":\"Winter ice in Upper Emerald Pool\",\"description\":\"\",\"caption\":\"Winter conditions at the Upper Emerald Pool\",\"crops\":[{\"aspectRatio\":\"1.78\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/primary\\/6720A4AD-AF48-3DDC-30F6E59461D8CEAF.jpg\"},{\"aspectRatio\":\"1\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/secondary\\/6720A4AD-AF48-3DDC-30F6E59461D8CEAF.jpg\"}]}],\"relatedParks\":[{\"states\":\"UT\",\"parkCode\":\"zion\",\"designation\":\"National Park\",\"fullName\":\"Zion National Park\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/zion\\/index.htm\",\"name\":\"Zion\"}],\"relatedOrganizations\":[],\"tags\":[\"upper emerald pool\",\"emerald pools\",\"Zion Canyon\",\"Zion\",\"loop trail\",\"out and back\",\"moderate hike\"],\"latitude\":\"37.2510388557\",\"longitude\":\"-112.95866872\",\"geometryPoiId\":\"b744d53b-74a6-4f82-bdb7-ec7f4a2c958e\",\"amenities\":[],\"location\":\"Emerald Pools Trailhead\",\"seasonDescription\":\"\",\"accessibilityInformation\":\"Wide, but unpaved, sandy trail with 200 ft (61 m) of elevation gain. Multiple sections of sandstone steps with some drop-offs.\\u00a0\",\"isReservationRequired\":\"false\",\"ageDescription\":\"\",\"petsDescription\":\"\",\"timeOfDayDescription\":\"\",\"feeDescription\":\"Park entrance fees apply.\",\"age\":\"\",\"arePetsPermittedWithRestrictions\":\"false\",\"activities\":[{\"id\":\"BFF8C027-7C8F-480B-A5F8-CD8CE490BFBA\",\"name\":\"Hiking\"}],\"activityDescription\":\"\",\"locationDescription\":\"Upper Emerald Pools Trail can be accessed from the Emerald Pools Trailhead at Zion Lodge (shuttle stop #5) or from The Grotto (shuttle stop #6).\\u00a0<br \\/> <br \\/> Zion Lodge<br \\/> Follow the sidewalk past the lodge lawn to the crosswalk. Cross the road then continue over the bridge to begin. From that point, hikers can either take the easier Lower Emerald Pool Trail on the right, or take the moderate Middle Emerald Pools Trail on the left.<br \\/> <br \\/> The Grotto<br \\/> To get to Upper Emerald Pools Trail from The Grotto, hike the moderate 2 mi (3.2 km) Kayenta Trail until it merges with the Emerald Pools Trails.\\u00a0\",\"doFeesApply\":\"false\",\"longDescription\":\"Upper Emerald Pools Trail is a moderate, 1 mile (1.6 km) trail that can be accessed via the Kayenta Trail, Lower Emerald Pools Trail, or Middle Emerald Pools Trail. Climb up stone steps to a sandy path that leads through pinyon-juniper woodlands to the\\u00a0base of towering canyon walls which boast majestic, seasonal waterfalls.\\u00a0Enjoy views of canyon landmarks such as Lady Mountain and The Great White Throne along the way. The trail ends at the sandy shore of Upper Emerald Pool.\\u00a0The pool is fragile and an essential water source for wildlife. Do not go into the water. <p paraeid=\\\"{92e3109f-98a1-4d6a-b251-2442efca65a4}{240}\\\" paraid=\\\"1866055056\\\">Connects to the other Emerald Pools trails and the Kayenta Trail.<br \\/> <br \\/> Restrooms and water filling stations are available at Zion Lodge and The Grotto.<\\/p>\",\"reservationDescription\":\"\",\"season\":[\"Winter\",\"Spring\",\"Summer\",\"Fall\"],\"topics\":[{\"id\":\"5BE55D7F-BDB6-4E3D-AC35-2D8EBB974417\",\"name\":\"Trails\"}],\"durationDescription\":\"\",\"arePetsPermitted\":\"false\",\"timeOfDay\":[\"Day\",\"Night\",\"Dawn\",\"Dusk\"],\"duration\":\"1-2 Hours\",\"credit\":\"\"}',1,'2023-09-10 21:06:07','2023-09-10 21:06:36'),(371,'Sand Bench','Emerald Pools Trailhead','nps','7D4532B5-C565-4849-A987-F40D6DDD2B91',7,'The Sand Bench Trail is primarily accessible from the Emerald Pools Trailhead at the Zion Lodge (shuttle stop #5). Follow the sidewalk past the lodge lawn to the crosswalk. Cross the road then continue over the bridge. From that point, take a left turn to begin the Sand Bench Trail. This is also where you can access Middle Emerald Pools Trail.  Sand Bench is also accessible from the Court of the Patriarchs (shuttle stop #4). From the up-canyon shuttle stop, cross the road to the wooden steps leading down to a sandy trail. At that point, hikers have the option to head up-canyon towards the Zion Lodge by taking a right turn, or continue back towards the Court of the Patriarchs to ascend the longer loop portion of the trail. Hiking only the loop portion of the trail makes the roundtrip closer to 5 miles (8 km).\\r\\n\\r\\n','The Sand Bench Trail is a 7.6 mi (12.2 km) roundtrip hike atop the remains of an ancient rockfall. This trail leads hikers along the Virgin River and then ascends a landslide that dammed the Virgin River 8,000 years ago and created a lake that extended to Angels Landing. This trail is a commercial horse trail from March to October, and it receives little shade in the hot, summer sun. Winter travel is possible, but watch for patches of ice.  Water and restrooms are available at The Zion Lodge. ',NULL,'Sandy trail with wooden and sandstone stairs, and some sections of high drop-offs. Overall elevation gain is 466 ft (142 m).','1-4 Hours','1',37.2510389,-112.9586687,NULL,'https://www.nps.gov/thingstodo/hike-sand-bench.htm',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,1,NULL,'{\"id\":\"7D4532B5-C565-4849-A987-F40D6DDD2B91\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/thingstodo\\/hike-sand-bench.htm\",\"title\":\"Sand Bench\",\"shortDescription\":\"The Sand Bench Trail is a 7.6 mi (12.2 km) roundtrip hike atop the remains of an ancient rockfall. This trail leads hikers along the Virgin River and then ascends a landslide that dammed the Virgin River 8,000 years ago and created a lake that extended to Angels Landing. This trail is a commercial horse trail from March to October, and it receives little shade in the hot, summer sun. Winter travel is possible, but watch for patches of ice.\",\"images\":[{\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/59D455C0-F4B1-2FE8-975E750A09DFD5AD.jpg\",\"credit\":\"NPS\\/ Darcy McKinley Lester\",\"altText\":\"A cloudy blue sky over red sandstone mountains, with green foliage dotting their foothills.\",\"title\":\"Sand Bench Trail\",\"description\":\"\",\"caption\":\"The Sand Bench Trail has excellent view of many Zion landmarks.\",\"crops\":[{\"aspectRatio\":\"1.78\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/primary\\/59D455C0-F4B1-2FE8-975E750A09DFD5AD.jpg\"},{\"aspectRatio\":\"1\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/secondary\\/59D455C0-F4B1-2FE8-975E750A09DFD5AD.jpg\"}]}],\"relatedParks\":[{\"states\":\"UT\",\"parkCode\":\"zion\",\"designation\":\"National Park\",\"fullName\":\"Zion National Park\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/zion\\/index.htm\",\"name\":\"Zion\"}],\"relatedOrganizations\":[],\"tags\":[\"hike\",\"day hike\",\"loop hike\",\"out and back\",\"Horse Trail\",\"sand bench trail\",\"Zion Canyon\"],\"latitude\":\"37.2510388557\",\"longitude\":\"-112.95866872\",\"geometryPoiId\":\"b744d53b-74a6-4f82-bdb7-ec7f4a2c958e\",\"amenities\":[],\"location\":\"Emerald Pools Trailhead\",\"seasonDescription\":\"Seasonal commercial horse tours are available from March to October. These tours are offered through a <a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/www.canyonrides.com\\/horseback-riding-zion-national-park\\/\\\">concessioner<\\/a>.\",\"accessibilityInformation\":\"Sandy trail with wooden and sandstone stairs, and some sections of high drop-offs. Overall elevation gain is\\u00a0466 ft (142 m).\",\"isReservationRequired\":\"false\",\"ageDescription\":\"\",\"petsDescription\":\"\",\"timeOfDayDescription\":\"\",\"feeDescription\":\"Park entrance fees apply.\",\"age\":\"\",\"arePetsPermittedWithRestrictions\":\"false\",\"activities\":[{\"id\":\"BFF8C027-7C8F-480B-A5F8-CD8CE490BFBA\",\"name\":\"Hiking\"}],\"activityDescription\":\"\",\"locationDescription\":\"The Sand Bench Trail is primarily accessible from the Emerald Pools Trailhead at the Zion Lodge (shuttle stop #5).\\u00a0Follow the sidewalk past the lodge lawn to the crosswalk. Cross the road then continue over the bridge. From that point, take a left turn to begin the Sand Bench Trail. This is also where you can access Middle Emerald Pools Trail.<br \\/> <br \\/> Sand Bench is also accessible from the Court of the Patriarchs (shuttle stop #4). From the up-canyon shuttle stop, cross the road to the wooden steps leading down to a sandy trail. At that point, hikers have the option to head up-canyon towards the Zion Lodge by taking a right turn, or continue back towards the Court of the Patriarchs to ascend the longer loop portion of the trail. Hiking only the loop portion of the trail makes the roundtrip closer to 5 miles (8 km).\",\"doFeesApply\":\"false\",\"longDescription\":\"The Sand Bench Trail is a 7.6 mi (12.2 km) roundtrip hike atop the remains of an ancient rockfall. This trail leads hikers along the Virgin River and then ascends a landslide that dammed the Virgin River 8,000 years ago and created a lake that extended to Angels Landing. This trail is a commercial horse trail from March to October, and it receives little shade in the hot, summer sun. Winter travel is possible, but watch for patches of ice.<br \\/> <br \\/> Water and restrooms are available at <a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/places\\/zion-lodge.htm\\\">The Zion Lodge<\\/a>.\\u00a0\",\"reservationDescription\":\"\",\"season\":[\"Winter\",\"Spring\",\"Summer\",\"Fall\"],\"topics\":[{\"id\":\"5BE55D7F-BDB6-4E3D-AC35-2D8EBB974417\",\"name\":\"Trails\"}],\"durationDescription\":\"\",\"arePetsPermitted\":\"false\",\"timeOfDay\":[\"Day\",\"Night\",\"Dawn\",\"Dusk\"],\"duration\":\"1-4 Hours\",\"credit\":\"\"}',1,'2023-09-10 21:06:07','2023-09-10 21:06:36'),(372,'Lower Emerald Pool Trail','Emerald Pools Trailhead','nps','6A7BB554-73E5-43ED-90C1-A327CADF55C7',7,'Emerald Pools Trailhead is across from the Zion Lodge (shuttle stop #5). From the shuttle stop, follow the sidewalk across the Lodge Lawn to the crosswalk. Cross the street and continue across the bridge. Follow the trail on the right to begin hiking Lower Emerald Pool Trail.\\r\\n\\r\\n','Lower Emerald Pool trail is a rough, paved trail that leads through pinyon-juniper woodland to the Lower Emerald Pool and ephemeral waterfalls.The pools are fragile and an essential water source for wildlife. Do not go into the water.  Connects to the other Emerald Pools trails and the Kayenta Trail.  Avoid cliff edges. Swimming is not permitted. Restrooms and water filling stations are available at the Zion Lodge.',NULL,'Narrow, roughly paved trail with several steps. Minor drop-offs. A metal handrail is located at the end.','1-2 Hours','1',37.2510389,-112.9586687,NULL,'https://www.nps.gov/thingstodo/hike-lower-emerald-pool-trail.htm',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,1,NULL,'{\"id\":\"6A7BB554-73E5-43ED-90C1-A327CADF55C7\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/thingstodo\\/hike-lower-emerald-pool-trail.htm\",\"title\":\"Lower Emerald Pool Trail\",\"shortDescription\":\"Lower Emerald Pool trail is a rough, paved trail that leads through pinyon-juniper woodland to the Lower Emerald Pool and ephemeral waterfalls.The pools are fragile and an essential water source for wildlife. Do not go into the water. Connects to the other Emerald Pools trails and the Kayenta Trail. Avoid cliff edges. Swimming is not permitted. Restrooms and water filling stations are available at the Zion Lodge.\",\"images\":[{\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/BDB9C3B7-0F11-4312-A8CC58A24D800D39.jpg\",\"credit\":\"NPS\",\"altText\":\"A waterfall flows off of a sandstone ledge into a pool below, surrounded by foliage.\",\"title\":\"Lower Emerald Pool Trail\",\"description\":\"\",\"caption\":\"View from the end of the Lower Emerald Pool Trail.\",\"crops\":[{\"aspectRatio\":\"1.78\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/primary\\/BDB9C3B7-0F11-4312-A8CC58A24D800D39.jpg\"},{\"aspectRatio\":\"1\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/secondary\\/BDB9C3B7-0F11-4312-A8CC58A24D800D39.jpg\"}]}],\"relatedParks\":[{\"states\":\"UT\",\"parkCode\":\"zion\",\"designation\":\"National Park\",\"fullName\":\"Zion National Park\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/zion\\/index.htm\",\"name\":\"Zion\"}],\"relatedOrganizations\":[],\"tags\":[\"emerald pools\",\"lower emerald pool\",\"Easy hike\",\"day hike\",\"out and back\",\"Loop\",\"Zion\",\"Zion Canyon\",\"zion lodge\"],\"latitude\":\"37.2510388557\",\"longitude\":\"-112.95866872\",\"geometryPoiId\":\"b744d53b-74a6-4f82-bdb7-ec7f4a2c958e\",\"amenities\":[],\"location\":\"Emerald Pools Trailhead\",\"seasonDescription\":\"Trail may be icy in winter.\",\"accessibilityInformation\":\"Narrow, roughly paved trail with several steps. Minor drop-offs. A metal handrail is located at the end.\",\"isReservationRequired\":\"false\",\"ageDescription\":\"\",\"petsDescription\":\"\",\"timeOfDayDescription\":\"\",\"feeDescription\":\"Park entrance fees apply.\",\"age\":\"\",\"arePetsPermittedWithRestrictions\":\"false\",\"activities\":[{\"id\":\"BFF8C027-7C8F-480B-A5F8-CD8CE490BFBA\",\"name\":\"Hiking\"}],\"activityDescription\":\"\",\"locationDescription\":\"Emerald Pools Trailhead is across from the Zion Lodge (shuttle stop #5). From the shuttle stop, follow the sidewalk across the Lodge Lawn to the crosswalk. Cross the street and continue across the bridge. Follow the trail on the right to begin hiking Lower Emerald Pool Trail.\",\"doFeesApply\":\"false\",\"longDescription\":\"Lower Emerald Pool trail is a rough, paved trail that leads through pinyon-juniper woodland to the Lower Emerald Pool and ephemeral waterfalls.The pools are fragile and an essential water source for wildlife. Do not go into the water.<br \\/> <br \\/> Connects to the other Emerald Pools trails and the Kayenta Trail.<br \\/> <br \\/> Avoid cliff edges. Swimming is not permitted. Restrooms and water filling stations are available at the Zion Lodge.\",\"reservationDescription\":\"\",\"season\":[\"Winter\",\"Spring\",\"Summer\",\"Fall\"],\"topics\":[{\"id\":\"5BE55D7F-BDB6-4E3D-AC35-2D8EBB974417\",\"name\":\"Trails\"}],\"durationDescription\":\"\",\"arePetsPermitted\":\"false\",\"timeOfDay\":[\"Day\",\"Night\",\"Dawn\",\"Dusk\"],\"duration\":\"1-2 Hours\",\"credit\":\"\"}',1,'2023-09-10 21:06:07','2023-09-10 21:06:36'),(373,'Middle Emerald Pools Trail','Emerald Pools Trailhead','nps','97C6C2F7-E45F-4192-9475-F2F90BF3E70E',7,'Emerald Pools Trailhead is across from the Zion Lodge (shuttle stop #5). From the shuttle stop, follow the sidewalk across the Lodge Lawn to the crosswalk. Cross the street and continue across the bridge. Follow the trail on the left to begin hiking Middle Emerald Pools.\\r\\n\\r\\n','Middle Emerald Pools Trail is a moderate, 2.2 mi (3.5 km) hike along an unpaved sandstone ledge above the Lower Emerald Pool Trail. The initial ascent is quite steep, bringing you to expansive views of main Zion Canyon below. Further along the trail, tucked into the red sandstone of the mountain, enjoy the shade and flora of pinyon-juniper woodlands. The trail then descends to emerald-colored pools of water above the small ephemeral waterfalls of Lower Emerald Pool Trail. The pools are fragile and an essential water source for wildlife. Do not go into the water.  Connects to the other Emerald Pools trails and the Kayenta Trail.  Avoid cliff edges. Swimming is not permitted. Restrooms and water filling stations are available at the Zion Lodge.',NULL,'Trail is not paved. The rocky, uneven terrain climbs 150 ft (46 m) of elevation with moderate drop-offs and cliff edges. Wooden stairs at the end provide access to the pools. The beginning of the trail has a steep grade.','1-2 Hours','1',37.2510389,-112.9586687,NULL,'https://www.nps.gov/thingstodo/hike-middle-emerald-pools-trail.htm',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,1,NULL,'{\"id\":\"97C6C2F7-E45F-4192-9475-F2F90BF3E70E\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/thingstodo\\/hike-middle-emerald-pools-trail.htm\",\"title\":\"Middle Emerald Pools Trail\",\"shortDescription\":\"Middle Emerald Pools Trail is a moderate hike along an unpaved sandstone ledge above the Lower Emerald Pool Trail. The initial ascent is quite steep, bringing you to expansive views of main Zion Canyon below. Further along the trail, tucked into the red sandstone of the mountain, enjoy the shade and flora of pinyon-juniper woodlands. The trail then descends to emerald-colored pools of water above the small ephemeral waterfalls of Lower Emerald Pool Trail.\",\"images\":[{\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/BAFA9BD5-AB3B-F0A3-6AAD61B9A644A40A.jpg\",\"credit\":\"NPS\\/Abi Farish\",\"altText\":\"A pool of green water on a sandstone cliff surrounded by green foliage with a mountain in the back.\",\"title\":\"Middle Emerald Pools Trail\",\"description\":\"\",\"caption\":\"View from the end of Middle Emerald Pools Trail.\",\"crops\":[{\"aspectRatio\":\"1.78\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/primary\\/BAFA9BD5-AB3B-F0A3-6AAD61B9A644A40A.jpg\"},{\"aspectRatio\":\"1\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/secondary\\/BAFA9BD5-AB3B-F0A3-6AAD61B9A644A40A.jpg\"}]}],\"relatedParks\":[{\"states\":\"UT\",\"parkCode\":\"zion\",\"designation\":\"National Park\",\"fullName\":\"Zion National Park\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/zion\\/index.htm\",\"name\":\"Zion\"}],\"relatedOrganizations\":[],\"tags\":[\"middle emerald pools\",\"emerald pools\",\"Zion\",\"Zion Canyon\",\"Moderate Hikes\",\"water\",\"loop trail\",\"out and back\"],\"latitude\":\"37.2510388557\",\"longitude\":\"-112.95866872\",\"geometryPoiId\":\"b744d53b-74a6-4f82-bdb7-ec7f4a2c958e\",\"amenities\":[],\"location\":\"Emerald Pools Trailhead\",\"seasonDescription\":\"\",\"accessibilityInformation\":\"Trail is not paved. The rocky, uneven terrain climbs 150 ft (46 m) of elevation with moderate drop-offs and cliff edges. Wooden stairs at the end provide access to the pools. The beginning of the trail has a steep grade.\",\"isReservationRequired\":\"false\",\"ageDescription\":\"\",\"petsDescription\":\"\",\"timeOfDayDescription\":\"\",\"feeDescription\":\"Park entrance fees apply.\",\"age\":\"\",\"arePetsPermittedWithRestrictions\":\"false\",\"activities\":[{\"id\":\"BFF8C027-7C8F-480B-A5F8-CD8CE490BFBA\",\"name\":\"Hiking\"}],\"activityDescription\":\"\",\"locationDescription\":\"Emerald Pools Trailhead is across from the Zion Lodge (shuttle stop #5). From the shuttle stop, follow the sidewalk across the Lodge Lawn to the crosswalk. Cross the street and continue across the bridge. Follow the trail on the left to begin hiking Middle Emerald Pools.\",\"doFeesApply\":\"false\",\"longDescription\":\"Middle Emerald Pools Trail is a moderate, 2.2 mi (3.5 km) hike along an unpaved sandstone ledge above the Lower Emerald Pool Trail. The initial ascent is quite steep, bringing you to expansive views of main Zion Canyon below. Further along the trail, tucked into the red sandstone of the mountain, enjoy the shade and flora of pinyon-juniper woodlands. The trail then descends to emerald-colored pools of water above the small ephemeral waterfalls of Lower Emerald Pool Trail.\\u00a0The pools are fragile and an essential water source for wildlife. Do not go into the water.<br \\/> <br \\/> Connects to the other Emerald Pools trails and the Kayenta Trail.<br \\/> <br \\/> Avoid cliff edges. Swimming is not permitted. Restrooms and water filling stations are available at the Zion Lodge.\",\"reservationDescription\":\"\",\"season\":[\"Winter\",\"Spring\",\"Summer\",\"Fall\"],\"topics\":[{\"id\":\"5BE55D7F-BDB6-4E3D-AC35-2D8EBB974417\",\"name\":\"Trails\"}],\"durationDescription\":\"Middle Emerald Pools is 2.2 miles (3.5 km) roundtrip (out-and-back). It may be combined with other Emerald Pools trails and the Kayenta trail for a longer hike.\",\"arePetsPermitted\":\"false\",\"timeOfDay\":[\"Day\",\"Night\",\"Dawn\",\"Dusk\"],\"duration\":\"1-2 Hours\",\"credit\":\"\"}',1,'2023-09-10 21:06:07','2023-09-10 21:06:36'),(374,'Angels Landing','Kayenta & West Rim Trailhead','nps','43CC4A81-2227-4E40-9403-6F7E2B27BC53',7,'The hike to Angels Landing is accessed from The Grotto (shuttle stop #6). From the shuttle stop, cross the road and bridge to the sandy West Rim Trail. Hike along the West Rim Trail to Scout Lookout, where the Angels Landing Trail begins. \\r\\n\\r\\n','On and after April 1, 2022 hikers continuing to Angels Landing from Scout Lookout are required to have a permit, obtained on recreation.gov.  The strenuous, challenging trail follows a steep, narrow ridge with uneven steps cut into the rock and chains anchored intermittently along the route. You must navigate a trail only a few feet wide in some areas, with exposed edges and sheer drop-offs of as much as 1,000 feet on each side. Please watch your footing and exercise extreme caution at all times. Be ready to yield to other hikers, as the trail is only wide enough for one person in many places. At the end of the hike, the trail widens slightly and there are areas to rest, snack, and soak in the spectacular views of Zion Canyon.  Restrooms are available at Scout Lookout and The Grotto. Water filling stations are also available at The Grotto. ',NULL,'Trail is unpaved, rocky, and less than 3 ft (1 m) wide in many sections. Chains act as handrails for multiple sections, with long drop-offs. Hiking traffic moves in both directions in limited space.','1-2 Hours','1',37.2592463,-112.9513419,NULL,'https://www.nps.gov/thingstodo/hike-angels-landing.htm',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,1,NULL,'{\"id\":\"43CC4A81-2227-4E40-9403-6F7E2B27BC53\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/thingstodo\\/hike-angels-landing.htm\",\"title\":\"Angels Landing\",\"shortDescription\":\"On and after April 1, 2022 hikers continuing to Angels Landing from Scout Lookout are required to have a permit, obtained on recreation.gov.\",\"images\":[{\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/23EF45DA-96CE-AEDD-E6FA82FF43279637.jpg\",\"credit\":\"NPS\",\"altText\":\"A hiker in a blue shirt holds onto chains as they traverse a sandstone cliff.\",\"title\":\"Hiker on Angels Landing\",\"description\":\"\",\"caption\":\"Hiker holds onto the chains of the Angels Landing Trail.\",\"crops\":[{\"aspectRatio\":\"1.78\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/primary\\/23EF45DA-96CE-AEDD-E6FA82FF43279637.jpg\"},{\"aspectRatio\":\"1\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/secondary\\/23EF45DA-96CE-AEDD-E6FA82FF43279637.jpg\"}]}],\"relatedParks\":[{\"states\":\"UT\",\"parkCode\":\"zion\",\"designation\":\"National Park\",\"fullName\":\"Zion National Park\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/zion\\/index.htm\",\"name\":\"Zion\"}],\"relatedOrganizations\":[],\"tags\":[\"Angels Landing\",\"Zion Canyon\",\"strenuous hike\",\"out and back\",\"west rim\",\"scout lookout\"],\"latitude\":\"37.2592462591\",\"longitude\":\"-112.951341871\",\"geometryPoiId\":\"8f9ea3a0-0722-4cae-9edf-3e562e0766dc\",\"amenities\":[],\"location\":\"Kayenta & West Rim Trailhead\",\"seasonDescription\":\"\",\"accessibilityInformation\":\"Trail is unpaved, rocky, and less than 3 ft (1 m) wide in many sections. Chains act as handrails for multiple sections, with long drop-offs. Hiking traffic moves in both directions in limited space.\",\"isReservationRequired\":\"true\",\"ageDescription\":\"\",\"petsDescription\":\"\",\"timeOfDayDescription\":\"\",\"feeDescription\":\"To enter the lottery for a permit, recreation.gov charges $6. If a permit is awarded, a fee of $3 per person will be charged to the same card used to apply.\\u00a0\",\"age\":\"\",\"arePetsPermittedWithRestrictions\":\"false\",\"activities\":[{\"id\":\"BFF8C027-7C8F-480B-A5F8-CD8CE490BFBA\",\"name\":\"Hiking\"}],\"activityDescription\":\"\",\"locationDescription\":\"The hike to Angels Landing is accessed from The Grotto (shuttle stop #6). From the shuttle stop, cross the road and bridge to the sandy West Rim Trail. Hike along the West Rim Trail to Scout Lookout, where the Angels Landing Trail begins.\\u00a0\",\"doFeesApply\":\"true\",\"longDescription\":\"On and after April 1, 2022 hikers continuing to Angels Landing from Scout Lookout are <a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/zion\\/planyourvisit\\/angels-landing-hiking-permits.htm\\\">required to have a permit<\\/a>, obtained on recreation.gov.<br \\/> <br \\/> The strenuous, challenging trail follows a steep, narrow ridge with uneven steps cut into the rock and chains anchored intermittently along the route. You must navigate a trail only a few feet wide in some areas, with exposed edges and sheer drop-offs of as much as 1,000 feet on each side. Please watch your footing and exercise extreme caution at all times. Be ready to yield to other hikers, as the trail is only wide enough for one person in many places. At the end of the hike, the trail widens slightly and there are areas to rest, snack, and soak in the spectacular views of Zion Canyon.<br \\/> <br \\/> Restrooms are available at Scout Lookout and The Grotto. Water filling stations are also available at The Grotto.\\u00a0\",\"reservationDescription\":\"<a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/zion\\/planyourvisit\\/angels-landing-hiking-permits.htm\\\">Permits are required to hike Angels Landing.\\u00a0<\\/a>\\u00a0<br \\/> <br \\/> In response to concerns about crowding and congestion on the trail, on and after April 1, 2022, everyone who hikes Angels Landing needs to have a permit. The pilot permit program reflects lessons learned when we metered the number of hikers on the trail in 2019 and 2021 and by distributing tickets to use the park shuttle system in response to COVID-19 in 2020<b>.<\\/b>\",\"season\":[\"Winter\",\"Spring\",\"Summer\",\"Fall\"],\"topics\":[{\"id\":\"5BE55D7F-BDB6-4E3D-AC35-2D8EBB974417\",\"name\":\"Trails\"}],\"durationDescription\":\"To reach the beginning of the Angels Landing Trail, hikers must ascend the West Rim to Scout Lookout. This duration only accounts for the 0.5 mi (0.3 km) Angels Landing Trail.\",\"arePetsPermitted\":\"false\",\"timeOfDay\":[\"Day\",\"Dawn\",\"Dusk\"],\"duration\":\"1-2 Hours\",\"credit\":\"\"}',1,'2023-09-10 21:06:07','2023-09-10 21:06:36'),(375,'West Rim to Scout Lookout','Kayenta & West Rim Trailhead','nps','BD4CA407-5BE0-426E-8339-F34DE6795D3A',7,'Access the trailhead at The Grotto (shuttle stop #6). Cross the road and over the bridge to begin the West Rim Trail.\\r\\n\\r\\n','The strenuous West Rim Trail ascends 1,488 ft (454 m) to Scout Lookout, high above the canyon floor. Hikers cross the Virgin River and then ascend a series of steep switchbacks along the canyon wall. The trail flattens out as it enters Refrigerator Canyon, offering relief from the heat and elevation gain of the previous segment, but resumes the climb shortly afterward at Walter’s Wiggles: a series of 21 switchbacks that leads hikers to Scout Lookout, a relatively flat, sandy area with some shade.  At Scout Lookout, there is a junction with the West Rim Trail and the route to Angels Landing. Many hikers choose to stop here to enjoy the views of Zion Canyon, or to look for the endangered California condors that call the cliffs home. Those looking to continue hiking to the end of Angels Landing will need a permit from recreation.gov beginning April 1, 2022.   Water and restrooms are available at The Grotto, and restrooms are available at Scout Lookout.',NULL,'Trail is paved, steep, and narrow in sections. Climbs 1488 ft (454 m) in elevation, without hand railings or fences. Refrigerator Canyon can be dark with low-visibility, especially in the early morning and late evening.','3-4 Hours','1',37.2592463,-112.9513419,NULL,'https://www.nps.gov/thingstodo/hike-to-scout-lookout.htm',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,1,NULL,'{\"id\":\"BD4CA407-5BE0-426E-8339-F34DE6795D3A\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/thingstodo\\/hike-to-scout-lookout.htm\",\"title\":\"West Rim to Scout Lookout\",\"shortDescription\":\"The strenuous West Rim Trail ascends 1,488 ft (454 m) to Scout Lookout, high above the canyon floor. Hikers cross the Virgin River and then ascend a series of steep switchbacks along the canyon wall. The trail flattens out as it enters Refrigerator Canyon, offering relief from the heat and elevation gain of the previous segment, but resumes the climb shortly afterward at Walter\\u2019s Wiggles: a series of 21 switchbacks that leads hikers to Scout Lookout.\",\"images\":[{\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/230B230B-08DA-0021-AE366EBBC3DAE7FA.jpg\",\"credit\":\"NPS\",\"altText\":\"A hiker with an orange backpack standing on sandstone looks at the Angels Landing peak.\",\"title\":\"Scout Lookout\",\"description\":\"\",\"caption\":\"Hiker on Scout Lookout looking towards Angels Landing and the Great White Throne.\",\"crops\":[{\"aspectRatio\":\"1.78\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/primary\\/230B230B-08DA-0021-AE366EBBC3DAE7FA.jpg\"},{\"aspectRatio\":\"1\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/secondary\\/230B230B-08DA-0021-AE366EBBC3DAE7FA.jpg\"}]}],\"relatedParks\":[{\"states\":\"UT\",\"parkCode\":\"zion\",\"designation\":\"National Park\",\"fullName\":\"Zion National Park\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/zion\\/index.htm\",\"name\":\"Zion\"}],\"relatedOrganizations\":[],\"tags\":[\"scout lookout\",\"west rim\",\"Angels Landing\",\"Zion Canyon\",\"Zion\",\"strenuous hike\",\"out and back\",\"viewpoint\"],\"latitude\":\"37.2592462591\",\"longitude\":\"-112.951341871\",\"geometryPoiId\":\"8f9ea3a0-0722-4cae-9edf-3e562e0766dc\",\"amenities\":[],\"location\":\"Kayenta & West Rim Trailhead\",\"seasonDescription\":\"\",\"accessibilityInformation\":\"Trail is paved, steep, and narrow in sections. Climbs 1488 ft (454 m) in elevation, without hand railings or fences. Refrigerator Canyon can be dark with low-visibility, especially in the early morning and late evening.\",\"isReservationRequired\":\"false\",\"ageDescription\":\"\",\"petsDescription\":\"\",\"timeOfDayDescription\":\"\",\"feeDescription\":\"Park entrance fees apply.\",\"age\":\"\",\"arePetsPermittedWithRestrictions\":\"false\",\"activities\":[{\"id\":\"BFF8C027-7C8F-480B-A5F8-CD8CE490BFBA\",\"name\":\"Hiking\"}],\"activityDescription\":\"\",\"locationDescription\":\"Access the trailhead at The Grotto (shuttle stop #6). Cross the road and over the bridge to begin the West Rim Trail.\",\"doFeesApply\":\"false\",\"longDescription\":\"The strenuous West Rim Trail ascends 1,488 ft (454 m) to Scout Lookout, high above the canyon floor. Hikers cross the Virgin River and then ascend a series of steep switchbacks along the canyon wall. The trail flattens out as it enters Refrigerator Canyon, offering relief from the heat and elevation gain of the previous segment, but resumes the climb shortly afterward at Walter\\u2019s Wiggles: a series of 21 switchbacks that leads hikers to Scout Lookout, a relatively flat, sandy area with some shade.<br \\/> <br \\/> At Scout Lookout, there is a junction with the West Rim Trail and the route to Angels Landing. Many hikers choose to stop here to enjoy the views of Zion Canyon, or to look for the endangered California condors that call the cliffs home. Those looking to continue hiking to the end of Angels Landing <a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/zion\\/planyourvisit\\/angels-landing-hiking-permits.htm\\\">will need a permit from recreation.gov<\\/a> beginning April 1, 2022.\\u00a0<br \\/> <br \\/> Water and restrooms are available at The Grotto, and restrooms are available at Scout Lookout.\",\"reservationDescription\":\"\",\"season\":[\"Winter\",\"Spring\",\"Summer\",\"Fall\"],\"topics\":[{\"id\":\"5BE55D7F-BDB6-4E3D-AC35-2D8EBB974417\",\"name\":\"Trails\"}],\"durationDescription\":\"\",\"arePetsPermitted\":\"false\",\"timeOfDay\":[\"Day\",\"Night\",\"Dawn\",\"Dusk\"],\"duration\":\"3-4 Hours\",\"credit\":\"\"}',1,'2023-09-10 21:06:07','2023-09-10 21:06:36'),(376,'Kayenta Trail','Kayenta & West Rim Trailhead','nps','9B081DDD-4C3E-47C0-901A-E92A159B0078',7,'The Kayenta Trailhead is located at The Grotto (shuttle stop #6). Use the crosswalk to reach the bridge over the Virgin River, then take a left up the sandstone steps to begin the hike.\\r\\n\\r\\n','The moderate, 2.0 mi (3.2 km) Kayenta Trail follows the Virgin River before ascending to the Lower and Upper Emerald Pool Trails. Provides panoramic views of Zion Canyon along the way. Long drop-offs are present along this trail. You can either return on the same trail, or make a loop using one of the Emerald Pools trails.   Water and restrooms are available at The Grotto.',NULL,'Trail is unpaved, and covers sandy, rocky, and uneven terrain. Sandstone steps are present at multiple points, with sections of long drop-offs. Total elevation gain is 150 ft (46 km). ','2-3 Hours','1',37.2592463,-112.9513419,NULL,'https://www.nps.gov/thingstodo/hike-kayenta-trail.htm',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,1,NULL,'{\"id\":\"9B081DDD-4C3E-47C0-901A-E92A159B0078\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/thingstodo\\/hike-kayenta-trail.htm\",\"title\":\"Kayenta Trail\",\"shortDescription\":\"The moderate, 2.0 mi (3.2 km) Kayenta Trail follows the Virgin River before ascending to the Lower and Upper Emerald Pool Trails. Provides panoramic views of Zion Canyon along the way. Long drop-offs are present along this trail. You can either return on the same trail, or make a loop using one of the Emerald Pools trails.\\u00a0 Water and restrooms are available at The Grotto.\",\"images\":[{\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/54128644-C0FE-3210-AB39C4918EED0D2A.jpg\",\"credit\":\"NPS\\/Lizz Eberhardt\",\"altText\":\"A set of red sandstone stairs lined with loose rocks and green foliage leads out of frame.\",\"title\":\"Kayenta Trail\",\"description\":\"\",\"caption\":\"Sandstone steps of the Kayenta Trail lead you up to the Emerald Pools\",\"crops\":[{\"aspectRatio\":\"1.78\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/primary\\/54128644-C0FE-3210-AB39C4918EED0D2A.jpg\"},{\"aspectRatio\":\"1\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/secondary\\/54128644-C0FE-3210-AB39C4918EED0D2A.jpg\"}]}],\"relatedParks\":[{\"states\":\"UT\",\"parkCode\":\"zion\",\"designation\":\"National Park\",\"fullName\":\"Zion National Park\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/zion\\/index.htm\",\"name\":\"Zion\"}],\"relatedOrganizations\":[],\"tags\":[\"kayenta\",\"Zion Canyon\",\"out and back\",\"loop trail\",\"moderate hike\",\"Zion National Park\"],\"latitude\":\"37.2592462591\",\"longitude\":\"-112.951341871\",\"geometryPoiId\":\"8f9ea3a0-0722-4cae-9edf-3e562e0766dc\",\"amenities\":[],\"location\":\"Kayenta & West Rim Trailhead\",\"seasonDescription\":\"\",\"accessibilityInformation\":\"Trail is unpaved, and covers sandy, rocky, and uneven terrain. Sandstone steps are present at multiple points, with sections of long drop-offs. Total elevation gain is 150 ft (46 km).\\u00a0\",\"isReservationRequired\":\"false\",\"ageDescription\":\"\",\"petsDescription\":\"\",\"timeOfDayDescription\":\"\",\"feeDescription\":\"Park entrance fees apply.\",\"age\":\"\",\"arePetsPermittedWithRestrictions\":\"false\",\"activities\":[{\"id\":\"BFF8C027-7C8F-480B-A5F8-CD8CE490BFBA\",\"name\":\"Hiking\"}],\"activityDescription\":\"\",\"locationDescription\":\"The Kayenta Trailhead is located at The Grotto (shuttle stop #6). Use the crosswalk to reach the bridge over the Virgin River, then take a left up the sandstone steps to begin the hike.\",\"doFeesApply\":\"false\",\"longDescription\":\"The moderate, 2.0 mi (3.2 km) Kayenta Trail follows the Virgin River before ascending to the Lower and Upper Emerald Pool Trails. Provides panoramic views of Zion Canyon along the way. Long drop-offs are present along this trail. You can either return on the same trail, or make a loop using one of the Emerald Pools trails.\\u00a0<br \\/> <br \\/> Water and restrooms are available at <a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/places\\/the-grotto-zion.htm\\\">The Grotto<\\/a>.\",\"reservationDescription\":\"\",\"season\":[\"Winter\",\"Spring\",\"Summer\",\"Fall\"],\"topics\":[{\"id\":\"5BE55D7F-BDB6-4E3D-AC35-2D8EBB974417\",\"name\":\"Trails\"}],\"durationDescription\":\"\",\"arePetsPermitted\":\"false\",\"timeOfDay\":[\"Day\",\"Night\",\"Dawn\",\"Dusk\"],\"duration\":\"2-3 Hours\",\"credit\":\"\"}',1,'2023-09-10 21:06:07','2023-09-10 21:06:36'),(377,'Canyon Overlook','Canyon Overlook Trailhead','nps','97CF105C-B416-4CDC-B84F-2A2C96D870DB',7,'Canyon Overlook Trailhead is located on the East Side of Zion. Follow State Route 9 through the Zion-Mount Carmel Tunnel to the trailhead. Parking is limited. \\r\\n\\r\\n','Canyon Overlook Trail is a moderate, 1 mi (1.6 km) trail on the East Side of Zion. The path begins with a series of sandstone steps with a metal handrail leading visitors over rocky terrain above a dark slot canyon. Near the end of the trail, the view opens up to an expanse of red and cream-colored slickrock. The end of the trail is marked by a fenced cliff edge facing the main Zion Canyon, with excellent views of the Towers of the Virgin. Keep an eye out for bighorn sheep along the trail.   Parking is extremely limited, be prepared to try mulitple times. Pit toilets are available in the parking area.   ',NULL,'Long drop-offs, mostly fenced. Rocky and uneven trail that climbs 163 ft (50 m). ','1-2 Hours','1',37.2130542,-112.9410238,NULL,'https://www.nps.gov/thingstodo/hike-canyon-overlook.htm',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,1,NULL,'{\"id\":\"97CF105C-B416-4CDC-B84F-2A2C96D870DB\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/thingstodo\\/hike-canyon-overlook.htm\",\"title\":\"Canyon Overlook\",\"shortDescription\":\"Long drop-offs, mostly fenced. Rocky and uneven trail ends at a viewpoint for Pine Creek Canyon and lower Zion Canyon. To enter or exit the parking area just east of the tunnel, you must turn right. Parking is extremely limited, be prepared to try multiple times. Pit toilets are available in the parking area.\",\"images\":[{\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/EB34DB47-9EBF-94FA-749C688629AD3876.jpg\",\"credit\":\"NPS\\/Daren Reehl\",\"altText\":\"Red sandstone slickrock dotted with green foliage with mountains in the background.\",\"title\":\"Canyon Overlook\",\"description\":\"\",\"caption\":\"View looking towards the end of the Canyon Overlook Trail and the Towers and Temples of the Virgin\",\"crops\":[{\"aspectRatio\":\"1.78\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/primary\\/EB34DB47-9EBF-94FA-749C688629AD3876.jpg\"},{\"aspectRatio\":\"1\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/secondary\\/EB34DB47-9EBF-94FA-749C688629AD3876.jpg\"}]}],\"relatedParks\":[{\"states\":\"UT\",\"parkCode\":\"zion\",\"designation\":\"National Park\",\"fullName\":\"Zion National Park\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/zion\\/index.htm\",\"name\":\"Zion\"}],\"relatedOrganizations\":[],\"tags\":[\"Zion\",\"East Side of the Park\",\"Canyon Overlook\",\"moderate hike\",\"Zion National Park\",\"viewpoint\",\"out and back\"],\"latitude\":\"37.2130542073\",\"longitude\":\"-112.941023815\",\"geometryPoiId\":\"2504667f-1cfc-4d3a-9d7d-4ecfb8ec2575\",\"amenities\":[],\"location\":\"Canyon Overlook Trailhead\",\"seasonDescription\":\"\",\"accessibilityInformation\":\"Long drop-offs, mostly fenced. Rocky and uneven trail that climbs 163 ft (50 m).\\u00a0\",\"isReservationRequired\":\"false\",\"ageDescription\":\"\",\"petsDescription\":\"\",\"timeOfDayDescription\":\"\",\"feeDescription\":\"Park entrance fees apply.\",\"age\":\"\",\"arePetsPermittedWithRestrictions\":\"false\",\"activities\":[{\"id\":\"BFF8C027-7C8F-480B-A5F8-CD8CE490BFBA\",\"name\":\"Hiking\"}],\"activityDescription\":\"\",\"locationDescription\":\"Canyon Overlook Trailhead is located on the East Side of Zion. Follow State Route 9 through the Zion-Mount Carmel Tunnel to the trailhead. Parking is limited.\\u00a0\",\"doFeesApply\":\"false\",\"longDescription\":\"Canyon Overlook Trail is a moderate, 1 mi (1.6 km) trail on the East Side of Zion. The path begins with a series of sandstone steps with a metal handrail leading visitors over rocky terrain above a dark slot canyon. Near the end of the trail, the view opens up to an expanse of red and cream-colored slickrock. The end of the trail is marked by a fenced cliff edge facing the main Zion Canyon, with excellent views of the Towers of the Virgin. Keep an eye out for bighorn sheep along the trail.<br \\/> \\u00a0 <p>Parking is extremely limited, be prepared to try mulitple times. Pit toilets are available in the parking area.<\\/p> <br \\/> \\u00a0\",\"reservationDescription\":\"\",\"season\":[\"Winter\",\"Spring\",\"Summer\",\"Fall\"],\"topics\":[{\"id\":\"5BE55D7F-BDB6-4E3D-AC35-2D8EBB974417\",\"name\":\"Trails\"}],\"durationDescription\":\"\",\"arePetsPermitted\":\"false\",\"timeOfDay\":[\"Day\",\"Night\",\"Dawn\",\"Dusk\"],\"duration\":\"1-2 Hours\",\"credit\":\"\"}',1,'2023-09-10 21:06:07','2023-09-10 21:06:36'),(378,'The Narrows','Riverside Walk Trailhead','nps','64075857-ACC6-4F4D-961B-1283D0F5475D',7,'The Narrows hike begins at the end of the Riverside Walk. Rivrside Walk is accessed at the Temple of Sinawava (shuttle stop #9).\\r\\n\\r\\n','The Narrows is a strenuous hike that follows the Virgin River through a narrow slot canyon. After 1 mile (0.6 km) on the easy, paved Riverside Walk, head down a set of stairs and into the river. The rest of your hike will take place in the Virgin River, where the flow rate can change day by day and the streambed is rocky with unstable footing. Flash flooding and hypothermia are constant dangers. The hike requires wading, walking, and sometimes swimming in the river.  Good planning, proper equipment, and sound judgment are essential for a safe and successful hike. There is no formal destination, so remember to turn around before you feel tired so you can safely finish your hike the same way you came.  No permit is required for this day hike, but travel beyond Big Spring and up Orderville Canyon requires aWilderness permit.  Water and restrooms are available at the Temple of Sinawava.',NULL,'The river is the trail. River rocks are slippery and uneven. Water depth is variable from ankle to chest height. The route is in shade for most of the day, even in summer.','1-8 Hours','1',37.2852131,-112.9476938,NULL,'https://www.nps.gov/thingstodo/hike-the-narrows.htm',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,1,NULL,'{\"id\":\"64075857-ACC6-4F4D-961B-1283D0F5475D\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/thingstodo\\/hike-the-narrows.htm\",\"title\":\"The Narrows\",\"shortDescription\":\"The Narrows is a strenuous hike that follows the Virgin River through a narrow slot canyon. After 1 mile (0.6 km) on the easy, paved Riverside Walk, head down a set of stairs and into the river. The rest of your hike will take place in the Virgin River, where the flow rate can change day by day and the streambed is rocky with unstable footing. Flash flooding and hypothermia are constant dangers.\",\"images\":[{\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/24D4BA84-FA56-0142-E2998BE721BC0F81.jpg\",\"credit\":\"NPS\",\"altText\":\"Red sandstone canyon walls frame the blue water of the Virgin River below.\",\"title\":\"The Narrows\",\"description\":\"\",\"caption\":\"View in The Narrows\",\"crops\":[{\"aspectRatio\":\"1.78\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/primary\\/24D4BA84-FA56-0142-E2998BE721BC0F81.jpg\"},{\"aspectRatio\":\"1\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/secondary\\/24D4BA84-FA56-0142-E2998BE721BC0F81.jpg\"}]}],\"relatedParks\":[{\"states\":\"UT\",\"parkCode\":\"zion\",\"designation\":\"National Park\",\"fullName\":\"Zion National Park\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/zion\\/index.htm\",\"name\":\"Zion\"}],\"relatedOrganizations\":[],\"tags\":[\"The Narrows\",\"Riverside Walk\",\"Virgin River\",\"Zion Canyon\",\"day hike\",\"water\"],\"latitude\":\"37.2852131125\",\"longitude\":\"-112.947693774\",\"geometryPoiId\":\"e9f41627-cb50-40f9-a9d6-76c9fc5de798\",\"amenities\":[],\"location\":\"Riverside Walk Trailhead\",\"seasonDescription\":\"The Narrows may close when the river is flowing over 140 cubic feet per second. This frequently occurs in monsoon season (late summer) and during spring runoff. Always check the weather and current conditions before beginning your hike.\",\"accessibilityInformation\":\"The river is the trail. River rocks are slippery and uneven. Water depth is variable from ankle to chest height. The route is in shade for most of the day, even in summer.\",\"isReservationRequired\":\"false\",\"ageDescription\":\"\",\"petsDescription\":\"\",\"timeOfDayDescription\":\"\",\"feeDescription\":\"Park entrance fees apply.\",\"age\":\"\",\"arePetsPermittedWithRestrictions\":\"false\",\"activities\":[{\"id\":\"BFF8C027-7C8F-480B-A5F8-CD8CE490BFBA\",\"name\":\"Hiking\"}],\"activityDescription\":\"\",\"locationDescription\":\"The Narrows hike begins at the end of the Riverside Walk. Rivrside Walk is accessed at the Temple of Sinawava (shuttle stop #9).\",\"doFeesApply\":\"false\",\"longDescription\":\"The Narrows is a strenuous hike that follows the Virgin River through a narrow slot canyon. After 1 mile (0.6 km) on the easy, paved Riverside Walk, head down a set of stairs and into the river. The rest of your hike will take place in the Virgin River, where the flow rate can change day by day and the streambed is rocky with unstable footing. Flash flooding and hypothermia are constant dangers. The hike requires wading, walking, and sometimes swimming in the river.<br \\/> <br \\/> Good planning, proper equipment, and sound judgment are essential for a safe and successful hike. There is no formal destination, so remember to turn around before you feel tired so you can safely finish your hike the same way you came.<br \\/> <br \\/> No permit is required for this day hike, but travel beyond Big Spring and up Orderville Canyon requires a<a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/zion\\/planyourvisit\\/thenarrows.htm\\\">Wilderness permit.<\\/a><br \\/> <br \\/> Water and restrooms are available at the Temple of Sinawava.\",\"reservationDescription\":\"\",\"season\":[\"Winter\",\"Spring\",\"Summer\",\"Fall\"],\"topics\":[{\"id\":\"5BE55D7F-BDB6-4E3D-AC35-2D8EBB974417\",\"name\":\"Trails\"}],\"durationDescription\":\"\",\"arePetsPermitted\":\"false\",\"timeOfDay\":[\"Day\",\"Night\",\"Dawn\",\"Dusk\"],\"duration\":\"1-8 Hours\",\"credit\":\"\"}',1,'2023-09-10 21:06:07','2023-09-10 21:06:36'),(379,'Riverside Walk','Riverside Walk Trailhead','nps','823A078C-D324-4648-B910-F15507E67244',7,'Riverside Walk is at the end of the Zion Canyon Scenic Drive at the Temple of Sinawava (shuttle stop #9). Follow the sidewalk past the shuttle stop and restrooms to begin your hike. \\r\\n\\r\\n','The Riverside Walk is a relatively flat and paved 2.2 mile (3.5 km) round-trip trail in the northern end of Zion Canyon. This easy trail follows the Virgin River as the sheer sandstone walls narrow in around you. The Riverside Walk is mostly flat through the first half-mile, although paving is irregular in some sections and minor drop-offs are present. After the first 0.4 miles (0.6 km), the trail is no longer wheelchair accessible due to steeper grades.  Look for exhibits along the trail to learn about canyon carving, erosion, and the ecological richness of this area.  This trail also leads to The Narrows. If you wish to stay dry, you can turn around at the end of the paved trail.   Water and restrooms are available at the Temple of Sinawava.',NULL,'Trail is paved, but only wheelchair accessible for the first 0.4 miles (0.6 km). Past that point there are several steep slopes and narrow paths that may be difficult to navigate. Deep sand may be present on pavement after rain. Elevation change is 57 ft (17 m).','1-2 Hours','1',37.2852131,-112.9476938,NULL,'https://www.nps.gov/thingstodo/hike-riverside-walk.htm',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,1,NULL,'{\"id\":\"823A078C-D324-4648-B910-F15507E67244\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/thingstodo\\/hike-riverside-walk.htm\",\"title\":\"Riverside Walk\",\"shortDescription\":\"The Riverside Walk is a relatively flat and paved 2.2 mile (3.5 km) round-trip trail in the northern end of Zion Canyon. This easy trail follows the Virgin River as the sheer sandstone walls narrow in around you. The Riverside Walk is mostly flat through the first half-mile, although paving is irregular in some sections and minor drop-offs are present. After the first 0.4 miles (0.6 km), the trail is no longer wheelchair accessible due to steeper grades.\",\"images\":[{\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/E187F7C6-9E0B-2F5A-129B7F885F29A4D7.jpg\",\"credit\":\"NPS\\/Abi Farish\",\"altText\":\"Two hikers in blue jackets walk along a sandy trail lined with a wooden rail and fall foliage.\",\"title\":\"Riverside Walk\",\"description\":\"\",\"caption\":\"Hikers on the Riverside Walk in autumn.\",\"crops\":[{\"aspectRatio\":\"1.78\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/primary\\/E187F7C6-9E0B-2F5A-129B7F885F29A4D7.jpg\"},{\"aspectRatio\":\"1\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/secondary\\/E187F7C6-9E0B-2F5A-129B7F885F29A4D7.jpg\"}]}],\"relatedParks\":[{\"states\":\"UT\",\"parkCode\":\"zion\",\"designation\":\"National Park\",\"fullName\":\"Zion National Park\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/zion\\/index.htm\",\"name\":\"Zion\"}],\"relatedOrganizations\":[],\"tags\":[\"Riverside\",\"Virgin River\",\"easy trail\",\"out and back\",\"river access\"],\"latitude\":\"37.2852131125\",\"longitude\":\"-112.947693774\",\"geometryPoiId\":\"e9f41627-cb50-40f9-a9d6-76c9fc5de798\",\"amenities\":[],\"location\":\"Riverside Walk Trailhead\",\"seasonDescription\":\"Riverside Walk is open year-round, but sections of the trail may close in winter due to icy and unsafe conditions. Check the park\'s <a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/zion\\/planyourvisit\\/conditions.htm\\\">current conditions<\\/a> before your trip.\",\"accessibilityInformation\":\"Trail is paved, but only wheelchair accessible for the first 0.4 miles (0.6 km). Past that point there are several steep slopes and narrow paths that may be difficult to navigate. Deep sand may be present on pavement after rain. Elevation change is 57 ft (17 m).\",\"isReservationRequired\":\"false\",\"ageDescription\":\"\",\"petsDescription\":\"\",\"timeOfDayDescription\":\"\",\"feeDescription\":\"Park entrance fees apply.\",\"age\":\"\",\"arePetsPermittedWithRestrictions\":\"false\",\"activities\":[{\"id\":\"BFF8C027-7C8F-480B-A5F8-CD8CE490BFBA\",\"name\":\"Hiking\"}],\"activityDescription\":\"\",\"locationDescription\":\"Riverside Walk is at the end of the Zion Canyon Scenic Drive at the Temple of Sinawava (shuttle stop #9). Follow the sidewalk past the shuttle stop and restrooms to begin your hike.\\u00a0\",\"doFeesApply\":\"false\",\"longDescription\":\"The Riverside Walk is a relatively flat and paved 2.2 mile (3.5 km) round-trip trail in the northern end of Zion Canyon. This easy trail follows the Virgin River as the sheer sandstone walls narrow in around you. The Riverside Walk is mostly flat through the first half-mile, although paving is irregular in some sections and minor drop-offs are present. After the first 0.4 miles (0.6 km), the trail is no longer wheelchair accessible due to steeper grades.<br \\/> <br \\/> Look for exhibits along the trail to learn about canyon carving, erosion, and the ecological richness of this area.<br \\/> <br \\/> This trail also leads to The Narrows. If you wish to stay dry, you can turn around at the end of the paved trail.\\u00a0<br \\/> <br \\/> Water and restrooms are available at the Temple of Sinawava.\",\"reservationDescription\":\"\",\"season\":[\"Winter\",\"Spring\",\"Summer\",\"Fall\"],\"topics\":[{\"id\":\"5BE55D7F-BDB6-4E3D-AC35-2D8EBB974417\",\"name\":\"Trails\"}],\"durationDescription\":\"\",\"arePetsPermitted\":\"false\",\"timeOfDay\":[\"Day\",\"Night\",\"Dawn\",\"Dusk\"],\"duration\":\"1-2 Hours\",\"credit\":\"\"}',1,'2023-09-10 21:06:07','2023-09-10 21:06:36'),(380,'La Verkin Creek Trail to Kolob Arch','Lee Pass Trailhead','nps','6AD66A92-B0EB-4064-A52A-2DDB02CF9143',7,'Lee Pass Trailhead is the second to last trailhead along the Kolob Canyons Road. Parking is limited. No water or restrooms available here.\\r\\n\\r\\n<h3>Wilderness Rules & Regulations apply:</h3> <h4>Group Size Limits</h4> <p>Large groups result in larger impacts. Trails in the Zion Wilderness have a group size limit in order to help protect the wilderness character of the area. Group size limits are strictly enforced. Groups are limited to a maximum of 12 people that share the same affiliation (e.g., school, club, scout group, colleagues, family, friends, or a combination thereof).<br /> Groups that exceed these limits may not split up and visit the same drainage or wilderness trail on the same day, but may split up and visit different areas.</p> <h4>Restrooms</h4> <p>Fill your water bottle at the Kolob Canyons Visitor Center. Restroom facilities are located at the Kolob Canyons Visitor Center and pit toilets are located at the Taylor Creek Trailhead, South Fork parking lot, and at the Kolob Canyons Viewpoint. Always use the restroom before hiking. If you are on the trail, be prepared to pack out human waste, toilet paper, diapers, and hygiene products. Human waste disposal bags are highly recommended to transport solid waste. Please follow <a href=\"https://www.nps.gov/zion/getinvolved/supportyourpark/leavenotrace.htm\">Leave No Trace</a> principles while in the park.</p> <h4>Pets</h4> <p><a href=\"https://www.nps.gov/zion/planyourvisit/pets.htm\">Pets are not permitted</a> on these trails. The only trail for pets in Zion National Park is the <a href=\"https://www.nps.gov/zion/planyourvisit/zion-canyon-trail-descriptions.htm\">Pa\'rus Trail.</a></p>','The La Verkin Creek Trail to the Kolob Arch allows hikers outstanding opportunities for solitude in a primitive area of Zion Wilderness. The trail begins at Lee Pass with stunning views of the Kolob Canyons as it crosses Timber Creek and continues to descend toward La Verkin Creek. After seven miles a spur trail leads hikers to a viewpoint where they can see the Kolob Arch. With a span of 287 feet and a thickness of 75 feet, the Kolob Arch is one of the world\'s largest natural arches.  ',NULL,'Strenuous trail with 1037ft / 316m of elevation change over 14mi / 22.5km. Rocky, sandy, uneven terrain with water crossings. ','7-9 Hours','1',37.4522214,-113.1913976,NULL,'https://www.nps.gov/thingstodo/la-verkin-creek-trail-to-kolob-arch.htm',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,1,NULL,'{\"id\":\"6AD66A92-B0EB-4064-A52A-2DDB02CF9143\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/thingstodo\\/la-verkin-creek-trail-to-kolob-arch.htm\",\"title\":\"La Verkin Creek Trail to Kolob Arch\",\"shortDescription\":\"The La Verkin Creek Trail to the Kolob Arch allows hikers outstanding opportunities for solitude in a primitive area of Zion Wilderness. The trail begins at Lee Pass with stunning views of the Kolob Canyons as it crosses Timber Creek and continues to descend toward La Verkin Creek. After seven miles a spur trail leads hikers to a viewpoint where they can see the Kolob Arch.\",\"images\":[{\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/C8607E36-E5AE-CA10-130C375E3FB44434.jpg\",\"credit\":\"NPS\\/Rendall Seely\",\"altText\":\"Looking up at an orange-red sandstone arch on a cliff-face with clear blue skies overhead.\",\"title\":\"Kolob Arch\",\"description\":\"\",\"caption\":\"The Kolob Arch is one of the world\'s largest natural arches.\",\"crops\":[{\"aspectRatio\":\"1.78\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/primary\\/C8607E36-E5AE-CA10-130C375E3FB44434.jpg\"},{\"aspectRatio\":\"1\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/secondary\\/C8607E36-E5AE-CA10-130C375E3FB44434.jpg\"}]}],\"relatedParks\":[{\"states\":\"UT\",\"parkCode\":\"zion\",\"designation\":\"National Park\",\"fullName\":\"Zion National Park\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/zion\\/index.htm\",\"name\":\"Zion\"}],\"relatedOrganizations\":[],\"tags\":[\"Arch\",\"Kolob\",\"Kolob Canyons\",\"Zion National Park\",\"out and back\",\"la verkin creek\",\"lee pass\",\"wilderness\",\"difficult\"],\"latitude\":\"37.4522213601\",\"longitude\":\"-113.191397606\",\"geometryPoiId\":\"19fee113-5a0f-4946-b9a6-c2db01162ccf\",\"amenities\":[],\"location\":\"Lee Pass Trailhead\",\"seasonDescription\":\"\",\"accessibilityInformation\":\"Strenuous trail with 1037ft \\/ 316m of elevation change over 14mi \\/ 22.5km. Rocky, sandy, uneven terrain with water crossings.\\u00a0\",\"isReservationRequired\":\"false\",\"ageDescription\":\"\",\"petsDescription\":\"Pets are not permitted on any trails within Kolob Canyons.\\u00a0\",\"timeOfDayDescription\":\"Overnight trips require a\\u00a0<a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/zion\\/planyourvisit\\/wilderness.htm\\\">Wilderness Permit<\\/a>.\",\"feeDescription\":\"Park entrance fees apply.\",\"age\":\"\",\"arePetsPermittedWithRestrictions\":\"false\",\"activities\":[{\"id\":\"BFF8C027-7C8F-480B-A5F8-CD8CE490BFBA\",\"name\":\"Hiking\"}],\"activityDescription\":\"<h3>Wilderness Rules & Regulations apply:<\\/h3> <h4>Group Size Limits<\\/h4> <p>Large groups result in larger impacts. Trails in the Zion Wilderness have a group size limit in order to help protect the wilderness character of the area. Group size limits are strictly enforced. Groups are limited to a maximum of 12 people that share the same affiliation (e.g., school, club, scout group, colleagues, family, friends, or a combination thereof).<br \\/> Groups that exceed these limits may not split up and visit the same drainage or wilderness trail on the same day, but may split up and visit different areas.<\\/p> <h4>Restrooms<\\/h4> <p>Fill your water bottle at the Kolob Canyons Visitor Center. Restroom facilities are located at the Kolob Canyons Visitor Center and pit toilets are located at the Taylor Creek Trailhead, South Fork parking lot, and at the Kolob Canyons Viewpoint. Always use the restroom before hiking. If you are on the trail, be prepared to pack out human waste, toilet paper, diapers, and hygiene products. Human waste disposal bags are highly recommended to transport solid waste. Please follow\\u00a0<a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/zion\\/getinvolved\\/supportyourpark\\/leavenotrace.htm\\\">Leave No Trace<\\/a>\\u00a0principles while in the park.<\\/p> <h4>Pets<\\/h4> <p><a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/zion\\/planyourvisit\\/pets.htm\\\">Pets are not permitted<\\/a>\\u00a0on these trails. The only trail for pets in Zion National Park is the\\u00a0<a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/zion\\/planyourvisit\\/zion-canyon-trail-descriptions.htm\\\">Pa\'rus Trail.<\\/a><\\/p>\",\"locationDescription\":\"Lee Pass Trailhead is the second to last trailhead along the Kolob Canyons Road. Parking is limited. No water or restrooms available here.\",\"doFeesApply\":\"false\",\"longDescription\":\"The La Verkin Creek Trail to the Kolob Arch allows hikers outstanding opportunities for solitude in a primitive area of Zion Wilderness. The trail begins at Lee Pass with stunning views of the Kolob Canyons as it crosses Timber Creek and continues to descend toward La Verkin Creek. After seven miles a spur trail leads hikers to a viewpoint where they can see the Kolob Arch. With a span of 287 feet and a thickness of 75 feet, the Kolob Arch is one of the world\'s largest natural arches. <p>\\u00a0<\\/p>\",\"reservationDescription\":\"\",\"season\":[\"Winter\",\"Spring\",\"Summer\",\"Fall\"],\"topics\":[{\"id\":\"5BE55D7F-BDB6-4E3D-AC35-2D8EBB974417\",\"name\":\"Trails\"}],\"durationDescription\":\"\",\"arePetsPermitted\":\"false\",\"timeOfDay\":[\"Day\",\"Night\",\"Dawn\",\"Dusk\"],\"duration\":\"7-9 Hours\",\"credit\":\"\"}',1,'2023-09-10 21:06:07','2023-09-10 21:06:36'),(381,'Pa\'rus Trail','Pa\'rus Trailhead','nps','6FD175EF-A82F-47AA-B055-8833EA1C1162',7,'Start from either the Zion Canyon Visitor Center (shuttle stop #1) or from Canyon Junction (shuttle stop #3).  Zion Canyon Visitor Center To reach the trailhead, follow the sidewalk through the Visitor Center plaza and past the shuttle stop to the bridge. Once you cross the bridge, follow the crosswalk to the trailhead adjacent to South Campground.   Canyon Junction  From the Canyon Junction shuttle stop, follow the sidewalk south (in the direction of the Watchman) under the roadway bridge.\\r\\n\\r\\nHike, bike, or walk a dog along the paved Pa\'rus Trail. Wildlife, such as deer, can often be seen in the meadows near the trail. Multiple river access points. Trailside exhibits.','\"Pa’rus\" is Paiute for “bubbling water.” The Pa’rus Trail follows the Virgin River and has some of the best views of the Watchman. The paved trail connects the Zion Canyon Visitor Center and Canyon Junction. It is accessible for wheelchairs, pets on leashes, and bicycles. Trailside exhibits cover a variety of subjects, including plant and animal life along the river, geology, and human history.  The Pa\'rus makes a good location for watching the sunset, stargazing, and astrophotography.   Access this trail from the Museum (Shuttle Stop #2) by using a short connector trail that is not designed for wheelchairs and not approved for bikes or pets.  Restrooms and water filling stations are available at the Visitor Center.',NULL,'Wide, cement-paved trail with 50 ft (15m) of elevation change. Angled trailside exhibits are not higher than 48 inches tall.','30-120 Minutes','1',37.2010471,-112.9867885,NULL,'https://www.nps.gov/thingstodo/hike-pa-rus-trail.htm',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,1,NULL,'{\"id\":\"6FD175EF-A82F-47AA-B055-8833EA1C1162\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/thingstodo\\/hike-pa-rus-trail.htm\",\"title\":\"Pa\'rus Trail\",\"shortDescription\":\"Pa\\u2019rus is Paiute for \\u201cbubbling water.\\u201d The Pa\\u2019rus Trail follows the Virgin River and has some of the best views of the Watchman. The paved trail connects the Zion Canyon Visitor Center and Canyon Junction. It is accessible for wheelchairs, pets on leashes, and bicycles. Trailside exhibits cover a variety of subjects, including plant and animal life along the river, geology, and human history.\",\"images\":[{\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/B9496B9A-01B7-0AB1-0D8BA0A554D4B4EA.jpg\",\"credit\":\"NPS\",\"altText\":\"A tan, paved trail cuts through a meadow of yellow grasses toward a triangular sandstone mountain.\",\"title\":\"Pa\'rus Trail\",\"description\":\"\",\"caption\":\"View from the Pa\'rus Trail as it follows the river between Canyon Junction and the Visitor Center.\",\"crops\":[{\"aspectRatio\":\"1.78\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/primary\\/B9496B9A-01B7-0AB1-0D8BA0A554D4B4EA.jpg\"},{\"aspectRatio\":\"1\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/secondary\\/B9496B9A-01B7-0AB1-0D8BA0A554D4B4EA.jpg\"}]}],\"relatedParks\":[{\"states\":\"UT\",\"parkCode\":\"zion\",\"designation\":\"National Park\",\"fullName\":\"Zion National Park\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/zion\\/index.htm\",\"name\":\"Zion\"}],\"relatedOrganizations\":[],\"tags\":[\"Parus Trail\",\"Zion\",\"Zion Canyon\",\"paved trail\",\"accessible trail\",\"Zion National Park\",\"Bike Hike Trail\",\"pets allowed\"],\"latitude\":\"37.2010471278\",\"longitude\":\"-112.986788498\",\"geometryPoiId\":\"7caf5f58-c2f8-4ae9-8bda-7fac4138edeb\",\"amenities\":[],\"location\":\"Pa\'rus Trailhead\",\"seasonDescription\":\"\",\"accessibilityInformation\":\"Wide, cement-paved trail with 50 ft (15m) of elevation change. Angled trailside exhibits are not higher than 48 inches tall.\",\"isReservationRequired\":\"false\",\"ageDescription\":\"\",\"petsDescription\":\"<h4><b>Please follow the rules of BARK!<\\/b><\\/h4> <p><br \\/> <b><u>B<\\/u><\\/b><b>ag your pet\'s poop<\\/b><br \\/> Pet owners are responsible for removing pet waste from all areas in the park including campgrounds, picnic areas, parking lots, roads, pet-friendly trails, and other developed areas.<br \\/> <br \\/> <b><u>A<\\/u>lways wear a leash<\\/b><br \\/> Pets must be restrained on a leash no longer than 6 feet.<br \\/> <br \\/> <b><u>R<\\/u>espect wildlife<\\/b><br \\/> Pets can harass or harm wildlife by making noise or scaring wildlife away.<br \\/> <br \\/> <b><u>K<\\/u>now where you can go<\\/b><br \\/> The only trail that allows pets is the Pa\\u2019rus Trail, which begins at the Zion Canyon Visitor Center.<br \\/> <br \\/> <b>Pets are<\\/b>\\u00a0<b>not permitted -\\u00a0<\\/b>on any other trails, wilderness areas, on shuttle buses, or in public buildings in Zion.<br \\/> <br \\/> <b>Additionally,\\u00a0<\\/b>properly restrained pets are welcome along public roads and parking areas, in the developed campgrounds and picnic areas, and on the grounds of the Zion Lodge.<br \\/> <br \\/> <b>Pets should not be left unattended.<\\/b>\\u00a0Zion is hot! The interior temperature of a vehicle can quickly warm to dangerous levels, during most months of the year. Leaving a pet unattended in a vehicle with environmental conditions that pose a health risk to the animal is prohibited. Properly restrained pets may be left unattended in developed campgrounds only when environmental conditions are safe for the animal, and the animal is not making unreasonable noise (barking, etc.).<br \\/> <br \\/> <a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/zion\\/planyourvisit\\/service-animals.htm\\\"><b>Service Animals<\\/b><\\/a>\\u00a0may accompany their owner to all park locations. Under the\\u00a0<a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/www.ada.gov\\/\\\"><b>Americans with Disabilities Act<\\/b><\\/a>\\u00a0(ADA),\\u00a0<b><i>Service animals<\\/i><\\/b>\\u00a0are defined as dogs that are individually trained to do work or perform tasks for people with disabilities. Dogs whose sole function is to provide comfort or emotional support do not qualify as service animals under the ADA.<br \\/> <br \\/> <a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/www.gpo.gov\\/fdsys\\/pkg\\/CFR-2016-title36-vol1\\/xml\\/CFR-2016-title36-vol1-sec2-15.xml\\\"><b>36 CFR 2.15<\\/b><\\/a>\\u00a0(Code of Federal Regulations) provides more details concerning pets within National Park Service areas. Pet owners not adhering to regulations may be cited (minimum fine is $100).<br \\/> <br \\/> Read the complete\\u00a0<a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/zion\\/learn\\/management\\/lawsandpolicies.htm\\\"><b>Superintendent\'s Compendium<\\/b><\\/a>\\u00a0for more details.<br \\/> <br \\/> Boarding kennels are available in the nearby towns of Rockville, Hurricane, St. George, Kanab, and Cedar City.<\\/p>\",\"timeOfDayDescription\":\"\",\"feeDescription\":\"Park entrance fees apply.\",\"age\":\"\",\"arePetsPermittedWithRestrictions\":\"false\",\"activities\":[{\"id\":\"BFF8C027-7C8F-480B-A5F8-CD8CE490BFBA\",\"name\":\"Hiking\"}],\"activityDescription\":\"Hike, bike, or walk a dog along the paved Pa\'rus Trail. Wildlife, such as deer, can often be seen in the meadows near the trail. Multiple river access points.\\u00a0Trailside exhibits.\",\"locationDescription\":\"Start from either the Zion Canyon Visitor Center (shuttle stop #1) or from Canyon Junction (shuttle stop #3).<br \\/> <br \\/> Zion Canyon Visitor Center<br \\/> To reach the trailhead, follow the sidewalk through the Visitor Center plaza and past the shuttle stop to the bridge. Once you cross the bridge, follow the crosswalk to the trailhead adjacent to South Campground.<br \\/> <br \\/> <br \\/> Canyon Junction\\u00a0<br \\/> From the Canyon Junction shuttle stop, follow the sidewalk south (in the direction of the Watchman) under the roadway bridge.\",\"doFeesApply\":\"false\",\"longDescription\":\"\\\"Pa\\u2019rus\\\" is Paiute for \\u201cbubbling water.\\u201d The Pa\\u2019rus Trail follows the Virgin River and has some of the best views of the Watchman. The paved trail connects the Zion Canyon Visitor Center and Canyon Junction. It is accessible for wheelchairs, pets on leashes, and bicycles. Trailside exhibits cover a variety of subjects, including plant and animal life along the river, geology, and human history.<br \\/> <br \\/> The Pa\'rus makes a good location for watching the sunset, stargazing, and astrophotography.\\u00a0<br \\/> <br \\/> Access this trail from the Museum (Shuttle Stop #2) by using a short connector trail that is not designed for wheelchairs and not approved for bikes or pets.<br \\/> <br \\/> Restrooms and water filling stations are available at the Visitor Center.\",\"reservationDescription\":\"\",\"season\":[\"Winter\",\"Spring\",\"Summer\",\"Fall\"],\"topics\":[{\"id\":\"5BE55D7F-BDB6-4E3D-AC35-2D8EBB974417\",\"name\":\"Trails\"}],\"durationDescription\":\"The Pa\'rus is 3.5 mile (5.6 km) from one end to the other.<br \\/> <br \\/> Visitors may also take an optional, unpaved connector trail to the Human History Museum (shuttle stop #2) about halfway through the trail. This short path is not designed for wheelchairs and not approved for bikes or pets.\",\"arePetsPermitted\":\"true\",\"timeOfDay\":[\"Day\",\"Night\",\"Dawn\",\"Dusk\"],\"duration\":\"30-120 Minutes\",\"credit\":\"\"}',1,'2023-09-10 21:06:07','2023-09-10 21:06:36'),(382,'Timber Creek Overlook','Timber Creek Overlook Trailhead','nps','9D7B17DE-663C-4A85-A09D-183C71E5EE96',7,'From the Kolob Canyons Visitor Center, follow the Kolob Canyons Road to the very end. This is where the Timber Creek Overlook Trailhead and parking area is.   Restrooms available at trailhead. Water available at Visitor Center. \\r\\n\\r\\n','The Timber Creek Overlook Trail is a short, relatively easy trail that follows a rocky ridge with minimal elevation gain, making it perfect for a quick walk to a stunning viewpoint. Once you reach the overlook, you\'ll be rewarded with views of Shuntavi Butte,Timber Creek, Kolob Terrace, and the Pine Valley Mountains. Looking south on the far horizon, visitors may see Mount Trumbull, 100 miles away at the north rim of the Grand Canyon. During the spring and early summer months, the Timber Creek Overlook Trail is covered in a beautiful display of desert wildflowers. The dirt trail is often muddy when wet.  A small picnic area is located a short distance from the trailhead. ',NULL,'Unpaved, uneven climb over rocky terrain on a narrow trail. Overlook at the end has steep drop-offs. Total elevation gain of 100ft (30m).','30-60 Minutes','1',37.4355110,-113.2019337,NULL,'https://www.nps.gov/thingstodo/timber-creek-overlook.htm',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,1,NULL,'{\"id\":\"9D7B17DE-663C-4A85-A09D-183C71E5EE96\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/thingstodo\\/timber-creek-overlook.htm\",\"title\":\"Timber Creek Overlook\",\"shortDescription\":\"Follows a ridge to views of Timber Creek, Kolob Terrace, and the Pine Valley Mountains.\",\"images\":[{\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/670F9B1A-A06D-8B7D-EC7FFF38D457B589.jpg\",\"credit\":\"NPS\\/Bryanna Plog\",\"altText\":\"Red sandstone rocks with three hikers standing on them looking out over a valley. Blue, cloudy sky.\",\"title\":\"Timber Creek Overlook\",\"description\":\"\",\"caption\":\"View from the Timber Creek Overlook.\",\"crops\":[{\"aspectRatio\":\"1.78\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/primary\\/670F9B1A-A06D-8B7D-EC7FFF38D457B589.jpg\"},{\"aspectRatio\":\"1\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/secondary\\/670F9B1A-A06D-8B7D-EC7FFF38D457B589.jpg\"}]}],\"relatedParks\":[{\"states\":\"UT\",\"parkCode\":\"zion\",\"designation\":\"National Park\",\"fullName\":\"Zion National Park\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/zion\\/index.htm\",\"name\":\"Zion\"}],\"relatedOrganizations\":[],\"tags\":[\"Zion\",\"Kolob Canyons\",\"timber creek overlook\",\"overlook\",\"viewpoint\",\"Zion National Park\",\"out and back\",\"Easy hike\",\"family hike\"],\"latitude\":\"37.4355110104\",\"longitude\":\"-113.201933722\",\"geometryPoiId\":\"947583a2-3aed-4ec9-bcc4-fea434e53767\",\"amenities\":[],\"location\":\"Timber Creek Overlook Trailhead\",\"seasonDescription\":\"\",\"accessibilityInformation\":\"Unpaved, uneven climb over rocky terrain on a narrow trail. Overlook at the end has steep drop-offs. Total elevation gain of 100ft (30m).\",\"isReservationRequired\":\"false\",\"ageDescription\":\"\",\"petsDescription\":\"Pets are not allowed on any trails in Kolob Canyons.\",\"timeOfDayDescription\":\"\",\"feeDescription\":\"Park entrance fees apply.\\u00a0\",\"age\":\"\",\"arePetsPermittedWithRestrictions\":\"false\",\"activities\":[{\"id\":\"BFF8C027-7C8F-480B-A5F8-CD8CE490BFBA\",\"name\":\"Hiking\"}],\"activityDescription\":\"\",\"locationDescription\":\"From the Kolob Canyons Visitor Center, follow the Kolob Canyons Road to the very end. This is where the Timber Creek Overlook Trailhead and parking area is.\\u00a0<br \\/> <br \\/> Restrooms available at trailhead. Water available at Visitor Center.\\u00a0\",\"doFeesApply\":\"false\",\"longDescription\":\"The Timber Creek Overlook Trail\\u00a0is a short, relatively easy trail that follows a rocky ridge with minimal elevation gain, making it perfect for a quick walk to a stunning viewpoint. Once you reach the overlook, you\'ll be rewarded with views of Shuntavi Butte,Timber Creek, Kolob Terrace, and the Pine Valley Mountains.\\u00a0Looking south on the far horizon, visitors may see Mount Trumbull, 100 miles away at the north rim of the Grand Canyon. During the spring and early summer months, the Timber Creek Overlook Trail is covered in a beautiful display of desert wildflowers. The dirt trail is often muddy when wet.<br \\/> <br \\/> A small picnic area is located a short distance from the trailhead.\\u00a0\",\"reservationDescription\":\"\",\"season\":[\"Winter\",\"Spring\",\"Summer\",\"Fall\"],\"topics\":[{\"id\":\"5BE55D7F-BDB6-4E3D-AC35-2D8EBB974417\",\"name\":\"Trails\"}],\"durationDescription\":\"\",\"arePetsPermitted\":\"false\",\"timeOfDay\":[\"Day\",\"Night\",\"Dawn\",\"Dusk\"],\"duration\":\"30-60 Minutes\",\"credit\":\"\"}',1,'2023-09-10 21:06:07','2023-09-10 21:06:36'),(383,'Climbing in Zion',NULL,'nps','CB9CCC3B-BD85-4FF6-B95C-B99D424230F9',7,'\\r\\n\\r\\n<a href=\"https://www.nps.gov/zion/planyourvisit/climbing.htm\">More Climbing information can be found on the park\'s website and at visitor centers.</a> <h4>Overnight Bivys</h4> <p>Permits are required for all overnight bivouacs, but are not required for day climbs. Bivy reservations can be made in advance for the most popular climbs. All permits must be picked up at the Zion Canyon Visitor Center the day before or the day of your climb.<br /> <a href=\"https://www.nps.gov/zion/planyourvisit/climbingpermits.htm\">Learn more about climbing bivy permits here.</a><br /> It is illegal to camp at the base of the wall or in your vehicle inside the park, unless in an established campground with a campground reservation.</p> <h4>Human Waste</h4> <p>Tube or bag human waste and carry it out. Do not drop your waste or leave it behind in any way.</p> <h4><strong>Guiding</strong></h4> <p>Commercial guiding is not allowed in the Zion Wilderness, which includes all Zion climbs.</p> <h4>Bolting and Route Development</h4> <p paraeid=\"{b4fd7152-a6a5-43ca-930d-9f03ebecb2be}{221}\" paraid=\"1761220262\">The National Park Service does not inspect, maintain, or repair bolts and other climbing equipment anywhere in the park. Make sure your climb is adequately protected by visually inspecting any pre-existing bolts or fixed pins. Your safety is your responsibility.</p> <p paraeid=\"{b4fd7152-a6a5-43ca-930d-9f03ebecb2be}{239}\" paraid=\"1940198881\">Zion National Park is world renowned for big wall and traditional climbing. Most routes in the park offer adventurous climbing utilizing traditionally placed protection such as cams, nuts, etc. Bolted routes, also known as sport climbs, are rare in Zion National Park, prohibited in the <a href=\"https://www.nps.gov/zion/planyourvisit/upload/ZION_Wilderness_Boundaries.pdf\">Zion Wilderness</a>, and are often sparsely protected.</p> <p paraeid=\"{183b8cf7-66a7-4e5a-9a08-1ad33ea914a7}{14}\" paraid=\"635002544\">A fixed anchor is defined as any piece of climbing equipment that is left in place to facilitate a safe ascent or rappel. Examples include, but are not limited to, bolts, pitons, and webbing. Only place fixed anchors as a last resort. Before placing fixed anchors on a route, think seriously about whether the route warrants them. All bolts must be placed using a hand drill only. Power drills are not allowed in Zion.</p> <p paraeid=\"{183b8cf7-66a7-4e5a-9a08-1ad33ea914a7}{30}\" paraid=\"946575846\">\"The placement of fixed climbing anchors, including climbing bolts, for the creation of bolt intensive face climbs, also called sport climbs, within the <a href=\"https://www.nps.gov/zion/planyourvisit/upload/ZION_Wilderness_Boundaries.pdf\">Zion Wilderness</a> is prohibited.\" -<a href=\"https://www.nps.gov/zion/learn/management/upload/Superintendents-Compendium-Final-Signed_Scanned-4-29-20.pdf\">Superintendent\'s Compendium</a>. Climbers should seek to develop routes which can be primarily protected by traditional climbing equipment, with bolts being used only when necessary.</p> <p paraeid=\"{183b8cf7-66a7-4e5a-9a08-1ad33ea914a7}{75}\" paraid=\"1228374145\">\"The establishment of bolt intensive face climbs is considered incompatible with wilderness preservation and management due to the concentration of human activity which they support and the types and levels of impacts associated with such routes.\" -<a href=\"https://www.nps.gov/policy/DOrders/DO_41.pdf\">NPS Director\'s Order 41</a></p> <p paraeid=\"{183b8cf7-66a7-4e5a-9a08-1ad33ea914a7}{30}\" paraid=\"946575846\">Equipment, including but not limited to fixed ropes, may not be left unattended for longer than 24 hours or it will be considered abandoned property. Abandoned property may be removed by the NPS and is subject to the following regulations:<strong> </strong><a href=\"https://www.ecfr.gov/cgi-bin/text-idx?SID=e70f79c6458dfd4c7acdf81313ce566e&mc=true&node=se36.1.2_122&rgn=div8\"><u>CFR 2.22</u></a></p> <p paraeid=\"{183b8cf7-66a7-4e5a-9a08-1ad33ea914a7}{75}\" paraid=\"1228374145\"> </p> <p paraeid=\"{183b8cf7-66a7-4e5a-9a08-1ad33ea914a7}{84}\" paraid=\"452813734\">If you are planning on establishing a new climbing route in Zion National Park, please come to the Wilderness Desk at the Zion Canyon Visitor Center to discuss the proposed route with a ranger. We can help you determine if your proposed route is in designated Wilderness and are happy to discuss any other important considerations. Contact the Wilderness staff at 435-772-0170 for additional information.</p>','CLICK HERE to download the new Climbing Guide to Seasonal Raptor Closures with all closure boundaries Zion National Park\'s 2,000-foot sandstone cliffs are world renowned for their big wall climbs and adventurous nature. Most routes in the park are not recommended for inexperienced climbers due to difficulty and the soft nature of the rock. While areas outside of the park offer abundant sport climbing and top roping possibilities, this is rare in the park, with bolted routes often having sparse protection.  The best conditions for climbing are from March through May and September through early November. Temperatures in the summer can soar over 100 degrees even on shaded walls. Additionally, summer months often bring monsoonal rainstorms. Unlike other rock types, desert sandstone is heavily weakened when wet. Avoid climbing in damp areas or after rainstorms. It often takes two or more days for the rock to be dry enough to climb. This means that critical holds are more likely to break, altering routes for future climbers. Additionally, climbing protection is less likely to hold resulting in greater fall distances and chance of injury. A collection of a climber-submitted topographic maps can be found at the Wilderness Desk at the Zion Canyon Visitor Center. The park suggests submitting topographic maps of all first ascents in the park upon completion. This helps future generations locate climbs and provides a historical account of climbing in Zion National Park. Bouldering There are two bouldering areas in the main canyon. One is 40 yards west of the south entrance, called The Globe. This is a house sized boulder that poses a variety of options and problems. The second is Drilled Pocket Boulder, located 0.5 mile north of the south entrance. It is located on the west side of the road and is a slab with an obvious south facing crack.',NULL,'Zion National Park\'s 2,000-foot sandstone cliffs are world renowned for their big wall climbs and adventurous nature. Most routes in the park are not recommended for inexperienced climbers due to difficulty and the soft nature of the rock. While areas outside of the park offer abundant sport climbing and top roping possibilities, this is rare in the park, with bolted routes often having sparse protection.',NULL,'1',NULL,NULL,NULL,'https://www.nps.gov/thingstodo/climbing-in-zion.htm',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,1,NULL,'{\"id\":\"CB9CCC3B-BD85-4FF6-B95C-B99D424230F9\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/thingstodo\\/climbing-in-zion.htm\",\"title\":\"Climbing in Zion\",\"shortDescription\":\"Zion National Park\'s 2,000-foot sandstone cliffs are world renowned for their big wall climbs and adventurous nature. Most routes in the park are not recommended for inexperienced climbers due to difficulty and the soft nature of the rock. While areas outside of the park offer abundant sport climbing and top roping possibilities, this is rare in the park, with bolted routes often having sparse protection.\",\"images\":[{\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/C96A4188-DB68-932C-7A8F04EC2F4E9348.jpg\",\"credit\":\"NPS\\/Jason Burton\",\"altText\":\"Red-orange sandstone cliff-face with a rock climber in a helmet making his way up.\",\"title\":\"Rock climbing in Zion\",\"description\":\"\",\"caption\":\"Climber on Iron Messiah\",\"crops\":[{\"aspectRatio\":\"1.78\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/primary\\/C96A4188-DB68-932C-7A8F04EC2F4E9348.jpg\"},{\"aspectRatio\":\"1\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/secondary\\/C96A4188-DB68-932C-7A8F04EC2F4E9348.jpg\"}]}],\"relatedParks\":[{\"states\":\"UT\",\"parkCode\":\"zion\",\"designation\":\"National Park\",\"fullName\":\"Zion National Park\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/zion\\/index.htm\",\"name\":\"Zion\"}],\"relatedOrganizations\":[],\"tags\":[\"climbing\",\"Rock Climbing\",\"Zion National Park\",\"Kolob Canyons\",\"bivy\",\"bouldering\"],\"latitude\":\"\",\"longitude\":\"\",\"geometryPoiId\":\"\",\"amenities\":[],\"location\":\"\",\"seasonDescription\":\"The best conditions for climbing are from March through May and September through early November. Temperatures in the summer can soar over 100 degrees even on shaded walls. Additionally, summer months often bring monsoonal rainstorms. Unlike other rock types, desert sandstone is heavily weakened when wet. Avoid climbing in damp areas or after rainstorms.\",\"accessibilityInformation\":\"<p>Zion National Park\'s 2,000-foot sandstone cliffs are world renowned for their big wall climbs and adventurous nature. Most routes in the park are not recommended for inexperienced climbers due to difficulty and the soft nature of the rock. While areas outside of the park offer abundant sport climbing and top roping possibilities, this is rare in the park, with bolted routes often having sparse protection.<\\/p>\",\"isReservationRequired\":\"false\",\"ageDescription\":\"\",\"petsDescription\":\"Pets are not allowed in Zion Wilderness. <a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/zion\\/planyourvisit\\/pets.htm\\\">The only trail in the park for pets is the Pa\'rus.<\\/a>\\u00a0\",\"timeOfDayDescription\":\"\",\"feeDescription\":\"<a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/zion\\/planyourvisit\\/climbing-permits.htm\\\">Permits are needed for all overnight trips<\\/a><br \\/> <br \\/> <strong>Permits are not required for day climbs, but they are required for all overnight bivouacs. It is illegal to camp at the base of the wall or in your vehicle.<\\/strong>\",\"age\":\"\",\"arePetsPermittedWithRestrictions\":\"false\",\"activities\":[{\"id\":\"B12FAAB9-713F-4B38-83E4-A273F5A43C77\",\"name\":\"Climbing\"}],\"activityDescription\":\"<a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/zion\\/planyourvisit\\/climbing.htm\\\">More Climbing information can be found on the park\'s website and at visitor centers.<\\/a> <h4>Overnight Bivys<\\/h4> <p>Permits are required for all overnight bivouacs, but are not required for day climbs. Bivy reservations can be made in advance for the most popular climbs. All permits must be picked up at the Zion Canyon Visitor Center the day before or the day of your climb.<br \\/> <a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/zion\\/planyourvisit\\/climbingpermits.htm\\\">Learn more about climbing bivy permits here.<\\/a><br \\/> It is illegal to camp at the base of the wall or in your vehicle inside the park, unless in an established campground with a campground reservation.<\\/p> <h4>Human Waste<\\/h4> <p>Tube or bag human waste and carry it out. Do not drop your waste or leave it behind in any way.<\\/p> <h4><strong>Guiding<\\/strong><\\/h4> <p>Commercial guiding is not allowed in the Zion Wilderness, which includes all Zion climbs.<\\/p> <h4>Bolting and Route Development<\\/h4> <p paraeid=\\\"{b4fd7152-a6a5-43ca-930d-9f03ebecb2be}{221}\\\" paraid=\\\"1761220262\\\">The National Park Service does not inspect, maintain, or repair bolts and other climbing equipment anywhere in the park. Make sure your climb is adequately protected by visually inspecting any pre-existing bolts or fixed pins. Your safety is your responsibility.<\\/p> <p paraeid=\\\"{b4fd7152-a6a5-43ca-930d-9f03ebecb2be}{239}\\\" paraid=\\\"1940198881\\\">Zion National Park is world renowned for big wall and traditional climbing. Most routes in the park offer adventurous climbing utilizing traditionally placed protection such as cams, nuts, etc. Bolted routes, also known as sport climbs, are rare in Zion National Park, prohibited in the\\u00a0<a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/zion\\/planyourvisit\\/upload\\/ZION_Wilderness_Boundaries.pdf\\\">Zion Wilderness<\\/a>, and are often sparsely protected.<\\/p> <p paraeid=\\\"{183b8cf7-66a7-4e5a-9a08-1ad33ea914a7}{14}\\\" paraid=\\\"635002544\\\">A fixed anchor is defined as any piece of climbing equipment that is left in place to facilitate a safe ascent or rappel. Examples include, but are not limited to, bolts, pitons, and webbing. Only place fixed anchors as a last resort. Before placing fixed anchors on a route, think seriously about whether the route warrants them. All bolts must be placed using a hand drill only. Power drills are not allowed in Zion.<\\/p> <p paraeid=\\\"{183b8cf7-66a7-4e5a-9a08-1ad33ea914a7}{30}\\\" paraid=\\\"946575846\\\">\\\"The placement of fixed climbing anchors, including climbing bolts, for the creation of bolt intensive face climbs, also called sport climbs, within the\\u00a0<a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/zion\\/planyourvisit\\/upload\\/ZION_Wilderness_Boundaries.pdf\\\">Zion Wilderness<\\/a>\\u00a0is prohibited.\\\" -<a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/zion\\/learn\\/management\\/upload\\/Superintendents-Compendium-Final-Signed_Scanned-4-29-20.pdf\\\">Superintendent\'s Compendium<\\/a>. Climbers should seek to develop routes which can be primarily protected by traditional climbing equipment, with bolts being used only when necessary.<\\/p> <p paraeid=\\\"{183b8cf7-66a7-4e5a-9a08-1ad33ea914a7}{75}\\\" paraid=\\\"1228374145\\\">\\\"The establishment of bolt intensive face climbs is considered incompatible with wilderness preservation and management due to the concentration of human activity which they support and the types and levels of impacts associated with such routes.\\\" -<a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/policy\\/DOrders\\/DO_41.pdf\\\">NPS Director\'s Order 41<\\/a><\\/p> <p paraeid=\\\"{183b8cf7-66a7-4e5a-9a08-1ad33ea914a7}{30}\\\" paraid=\\\"946575846\\\">Equipment, including but not limited to fixed ropes, may not be left unattended for longer than 24 hours or it will be considered abandoned property. Abandoned property may be removed by the NPS and is subject to the following regulations:<strong>\\u00a0<\\/strong><a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/www.ecfr.gov\\/cgi-bin\\/text-idx?SID=e70f79c6458dfd4c7acdf81313ce566e&mc=true&node=se36.1.2_122&rgn=div8\\\"><u>CFR 2.22<\\/u><\\/a><\\/p> <p paraeid=\\\"{183b8cf7-66a7-4e5a-9a08-1ad33ea914a7}{75}\\\" paraid=\\\"1228374145\\\">\\u00a0<\\/p> <p paraeid=\\\"{183b8cf7-66a7-4e5a-9a08-1ad33ea914a7}{84}\\\" paraid=\\\"452813734\\\">If you are planning on establishing a new climbing route in Zion National Park, please come to the Wilderness Desk at the Zion Canyon Visitor Center to discuss the proposed route with a ranger. We can help you determine if your proposed route is in designated Wilderness and are happy to discuss any other important considerations. Contact the Wilderness staff at 435-772-0170 for additional information.<\\/p>\",\"locationDescription\":\"\",\"doFeesApply\":\"false\",\"longDescription\":\"<p><a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/zion\\/planyourvisit\\/upload\\/2022-Climbers-Guide-to-Seasonal-Raptor-Closures.pdf\\\">CLICK HERE to download the new Climbing Guide to Seasonal Raptor Closures with all closure boundaries<\\/a><\\/p> <p>Zion National Park\'s 2,000-foot sandstone cliffs are world renowned for their big wall climbs and adventurous nature. Most routes in the park are not recommended for inexperienced climbers due to difficulty and the soft nature of the rock. While areas outside of the park offer abundant sport climbing and top roping possibilities, this is rare in the park, with bolted routes often having sparse protection.<br \\/> <br \\/> The best conditions for climbing are from March through May and September through early November. Temperatures in the summer can soar over 100 degrees even on shaded walls. Additionally, summer months often bring monsoonal rainstorms. Unlike other rock types, desert sandstone is heavily weakened when wet. Avoid climbing in damp areas or after rainstorms. It often takes two or more days for the rock to be dry enough to climb. This means that critical holds are more likely to break, altering routes for future climbers. Additionally, climbing protection is less likely to hold resulting in greater fall distances and chance of injury. A collection of a climber-submitted topographic maps can be found at the Wilderness Desk at the Zion Canyon Visitor Center. The park suggests submitting topographic maps of all first ascents in the park upon completion. This helps future generations locate climbs and provides a historical account of climbing in Zion National Park.<\\/p> <h4><strong>Bouldering<\\/strong><\\/h4> <p>There are two bouldering areas in the main canyon. One is 40 yards west of the south entrance, called The Globe. This is a house sized boulder that poses a variety of options and problems. The second is Drilled Pocket Boulder, located 0.5 mile north of the south entrance. It is located on the west side of the road and is a slab with an obvious south facing crack.<\\/p>\",\"reservationDescription\":\"<a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/zion\\/planyourvisit\\/climbing-permits.htm\\\">Permits are needed for all overnight trips<\\/a><br \\/> <br \\/> <strong>Permits are not required for day climbs, but they are required for all overnight bivouacs. It is illegal to camp at the base of the wall or in your vehicle.<\\/strong>\",\"season\":[\"Winter\",\"Spring\",\"Fall\"],\"topics\":[{\"id\":\"F0F97E32-2F29-41B4-AF98-9FBE8DAB36B1\",\"name\":\"Geology\"},{\"id\":\"4244F489-6813-4B7A-9D0C-20CE098C8FFF\",\"name\":\"Rock Landscapes and Features\"},{\"id\":\"5BE55D7F-BDB6-4E3D-AC35-2D8EBB974417\",\"name\":\"Trails\"}],\"durationDescription\":\"\",\"arePetsPermitted\":\"false\",\"timeOfDay\":[\"Day\",\"Night\",\"Dawn\",\"Dusk\"],\"duration\":\"\",\"credit\":\"\"}',1,'2023-09-10 21:06:07','2023-09-10 21:06:36'),(384,'Taylor Creek Trail','Kolob Canyons - Taylor Creek Trailhead','nps','7B0A7CE7-2553-4A51-9E7C-F1F77DC253A4',7,'From the Kolob Canyons Visitor Center in New Harmony, Utah, drive along the Kolob Canyons Road to Taylor Creek Traihead.   Restrooms available at the trailhead. Water available at the Visitor Center.  Note: The Kolob Canyons section of the park is about 1 hour from the Zion Canyon area. There is no shuttle service in Kolob Canyons. \\r\\n\\r\\n<h3>Wilderness Rules & Regulations apply:</h3> <h4>Group Size Limits</h4> <p>Large groups result in larger impacts. Trails in the Zion Wilderness have a group size limit in order to help protect the wilderness character of the area. Group size limits are strictly enforced. Groups are limited to a maximum of 12 people that share the same affiliation (e.g., school, club, scout group, colleagues, family, friends, or a combination thereof).<br /> Groups that exceed these limits may not split up and visit the same drainage or wilderness trail on the same day, but may split up and visit different areas.</p> <h4>Restrooms</h4> <p>Fill your water bottle at the Kolob Canyons Visitor Center. Restroom facilities are located at the Kolob Canyons Visitor Center and pit toilets are located at the Taylor Creek Trailhead, South Fork parking lot, and at the Kolob Canyons Viewpoint. Always use the restroom before hiking. If you are on the trail, be prepared to pack out human waste, toilet paper, diapers, and hygiene products. Human waste disposal bags are highly recommended to transport solid waste. Please follow <a href=\"https://www.nps.gov/zion/getinvolved/supportyourpark/leavenotrace.htm\">Leave No Trace</a> principles while in the park.</p> <h4>Pets</h4> <p><a href=\"https://www.nps.gov/zion/planyourvisit/pets.htm\">Pets are not permitted</a> on these trails. The only trail for pets in Zion National Park is the <a href=\"https://www.nps.gov/zion/planyourvisit/zion-canyon-trail-descriptions.htm\">Pa\'rus Trail.</a></p>','The Taylor Creek Trail leads hikers deep into a narrow box canyon toward the Double Arch Alcove, where erosion has carved out natural openings in the Navajo sandstone. As the trail reaches the mouth of the canyon it enters the Zion Wilderness and begins to crisscross Taylor Creek. Winter conditions can be very icy and water crossings in the spring will get your feet wet. The trail passes the geologic formation of the Kanarraville Fold and two historic homestead cabins built in the early 1930s before arriving at the Double Arch Alcove.',NULL,'Unpaved, uneven terrain that varies from rocky and sandy with several water crossings. Trail climbs an elevation of 450ft (137m). ','3-4 Hours','1',NULL,NULL,NULL,'https://www.nps.gov/thingstodo/taylor-creek-trail.htm',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,1,NULL,'{\"id\":\"7B0A7CE7-2553-4A51-9E7C-F1F77DC253A4\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/thingstodo\\/taylor-creek-trail.htm\",\"title\":\"Taylor Creek Trail\",\"shortDescription\":\"Kolob Canyons hike. Follows the Middle Fork of Taylor Creek past two homestead cabins to Double Arch Alcove. Group size limit on Wilderness trails is 12 people.\",\"images\":[{\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/6608454A-01D8-CF6A-A528D9C668EF9426.jpg\",\"credit\":\"NPS\\/Lizz Eberhardt\",\"altText\":\"Red sandstone cliff face with an alcove at the bottom and an arch above.\",\"title\":\"Double Arch Alcove on Taylor Creek\",\"description\":\"\",\"caption\":\"The Double Arch Alcove marks the end of the Traylor Creek Trail.\",\"crops\":[{\"aspectRatio\":\"1.78\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/primary\\/6608454A-01D8-CF6A-A528D9C668EF9426.jpg\"},{\"aspectRatio\":\"1\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/secondary\\/6608454A-01D8-CF6A-A528D9C668EF9426.jpg\"}]}],\"relatedParks\":[{\"states\":\"UT\",\"parkCode\":\"zion\",\"designation\":\"National Park\",\"fullName\":\"Zion National Park\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/zion\\/index.htm\",\"name\":\"Zion\"}],\"relatedOrganizations\":[],\"tags\":[\"Zion\",\"Kolob Canyons\",\"taylor creek\",\"out and back\",\"moderate hike\",\"Zion National Park\",\"wilderness\",\"historic\"],\"latitude\":\"\",\"longitude\":\"\",\"geometryPoiId\":\"\",\"amenities\":[],\"location\":\"Kolob Canyons - Taylor Creek Trailhead\",\"seasonDescription\":\"\",\"accessibilityInformation\":\"Unpaved, uneven terrain that varies from rocky and sandy with several water crossings. Trail climbs an elevation of 450ft (137m).\\u00a0\",\"isReservationRequired\":\"false\",\"ageDescription\":\"\",\"petsDescription\":\"Pets are not allowed on any trail in Kolob Canyons.\\u00a0\",\"timeOfDayDescription\":\"\",\"feeDescription\":\"Park entrance fees apply.\\u00a0\",\"age\":\"\",\"arePetsPermittedWithRestrictions\":\"false\",\"activities\":[{\"id\":\"BFF8C027-7C8F-480B-A5F8-CD8CE490BFBA\",\"name\":\"Hiking\"}],\"activityDescription\":\"<h3>Wilderness Rules & Regulations apply:<\\/h3> <h4>Group Size Limits<\\/h4> <p>Large groups result in larger impacts. Trails in the Zion Wilderness have a group size limit in order to help protect the wilderness character of the area. Group size limits are strictly enforced. Groups are limited to a maximum of 12 people that share the same affiliation (e.g., school, club, scout group, colleagues, family, friends, or a combination thereof).<br \\/> Groups that exceed these limits may not split up and visit the same drainage or wilderness trail on the same day, but may split up and visit different areas.<\\/p> <h4>Restrooms<\\/h4> <p>Fill your water bottle at the Kolob Canyons Visitor Center. Restroom facilities are located at the Kolob Canyons Visitor Center and pit toilets are located at the Taylor Creek Trailhead, South Fork parking lot, and at the Kolob Canyons Viewpoint. Always use the restroom before hiking. If you are on the trail, be prepared to pack out human waste, toilet paper, diapers, and hygiene products. Human waste disposal bags are highly recommended to transport solid waste. Please follow\\u00a0<a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/zion\\/getinvolved\\/supportyourpark\\/leavenotrace.htm\\\">Leave No Trace<\\/a>\\u00a0principles while in the park.<\\/p> <h4>Pets<\\/h4> <p><a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/zion\\/planyourvisit\\/pets.htm\\\">Pets are not permitted<\\/a>\\u00a0on these trails. The only trail for pets in Zion National Park is the\\u00a0<a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/zion\\/planyourvisit\\/zion-canyon-trail-descriptions.htm\\\">Pa\'rus Trail.<\\/a><\\/p>\",\"locationDescription\":\"From the Kolob Canyons Visitor Center in New Harmony, Utah, drive along the Kolob Canyons Road to Taylor Creek Traihead.\\u00a0<br \\/> <br \\/> Restrooms available at the trailhead. Water available at the Visitor Center.<br \\/> <br \\/> <em>Note: The Kolob Canyons section of the park is about 1 hour from the Zion Canyon area. There is no shuttle service in Kolob Canyons.\\u00a0<\\/em>\",\"doFeesApply\":\"false\",\"longDescription\":\"The Taylor Creek Trail leads hikers deep into a narrow box canyon toward the Double Arch Alcove, where erosion has carved out natural openings in the Navajo sandstone. As the trail reaches the mouth of the canyon it enters the Zion Wilderness and begins to crisscross Taylor Creek. Winter conditions can be very icy and water crossings in the spring will get your feet wet. The trail passes the geologic formation of the Kanarraville Fold and two historic homestead cabins built in the early 1930s before arriving at the Double Arch Alcove.\",\"reservationDescription\":\"\",\"season\":[\"Winter\",\"Spring\",\"Summer\",\"Fall\"],\"topics\":[{\"id\":\"5BE55D7F-BDB6-4E3D-AC35-2D8EBB974417\",\"name\":\"Trails\"}],\"durationDescription\":\"\",\"arePetsPermitted\":\"false\",\"timeOfDay\":[\"Day\",\"Night\",\"Dawn\",\"Dusk\"],\"duration\":\"3-4 Hours\",\"credit\":\"\"}',1,'2023-09-10 21:06:07','2023-09-10 21:06:36'),(385,'Grotto Trail','Grotto Trailhead','nps','64535736-992B-4D04-8BF6-3F1965A6A01A',7,'The Grotto Trail can be accessed from behind the shuttle stop at the Zion Lodge (shuttle stop #5) or behind the restrooms at The Grotto (shuttle stop #6).\\r\\n\\r\\nThe Grotto Trail connects shuttle stop #5 at the Zion Lodge to shuttle stop #6 at The Grotto.','The Grotto Trail is an easy, 1.0 mi (1.6 km) round trip hike that connects the Zion Lodge to The Grotto Picnic Area. This relatively flat and shaded hike is good for a leisure walk in the park, or it can be combined with the Emerald Pools and Kayenta Trails to create a loop. Look for wildlife such as birds, turkey, and deer along this walk.  Restrooms are available at both the Lodge and the Grotto.',NULL,'Unpaved, sandy trail that climbs 35 ft (11 m) of elevation. The trail is mostly wide and flat, but one section is rocky and narrow.','15-45 Minutes','1',NULL,NULL,NULL,'https://www.nps.gov/thingstodo/hike-grotto-trail.htm',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,1,NULL,'{\"id\":\"64535736-992B-4D04-8BF6-3F1965A6A01A\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/thingstodo\\/hike-grotto-trail.htm\",\"title\":\"Grotto Trail\",\"shortDescription\":\"The trail connects the Zion Lodge to the Grotto and is near the road most of the way. This trail is a great place to view wildlife. Restrooms are available at both the Lodge and the Grotto. When the shuttle is not in operation and the parking is full at the Grotto, this trail is a lovely alternate way to reach the Grotto.\",\"images\":[{\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/EAB97966-A970-0337-676E0D30BBC362C3.jpg\",\"credit\":\"NPS\",\"altText\":\"A tan path crossing green grass into a stand of trees. A red sandstone mountain is to the left.\",\"title\":\"Grotto Trail\",\"description\":\"\",\"caption\":\"View from the Grotto Trail\",\"crops\":[{\"aspectRatio\":\"1.78\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/primary\\/EAB97966-A970-0337-676E0D30BBC362C3.jpg\"},{\"aspectRatio\":\"1\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/secondary\\/EAB97966-A970-0337-676E0D30BBC362C3.jpg\"}]}],\"relatedParks\":[{\"states\":\"UT\",\"parkCode\":\"zion\",\"designation\":\"National Park\",\"fullName\":\"Zion National Park\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/zion\\/index.htm\",\"name\":\"Zion\"}],\"relatedOrganizations\":[],\"tags\":[\"easy\",\"grotto\",\"out and back\",\"Zion\",\"Zion Canyon\",\"Zion National Park\",\"kid friendly\",\"short hike\"],\"latitude\":\"\",\"longitude\":\"\",\"geometryPoiId\":\"\",\"amenities\":[],\"location\":\"Grotto Trailhead\",\"seasonDescription\":\"\",\"accessibilityInformation\":\"Unpaved, sandy trail that climbs 35 ft (11 m) of elevation. The trail is mostly wide and flat, but one section is rocky and narrow.\",\"isReservationRequired\":\"false\",\"ageDescription\":\"\",\"petsDescription\":\"\",\"timeOfDayDescription\":\"\",\"feeDescription\":\"Park entrance fees apply.\\u00a0\",\"age\":\"\",\"arePetsPermittedWithRestrictions\":\"false\",\"activities\":[{\"id\":\"BFF8C027-7C8F-480B-A5F8-CD8CE490BFBA\",\"name\":\"Hiking\"}],\"activityDescription\":\"The Grotto Trail connects shuttle stop #5 at the Zion Lodge to shuttle stop #6 at The Grotto.\",\"locationDescription\":\"The Grotto Trail can be accessed from behind the shuttle stop at the Zion Lodge (shuttle stop #5) or behind the restrooms at The Grotto (shuttle stop #6).\",\"doFeesApply\":\"false\",\"longDescription\":\"The Grotto Trail is an easy, 1.0 mi (1.6 km) round trip hike that connects the Zion Lodge to The Grotto Picnic Area. This relatively flat and shaded hike is good for a leisure walk in the park, or it can be combined with the Emerald Pools and Kayenta Trails to create a loop. Look for wildlife such as birds, turkey, and deer along this walk.<br \\/> <br \\/> Restrooms are available at both the Lodge and the Grotto.\",\"reservationDescription\":\"\",\"season\":[\"Winter\",\"Spring\",\"Summer\",\"Fall\"],\"topics\":[{\"id\":\"5BE55D7F-BDB6-4E3D-AC35-2D8EBB974417\",\"name\":\"Trails\"}],\"durationDescription\":\"\",\"arePetsPermitted\":\"false\",\"timeOfDay\":[\"Day\",\"Night\",\"Dawn\",\"Dusk\"],\"duration\":\"15-45 Minutes\",\"credit\":\"\"}',1,'2023-09-10 21:06:07','2023-09-10 21:06:36'),(386,'Watchman Trail','Watchman Trailhead','nps','671CA42A-5287-4EC6-AD13-4841D2F9B724',7,'To reach the trailhead from the Zion Canyon Visitor Center, follow the sidewalk through the Visitor Center plaza and past the shuttle stop to the crosswalk. Stay along the river to begin the trail.\\r\\n\\r\\n','The Watchman Trail is a moderate out-and-back hike accessed from the Zion Canyon Visitor Center. To reach the trailhead, follow the sidewalk through the Visitor Center plaza and past the shuttle stop to the crosswalk. Stay along the river to begin the trail.  From the relatively flat beginning section of trail by the river, this unpaved trail climbs 368 ft (112 m) to a magnificent viewpoint of the Watchman, Temples and Towers, lower Zion Canyon, and the Town of Springdale. The terrain is sandy, rocky, and uneven, with several moderate drop-offs.  Restrooms and water filling stations are available at the Visitor Center.  Bikes are not permitted.',NULL,'Trail is not paved. Sandy, rocky, and uneven terrain with a moderate climb in elevation. Moderate drop-offs throughout with a cliffside overlook at the end.','1-2 Hours','1',NULL,NULL,NULL,'https://www.nps.gov/thingstodo/hike-watchman-trail.htm',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,1,NULL,'{\"id\":\"671CA42A-5287-4EC6-AD13-4841D2F9B724\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/thingstodo\\/hike-watchman-trail.htm\",\"title\":\"Watchman Trail\",\"shortDescription\":\"The Watchman trail is out-and-back. From the relatively flat beginning section of trail by the river, this unpaved trail climbs 368 ft (112 m) to a magnificent viewpoint of the Watchman, Temples and Towers, lower Zion Canyon, and the Town of Springdale. The terrain is sandy, rocky, and uneven, with several moderate drop-offs.\",\"images\":[{\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/B552513D-B184-71EB-93B3DB369AC99AE0.jpg\",\"credit\":\"NPS\\/Abi Farish\",\"altText\":\"View from a sandy overlook of a triangular sandstone mountain with green foliage on the foothills.\",\"title\":\"Watchman Overlook\",\"description\":\"\",\"caption\":\"View from the overlook at the end of Watchman Trail\",\"crops\":[{\"aspectRatio\":\"1.78\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/primary\\/B552513D-B184-71EB-93B3DB369AC99AE0.jpg\"},{\"aspectRatio\":\"1\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/common\\/uploads\\/cropped_image\\/secondary\\/B552513D-B184-71EB-93B3DB369AC99AE0.jpg\"}]}],\"relatedParks\":[{\"states\":\"UT\",\"parkCode\":\"zion\",\"designation\":\"National Park\",\"fullName\":\"Zion National Park\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.nps.gov\\/zion\\/index.htm\",\"name\":\"Zion\"}],\"relatedOrganizations\":[],\"tags\":[\"Watchman\",\"Zion Canyon\",\"hiking\",\"moderate hike\",\"overlook\",\"Zion National Park\",\"out and back\"],\"latitude\":\"\",\"longitude\":\"\",\"geometryPoiId\":\"\",\"amenities\":[],\"location\":\"Watchman Trailhead\",\"seasonDescription\":\"This hike is accessible throughout the year. The trail may be muddy or icy during the winter and monsoon season. Limited shade in the summer.\",\"accessibilityInformation\":\"Trail is not paved. Sandy, rocky, and uneven terrain with a moderate climb in elevation. Moderate drop-offs throughout with a cliffside overlook at the end.\",\"isReservationRequired\":\"false\",\"ageDescription\":\"\",\"petsDescription\":\"\",\"timeOfDayDescription\":\"\",\"feeDescription\":\"Park entrance fees apply.\\u00a0\",\"age\":\"\",\"arePetsPermittedWithRestrictions\":\"false\",\"activities\":[{\"id\":\"BFF8C027-7C8F-480B-A5F8-CD8CE490BFBA\",\"name\":\"Hiking\"}],\"activityDescription\":\"\",\"locationDescription\":\"To reach the trailhead from the Zion Canyon Visitor Center, follow the sidewalk through the Visitor Center plaza and past the shuttle stop to the crosswalk. Stay along the river to begin the trail.\",\"doFeesApply\":\"false\",\"longDescription\":\"The Watchman Trail is a moderate out-and-back hike accessed from the Zion Canyon Visitor Center. To reach the trailhead, follow the sidewalk through the Visitor Center plaza and past the shuttle stop to the crosswalk. Stay along the river to begin the trail.<br \\/> <br \\/> From the relatively flat beginning section of trail by the river, this unpaved trail climbs 368 ft (112 m) to a magnificent viewpoint of the Watchman, Temples and Towers, lower Zion Canyon, and the Town of Springdale. The terrain is sandy, rocky, and uneven, with several moderate drop-offs.<br \\/> <br \\/> Restrooms and water filling stations are available at the Visitor Center.<br \\/> <br \\/> Bikes are not permitted.\",\"reservationDescription\":\"\",\"season\":[\"Winter\",\"Spring\",\"Summer\",\"Fall\"],\"topics\":[{\"id\":\"5BE55D7F-BDB6-4E3D-AC35-2D8EBB974417\",\"name\":\"Trails\"}],\"durationDescription\":\"Watchman trail is 3.3 miles (5.3 km) roundtrip. On average, it takes about 2 hours to complete.\",\"arePetsPermitted\":\"false\",\"timeOfDay\":[\"Day\",\"Night\",\"Dawn\",\"Dusk\"],\"duration\":\"1-2 Hours\",\"credit\":\"\"}',1,'2023-09-10 21:06:07','2023-09-10 21:06:36'),(387,'Spencer Trail','8011899','hikingproject','7006104',4,'This challenging and rugged trail has views that only improve the higher you go.  The distance is short, but the trail gains elevation quickly through tricky rock and scree, making this a more strenuous undertaking than it might appear initially.  \r\n\r\nAfter passing the historical buildings of Lees Fort and the nearby mining debris, follow the wide dirt road over flat terrain upriver.  Keep an eye out for the sunken wreck of a paddle boat and more mining relics.  The exposed and shadeless Spencer Trail branches off to the left (northeast) leaving the river and starting to ascend through the hills.  Alternately traverse exposed cliffs, thread through boulders and climb sandy stone steps to gain precious elevation.  After another set of steeper, sustained switchbacks, you reach the plateau.  Spend some time exploring at the top to experience all the following views:  Page, Paria River, Lake Powell, Glen Canyon, Echo Peaks, Vermilion Cliffs, Navajo Mountian, Lee\'s Ferry.\r\n\r\nHistorical notes:  this trail was named after Charles Spencer who was in charge of the gold mining operations here.  He built the Spencer Trail to transport coal to the mine from Utah.','This trail consists of a steep series of switchbacking steps to a fantastic view of amazing geology.',NULL,NULL,NULL,'No Dogs',36.8663569,-111.5867932,'Views',NULL,'Glen Canyon National Recreation Area','http://www.nps.gov/glca/index.htm',NULL,'Difficult',4659,3134,NULL,1.9,'Point to Point',NULL,1586,64,0.16,0.36,NULL,NULL,'https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/gpx/7006104','8011899 | https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/7006104/spencer-trail',1,'2023-09-13 21:03:49','2023-09-19 06:00:30'),(388,'Horseshoe Bend Trail','8011899','hikingproject','7045597',5,'Family Friendly\n            \n                An easy hike...and amazing views of a natural wonder!','A sandy but easy trail to an unbelievable view and natural wonder!',NULL,NULL,NULL,'Leashed',36.8766765,-111.5028636,'Views',NULL,'Glen Canyon National Recreation Area','http://www.nps.gov/glca/index.htm',NULL,'Easy/Intermediate',4356,3914,NULL,0.7,'Point to Point',NULL,110,531,0.16,2.73,NULL,NULL,'https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/gpx/7045597','8011899 | https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/7045597/horseshoe-bend-trail',1,'2023-09-13 21:03:51','2023-09-19 06:00:30'),(389,'Antelope Canyon Trail','8011899','hikingproject','7047726',5,NULL,'Needs Summary',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,36.9024983,-111.4112624,'',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'Easy/Intermediate',4104,3967,NULL,0.6,'Loop',NULL,135,138,0.09,0.22,NULL,NULL,'https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/gpx/7047726','8011899 | https://www.hikingproject.com/trail/7047726/antelope-canyon-trail',1,'2023-09-13 21:03:51','2023-09-19 06:00:30');
